window.BOOK = {"parts": [{"title": "Book One", "sub": "The Vanishing", "chapters": [{"title": "The Kitchen, 3:47am", "kind": "main", "src": "opening", "n": 1, "part": "Book One. The Vanishing", "blocks": [{"img": "res_apartmentempty.jpg", "cap": "The kitchen of the Uedera flat before four in the morning"}, {"p": "The plate was still upside down on the counter."}, {"p": "Rei stood in the dark kitchen and looked at it. A white plate turned over a second plate, the way he had left it at nine o'clock. Under it was rice and the ginger pork Gin liked, gone cold and grey. Nobody had lifted it. Nobody had eaten."}, {"p": "He was holding the kettle. He did not remember picking it up. The clock on the stove said 3:47."}, {"p": "The flat was very quiet. It was two small rooms and a kitchen, built for two people, and tonight it held only one. Outside, Tokyo had reached the hour when even the trucks go quiet. Somewhere below, a vending machine hummed on the corner of the street. There was one on almost every corner in the city, lit up all night, selling cold tea and canned coffee to nobody."}, {"p": "It was August 2015, the hottest part of the Tokyo summer. Even at this hour the air was thick and warm, and the window stood open for any breeze that might come. In a few days Obon would begin. Obon is the time in August when, people believe, the dead come home. For three or four days families clean their graves, light small fires at the door to show the spirits the way, hang paper lanterns and leave food on the family altar. Then they send the dead back again. Many people were already leaving the city on crowded trains to visit their hometowns and family graves. The people who stayed were hanging lanterns in the shopping streets."}, {"p": "In most places Obon would only be a custom. In Japan in 2015 it was a little more than that. Everyone knew the veil was real, and everyone knew it grew thinner in this week."}, {"p": "The flat was in Kita, the north ward of Tokyo. Kita was not a famous part of the city. It was a place of narrow streets, old apartment blocks, small family shops and railway lines, with the wide Arakawa river along its northern edge. Most people who lived there had lived there a long time. Rei had lived there all his life, in this flat, on a hill above Ōji station, with his brother."}, {"img": "char_uedera-gin.jpg", "cap": "Uedera Gin"}, {"p": "His brother was Uedera Gin. He was twenty-eight, nine years older than Rei, a tall man with long black hair that he wore loose past his jaw and never remembered to cut. Gin was a field officer for the Civil Veil Division."}, {"p": "Everyone in Japan knew what the Civil Veil Division was, the way they knew what the fire brigade was. Between the world of the living and the world of the dead there is a thin boundary, and people call it the veil. Usually it holds. Sometimes it grows thin, and things come through. Some of those things are only lost and sad. Some are dangerous. The Division was the government office that dealt with them. People called it the CVD. Its officers went into the houses and streets where the veil had gone wrong and put things right, or tried to."}, {"p": "So Gin kept strange hours. Everyone who knew him knew that. His work did not stop at five. But he called. He always called. Ten minutes late meant a message. An hour late meant a phone call and a voice saying, “Eat without me, I'm fine, go to bed.” In nineteen years he had never once let a night end without a word."}, {"p": "Rei checked his phone. No message. He checked it again, because lately he checked everything twice. The lock. The stove. The date. For a few weeks now the city had felt to him like a held breath, and he had told nobody, because there was nothing to tell. It was just a feeling. It was just a habit he did not like."}, {"img": "char_rei.jpg", "cap": "Uedera Rei, nineteen"}, {"p": "In the black glass of the window his own face looked back at him. Tired eyes. A jaw more like Gin's than his own. <em>Rei,</em> he thought, as if he needed the face in the glass to agree that this was his name. It agreed. He did not know why that felt like a relief."}, {"p": "He switched on the living-room lamp. He never did that at night. But lately the dark had started to feel as if it was paying attention, and he wanted it to stop."}, {"p": "Gin's door was shut. Rei knocked once, softly, feeling stupid, and opened it."}, {"img": "res_apartmentginroom.jpg", "cap": "Gin's room, the bed made and the desk lamp burning"}, {"p": "The bed was made with square corners. The chair was pushed all the way under the desk. And the desk lamp was on. A small yellow circle of light lay on the bare wood, as if Gin had been working under it a minute ago and had stepped out to take a call. Nothing lay in the circle. No pen, no paper, no cup."}, {"p": "On the shelf above the desk stood the notebook Gin wrote in at night. He always closed it when Rei came near. Leaning against the base of the lamp was a photograph of the two of them years ago, squinting into the sun on some beach Rei could barely remember. Gin kept it where he could see it from the bed."}, {"p": "The room did not feel empty. It felt left behind. Rei could not tell if that was true, or if he was doing what Gin had always warned him about. Finding shapes in the quiet, because the quiet frightened him."}, {"p": "Back in the kitchen he filled the kettle and did not light the gas. Two cups sat upside down on the rack. Gin always set out two, though he was almost never home for the second."}, {"p": "Then Rei looked at the hook by the front door, and his stomach went cold."}, {"p": "Gin's keys were not on it."}, {"p": "The hook hung in the genkan. In a Japanese home the genkan is the small entrance just inside the front door, a step lower than the rest of the floor, where everyone takes off their shoes before stepping up into the house. Gin's keys had hung there every day since Rei was small enough to be lifted up to reach them. Gin put them on the hook first thing every morning, before coffee, before anything, the way some people touch a doorframe for luck. The brass hook was worn bright where his thumb went."}, {"p": "Rei had a private joke about it. Some mornings he slid the keys a few centimetres along the hook to see if Gin would notice. Gin never said a word. Rei had always taken that to mean he hadn't."}, {"p": "Now the hook was bare."}, {"p": "He crossed to the front door. It was not locked. It was not even shut. The latch rested against the frame without catching, and the door stood a finger's width open onto the night."}, {"p": "Rei had lived in this flat his whole life. He had never once known Gin to leave the door like that."}, {"p": "He pushed it open. Like most small apartment buildings in Tokyo, theirs had no inside hallway. Each front door opened onto a long concrete walkway along the outside of the building, with a railing on one side and the street below. The walkway was empty. The orange street lamp lit the railing, the bicycles parked below and the row of closed doors, exactly as it did every night. Two doors down, a neighbour's futon was still hung over the rail to air. Nothing moved. The city went on being large and ordinary and uninterested, and somehow that was worse than if something had moved."}, {"p": "Something glinted on the concrete just past the doorstep."}, {"p": "A key. A single brass door key on a plain ring, lying where a hand had dropped it or set it down. Rei crouched and picked it up. It was cold, properly cold, the cold of metal that has lain out all night. Beside it, crushed flat, lay the stub of a cigarette smoked exactly halfway."}, {"p": "It was Gin's brand. Gin smoked half a cigarette on the step every morning and then put it out, because Rei complained about the smell inside. He never smoked at night."}, {"p": "Rei looked left along the walkway, then right, then down to the street. Empty. Asleep. Perfect. There was nothing else at all, and that was the part that stayed with him afterward. How completely nothing else there was."}, {"p": "He went back inside and shut the door, and this time he heard the latch catch."}, {"p": "Only then did he see the book on the kitchen table. He must have walked past it twice."}, {"p": "It was Gin's archive. That was what Gin called it. It was a thick field book with soft corners, and Gin carried it everywhere, into places Rei was not allowed to know about and out again, year after year. Gin did not leave it on tables. He did not leave it anywhere. And yet here it lay, open, squared to the edge of the table, as if someone had wanted it found by the first person to sit down."}, {"p": "It was open at the first page. The handwriting was Gin's, small and exact, the print of a man who trusted ink more than memory. Rei read it standing up."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Personal Archive. Opening Note", "lines": ["If you found this: the keys were on the hook because I put them there. The lamp was on because I was working. The door was not quite closed because I heard something in the corridor and I looked up and then I kept writing and I forgot to go back and close it.", "Whatever you are looking for... the world is in here. I documented everything I know. Six years of field work, case files, entity taxonomy, practitioner profiles, the political landscape of who controls what and why. The archive is complete. It functions without me.", "Start at chapter one. Read in order the first time. The world builds on itself and some things will not make sense until other things do. The index is in the field kit, left pocket.", "I was going to annotate the later sections more thoroughly. I ran out of time. The notes in the margins are the annotations. Read the margins.", "Uedera Gin. Kita-ku, Tokyo, 2015."]}}, {"p": "Rei read it again. Then he looked up."}, {"p": "The lamp in Gin's room was burning. That part was true."}, {"p": "The front door had not been quite closed. That part was true."}, {"p": "The hook by the door was empty."}, {"p": "The note had been written for a night when Gin heard something outside, looked up, and went back to his writing. It had not been written for a night when he got up from the table and went out to see what it was."}, {"p": "Rei turned the page with a hand that was not quite steady. The next page was headed <em>The World</em>. It was dense, careful writing about openings and membranes, the kind of thing Gin said at dinner when he was tired and forgot who he was talking to. Halfway down, one sentence stopped Rei so hard that he put his palm flat on the table."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Personal Archive. The World", "lines": ["The archive you are reading is set in the Kita district of the city. A specific apartment. A desk lamp that was on because I was working. A field investigator's archive, open on the desk.", "If you are reading this at 3:47 in the morning: that is approximately when Rei found it. That detail is in the archive for a reason. Keep reading."]}}, {"p": "He looked at the stove clock. It said 3:52."}, {"p": "He made himself think it through slowly. Either Gin had known, weeks or months ago, the exact hour of the exact night his brother would come to this table. Or somebody had written that line since. Rei did not know which was worse. He found he did not want to know yet."}, {"p": "He closed the book. He put it in his bag. He put the cold key in his pocket, where it stayed cold against his leg. Then he let himself think the sentence all the way to the end, the one he had been holding off since he saw the empty hook."}, {"p": "<em>Something has happened to Gin.</em>"}, {"p": "There. It was in the room now. He could not put it back."}, {"p": "He could wait until morning. He could call the Division and let the proper people fail to find his brother. Nobody would blame him. Rei stood in the kitchen with the book heavy on his shoulder and knew he was not going to wait. He stepped down into the genkan and pushed his feet into his shoes without untying them, the way Gin always told him not to."}, {"break": "The slope down to Ōji"}, {"p": "Itsuki answered his message before he reached the bottom of the stairs."}, {"p": "Kuroda Itsuki was awake. Itsuki was always awake at this hour, painting or writing code or both, and he had been Rei's best friend since they were twelve. Rei had typed four lines with his thumb while he locked the door. <em>Gin didn't come home. Door was open. His key was on the walkway. I'm going down to Ōji.</em>"}, {"p": "<em>I'm up,</em> Itsuki wrote back. <em>I was going to message you. He didn't answer me either, since Wednesday. Tell me what you need and I'll find it.</em>"}, {"p": "Rei did not need to say where he was going. They both knew Gin's way home. It ran down the long slope to the overpass at Ōji, and the overpass was the last place anyone could say for certain that Gin had been ordinary."}, {"p": "The street outside was warm and damp, like the inside of a mouth. Rei walked down the hill past houses with their shutters closed and their air conditioners dripping onto the pavement. Above the trees of Asukayama, the small park that rises on a hill beside Ōji station, the sky was still black. In the daytime Asukayama was full of children and old men playing shogi on benches. At this hour it was a dark shape with no sound in it at all. The cicadas had not started yet. In an hour they would be screaming in every tree."}, {"img": "place_konbini_night.jpg", "cap": "A konbini on a Kita corner before dawn"}, {"p": "At the bottom of the slope a konbini glowed white on the corner. A konbini is a Japanese convenience store, small and bright and open all night, where people buy rice balls, coffee, stamps and phone credit at any hour. Through the glass Rei saw the young clerk asleep on his feet behind the counter, his head nodding. Someone had already taped a paper lantern by the door for Obon, white with blue writing. A newspaper delivery man went past on a small motorbike, the first of the morning, and did not look at Rei. Rei kept walking, and the light of the konbini fell away behind him."}, {"p": "Rei had crossed the Ōji overpass a thousand times without a thought. Tonight, halfway across, he walked into cold."}, {"p": "It was not a draught. It was a column of cold air that did not belong to August. It stood in the middle of the walkway like a person waiting for someone. The hair rose on his arms. Under the noise of the first delivery trucks he heard something very small. A sound like a child trying not to cough."}, {"p": "He stopped."}, {"p": "Something was caught there. He could not have said how he knew. It was the same way he knew when a room had just had an argument in it, or when a man on the train was about to cry. Gin called it noticing. Rei called it nothing at all, because it was just how his head worked."}, {"p": "The thing in the cold felt him notice it. It went very still, the way small animals go still when something large finally looks their way."}, {"p": "“It's all right,” Rei said under his breath, to nobody, to it. “I'm not going to do anything.”"}, {"p": "He stood there with his hands in his pockets until the trucks had gone past. Slowly the cold grew thinner. Before it went, it let something go, the way a fist opens at the end."}, {"p": "It was not words. It was a picture, laid in his mind as clearly as a photograph. This bridge, at night. A man crossing it, walking north. The street lamp behind him. And his shadow falling the wrong way. A shadow should stretch away from the light. This one did not. It lay ahead of the man on the concrete, leading him, as if something walked half a second in front of the man it belonged to."}, {"p": "It was the night Gin stopped answering."}, {"p": "Then the cold was gone. The overpass was only an overpass, and Rei was standing in the middle of it with his heart beating hard."}, {"p": "He typed it into the notes on his phone before he could start doubting it. <em>A man with the wrong shadow. Ōji overpass. The night Gin vanished.</em>"}, {"p": "Then he looked down."}, {"p": "The street lamp was behind him. His own shadow lay behind him too, long and thin, where a shadow belongs. He watched it for a long moment anyway, to be sure it stayed there."}, {"p": "His phone buzzed. It was Itsuki. <em>Come to the studio. Don't walk back over the bridge.</em>"}, {"p": "A second message came a moment later. <em>I think I've been painting it.</em>"}]}, {"title": "The Friend", "kind": "main", "src": "itsuki", "n": 2, "part": "Book One. The Vanishing", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei did not walk back over the bridge. He went the long way round to Ōji station and waited outside the gates with two drunk students and a cleaner in a grey uniform until the station opened. The first train of the morning came in at last. It was a Keihin-Tōhoku line train, silver with a sky-blue stripe down its side, the line that runs north to south through Kita and on into the middle of Tokyo. Rei touched his Suica card to the gate, and the gate gave its small electronic beep, the sound of every day in the city."}, {"p": "The carriage was nearly empty and much too cold. The air conditioning on Tokyo trains in August is always too cold. A man in a white shirt slept across from him with his arms folded over his briefcase and his tie loose. At the far end a young woman in a waitress uniform ate a rice ball very slowly, looking at nothing. Station names went past in a calm recorded voice. Tabata. Nippori. Uguisudani. Outside, the sky over the rooftops was turning from black to a dirty grey."}, {"p": "At Ueno he got off and climbed up through the park. Ueno park sits on a hill above the station, full of old trees, museums and temples. At this hour it belonged to the crows and to a few homeless men asleep on flattened cardboard under the trees. The first cicadas were starting, one at a time, like engines warming up. On the far side of the park, behind a long wall, stood Geidai. Geidai is the short name for Tokyo University of the Arts, the most famous art school in Japan. Itsuki had a studio there, high up in an old building, and he often worked in it through the night."}, {"img": "scene_itsuki_dawn.jpg", "cap": "Rei and Itsuki in the studio at dawn, among the eleven bridges"}, {"p": "Eleven bridges leaned against the walls of the studio, and every one of them was wrong."}, {"p": "Rei counted them while Itsuki locked the door behind him. There were eleven canvases of the Ōji overpass, in every light. Dawn, rain, a summer noon so white it hurt. Some had been scraped back to the bare cloth and painted over. One had been slashed once with a palette knife and then carefully taped together from behind. In all of them, in the middle of the walkway, there was a place where the paint went muddy and strange. It was as if Itsuki's brush had kept trying to put something there and he had kept refusing to let it."}, {"p": "The room smelled of linseed oil and cold konbini coffee. Through the high windows the art school was grey and asleep. The cleaners would not come for another hour."}, {"p": "“I never sold one,” Itsuki said. “People ask. I always say they're not finished.” He laughed, and it did not work. “They're not finished.”"}, {"img": "char_kuroda-itsuki.jpg", "cap": "Kuroda Itsuki, with paint under every nail"}, {"p": "Kuroda Itsuki was twenty. He had a grown-out bleached streak in his black hair, paint under every nail, and a laptop he had rebuilt three times balanced on a stool beside the easel, its screen full of code. He and Rei had been friends since middle school. For the last two years he had also been Gin's illustrator. Gin brought him pages of strange symbols, circles and broken lines and knots, and asked him to draw them clean. Itsuki drew them and thought they were diagrams. He had also built Gin a search program for the archive, so Gin could find a case by a single word."}, {"p": "“Tell me,” Itsuki said. “All of it. Don't make it nice.”"}, {"p": "Rei told him. The plate. The lamp. The open door, the cold key and the half-smoked cigarette. The column of cold on the overpass, and what the cold had shown him."}, {"p": "When he got to the shadow, Itsuki's brush stopped moving."}, {"p": "He laid it across the jar, very carefully, and turned to look at the canvases. He looked at them the way a person looks at a dog that has growled for the first time."}, {"p": "“A shadow that goes ahead,” he said. “Against the light.”"}, {"p": "“You know what it means.”"}, {"p": "“I know what my grandmother said it meant. I was six.” Itsuki rubbed his face and left a smear of blue on his cheekbone. “It means there are two of them. The man, and something that walks with him. Or in him. She said you could tell a man who'd been ridden because his shadow got there first.” He pointed with his chin at the muddy places in the paintings. “That's why they keep coming out wrong. I've been painting something that was already standing there.”"}, {"p": "Rei had always known the Kuroda were not an ordinary family. Itsuki had told him that much at fourteen, in a voice that meant <em>do not ask me again</em>. They were one of the old families Gin called the Towers. These were families with power in the blood, passed down for centuries, and old money to match. The gift in the Kuroda blood was teleportation, the power to go from one place to another without crossing the space between. In Itsuki it had never opened. Every test anyone had ever done on him came back blank, as if there were nothing there at all. So the family had steered him away from their work and sent him to art school, and he had gone gladly."}, {"p": "He had told Rei the rest only once, drunk, on a roof. The gift had not opened properly. That was different from not opening at all."}, {"p": "“I can get us closer,” Itsuki said now. “To wherever that man went. Tonight. Before it goes cold.”"}, {"p": "“How?”"}, {"p": "Itsuki did not answer at once. He went to the nearest canvas, the slashed one, and put two fingers flat against the painted concrete of the bridge. Rei watched the paint around his fingertips. For a second he could have sworn the surface gave a little, like skin."}, {"p": "“My own oils hold best,” Itsuki said quietly. “The place has to still be there. It has to still look like I painted it, or I come out somewhere close and wrong. Two copies of the same picture make a door between them. It never goes backwards in time. I tried.” He took his fingers away. There was a faint pale print on the paint. “It hurts going through. And every time, it takes a bit more.”"}, {"p": "“A bit more of what?”"}, {"p": "Itsuki shrugged. “It's fine.”"}, {"p": "He said it in exactly the voice that had always meant it was not."}, {"p": "“And tonight the bridge doesn't look like my pictures,” he went on, before Rei could push. “Something's standing on it. The door would put me where it wanted.” He turned around. “Or there's my family. Sei can reach anywhere in the ward. It wouldn't cost me a thing.” His mouth twisted. “It would cost you. They'll want to know everything. A Kuroda who knows your business is a debt with no number on it.”"}, {"p": "Rei looked at the pale print on the canvas. He looked at the blue smear on his friend's face and the shadows under his eyes."}, {"p": "“Your family,” he said. “If somebody owes them, it's me.”"}, {"p": "Itsuki opened his mouth to argue. Then he closed it and picked up his jacket. That was how Rei knew how frightened he was."}, {"p": "They walked. Nezu lies just below the hill of Ueno park, on its western side, and it takes ten minutes to go down into it on foot. It is one of the oldest corners of Tokyo, a maze of narrow lanes, wooden houses, potted plants on doorsteps and small temples squeezed between homes. Itsuki talked about the tile on the way down, about his brother and the house. Rei listened and watched the lanes. A cat sat on a wall and did not move as they passed. An old woman was already out in her cotton apron, sweeping the step in front of her house, and she stopped sweeping to watch the two young men go by."}, {"img": "tower_kuroda.jpg", "cap": "The Kuroda family, the Teleportation house in Nezu"}, {"p": "Nezu was still dark at the bottom of the hill. Old money lived here, and old silence. The Kuroda compound did not have a sign. It had a wall that had outlived three governments, a gate of black wood, and over everything a stillness that was not peace. It was attention. The whole house felt like something holding its breath to hear better."}, {"p": "Itsuki talked all the way up the lane, fast and low, the way he did when he was scared."}, {"p": "“There are seven of them. Seven families in Japan. Gin had a whole chapter.” He kicked a stone. “They were doing this for centuries before the government admitted any of it was real, and they've never forgiven the government for being late. Mine are the strangest of the seven. We don't sit on councils. We don't go to the parties. The Division asks for help, and if the case is interesting the family helps, and if it isn't the family doesn't, and nobody can make them.” He almost smiled. “The Division stopped trying years ago. They just send very polite letters.”"}, {"p": "A woman was waiting at the gate. She had Itsuki's cheekbones and none of his softness. She did not look at Rei at all. She looked at the air behind him, as if she could read in it the exact path he had walked up the lane."}, {"p": "“Tempered,” Itsuki breathed, before they reached her. “My cousin. They started her at four.”"}, {"p": "Rei did not know the word. He kept it to think about later."}, {"p": "“You brought an outsider,” the cousin said to Itsuki."}, {"p": "Her hand rested on the gatepost. Rei saw that a mark had been cut into the wood under her fingers. It was a sigil, a magic sign, and it sat just at the edge of what his eyes could hold. It looked like the symbols Gin had brought Itsuki to draw. But it was not a drawing. Rei could feel it in his teeth."}, {"p": "“He's Gin Uedera's brother,” Itsuki said."}, {"p": "Something passed over the cousin's face and was put away. “Then he should not be here at all.”"}, {"p": "“His brother's missing. A man crossed the Ōji bridge the night it happened with his shadow in front of him.”"}, {"p": "The cousin was quiet for a moment. When she spoke again her voice had dropped."}, {"p": "“A wrong shadow carrying weight,” she said. “You're describing a seated one.”"}, {"p": "She did not explain. She did not need to. The way she said it made the dark lane colder. Rei understood that whatever a seated one was, this family had been afraid of it for longer than the Division had existed."}, {"p": "Itsuki had gone pale. “Don't make me use the name,” he said to her, very low. “If I spend it to get Sei, I don't get it back. And everyone will know what I spent it on.”"}, {"p": "The cousin studied him the way a buyer studies a price. Then she stepped aside."}, {"p": "Kuroda Sei received them in a room with nothing in it but a low table, the straw tatami mats on the floor and the grey beginnings of light at the paper screens."}, {"p": "He was Itsuki's older brother. He was twenty-two, Rei would learn later, and younger than Rei expected for the person a whole house went quiet for. He was the stillest human being Rei had ever seen. He did not fidget. He did not blink often. He did not fill silences. His weight sat slightly back, as if he were always ready to be somewhere else."}, {"p": "On the table lay two things. One was a stone the colour of an old bruise, set in lacquer so old it had turned to something like glass. The other was a piece of grey roof tile, ordinary and chipped. Sei's right hand rested on the tile. There was a scar across the back of it."}, {"p": "Itsuki had told Rei about the tile on the way down the hill. It came from the oldest building in the Kuroda district, which was torn down when Sei was fourteen. Sei had climbed in and taken a piece before it came down. Their parents had left Japan three years later, pushed out by people Itsuki would not name, and Sei had stayed to keep the family's ground. Alone. At seventeen."}, {"p": "Sei looked at Rei for a long time."}, {"p": "“Uedera,” he said at last. His voice was quiet and very precise, like a man laying out tools. “Your brother is Division. That makes you Division-adjacent. My family has a grievance with that category. Not with you. With the category.”"}, {"p": "“What grievance?”"}, {"p": "“In your brother's third year in this ward, a Division operation in Kita broke something my family had kept there for three generations. An arrangement. It held the veil steady on our streets, and nobody in your building ever knew it was there.” His fingers did not move on the tile. “Their file says the case closed successfully. I have been holding the pieces together with my own hands since I was seventeen.”"}, {"p": "Rei felt Itsuki go stiff beside him."}, {"p": "“Was it Gin?” Rei asked. “Did he do it?”"}, {"p": "“Find the file.” Sei said it without heat. “I won't tell you what's in it. You'll read it and understand without being told, or you won't. Then we'll see what you offer.”"}, {"p": "There was an easy answer, the polite one that would cost nothing. Rei could feel it sitting in his mouth. He did not say it."}, {"p": "“I don't know what my brother's unit did to you,” he said. “I didn't know any of this until tonight. I'll find the file. I won't pretend I've read it before I have.”"}, {"p": "Something in Sei's stillness shifted by a single degree. It was not warmth. It was more like a line being written in an account book."}, {"p": "“The Uedera are not a Tower,” he said. “Did your brother ever tell you that? You have the blood and none of the house. No wall. No money. No seat on any council.” He tilted his head a little. “That's why nobody ever protected you. It might also be why nobody ever came looking.”"}, {"p": "Rei did not understand that. He kept it anyway, the way he had kept the word <em>tempered</em>, to turn over later."}, {"p": "The cousin had followed them in. Rei did not see her move. He only felt the air in the room grow thick all at once, heavy and wrong, like breathing through wet cloth. He opened his mouth to say his brother's name and found that he could not. The word would not come. It simply was not there."}, {"p": "Sei glanced at her. That was all. A glance, the way a parent stops a child reaching for a hot stove. The air went thin again. Rei's breath came back with Gin's name on the end of it."}, {"p": "The cousin bowed slightly and left. Nobody mentioned it."}, {"p": "“A wrong shadow at Ōji,” Sei said into the silence, “on the night a Division officer stopped answering.” It was not a question. Something about the case had caught on him, and on the family, for reasons that were theirs. “I'll reach once. Toward the man, not toward your brother. Put your hand on the stone.”"}, {"p": "Rei put his hand on the stone. It was warm, like something alive."}, {"p": "“And don't be stupid,” Sei added, “in the half-second you're nowhere.”"}, {"p": "The world folded."}, {"p": "It did not hurt. That was the wrong part. For half a heartbeat Rei was nowhere at all, in a place where <em>here</em> and <em>there</em> were the same word. And the nowhere was not empty. It was crowded. It was loud. Things turned toward him in it, the way heads turn in a train carriage when someone sits down."}, {"p": "Then concrete. Damp air. A low underpass beside water, the black smell of a river that was not quite a river, and the faint leftover shape of someone who had stood exactly here not long ago."}, {"p": "Knowledge settled into him as the fold let go. It was the family's kind of knowledge, old and without pity. The thing the man carried was a seated one, of the highest grade, and the register it fed on was Duty."}, {"p": "Rei did not really know the word register yet. He would learn it soon. Each being from the other side feeds on one human feeling, and grows strong where that feeling is strong. That feeling is its register. Grief is one register, Rage another, Fear another. There are thirteen in all. This one fed on Duty, the feeling of a thing that must be done because it is owed."}, {"p": "Sei's voice reached him across the fold, flat and very far away. “We do not go near a seated one. Find your brother, Uedera. But do not bring it to our door.”"}, {"break": "The train home"}, {"p": "Itsuki found him by the river twenty minutes later, out of breath, and they rode an early train north together with their shoulders touching. Neither of them said anything for three stations."}, {"p": "The train was filling up now. The sun was out, already hot through the glass. Men in short-sleeved white shirts stood holding the straps and reading their phones. Two schoolgirls in summer uniform shared one pair of earphones, one bud each, and laughed at the same moment at something only they could hear. By the door an old man held a bundle on his knees, wrapped in a purple furoshiki, the square cloth people in Japan use to carry things. Through the knot Rei could see the tops of a box of sweets and a bunch of flowers. Offerings, for a family grave or a family altar. The old man saw Rei looking and gave a small nod, as if they both knew what week it was."}, {"p": "“He never reaches for outsiders,” Itsuki said finally, to the window. “Never. I've asked him for things my whole life.”"}, {"p": "“Then why did he?”"}, {"p": "Itsuki was quiet for a while."}, {"p": "“Because of Gin,” he said. “The family has an opinion about your brother. I always thought it was a bad one.” He turned from the glass and looked at Rei, and his face was frightened in a new way. “Rei. When you said Gin's name in that room, my brother flinched.”"}]}, {"title": "From Gin's Book. The Seven Houses", "kind": "gin", "src": "gin_02_towers", "blocks": [{"p": "At Ōji, Itsuki stayed on the train. Rei stepped down onto the platform, and before the doors closed he typed one word to him. <em>Tempered?</em> The doors slid shut. The sky-blue stripe of the train pulled away north toward Akabane, and the station jingle played its short bright tune for the next one."}, {"p": "He did not open the archive. Gin had told him to read it in order, and he was not ready to break the first rule his brother had left him. But Itsuki had drawn the Tower chapter for Gin in the spring, seven portraits, one for each family, and he still had photographs of every page. They began to arrive while Rei sat on a bench at the end of the platform. Commuters flowed past him in their white shirts, fanning themselves, already sweating at seven in the morning. Itsuki sent the pages one by one, without a word."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Personal Archive. Chapter Sixteen, preface", "lines": ["The Towers are old. Some of them older than the CVD's institutional precursors, older than the Accord, older than any public acknowledgment that the veil was real. What the CVD built in 1962, the Tower Families had been building privately for centuries: training programs, ability tempering, assessment methodology, operational protocols.", "The Tower Families are not the system's enemies. They are something more complicated: its predecessors, its sponsors, and its most persistent critics, simultaneously. They are inside the institution and above it and outside it all at once.", "I have three Tower Family contacts. I would work with all three again. I would not have dinner with any of them.", "G.U."]}}, {"img": "tower_families.jpg", "cap": "The Tower families, old money and older arrangements"}, {"p": "The next page was not in Gin's voice. It was a copy of a report from the Grid. Rei knew the name the way most people in Kita knew it, as a rumour. The Grid was not a company or a club. It was a loose network of people with power who had never joined the Division, or who had left it. They had no office and no leader. They helped each other, and sometimes they helped strangers, and they kept their own records. Gin, it seemed, had read them."}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "Grid Intelligence. What the Tower Families Are", "lines": ["Tower Family is not a formal designation. It is a term from the Grid's intelligence vocabulary for lineages old enough that their veil practice predates modern institutions, wealthy enough to stay independent of them, and established enough that both the CVD and the Grid consider their cooperation worth keeping.", "There are approximately thirty Tower Family lineages globally. Seven operate with significant influence in Japan.", "They range from samurai lineages who folded veil practice into warrior tradition centuries ago, to nineteenth-century merchant families who learned it from older houses, to Meiji-era industrial families whose founders developed abilities under extreme stress.", "What they share. Generational wealth. Training programs that predate and sometimes outperform the CVD academy. And one political posture, held for centuries. They cooperate with whatever framework governs the public world, and keep enough independence that cooperation is always a choice and never a requirement."]}}, {"p": "Then came the page Rei had asked for without knowing it. It explained the word <em>tempered</em>."}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "The Tempering System. How Towers Manufacture Rank Above Natural Ceiling", "lines": ["Natural rank is the aperture a practitioner is born with. Standard CVD training develops a practitioner to that ceiling and stops. Tower training does not stop.", "How it works. Sustained high-intensity exposure to controlled veil environments from early childhood, with senior practitioners present to monitor and stabilize. Each generation pushes the ceiling slightly. The next generation starts slightly above where the last one ended.", "The cost. Tower children have a different childhood. Heavy veil exposure from an early age. Some develop faster than projected. Some develop veil-absorption complications that the family manages privately. The cost is not documented in any public record.", "What it produces. Practitioners who typically register two to three rank levels above the family's lineage baseline. Arbiter rank is common in the Towers. Vanguard rank emerges from the deepest programs.", "What it does not produce. Reliable development of a second path. The Towers have optimized single-path depth to an extraordinary degree. The second path stays closed for most of them. They know this. They research it. They have not solved it."]}}, {"p": "Rei read it twice to be sure he understood. Every person with power is born with a certain amount of it. Gin's book called that the aperture, the size of the opening a person has onto the other side. Ordinary training helps a person grow up to that natural limit and no further. The Tower families did not accept the limit. They put their children near the veil from the age of four or five, again and again, under watch, until the opening grew wider than nature had made it. Each generation went a little further than the last. Arbiter and Vanguard were very high ranks, he could tell that much. The cost was the children. Rei thought of the cousin at the gate, who had started at four, and who looked at the air instead of at people."}, {"p": "Beside the last line, in the margin, Gin had drawn a small pencil mark and nothing else. Rei looked at it for a long time. It was the mark Gin made when something mattered to him and he had decided not to say why."}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "The Ancestral Deals", "lines": ["The oldest Tower lineages, pre-Edo in Japan, made arrangements with entities over centuries of cohabitation and cooperation. Specific entities agreed to provide stability support, environmental management and in some cases active help with tempering, in exchange for resources and protections the families could provide.", "Some of these arrangements are still active. The entities involved are not in any CVD case file, because the families have ensured they never needed to be."]}}, {"p": "Rei thought of Sei's hand on the roof tile, and of an arrangement that had held the veil steady on Kita's streets for three generations, until someone from Gin's building broke it. He read the next page more slowly."}, {"img": "tower_tsukishiro.jpg", "cap": "The Tsukishiro family, the oldest of the seven lineages"}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "The Seven Families. Japan", "lines": ["Tsukishiro. Oldest lineage, practice documented back to the Heian period. Highest tempering, consistently produces Arbiters, one Vanguard in this generation. Two seats on the CVD advisory council since its founding. An arrangement of at least three centuries with a grief-register spirit tied to the estate.", "Fujiwara-Kage. Edo-period investigation lineage, founded beside the shogunate's intelligence service. Clairsentient Arbiters. The most embedded in the CVD. Two family members inside it now, one in the Research Division and one in the Auditor network.", "Orimoto. Meiji-era industrial family, founder's Telekinesis woke under factory stress. Telekinesis and Psychokinesis, suppression-focused training. Formally cooperative, operationally independent. The most Grid-adjacent of the seven.", "Higashiyama. Psychometabolism lineage. The most reliable field medics in the country. The CVD buys their training method without saying where it came from. Politically neutral, operationally indispensable.", "Kuroda. Teleportation lineage. The most politically isolated of the seven. Near-complete operational independence. Will cooperate with the CVD on cases that interest them. Cannot be compelled on cases that do not. The CVD has stopped trying.", "Akiyama. Contested. A six-year internal dispute. The young want to renegotiate an ancestral arrangement the old consider binding. The entity involved in the arrangement has opinions. Three unreported entity events in the compound in two years.", "Nishimura. The newest, third generation only. Eager for CVD ties in ways the older families find slightly undignified. Will either learn the politics the Tower position requires or be absorbed into the CVD's asset pipeline before they understand what happened to them."]}}, {"p": "Itsuki had drawn the Kuroda portrait last. Rei could tell, because it was the only one where the face was turned away."}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "The Tower Families and the CVD. What It Gets, and What It Pays", "lines": ["What the CVD gets. High-rank practitioners. Training methods. Political cover where Tower connections outweigh the CVD's. Stability management in Tower-associated areas. Ancestral intelligence about entities the CVD's own records do not contain.", "What the CVD pays. Looking the other way. Not pursuing registration violations the Towers commit. Not investigating ancestral arrangements that would technically require review. Not applying the Accord's international reporting rules to Tower operations that cross borders.", "The unstated deal. Tower practitioners who enter CVD service get better assignments, faster advancement and quiet exemptions. It is in no policy document. It is visible in the career records. Both parties understand the deal. Neither has written it down.", "You will meet them eventually, usually at the point where your case brushes something their family arranged a century ago, and they will be unfailingly polite about not helping you."]}}, {"p": "Under that page Gin had written one more note, in the margin, in the pen he used for things that were his own and not the Division's."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's margin, beside the deal", "lines": ["The Uedera family is not a Tower Family. We do not have the generational wealth, the political connections, or the tempering program infrastructure. What we have is the lineage capability without the institutional framework that would make it visible and valuable to the Towers.", "I have occasionally wondered whether a Tower assessor who knew what to look for would have identified what our lineage carries. I am noting that it did not happen and that I am not sure whether to be relieved or not.", "G.U."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Working Near Tower Families. What to Know", "lines": ["Tower practitioners are not better than you. They have had more structured development and a higher ceiling. None of that makes them more capable in the case you are both working. Know the difference between rank and experience.", "The ancestral arrangements are the part to be careful around. An entity that has been party to a Tower arrangement for generations has a status that is not in the standard taxonomy. If an entity in a Tower context fails to respond to protocols that should work, stop, and find out what arrangement the family has before you continue.", "If a Tower practitioner seems to know more about your case than they should, they probably do. Treat their read as data, not as threat."]}}, {"p": "The platform had filled and emptied twice. The sun was up over the station roof, and the cicadas in the trees of Asukayama had started in earnest, a long electric scream that would not stop now until dark. Rei typed one line to Itsuki. <em>Your brother said we're the blood without the house.</em> The dots appeared and vanished three times before the answer came. <em>He's right. It's the nicest thing he's said about anyone in years.</em>"}], "n": 2}, {"title": "The Archive", "kind": "main", "src": "m_archive", "n": 3, "part": "Book One. The Vanishing", "blocks": [{"img": "res_archive.jpg", "cap": "Rei at the kitchen table with Gin's archive open"}, {"p": "The index card had not been there last night."}, {"p": "Rei was sure of it. He had held the archive open at the first page in the kitchen at four in the morning and read the Opening Note twice. Now it was nearly noon. The flat was bright and hot, and the cicadas were going in the big zelkova tree outside the window. When he opened the cover, a card slid out onto the table, as if it had been waiting for him to look away."}, {"p": "It was Gin's hand. Small, exact, but pressed so hard that the pen had dented the card right through to the back."}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Index card, under the front cover", "lines": ["If I stop writing, read the Archive in order. Out of order it lies by omission.", "Don't trust my handwriting after the 14th."]}}, {"p": "Rei turned it over. The back was blank. He looked around the flat. The door was locked with the chain on. The window had been shut and latched before he went to Nezu. Nobody had been in. He was almost certain nobody had been in."}, {"p": "The fourteenth. Of August? This August, with Obon about to begin? He could not tell if the card was warning him about something that had already happened or something still on its way. He put it back under the cover exactly where it had been, the way a person puts things back in a dead man's room. Then he hated himself for thinking it."}, {"p": "He cleared the cold dinner off the table without looking at the second plate. He set the archive down in the space. It was heavier than a book that size should be. Gin had once told him that the difference between a practitioner who lives and one who doesn't is whether they wrote it down before the night made them doubt it. A practitioner was the Division's word for anyone who used the power. Rei had thought it was a joke."}, {"p": "He started at the beginning, and he read in order."}, {"break": "The foreword"}, {"p": "The foreword had no date. Under the heading Gin had written only <em>Written in the morning. Coffee going cold on the left.</em>"}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Foreword. Personal Archive", "lines": ["I almost forgot my keys again today.", "I do this every morning. I put them on the hook by the door because I read somewhere that building habits around physical anchors helps people like us stay grounded. People whose minds tend to slip sideways before the rest of them catches up. I put them on the hook. I make coffee. I smoke half a cigarette on the step outside because Rei complains about the smell in the apartment even though he leaves his own cigarettes everywhere.", "I have been a field operative for six years. I have performed 340 documented exorcisms. I have a near-perfect case closure rate in a district that most senior operatives request transfers out of. I cannot reliably remember where I put my keys.", "Rei thinks this is funny. He has started moving them slightly, just a few centimeters, to see if I notice. I notice. I don't tell him I notice. It's the most reliable part of my morning."]}}, {"p": "Rei sat back in his chair. Across the kitchen the hook by the door was still empty. In his pocket, under the cold key, was the half cigarette he had picked up off the walkway without knowing why he kept it. <em>On the step outside.</em> Every morning. Never at night."}, {"p": "So last night Gin had gone out onto the step in the dark, as if it were morning, and smoked half a cigarette, and put it out, and not come back in."}, {"p": "All those mornings. All those few centimetres. Rei had thought it was a joke Gin never caught. Gin had caught it every time, and kept quiet about it, and written it down as the most reliable part of his day. Rei pressed the heel of his hand against his eyes until the kitchen came back in coloured spots. Then he went on reading, because the card said in order, and he did not trust himself to stop."}, {"p": "The foreword ended with a note in a different pen. It was a little faster, as if written on the way out of the door."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Foreword. A note at the bottom of the page", "lines": ["Rei, if this is you, and I know there is a reasonable chance this is you, the rent is paid through the end of the month. There is food in the freezer. The contact number on page 47 is someone who will help you if you ask correctly. Do not lead with what you need. Ask how they are first. They will respect that.", "I know you don't take things seriously. I know you think that's armor. You are smarter than I was at your age. You are also more exposed than you know. Read Chapter 5 twice.", "Keys were on the hook. Coffee was terrible. Left on time anyway."]}}, {"p": "Rei got up and opened the freezer. It was full. Rice portioned into flat bags, labelled in Gin's print. Curry. Gyoza dumplings. Enough for weeks. He stood in the cold breath of it longer than he needed to. Then he turned to page 47. There was a phone number on it and nothing else. No name. He copied it into his phone and did not call it yet. He did not know yet what he needed, and Gin had told him not to lead with that anyway."}, {"break": "Chapter One. What Everyone Knows"}, {"p": "Chapter One was written as an official document, with a stamp at the top that said it was for CVD staff only. Rei knew most of it the way everyone knew it. He read it anyway, because Gin had told him to, and because it was strange to see the ordinary world written down as if someone might need it explained."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "Working Definition. The Veil. CVD Standard Reference", "lines": ["Official term. The Veil (also the Membrane, the Threshold, regional variants exist).", "Classification. Naturally occurring psychic boundary layer between the living world and post-mortem dimensional space.", "Permeability. Variable. Influenced by geography, population density, historical trauma, individual practitioner ability, and factors not yet fully catalogued.", "Public awareness. General. Not classified. Managed through normalization rather than concealment since 1962 Tokyo Accord."]}}, {"p": "Every child in Japan learned the outline in school. The veil was real. Some people could feel it and some could not. There was a Division for it, the way there was a department for water and roads. Rei had never once wondered who decided it would be that way. Gin's page told him, in a dry paragraph he could almost hear in his brother's voice."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "What Everyone Knows. From the official text", "lines": ["The Veil exists. It has not been disputed in any serious academic, governmental, or theological context for approximately ninety years. The gap between what practitioners documented and when governments formally acknowledged it is about two hundred years. Two hundred years of institutional notes filed under 'anomalous' by people who knew perfectly well what they were looking at.", "The decision to normalize rather than conceal was made for practical reasons. Concealment creates panic when it fails. Normalization creates bureaucracy, which is manageable. The 1962 Tokyo Accord established the framework still in use today: the Veil is real, practitioners are registered, entities are classified, and the Civil Veil Division maintains order at the boundary.", "What matters operationally is what a thing does, not what it is. CVD Training Manual, p.3"]}}, {"p": "So that was the Accord. In 1962, at a meeting in Tokyo, governments had agreed to stop hiding the veil. Instead of keeping it secret, they made it ordinary. They gave it forms, offices and rules. People with power would be written down. Things that came through would be sorted into types. And the Division would keep order at the edge."}, {"p": "In the margin, in pencil, Gin had argued with himself."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's margin, beside the Accord", "lines": ["The Accord has been updated four times. The current version has been in effect since 1991. I have read all four versions. The 1962 original is the most honest. The subsequent versions are more careful.", "The CVD operates in forty-three countries. In Japan it is under the Ministry of Internal Affairs. In 2015 it has approximately 340 registered field operatives across the country, of which I am one. The registered network represents, in my estimate, roughly forty percent of the practitioners actually operating. The other sixty percent work through the Grid, work alone, or do not know what they are.", "I wrote the line about what a thing does for the training manual five years ago. It is the right advice for your first six months. After that, you start asking the questions anyway. Everyone does."]}}, {"p": "<em>Or do not know what they are.</em> Rei read that line again and found that his mouth had gone dry. He could not have said why."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "Public Knowledge Framework. What Citizens Are Told", "lines": ["The Veil. Real. A natural boundary layer. Managed by Civil Veil Division and licensed practitioners.", "Psychic ability. Real. Occurs in approximately 12% of the population at measurable levels. Registration encouraged, required at Level 3+.", "Entities. Real. Classified into manageable categories. Most are non-threatening. Dangerous entities are handled by CVD.", "Possession. Real but rare. Occurs in cases of extreme veil integrity failure. CVD response available 24 hours.", "Death paths. Multiple confirmed. Details vary by jurisdiction and are considered a matter of personal and religious belief beyond CVD mandate.", "The Font. Not officially acknowledged at public level. Classified research only."]}}, {"p": "<em>What the public is given,</em> Gin had written under it. Below the table he had copied out the promise everyone knew. <em>Register, and the Division will support you, protect you in law, let you work and give you a rank.</em> Then, underneath, came Gin's own line, underlined once."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's margin", "lines": ["What registration also provides, which the public framework does not emphasize: your name, your level, your known abilities, your current address, and your assessed threat potential in a database accessible to the Veil Protocol division, the Tower family councils, and seventeen other agencies whose mandates are not fully public."]}}, {"p": "Rei had never been registered. He had never been measured by anyone. In nineteen years nobody from the Division had come to the door to ask. They asked every child of every officer sooner or later, and his brother was an officer. He had never once thought about why."}, {"p": "Between the official pages there was a field note in Gin's own voice. Its only date was <em>A Tuesday. Third week of October. Rei had an exam.</em> Rei remembered the exam. He had failed half of it and come home and slept on the couch in his clothes."}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "Field Note. Case 7-114", "lines": ["Elderly woman in Nerima, third floor apartment, has been cohabiting with what she calls “my late husband” for four years since his passing. Neighbors reported lights, sounds, temperature fluctuations.", "When I arrived she offered me tea. She poured two cups. The second one she placed at the empty chair across from her without looking at it.", "Her late husband, Kenji, 74. By all behavioral indicators he retained enough of himself to constitute a Type 2 spirit, good alignment, low permeability risk, stable emotional signature. He was, as far as I could determine, sitting in that chair because he was not ready to leave and she was not ready for him to.", "I wrote: No actionable threat identified. Recommend monitoring. Case closed pending review.", "I drank the tea. I did not drink the second cup. I said goodbye to the empty chair on my way out, because it seemed like the right thing to do."]}}, {"p": "A yellow sticky note was stuck to the bottom of the page, curling at one corner. <em>Sometimes an old man just wants to finish his tea. Learning to tell the difference is the job.</em>"}, {"p": "Rei looked at the two cups upturned on the rack. He thought about a man who had said goodbye to an empty chair in Nerima, then come home the same night and asked his brother how the exam went."}, {"img": "agenda_board.jpg", "cap": "Gin's map of the unregistered network of northern Tokyo"}, {"p": "It took him the rest of the afternoon to see the shape of the whole thing. When he did, it came clear all at once, the way a face comes out of a blurred photograph once a person stops trying to see it. The archive was not a diary. It was a ring of keys."}, {"p": "Gin had mapped the unregistered people of northern Tokyo, the ones the Division's lists did not count. He had done it better than the Division ever could. It was not a list of suspects. It was a map of trust. Who would open a door for whom, at what hour, after which password. And on page 147 he had locked the live parts of it behind four people. Four main contacts. A baker who did not know what he was. A clinic behind a blue curtain. A café that kept Tuesday evenings free for the kind of customer who came in already knowing. A shrine room behind a Korean restaurant in Shin-Ōkubo, the Korean quarter of the city, on the west side."}, {"p": "None of them would speak to a stranger. All of them, the margins said in Gin's smallest hand, owed him. Not money. The dangerous kind of debt, the kind a careful man collects only once. He had never collected. He had left them for Rei instead. That was either the most generous thing his brother had ever done or the most dangerous. To walk this map was to announce, to everyone Gin had protected, that the man who kept them safe was gone."}, {"p": "The first contact was the baker."}, {"p": "Rei went out into the full heat of the afternoon. It was the kind of Tokyo heat that comes up off the pavement as well as down from the sky, thirty-four degrees and so humid that his shirt stuck to his back before he reached the station. He rode the Keihin-Tōhoku one stop north to Higashi-Jūjō and walked west through the back streets toward Jūjō. A green Saikyō line train rattled through a level crossing while the warning bells rang. Old women sat in the shade of shop awnings with paper fans. A shopping street had hung its Obon lanterns in a long row, red and white, and they swayed a little though there was no wind. Rei noticed that, and then made himself stop noticing it."}, {"p": "The bakery was on a narrow street behind Jūjō station. It had two tables, a glass case and the smell of melon bread, the sweet round buns with a crisp sugar crust that every child in Japan grows up on. The man behind the counter was in his fifties, round, floury to the elbows. Gin's margin said he did not know what he was. Rei watched him for a minute from the doorway and saw what Gin meant. Every time a customer came in, the baker looked up half a second before the bell rang."}, {"p": "When the shop was empty Rei went to the counter. The baker smiled the shop smile. Then he looked properly at Rei's face, and the smile stopped."}, {"p": "Rei watched the recognition cross him. The small flinch. The quick rethink. The careful arranging of his face into something that gave nothing away. Rei understood that he had walked into a story that was already half told, and that he was the only person in it who did not know the plot."}, {"p": "“You're his brother,” the baker said."}, {"p": "Everything in Rei wanted to say <em>where is he, what was he doing, what do you know.</em> He thought of the note at the bottom of the foreword."}, {"p": "“How are you?” he asked instead. “How have you been keeping?”"}, {"p": "The baker blinked. Then something in his shoulders let go. He told Rei, slowly, about his knees, and his daughter in Osaka, and the August heat that made the dough rise wrong. Rei listened to all of it. When it was finished, the baker turned the sign on the door to <em>Closed</em> and poured them both a glass of cold barley tea, the brown, slightly bitter tea that every house in Japan keeps in the fridge in summer."}, {"p": "From Jūjō he went on to Akabane, the last stop in Kita before the river, a busy, cheerful district of cheap bars and covered shopping arcades. Then to the café. The trains were crowded with families going home for Obon, with suitcases and paper bags of gifts, and small children asleep against their parents. At every station the doors opened on the same wall of heat. By the time the sun went down behind the buildings, Rei had asked three people how they were, had listened to every answer, and had drunk more tea than he wanted."}, {"p": "The baker told him a piece of it. By evening the clinic and the café had told him more. Not all of it. None of them would ever tell him all of it, because Gin had trained them not to, and grief makes people loyal to the rules of the dead. But a piece. In his last weeks, they said, Gin had been <em>wrong</em>, the way a room is wrong after something has stood in it. He had been carrying something carefully and quietly, the way a person carries a thing they have decided to die for rather than put down. And whatever it was, he had been certain. They all said this, each in their own words, and it was the part that frightened Rei most."}, {"p": "Gin had been protecting <em>him</em>."}]}, {"title": "From Gin's Book. What Nobody Says Out Loud", "kind": "gin", "src": "gin_03_nobody_says", "blocks": [{"p": "He came home on a late train, standing, pressed between a man who smelled of beer and grilled chicken and a woman fanning herself with a folded train timetable. When he climbed the hill from Ōji station it was dark, and still hot. In the doorways of a few houses, small paper lanterns had been lit for the dead. A dog barked behind a fence and then stopped, all at once, as if someone had told it to."}, {"p": "The baker's barley tea was still sour in Rei's mouth when he got in. He turned to Chapter Two, because it came next. The preface was dated <em>A Friday. Rain. Rei forgot his umbrella again. Left mine by the door for him.</em> Rei remembered the umbrella. He had not known it was Gin's. He read the chapter at the kitchen table with both lamps on. Twice he got up to check the lock, and both times it was locked."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Personal Archive. Chapter Two, preface", "lines": ["What follows is what I know after six years of field work, two years of archive access I was not supposed to have, and approximately four hundred conversations with people who told me things they wouldn't have told me if they had known I was writing them down.", "The official framework presented in Chapter 1 is accurate in its facts. It is dishonest in its framing. The Veil is real. Practitioners are registered. The Division manages the boundary. What it omits is the question every new practitioner eventually asks and never gets a direct answer to: managed for whose benefit?", "This chapter will make you angry. Let the anger make you careful rather than reckless. Reckless anger gets people killed. Careful anger builds things.", "Keys on the hook. Coffee finished. Rei took the umbrella. Good."]}}, {"img": "ch4_ranktiers.jpg", "cap": "Practitioners ranked by class, as the CVD sorts them"}, {"p": "The first document was a secret report from inside the Division. It did not sort people by how much power they had. It sorted them by who they were, where they came from and how much money their families had. Rei had never seen it put so plainly."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "Psychic Class Structure. Internal CVD Sociological Assessment (Restricted). Level 4+ Clearance", "lines": ["Class One. Tower Families. Generational psychic bloodlines with economic resources to match. Children are assessed at 16, trained privately before registration, and enter the system already equipped and connected. They set the standards everyone else is measured against.", "Class Two. Institutional Practitioners. Middle-class or upwardly mobile individuals identified through standard channels. Registered, trained through CVD programs, employed in civil roles. The backbone of the official system. Given enough to make loyalty rational.", "Class Three. Unregistered / The Grid. Practitioners who fell through identification channels, refused registration, or actively avoid the system. Disproportionately from lower economic strata, immigrant communities, and marginalized populations. Higher per-capita ability levels than the registered population, per suppressed internal studies.", "Class Zero. Protocol Targets. Practitioners assessed as Arbiter rank or above (Level 5+ in CVD classification) who are unregistered or uncontrolled. Not a class in the social sense. The primary focus of the Veil Protocol division's active mandate."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Post-it, red ink", "lines": ["The suppressed studies referenced above are real. I have copies.", "Trauma and desperation thin the veil faster than comfort and training. The people the system was designed to manage are more powerful than the system knows. It is not about order. It is about the math."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the red note slowly. Gin meant that hard lives made people stronger on the other side, not weaker. The poor, the frightened, the people with nowhere else to go were the ones whose power grew fastest. And they were exactly the people the Division had been built to keep an eye on."}, {"p": "Folded into the page was a cheap photocopied flyer, soft from being carried in a pocket. It had no letterhead. At the bottom someone had stamped <em>Other Side Distribution</em> in blurred purple ink. Rei would learn later that this was how the Grid signed things."}, {"doc": {"kind": "letter", "title": "What They Tell You When You Register (and What They Don't)", "lines": ["They will tell you: registration protects you. And it does. Registered practitioners have legal standing. Unregistered ones have whatever they can argue in the moment.", "They will tell you: registration gives you access to resources. Also true. CVD training programs are genuinely good. The equipment they issue to Level 3+ field operatives is better than most of what circulates on the Grid's black market. The salary is stable.", "They will not tell you: your file is accessible to seventeen agencies. Your threat assessment, a number generated by an algorithm they will not explain, follows you permanently and can be updated without your knowledge. Above Arbiter rank, the recruitment offer and the compulsory conscription offer look very similar.", "They will not tell you about the Protocol's active operations list. Being on it is not a consequence of wrongdoing. It is a consequence of being interesting at the wrong level.", "The Grid does not tell you to refuse registration. What the Grid tells you is: know what you are agreeing to. Then make the choice that keeps you alive and functional."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Gin's post-it on the flyer", "lines": ["If you're reading this and you're a 5 or above, assume you're already on it. Act accordingly.", "I am registered. I chose this. Registration is a tool like any other tool, useful when you control it, dangerous when it controls you. Know which situation you are in."]}}, {"p": "The next document had a different stamp, darker and heavier, and Gin had not written a caption under it. <em>Veil Protocol Division. Classified Reference. Level 5+ Clearance Required. Top Secret. Protocol Eyes Only.</em> Rei had never heard of a Veil Protocol division. Reading the first paragraph, he understood that this was the point. It was a separate operation that lived hidden inside the Division, like a second skeleton under the skin. Gin had written one line above the stamp, small. <em>I work for the sixty percent officially. I know about the forty percent because six years of field work teaches you to read what is not written in the operational briefings.</em>"}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "The Veil Protocol. Actual Mandate. Classified Mandate Components", "lines": ["Component One. Catalogue. Identification and detailed profiling of all practitioners at Level 3 and above, registered or unregistered. The registered database is the foundation. Unregistered practitioners are identified through field surveillance, informant networks, entity interaction monitoring, and algorithmic flagging of veil activity patterns.", "Component Two. Weaponize. Development of military and intelligence applications for psychic ability. Ability amplification, forced ability development, entity recruitment and control, veil manipulation at scale. Conducted at facilities that do not appear on public CVD organizational charts. Staffed by practitioners whose registration files list them as retired, deceased, or transferred to administrative roles.", "Component Three. Control. Neutralization of practitioners assessed as uncontrollable variables. Coercive recruitment (offer followed by compulsion if refused), indefinite detention under veil security protocols, ability suppression through pharmaceutical and procedural means, and, in cases assessed as extreme risk, termination. The threshold for extreme risk is not public. It is not consistent. It has been applied to practitioners whose primary offense was refusing recruitment."]}}, {"p": "Rei put the three parts into plain words for himself, because he needed to be sure. First, find everyone with real power and write them down. Second, learn to use that power as a weapon. Third, deal with anyone who would not be used. The last word of the last part was <em>termination</em>. He knew what it meant. He read it anyway, twice."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's margin, urgent", "lines": ["The Component Three threshold document, the internal guidelines for what constitutes extreme risk, I have also obtained. It is in a separate file. If you are reading this archive and I am not available: the file is at the location described in the note on page 147. Do not access it through any networked device.", "I was offered registration twice before I accepted. The first offer was polite. The second offer was not."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "The Towers and Component Three", "lines": ["The Tower families are aware of the Protocol's full mandate and have negotiated their position within it. Their members are not subject to Component Three. They provide practitioners to Protocol research programs voluntarily, and in exchange their bloodlines are classified as protected assets rather than potential threats.", "Translation: the old money families agreed to cooperate with the government's psychic weapons program in exchange for their children being marked safe. Everyone else's children are not marked safe. This is the arrangement. It has been the arrangement for forty years. The people who benefit from it call it stability. The people who don't call it something else."]}}, {"p": "Rei read that page three times. He thought of Sei, alone at seventeen in a house whose children were marked safe. He thought of Itsuki, who had run from that house to paint bridges. He thought of himself, nineteen, never measured, in a flat that belonged to no family anyone had heard of. Nobody had marked him anything. He had always taken that to mean nobody had noticed him. It occurred to him for the first time that Gin might have worked very hard to keep it that way."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Chapter Two. Closing Note", "lines": ["Same day. Later. Rei's light is off. First time before midnight in a week.", "A practitioner I worked a case with in Koenji, Level 4, registered, twenty-four, asked me once why I stayed in the system if I knew what I knew. She had just seen the inside of a Protocol facility for the first time.", "I told her: because the work is real. The entity in the Nerima apartment that I didn't disperse because the old woman needed him there, he was real, and his wife was real, and that mattered regardless of what the system around it was doing.", "I also told her: the system is a tool. Tools do not have loyalty. Do not give yours to a tool.", "Keys were on the hook. Didn't check, just knew. That's new."]}}, {"p": "Rei closed the book on his finger and sat with the last line. <em>Didn't check, just knew.</em> When Gin wrote it, it had sounded like a man getting better at something. Reading it now, across from the empty hook, it sounded like a man who had stopped looking."}], "n": 3}, {"title": "The Last Week", "kind": "main", "src": "m_lastweek", "n": 4, "part": "Book One. The Vanishing", "blocks": [{"p": "Akabane was two stops north of Ōji on the Keihin-Tōhoku line. Rei stood by the door all the way, because the seats were full of people with luggage. At Akabane the platform was crowded with families changing trains for the long lines north, toward Saitama and beyond, carrying boxes of sweets in shop paper to give to relatives. Outside the station, music played from speakers in a covered shopping arcade, and the smell of frying oil came out of the small bars that were already open at midday. Rei turned away from all of it into a quiet side street, where the clinic was."}, {"img": "char_hayashi-fumiko.jpg", "cap": "The doctor behind the blue curtain at the Akabane clinic"}, {"p": "The woman behind the blue curtain held the discharge form up to the window. The light came through the paper, and Gin's signature floated on it like something under water."}, {"p": "“Look,” she said. “There.”"}, {"p": "Rei looked. The clinic was two rooms over a dry cleaner's, with a blue cotton curtain across the door of the back room and a smell of antiseptic and incense. The woman was small and sharp and did not give her name. She had been patching up people the hospitals would have reported for longer than Rei had been alive. In all that time, she said, Gin had signed for a great many of them."}, {"p": "“I know his hand better than my sister's,” she said. “This is the Friday. The drowning out by the Arakawa. He closed it in a day. He never closes anything in a day.” She tilted the form. “See the G?”"}, {"p": "Gin's G had always leaned left, as if it were trying to take up less room than it was given. This one stood up straight."}, {"p": "“A little,” Rei said."}, {"p": "“A little,” she agreed. She put the form down very gently, the way she might lay down a hand that had stopped moving. “That's how it starts.”"}, {"p": "Piece by piece, Gin's contacts gave him a week. It was Gin's last ordinary week, rebuilt out of the things he had done that were not ordinary."}, {"p": "On the Thursday he had skipped a Division briefing, the first he had missed in nine years. A man like Gin did not skip a thing he had done nine hundred times unless what he was doing instead was bigger. On the Friday he had closed the drowning case too fast and too clean. And on the Saturday he had gone to an address that was in no file, no register, no map the Division kept. His own archive named it only once, in a line by itself. <em>The low place, where the water remembers.</em>"}, {"p": "After the Saturday, the entries in the archive stopped being entries and became arguments. Gin was arguing with himself on paper, and the lines grew tighter as they went. The argument was about a debt. It had never been his debt to pay. And yet he kept asking if paying it might be the only honest thing left to do."}, {"p": "Rei rode home through the hottest part of the afternoon. The sky over Kita was white, with no colour in it at all. On the train a boy of about ten pressed his face to the glass and asked his mother if Grandpa would really come home this week. She said yes, if they lit the fire at the door properly. The boy nodded, very serious, as if he had been given a job. From Ōji station he climbed the hill past the dark green trees of Asukayama, and the cicadas were so loud there that he could feel the sound on his skin."}, {"break": "Chapter Three. The Veil Itself"}, {"p": "Rei needed to know what kind of place the low place was. The archive would not let him skip ahead. So he read Chapter Three, in order, on the floor of Gin's room with his back against the bed. The preface was dated in winter. <em>The heating in the apartment is broken again. Rei is wearing three sweaters.</em>"}, {"p": "Gin had first touched the other side at twenty-two, he wrote, on a possession case in a Kita building that had been gathering suffering for decades. For four seconds he had been in two places at once. It had taken him three weeks to come all the way back. <em>The other side is not dark,</em> he had written. <em>It is loud.</em>"}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Chapter Three, a page pinned with a paperclip", "lines": ["“The membrane is not a wall. It is the only thing that makes the distinction between here and there meaningful. Without it, both sides would be present simultaneously and neither would be navigable.” Practitioner account, identity withheld, 34 years field experience.", "The other side is not the absence of this world. It is this world without the membrane that makes it livable.", "Entities that cross into the living world are not traveling from a distant place. They are pressing through a membrane from a state of existence that is immediately adjacent to this one. A thin veil requires less effort. A weakened practitioner is a local thinning.", "You are not a target. You are a door. The entity is not interested in you specifically in most cases. It is interested in the passage you represent. What it does once it has crossed, who it becomes interested in, is a different question."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the page slowly. The other side was not far away, it said. It was right here, on the other side of a thin skin. And a person with power, when that person grew tired or hurt, became a thin place in that skin. A door."}, {"img": "ch20_layered.jpg", "cap": "Tokyo, an old city lived in for centuries"}, {"p": "Old cities were thin cities, the chapter said. Tokyo had been lived in for centuries, and the weight of all its ended lives pressed against the membrane from the other side. A practitioner in Tokyo with only a little power did not need to reach for the other side. It reached for them."}, {"p": "Then Gin wrote about March 2011. Everyone in Japan remembered that month. On the eleventh of March a huge earthquake struck under the sea off the north-east coast, and the wave that followed it came over the towns on the shore. That morning, Gin wrote, had thinned the veil further. <em>The wave took close to twenty thousand,</em> he had written. <em>Four years on it is thinner than when I started, not thicker.</em>"}, {"p": "Then came a diagram, drawn in pencil and carefully inked over. A line across the top of the page for the living world. Under it, the membrane. Under that, three bands, each one darker than the last."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "The Layers of the Other Side", "lines": ["The living world. Where you stand. The default.", "The membrane. The veil. Organised by significance, not distance.", "I. The Threshold. Proximate, veil-adjacent. The recent dead, the disoriented, those who have not yet resolved into a path. Suffering beings anchored nearby bleed into it. Dense, chaotic, loud. This is where mediums, readers and exorcists actually work. Manageable with your strength intact. Dangerous when you are depleted. Danger moderate to high.", "II. The Deep. Established, entity territory. Spirits and demons that have resolved into their nature. Ancient suffering beings. Entities that have been there long enough to develop culture, alliances, hierarchies, territories, agendas. They have intent. Level 5+ with controlled method only. Unsanctioned access is the main source of high-level possessions. Danger high.", "III. The Font. Source, origin point. Generates the veil. Absorbs what returns to it. Approached as a direction, not a destination. Entities that reach it dissolve completely. It is not described as painful. It is described as coming home. That is the part that frightens. The wanting. No return."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Gin's note beside the Deep", "lines": ["I have been here twice. Both times deliberately, both times with preparation. It is not the same as the Threshold. The entities here know you are there. They have had time to develop opinions about visitors."]}}, {"p": "Rei sat with the diagram on his knees for a long time. He went over it in his head the way he would explain it to a friend. Just under the skin of the world was the Threshold, crowded and noisy, full of the newly dead who did not yet know where to go. Below that was the Deep, where old things lived that had their own wishes, their own friends and enemies, their own kingdoms. And at the very bottom was the Font, where everything came from and everything went back to, and nothing came out again."}, {"p": "The low place, where the water remembers. He did not know which band it belonged to. But he understood now that the other side had a top and a bottom, and that the things at the bottom had politics. And his brother had gone down there on purpose twice, and written about it in the same small print he used for shopping lists."}, {"p": "Slowly, and against his will, Rei began to understand what had happened. Understanding was worse than not understanding. It closed off the comfortable lie that his brother had simply been taken. Gin had not been taken. He had been <em>persuaded</em>, and he was being persuaded still. The cruel part was this. The thing inside him had never once asked him to want something selfish. It had asked him to do what was right."}, {"p": "It had found an empty place in the order of the world, a place where an old duty had been left undone. Then it had made every step toward disaster look like a duty Gin already owed. In the end Gin could no longer tell which wishes were his own and which were its. They sounded the same."}, {"p": "<em>You are not a target. You are a door.</em> Rei read the sentence again in Gin's print and understood that Gin had written it about other people. It had never occurred to him, or it had occurred to him and he had not written that part down, that a door can be talked into opening from the inside."}, {"p": "He was still in there. Rei held on to that. Gin was not a cage to be broken open. He was not a man replaced by a monster wearing his coat. He was a person being slowly out-argued by something that sounded exactly like his own conscience. It did not shout. It was simply there every hour, patient as the tide."}, {"p": "Rei went back to the page about doors to copy the line into his phone. That was when he saw the tick."}, {"p": "It was in the margin beside <em>A weakened practitioner is a local thinning.</em> A small neat check mark in black ink. Gin did not tick things. Gin underlined, or wrote a word, or drew his little pencil mark when something mattered and he would not say why. This was none of those. It was upright and even and perfectly made. It was the tick of someone marking a sentence as correct."}, {"p": "Rei turned back through the chapter. He found two more. One beside <em>you are not a target, you are a door.</em> One beside the Deep, under <em>they have had time to develop opinions about visitors.</em>"}, {"p": "Each one was in the same steady hand. Each one was a little more confident than his brother had ever been."}]}, {"title": "The Door That Locks Itself", "kind": "main", "src": "m_door", "n": 5, "part": "Book One. The Vanishing", "blocks": [{"p": "There was a line on the floor of Gin's genkan where August stopped."}, {"p": "It ran from wall to wall, a hand's width inside the door, exactly where the step up into the flat began. On one side of it was the wet heat of Obon week and the cicadas grinding away through the open window. On the other side was cold. It was not the cold of a draught. It was like the cold at the bottom of a well in summer. It felt old, like air that had been shut in a room for years."}, {"img": "ch11_clearance.jpg", "cap": "Iwata Shūji, the building's handyman, at work in the flat"}, {"img": "char_iwata-shuji.jpg", "cap": "Iwata Shūji, the building handyman, with his toolbox"}, {"p": "Iwata Shūji was the handyman for the building. He had fixed forty-one doors in it over nineteen years, and he told Rei so before he told him anything else. He wanted it understood from the start that he was not a man who believed in things. He knelt in the genkan of Gin's flat with his toolbox open and his reading glasses pushed down his nose, and he talked the whole time. He talked the way some men talk when they are afraid of the quiet, building a wall of words against it."}, {"p": "Rei knew how to read that cold without having to think about it. It was the first thing in a Grid flyer Gin had folded into Chapter Three. <em>Temperature is the first indicator. Not physical temperature, veil temperature.</em> Veil cold did not spread the way real cold spreads. It sat in one place and pointed. The second sign was density, the heaviness of air before a storm that never came. The third was sound, which was not a sound at all, but the feeling that the silence had weight and was listening back. Gin had added a fourth sign in his own hand. <em>Familiarity. The threat that feels like home.</em> Rei stood on the warm side of the line and felt all four at once, in the doorway he had walked through every day of his life."}, {"p": "“It started after Obon began,” Iwata said, not looking at him. He was working a screwdriver he didn't need into a screw that didn't need it. “The tenant downstairs said her ceiling sweated. I came up to check the seal on the window. There's no leak. There's nothing. But you stand here...” He tapped the doorstep with the screwdriver, twice, on the exact line where the cold began. “...and you'll feel the building think you're a stranger.”"}, {"p": "He laughed at his own words, and the laugh came out thin. Rei was standing in his brother's home, in the empty place where Gin's coat used to hang, and the cold was reading him. It was feeling for him in the dark and deciding what he was. Iwata had described it exactly. Rei understood that with a flat, quiet feeling that was worse than fear."}, {"break": "Stay sloppy"}, {"p": "He knew what to do when something read him. He had known since before he could read."}, {"img": "scene_kitchen_lesson.jpg", "cap": "Gin at fourteen teaching small Rei to breathe at the kitchen table"}, {"p": "He was five, maybe six, and it was this kitchen. The table had been too high, so Gin had put him on a cushion. Gin was fourteen and very serious. He made Rei breathe out slowly and then let his attention go loose, like letting a balloon string slip through his fingers. Not gone. Just loose. “Like you're bored,” Gin had said. “Like you're thinking about lunch.” They practised it every evening for a year, the way other children practised times tables. If anyone ever made the air feel funny, Gin said, that was what Rei should do. He should not hold his breath. He should not go still and careful. He should go a little bit stupid, on purpose, and stay that way until the funny feeling went away."}, {"p": "“Why?” Rei had asked."}, {"p": "“Because the wrong breath at the wrong moment marks you,” Gin had said. Then, because Rei was six and looked frightened, he had ruffled his hair and given him the version a child could carry. “Stay sloppy, Rei. Stay unremarkable. That's the whole trick.”"}, {"p": "Rei had stayed sloppy for thirteen years. It was the skill he had practised most in his life. He had always thought it was just who he was. A well-liked, unfocused, ordinary boy. The intern who filed paper and forgot his umbrella."}, {"p": "Now he did it again, in the doorway, with the cold pressing on him. He breathed out. He let his attention go loose and a little foolish. He thought on purpose about whether there was any rice left in the freezer. He did not tighten. The cold passed over him the way a hand passes over a table in the dark, looking for something. It found a slightly bored young man thinking about lunch, and it moved on."}, {"p": "Iwata had noticed nothing. He was still talking."}, {"p": "Rei asked him, carefully, when he had last seen Gin. Iwata thought about it for longer than the question should have needed."}, {"p": "“That's the thing,” he said. “I saw him on the Sunday. Said good morning on the stairs. Polite man, your brother. Always was.” He set the screwdriver down. “But the missus in 3C, she swears she saw him on Friday, by the door, fitting his key. And I checked the log. He hasn't badged the front entrance since the Wednesday. So one of us is wrong about a man we both know on sight.”"}, {"p": "He said it like a small mistake in the accounts. He did not hear yet what he had said. The building had been letting in someone who looked like Gin, on days Gin was not here, through a door that had started to make its own decisions."}, {"p": "He showed Rei the lock, because the lock was the reason he had been called, and a lock was something he could hold. Gin's key, the one Rei had picked up cold off the walkway at four in the morning, slid halfway into the lock and stopped."}, {"p": "Iwata took it from him and tried it himself. Gently, then less gently. His craftsman's patience turned slowly into the small insult a tradesman feels when a thing he understands refuses to behave. “This is his key,” he said. “I cut the spare myself, three years ago. It fit yesterday. I oiled it yesterday.” He drew it out and held the teeth up to the light, and found nothing wrong with them. The fault was not in the key. The fault was in the lock. Sometime between yesterday and now, it had quietly become a lock for a different door."}, {"p": "Then he showed Rei the other thing. It was the thing he had not wanted to call about, and had called about anyway. He pulled the door shut from the inside and let go."}, {"p": "The deadbolt turned. By itself. There was no hand on it, and no draught strong enough to move it. The bolt turned its full quarter-turn and slid home with a heavy, satisfied <em>chunk</em>. The flat had locked them both in."}, {"p": "Iwata stared at the brass knob as if it had spoken to him in a language he half remembered from childhood. For a long moment neither of them reached for it. Reaching for it meant admitting that something in the room had wanted the door shut, and had got its wish."}, {"p": "“It does that now,” Iwata said, very quietly. The wall of words was finally down. “Three times since Thursday. It locks itself. Like it's keeping something in. Or...” He could not stop the next part. It came out of him sideways. “...like it forgot which side the inside is on.”"}, {"p": "Rei turned the bolt back. It came easily, oiled and ordinary, a thing a man had built and a man could undo. But he had felt it now, the small will in the room. The flat was no longer just a place his brother was missing from. It was taking part. The cold by the genkan, the changed lock and the bolt that locked itself were not three separate faults. They were one fact, told three ways, by a home that had started to speak."}, {"p": "Years ago, in the voice Rei had always trusted most, Gin had told him that a place holds the shape of whoever lives there, the way a glove holds the shape of a hand. This place was holding a shape. It was just not his brother's any more."}, {"p": "Iwata packed his toolbox slowly and carefully, with a kind of ceremony he would never have admitted to. At the door he stopped. He opened it now with his own master key, because Gin's key no longer worked and he would not say so out loud again. Then he gave Rei the thing he had been circling for an hour."}, {"p": "“My grandmother kept the house in Niigata,” he said. “She used to say a house knows when the owner's debt comes due. She said the cold spot was the house making room.” He shook his head at himself, ashamed to repeat it. He was a man who fixed things, repeating a thing that could not be fixed. “Making room for what, I asked her. She never said. I think she didn't like the answer either.”"}, {"p": "Then he was gone down the stairs. Rei was alone in the doorway of his brother's flat in the high heat of Obon, the week when the line between the living and the dead is thinnest and the dead come home across the water. And the lock had been changed for whatever was coming across."}]}, {"title": "Chapter Six, the Addendum", "kind": "main", "src": "m_door#2", "n": 6, "part": "Book One. The Vanishing", "blocks": [{"p": "Outside, the afternoon went on as if nothing were wrong. The walkway was bright and hot, and the concrete smelled of dust and of the water someone had splashed on it to cool it down. From an open window on the floor below came the sound of a television showing the national high school baseball tournament, which every August fills the afternoons of the whole country. A bat cracked. A crowd cheered in a stadium far away in the west. From another flat came the smell of incense, burning on a family altar for the dead who were on their way home. Two doors down, the neighbour's futon still hung over the rail."}, {"p": "He did not go inside. He sat down on the warm side of the cold line, with his back against the wall of the outside walkway, and opened the archive on his knees at the place he had reached in order. It was the end of Chapter Four, the section on suppression. The heading said <em>Read it twice. Nobody knows this exists. This is why high-rank practitioners die.</em> Halfway down the page, Gin had stopped writing as a field officer and started writing to him."}, {"img": "tk_suppression.jpg", "cap": "Suppression, a gift held below the surface"}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Suppression. Technical reference, with Gin's notes", "lines": ["What it is. A rare secondary ability allowing a practitioner to mask their true rank from external assessment, including Auditor reading, entity perception, and most detection equipment. The rank remains. The broadcast changes.", "What it is not. A stability technique. A suppressed practitioner at 12% is still at 12%, presenting differently, at the same risk. Nor does it hide the structural thinning a high-rank aperture produces. It hides the signal. It cannot hide the effect.", "Family lineage. Appears in family lines more strongly than any other secondary ability. The Uedera family variant produces an environmental interference pattern: the absence reads as background noise, not deliberate masking.", "Rei Uedera. Presented rank: D. Null-signature reads as distracted, unfocused, a Sensitive rank not paying attention. This is the Uedera suppression variant functioning as trained. The disguise is working.", "Actual rank: A. Possibly touching S at ceiling.", "To Rei, if this is you: your null reads as distraction. Do not tighten it. The slightly sloppy, slightly unfocused quality is the disguise and it works because it is consistent. The day you present a clean null it will read as deliberate masking and someone will start asking why. Stay sloppy. Stay unremarkable. Read Chapter 5 twice. Then read it again."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the page once, then again, slowly, until he was sure what it said. Suppression was a way of hiding how much power a person had. The power stayed the same. Only what other people could feel of it changed. In the Uedera family it worked in a particular way. It did not make a person feel empty, which would look suspicious. It made them feel like noise, like someone not paying attention. That was what Gin had taught him at the kitchen table. And everyone who had ever looked at Rei had felt a D, a low and harmless grade. Under the noise, the page said, he was an A."}, {"p": "Rei looked at the single letter and did not feel anything for a while. He did not know yet exactly where an A sat on the Division's scale. He knew that his brother, after six years, was officially a C. He had always been the boy who was bad at exams and good at noticing, who fell asleep on the couch in the afternoons and never knew why he was so tired. Gin had known why. Gin had written it down in a book, put the book on a shelf and made him rice. Rei turned the pages forward through Chapter Five and Chapter Six, faster than Gin would have liked, until he found the note Gin had put at the very end of the addendum, on its own page."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Personal Archive. Private Note", "lines": ["I have been debating including this since I began the archive. Including it now.", "The family has never passed information about this. My parents did not know what they were carrying.", "What I know about my ancestors is from their case files. Two of them were field operatives during the early period, before the CVD's current structure existed, when a specific unit was deployed to manage what was crossing over from the other side. In those files there are moments where the documented response should not have been possible for a single-path practitioner at the recorded rank.", "They had blocked one path each, maybe two. Not unlocked, blocked, held unused alongside whatever they presented as. The same way I present as C and hold the rest back. They never opened what they had. They just held it there, and occasionally, under sufficient pressure, something else moved.", "I have unlocked four paths. Not fully. But they are present and accessible and when I push hard enough in the right direction they engage. I have been developing it deliberately for four years without telling anyone, because the ability taxonomy from the CVD does not have a category for what I am describing.", "Rei. I believe he has the same profile. The way his Clairsentience bleeds into other registers occasionally in ways that a single-path ability should not. The specific chaos of his passive output, which reads as multiple things interfering with each other rather than one thing running loud. I believe he has more than one path available to him. I believe he has not accessed any of them deliberately.", "I have not told him this. I am going to have the conversation. I am documenting this commitment here, again, in the hope that committing it to the archive creates the accountability I have not been able to create through internal resolve alone.", "Keys on the hook. Said something I have not said before. The archive is more complete than it was this morning."]}}, {"p": "A path, Rei knew from the earlier chapters, was the kind of power a person had. Reading what people and places give off was one path. Moving things without touching them was another. Most people with power had one path, and only one, for their whole life."}, {"p": "So that was the secret. It was not a curse. It was not a superpower. It was a family of people born with more than one door open, who had learned, one generation after another, to stand in front of the extra doors so nobody would see them. Rei had been born with two. He could feel the second one now that he knew to look, the way a person suddenly notices a sound that has been in the room all along. It was nothing he could name. It was only the sense that the noise Gin had taught him to make had always been covering more than one thing."}, {"p": "Gin had been going to tell him. He had written that down twice, in two different chapters, and then he had run out of time."}, {"p": "Rei thought about the entries in the archive that stopped being entries after the fourteenth. He thought about the case Gin had closed too fast, the drowning, and the address in no file. He thought about a debt that had never been Gin's to pay, and the brother who would pay it anyway, with the only thing he had left, which was his own place in his own life."}, {"p": "And he understood, sitting there, that this was what it looked like from the outside. Not a disappearance. Not violence. Nothing the Division had a form for. A change of tenant. A man slowly handing himself over to whatever he had agreed to let in. And the only witnesses were a handyman who did not believe in things and a brother who was learning, room by room, to read the signs before he could bear to say what they meant."}, {"p": "He took the key, Gin's useless key that fit nothing now, and put it in his pocket anyway. It was the shape of a door Gin had once been able to open. Rei was not ready to be a man who threw away the last key to his brother."}, {"p": "Behind him, as he pulled the door shut from the warm side, the deadbolt turned. He did not reach for it. He let the flat keep what it was keeping. He went down the stairs into the heat, and the cicadas took him back. He carried the cold out into August in the small of his back, where it would sit for days, reading him, deciding what he was. And every time it looked, it would find a slightly bored young man, thinking about lunch."}]}, {"title": "The Briefing They Kept Quiet", "kind": "main", "src": "m_briefing", "n": 7, "part": "Book One. The Vanishing", "blocks": [{"p": "The next morning Rei walked down the hill to Ōji station. It was barely nine and already thirty-two degrees. Salarymen came the other way with their jackets over their arms, wiping their necks with small towels. At the bottom of the hill a Toden Arakawa tram rolled past on the street, a single small carriage, ringing its bell. The tram is the last of the old streetcars that once ran all over Tokyo, and it still crosses Kita from end to end, stopping at little platforms beside its track. A grandmother on board held a bunch of flowers wrapped in newspaper on her lap. Behind the station, the trees of Asukayama were loud with cicadas. Rei crossed to the east exit."}, {"p": "The building looked like a tax office. It was meant to."}, {"p": "It stood a minute's walk from the east exit of Ōji station, a grey block of no particular style, the kind of building the eye slides off. Rei had walked past it most of his life without once looking up. That was what it was for."}, {"p": "Gin had written about it on a page Rei had read the night before. It was a list of the building's rooms that he had put together over six years, with a note on how he knew each one. <em>The grey block by Ōji Station. Home, most days.</em> The Division had not put it there by accident. Under it ran the place where the fault lines beneath the ward crossed, the thinnest point of the veil in the whole district. Around it lay the worst ground the district had. The freight-yard blocks to the north. The station underpass to the south. The riverside memorial to the east. The old market arcade to the west. <em>They built here to be close to all of it,</em> Gin had written, <em>and to be standing on the access while they did.</em> Every CVD office in the world was built the same way, he said. It sat in the middle of its city's worst ground, on the thinnest point, looking like nothing."}, {"p": "The plate by the door said <em>Civil Veil Division</em> and, underneath, <em>Ministry of Internal Affairs.</em> Gin had once put its purpose more plainly than the plate did. Under all the official language and the secret programs, he said, what the Division actually did on a Tuesday afternoon was keep count of the things from the other side that were loose in the living world, and keep the number down. Nobody in Kita called it by the name on the plate. Older people called it the Directorate, and so did everyone who worked inside it. After a while, so did Rei."}, {"p": "Inside, the intake hall was the coldest warm room in Tokyo. They kept it at exactly the temperature that let a salaryman keep his jacket on and a frightened person keep their hands from shaking. After four minutes in one of the plastic chairs bolted to the floor, Rei could no longer tell which of the two he was. The clerk behind the glass had a machine that gave out numbered tickets, a stamp, and a face set into a careful blankness. It was the face of a person who had decided in advance that whatever he had come to report was already dealt with."}, {"p": "He slid a slip with his brother's name across the steel tray under the window and watched her type it. He watched for the small light of recognition that should cross any face when a name comes up on a screen. It did not come."}, {"p": "“Uedera Gin,” she read back, flat, and turned the monitor a careful two degrees away from him."}, {"p": "He told her Gin had been gone since Wednesday night. He told her about the door that locked itself, and the neighbour who had seen a man with Gin's face fit a key on a day Gin never badged into the building. He heard his own voice doing what voices do in rooms like this. It went reasonable. It went small. It sanded the edges off the impossible, so the impossible would fit through the slot under the glass."}, {"p": "She listened without moving and without any sign of feeling. When he finished, she did not write anything down. “I'm showing him as current,” she said. “Status active. Last verification...” She leaned toward the screen, and he leaned with her, and they both read a date. The date was three days ago."}, {"p": "Three days ago Gin's flat had been a held breath and an empty key hook. Three days ago someone in this building had looked at his brother's file and certified that he was, in the words of the form, <em>present and accounted for.</em>"}, {"p": "Rei asked the only question that mattered, which was who. The clerk's hand found the stamp without her looking at it, the way a hand finds the rail on a stair it has climbed a thousand times."}, {"p": "“Verifications are system-generated,” she said. The sentence was worn smooth, like something repeated until it stopped meaning anything. “If you'd like to file a discrepancy, take a ticket.”"}, {"p": "The machine ticked. A paper ticket slid out toward him with a number on it. Holding it, Rei understood what he had been given. It was his place in a line made to be longer than his patience. It came from an office that had already decided his brother was not missing, because someone had taken the trouble to make sure the form would say so."}]}, {"title": "The Waiting Chairs", "kind": "main", "src": "m_briefing#2", "n": 8, "part": "Book One. The Vanishing", "blocks": [{"img": "ch9_identify.jpg", "cap": "The waiting chairs in the intake hall"}, {"p": "The hall was fuller than he expected. Obon week was a busy week here, he guessed. An old woman in a dark kimono sat very straight with a framed photograph of a man on her lap, holding it face down. A young mother rocked a baby that would not stop crying, and every few minutes she looked up at the ceiling, as if the crying had a reason up there. A man in workman's overalls, still dusty from a building site, filled in a form with great care, pressing so hard that the pen went through the paper. Nobody spoke to anybody. A television high on the wall showed the weather. Thirty-five degrees. Take care against heatstroke. Drink water."}, {"p": "While he waited, he read what the hall gave him to read. On a clipboard chained to the counter there was a stack of registration forms, the kind people filled in when they walked in off the street to be measured. The second page was a grid of tick boxes. Gin had told him once, without explaining, that every number in the Directorate came from that one page."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "Civil Veil Division. Practitioner Intake. Section B. Rank and Path (for assessor use)", "lines": ["Rank (natural). Tick one. Assessed by the panel. Do not self-assess.", "☐ Null. No aperture. About 60 in 100 people. Cannot perceive, cannot be trained.", "☐ Sensitive. Passive bleed only. Feels the shiver and forgets it.", "☐ Postulate. Level 1. About 20 in 100. Passive perception, mostly felt as instinct. Low monitor.", "☐ Adept. Level 2. About 10 in 100. First rank where deliberate use is possible. Registration encouraged.", "☐ Ritualist. Level 3. Grade C. About 5 in 100. The working grade of field operations. Registration mandatory.", "☐ Channel. Level 4. Grade B. About 3 in 100. The other side begins to notice back. Flagged.", "☐ Arbiter. Level 5. Grade A. About 1.5 in 100. Thins the local membrane whether used or not. Priority.", "☐ Vanguard. Grade S. Hundreds in the world. The city notices. Cannot be compelled.", "☐ Singularity or Guardian. Grade SS, fifty to eighty in the world. Grade SSS, single digits.", "Axis. Not a rank. Not to be ticked.", "Path (primary). Tick one. Path is independent of rank.", "☐ Clairsentience. About 28 in 100 practitioners. Reads what people, objects and places give off. Low cost per use, high over time.", "☐ Telepathy. About 18 in 100. Enters minds. High cost per use. Most regulated.", "☐ Telekinesis. About 22 in 100. Force at a distance. Leaves signatures for hours.", "☐ Psychometabolism. About 14 in 100. The body. Healing others is among the highest costs of any path.", "☐ Psychokinesis. About 11 in 100. Heat, current, sound, light. Very high cost.", "☐ Teleportation. About 7 in 100. Transit through the Threshold. Extreme cost. Maximum priority.", "Natural rank is where you sit without training. Trained rank is where development can reach. Zero cannot be trained into anything."]}}, {"p": "Rei went down the form slowly. It was the whole ladder in one place. At the bottom were most people, who could feel nothing at all. Above them, smaller and smaller groups, each able to do more than the last. Ritualist was the grade most field officers had, the C. Then Channel, the B. Then Arbiter, the A, only one or two people in a hundred, the first rank where a person thinned the veil around them just by being alive. Above that the numbers stopped being numbers and became a few hundred people in the whole world, then a few dozen, then a handful. The second half of the form was the paths, the six kinds of power, and it did not matter how strong a person was. Anyone at any rank had one of them."}, {"p": "Rei looked at the boxes for a long time. Somewhere in this building there was a copy of the form with Gin's name on it and a tick against <em>Ritualist</em>. Gin had sat in the intake chair, been measured as a Ritualist, and stayed a Ritualist for six years. There was no copy with Rei's name. He had never ticked anything. Yesterday, on the walkway, he had read a single letter in Gin's print, the A after <em>Actual rank</em>. On this form that was the box marked <em>Arbiter</em>, the first line on the page with the word <em>Priority</em> beside it."}, {"p": "He put the clipboard back exactly where it had been."}, {"p": "At ten past ten a woman came through the inner door and walked the length of the hall. She was not a clerk. She carried nothing. She walked slowly, the way a person walks through a gallery, and as she passed each row of chairs the people in them shifted without knowing why. Rei knew what she was before she was halfway down the room. He had read about her kind two nights ago, on a page of Gin's book with a red stamp across the top."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "CVD Field Classification. Auditor Profile. Restricted Access", "lines": ["Designation. Auditor. Psychic signature assessment operative.", "Ability basis. Clairsentience variant. Signature reading.", "Function. Real-time assessment of practitioner power level, veil integrity status, ability type, and active use indicators.", "Assessment range. Standard 15m radius passive read. Active scan up to 200m with ability use. Signature lingering readable up to 6 hours post-use at location.", "Limitations. Cannot read suppressed practitioners reliably. Suppression presents as anomalous null-signature, identifiable as suppression but not readable through it. Estimated 2 to 4% of the practitioner population possesses suppression ability.", "Psychological profile. High veil sensitivity. Elevated risk of chronic low-integrity degradation. Standard rotation 6 months active field, 3 months supervised recovery. Non-compliance with rotation correlates with 340% increased possession risk.", "CVD deployment. Registration processing centers, field surveillance units, Protocol active operations support, Tower family contracted assessment services."]}}, {"p": "An Auditor, then. A person whose whole job was to feel how much power the people around her had, the way a sniffer dog smells a bag. Gin's note had been underneath the page. <em>An Auditor can tell that someone is suppressing. The null reads as a specific kind of absence, like a space where a sound should be. The Uedera family suppression variant is, as far as I am aware, undetected in the literature. I have been careful to ensure it stays that way.</em>"}, {"p": "A clean silence would be a hole in the room, and holes got noticed. Gin had not taught him to make a hole. Gin had taught him to make noise."}, {"p": "The page said fifteen metres. Rei counted the chairs between them to measure it, and he felt the moment she came inside the circle. It was a faint pressure, like someone reading over his shoulder. He did not go still. He did not go careful. He breathed out and let his attention go loose and a little foolish. He looked at the ticket in his hand and wondered, honestly, whether the vending machine by the door took Suica cards. He was a tired nineteen-year-old in yesterday's T-shirt who had come to complain about a form. He let himself be exactly that, and nothing behind it."}, {"p": "Her eyes passed over him and did not stop. Whatever she felt there was static, the hum of a crowded room, background. She moved on down the row and through the far door, and the hall let its breath out."}, {"p": "Rei sat with his hands between his knees until they stopped shaking. <em>Stay sloppy. Stay unremarkable.</em> It had worked. It had worked in the one building in the city built to catch him. He could not decide whether that made him feel safe or entirely alone."}, {"p": "Eventually they sent him to a man named Sekine. His office was a glass box on the fourth floor, with a view of the air-conditioning units on the next roof and a plant slowly dying in a corner. Sekine was kind in the way that costs nothing. He stood when Rei came in. He offered him the good chair. He used Gin's given name as if they had been at school together. Within ninety seconds Rei understood that the kindness was the surface. It was the soft covering the building wore, so that its hardness never had to touch anyone directly."}, {"p": "Sekine agreed with everything. He agreed that the situation was concerning. He agreed that the verification looked, on its face, irregular. He agreed, with a small sad tilt of the head, that he could find no record of who had authorised it. He agreed so completely and so soon that Rei realised agreeing was how this office said no."}, {"p": "Rei asked him to mark Gin missing. He asked him to change the one field on the form that would turn the whole machine the other way, that would put his brother's face on a notice board instead of a verification on a server."}, {"p": "Sekine folded his hands on the desk, very gently. “Here is the difficulty,” he said. And the difficulty, when he laid it out, was perfect. To file a missing-person report on a registered officer, the officer's supervisor had to sign it too. Gin's supervisor's desk had, as of the last staff list, nobody at it. The chair was empty, waiting for an appointment that kept not happening."}, {"p": "“So you see,” Sekine said, almost apologetic, “there is no one with the standing to declare him gone.”"}, {"p": "The sentence landed in the cold warm room and stayed there. A man had been erased, not by force but by paperwork, by the careful removal of every official who had the authority to notice."}, {"p": "Rei watched Sekine's face for the lie and did not find one, and that was the worst of it. He was not lying. He was reciting. He was a decent man reading Rei the rules of a game. And the rules had been written, recently and quietly, by someone who wanted exactly this room to give exactly this answer."}, {"p": "When Rei asked who had left the supervisor's chair empty, Sekine glanced, just once, at the dying plant, as if it might know. The previous supervisor, he said, had taken a personal leave in late July. “For health. He'd been...” Sekine searched for the word a kind man uses for a colleague who fell apart. “...helped, I think, by someone. A consultation. He spoke very warmly of it.”"}, {"p": "The word <em>helped</em> went into Rei like cold water down a wall. He had heard that word before, in his brother's flat, in the voice of a thing that did favours. He knew now that the briefing they had kept quiet was never a briefing at all. It was a room being cleared."}, {"p": "He left through the same gate he had come in by. On instinct, the bad, useful instinct of a person who has stopped believing the official story, he took out the visitor's pass they had clipped to him at the door on a lanyard. He looked at it properly for the first time."}, {"p": "It was not a visitor's pass. The clerk, somewhere in the long blank routine of intake, had made a mistake, or been made to make one. The card in his hand gave low-level access to the building under a name that was not his. It was a temporary pass issued, the small print said, <em>on behalf of the supervising desk, G. Uedera.</em>"}, {"p": "For one impossible second Rei read it as a message from his brother, a hand reaching out of the machine. Then the colder reading arrived. The empty chair was not empty. Something was sitting in it, long enough to issue passes, quietly, in his brother's name. And it had just, by accident or on purpose, handed him the keys to the building that said Gin was not missing."}, {"img": "cvd_withheld.jpg", "cap": "The corridor outside intake and the plain door halfway along"}, {"p": "The corridor outside intake ran long and bright under white strip lights toward the lifts. Halfway along it he passed a plain door with a plain plate. <em>Assessment and Registry.</em> Below it, on a slip of card in a holder, were the names of that week's panel, and the second of them was <em>Okada S.</em> The door stood open a hand's width. Inside there was a single chair facing a desk, an instrument on the desk, and nothing else. Rei knew that room from Gin's list too. <em>The intake chair. Where an aperture becomes a rank and a number. I have sat in it lying.</em> Six years of sitting in that chair and being measured wrong on purpose. <em>It is the most important work I ever did,</em> Gin had written, <em>and no one will ever know I did it.</em> Rei did not slow down. Slowing down would have been interesting."}, {"p": "The cold that lived in Gin's flat was here too, he realised. Thinner. Weaker. A single thread of it lay along the floor like a tripwire. He could feel where it led. It did not lead toward the exit. It led deeper, toward the archive floors below, toward the locked registry where the verification had been made. He stood at the point where the way out and the way in divided. In his fist was a pass that should not exist, issued in the name of a missing man. Around him, under the bright lights, was a building that had decided, on excellent paper, that nothing was wrong."}, {"p": "Somewhere below him a printer was running. He could hear it through the floor, the soft, patient sound of a document printing itself, certifying again, on schedule, that Uedera Gin was present and accounted for. A small machine was keeping a large lie alive, while the city outside ground out its cicadas and lit lanterns for the dead it was willing to admit were gone."}, {"p": "He thought of the word <em>helped.</em> He thought of an empty chair that gave out keys. And he understood, with the flat clearness that had replaced fear in him over these three days, what he had to do. To find his brother he would have to walk in the one direction this building had been built to stop him going. And he would have to do it with his brother's name clipped to his shirt, on a pass that someone, or something, had made out of Gin's stolen authority."}]}, {"title": "The Form That Files Itself", "kind": "main", "src": "m_form", "n": 9, "part": "Book One. The Vanishing", "blocks": [{"p": "Somewhere in the dark ahead, a printer breathed out a page."}, {"img": "cvd_sublevel.jpg", "cap": "The way down to the registry floor"}, {"p": "The registry floor was two levels below the dying plant and the kind man. Rei went down by the back stairs, because the lifts had cameras and the stairs, as far as he could see, did not."}, {"p": "The stairwell was painted the same grey as the outside of the building. Each landing had a fire extinguisher, a green running-man exit sign and a small window of frosted glass that let in a dull white light. On the first landing down the air was still warm. On the second it was not. The sounds of the building fell away above him, the phones, the ticket machine, the quiet voices. By the bottom of the stairs Rei could hear only his own breathing and his shoes on the concrete, and his breath had started to show faintly in front of his face, in August. At the bottom there was a heavy door with a card reader. He held up the borrowed pass. The reader thought about it for a pause long enough to make his scalp prickle. Then it decided that he was allowed."}, {"p": "Inside, the cold was no longer a thread. It filled the whole room, the way weather fills a valley. Rows of grey filing cabinets stood in the half-light like people who had been waiting a long time. The only sound was the printer he had heard through the floor. It was a single machine at the end of the central aisle, breathing out a page every few minutes into a tray already thick with pages."}, {"p": "He walked toward it past drawers labelled in handwriting too even to be human. As he walked, he understood that this was not a place where records were stored. It was a place where records were kept alive. They were fed, certified and renewed, so that the people they described could be quietly held in whatever state the building wanted them in."}, {"p": "The page in the tray was his brother."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "Civil Veil Division. Registry. Status Verification", "lines": ["UEDERA GIN", "STATUS: ACTIVE", "VERIFICATION: AUTOMATIC", "NEXT: 06:00"]}}, {"p": "The page beneath it was the same. And the one below that, the same, and the same. A stack of identical certificates went back through the days in layers. Each one was a small official act of looking at an empty chair and writing <em>present.</em>"}, {"p": "He lifted the stack, and the dates ran backward under his thumb. Three days. A week. Two weeks. Then the pattern broke. The oldest pages in the tray were different. They were not automatic. They had been signed, once, by a person, before the machine took over. And the person who started the lie had written a short note in the margin of the first one, with an initial. No machine would ever write a note like that. The ink had gone the brown of old blood."}, {"p": "<em>Per consultation. Y. He asked so little.</em>"}, {"p": "Rei stood among the cold cabinets with the proof in his hands, and he saw how the whole thing worked. Someone had started this by hand. Someone had sat at a desk in late July and made the first false certificate himself, willingly, gratefully. A consultation had asked very little of him, and had given him, Rei could only guess, something he wanted very much. Then he had taught the machine to do the rest. He had set it to renew Gin every morning at six, and then walked away into a personal leave for his health. And the form had been filing itself ever since, keeping Rei's brother <em>present</em> in the only record that could ever have declared him gone."}, {"p": "He should have left. He photographed the signed pages, and he should have turned for the door. Instead the cold pulled him sideways, down a row he had no reason to enter, the way grief pulls a hand toward a drawer it should not open."}, {"img": "res_cvdarchive.jpg", "cap": "The older cabinets of the registry, labelled with names"}, {"p": "The cabinets here were older. Their labels were names, and the names had small coloured tabs. After the third one he understood the colours. Green meant active. Green was the lie. There were more green tabs in this row than any building should need. A whole quiet harvest of people the registry was certifying as present, each with a tray, each with a printer somewhere bringing them back to life on paper at six in the morning. Beside each of the oldest, in the same brown ink, was the same note. <em>Per consultation. Y.</em>"}, {"p": "The thing that had done this had a name on the streets of Kita, though Rei did not learn it until later. People there called it the Healer. It had been busy for much longer than Gin's three days. It had asked so little of so many, and filed every favour in a room two floors below the kindest man Rei had ever met."}, {"p": "He thought of the secret page in Gin's book, the one with the heaviest stamp. <em>Component One. Catalogue. Identification and detailed profiling of all practitioners at Level 3 and above.</em> At the kitchen table it had been words on paper. Now he was standing inside it. This was what a catalogue looked like when someone had their hand on the drawer. A name, a tab, a status, a printer. The building did not need to find a person to hold him. It only needed to keep writing down that he was where it wanted him to be."}, {"p": "At the end of the row the labels reached the U's. He found the drawer before he had decided to look for it. <em>UEDERA.</em> Inside there was one folder, with a green tab and his brother's name on it. It was thick with six years of assessments, and every one of them read C."}, {"p": "There was no second folder. Rei went through the drawer twice with cold fingers, and then the drawers on either side of it, in case a card had been put in the wrong place. There was nothing with his name on it anywhere. He remembered a line from the preface to Gin's Chapter Four, written on a Wednesday when they had eaten ramen and Rei had fallen asleep on the couch. <em>Rei's file in the CVD system does not exist. He has never been formally assessed.</em> He had read it as a fact. Standing in this room, he read it again as a piece of work. Somebody had kept that drawer empty for nineteen years, in a building whose whole purpose was to fill it."}, {"p": "He closed the drawer very gently, as if the empty space inside it could be bruised."}, {"p": "One drawer was already open. Rei had not opened it. It stuck an inch out into the aisle, as if someone had just looked something up. The file inside was not green, and it was not under any name he knew. It was an index, a list of the registry's own lists. And the entry it lay open at was not a person. It was a seat."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "Registry Index. Domains", "lines": ["DOMAIN XIII", "REGISTER: NULL-EMOTIVE (UNCLASSIFIED)", "STATUS: VACANT", "SINCE: 2009", "VERIFICATION: PENDING APPOINTMENT"]}}, {"p": "The same words. The same even, machine-like hand. But here they described a seat, not a man. Thirteen registers, Rei remembered, and here was the thirteenth, written up like a chair in some other office entirely. The building was keeping it <em>vacant</em> the same way it kept his brother <em>active</em>. It wrote the condition it wanted down every morning, again and again, until the truth gave up arguing."}, {"p": "Rei looked at the word <em>null-emotive</em>. It meant, as far as he could tell, a register with no feeling in it at all. He thought of the Kuroda house, and the fold, and the word that had settled in him there with no pity in it. <em>Duty.</em> The building had a name for this seat, and it was the wrong one. Nobody here had looked closely enough to see what kind of chair it was. Looking at it, he understood that he had walked out of his brother's disappearance and into the edge of something bigger that used the same pen. Whatever was thinning the wall between the worlds was also a clerk. It did its largest work on the smallest pieces of paper."}, {"p": "The cold sharpened, the way a held breath sharpens just before it is let go. He became aware that the open drawer had not been left open by chance. It had been left open for him. The pass that should not exist had not been a simple mistake. It had walked him down two floors and along one row to a drawer that stuck out into the aisle at exactly the height of his eyes. And somewhere a kind, busy thing was watching what he would do with an empty seat it had gone to the trouble of showing him."}, {"p": "He did the only thing he knew how to do. He breathed out and let his attention go loose, and he looked at the drawer the way a bored intern looks at a drawer. It did not help. Whatever was watching had not come to measure his rank. It had come to see what he wanted."}, {"p": "The favour had found him. It had not asked for anything yet. For now it was only letting him read. It was letting him feel the link between a brother kept present and a seat kept empty. And it was trusting, the way it trusted everyone, that once he saw how little it asked, he would want very much to be helped."}, {"p": "He came up out of the building into the full weight of the August morning, and the city did not know. That was the thing that nearly took his legs out from under him on the steps of the Directorate. Tokyo was going about Obon. Strings of lanterns hung between the lampposts for the dead who were allowed to come home. A woman was arranging chrysanthemums in a bucket outside a konbini, the white and yellow flowers people take to graves. A child was being walked to the swimming pool with a towel around his neck. And two floors below all of it a machine was certifying Rei's brother alive on a schedule, and none of these warm, ordinary, breathing people would ever take a ticket in that line."}, {"p": "He had the photographs in his phone. He had the pass on its lanyard, which he had not given back. He stood in the heat, shivering. The cold had marked him as a stranger, and he carried that with him now, wherever he went."}, {"p": "The cicadas ground on. Somewhere over the rooftops a temple bell rang slowly. People say that sound carries to the other side. Rei thought about which side his brother was on. Not dead. The registry insisted he was not dead. Only <em>active.</em> Only <em>present.</em> Only renewed at six each morning by a form that had learned to file itself, from the moment a tired official decided a favour had asked so little of him."}, {"p": "He thought about Sekine's dying plant and the chair with no one in it. He thought about a row of green tabs and a drawer that had opened itself to show him an empty seat. And he understood that the briefing they had kept quiet was the truest document in the building. It was not a thing said. It was a thing carefully <em>not</em> said, an absence managed so smoothly that the only way to see it was to become the kind of person who notices what is missing. Over three days in his brother's cold home, he had become that person."}, {"p": "He took out the lanyard one more time, there on the steps, in the lantern light and the heat, and looked at the pass that carried the authority of a man who was not gone. He could give it back. He could walk into the ordinary morning and let the form file itself for ever."}, {"p": "He put it back in his pocket, next to Gin's useless key. He would come back tomorrow, before six, and be standing at the printer when it tried to renew his brother. He would see for himself whether an absence that had been written down could also be crossed out. Or whether the only thing his borrowed key really opened was the next room in a trap. A trap that had been certifying him, too, as present and accounted for, from the moment he took a ticket."}]}]}, {"title": "Book Two", "sub": "The Handwriting", "chapters": [{"title": "The Lucid Window", "kind": "main", "src": "m_lucid", "n": 10, "part": "Book Two. The Handwriting", "blocks": [{"p": "The call log had turned into a column of his brother's name. Gin, Gin, Gin, all the way down the screen in small red letters. Red meant a call that nobody answered. It looked like names cut into a memorial stone."}, {"img": "res_apartmentlivingroom.jpg", "cap": "The kitchen table under the one lamp"}, {"p": "Rei sat at the kitchen table under the one lamp and looked at it. It was late, and the flat was hot and still. Outside, the cicadas had not stopped since noon. In August in Tokyo they never really stop. They sit in every tree and scream until long after dark, a dry, grinding noise that gets inside the ears and stays there."}, {"p": "Along the canal behind the building, a neighbour was hanging the first Obon lantern of the week. It was a small box of white paper on a pole, with a candle bulb inside. The warm wind pushed it, and it knocked against the pole again and again. It sounded like a knuckle on a door."}, {"p": "Rei had called nineteen times since the night he found the hook by the door empty. Each time the line had rung on and on. And each time the silence at the other end had not felt empty. It felt held. It was the silence of a phone lying in someone's hand while that someone decided whether to answer it."}, {"p": "He told himself it was only a dead battery. A phone left in a drawer. Gin forgot things. Once he had left his phone in a bowl of dry rice for two days, to dry it out after the rain, and then forgotten it was there. Rei held on to that story for as long as it would hold him."}, {"p": "Then he pressed the name a twentieth time."}, {"p": "It rang twice. Then the ringing stopped. There was no voicemail tone. There was no hiss of a pocket or rustle of cloth. There was only breathing. It was slow and level and a little too careful, like someone counting."}, {"p": "\"Rei.\""}, {"p": "It was Gin's voice. Every part of it was right. The slight roughness at the bottom. The way the name went up a little at the end. But it was right the way a photograph is right. Nothing moved behind it. It said his name as if it were reading the word off a card someone had handed it."}, {"p": "Rei's mouth had gone dry. He had planned a dozen things to say, and every one of them was gone. What came out was the oldest thing he had."}, {"p": "\"Gin. Nii-san. It's me. Talk to me.\""}, {"p": "<em>Nii-san</em> means big brother. It is what a younger brother calls an older one at home. It came out of him now without his asking."}, {"p": "Something on the other end caught. It was not a word. It was a breath taken wrong. It was the breath a man takes when he has been held under water and his face has just come up into the air."}, {"p": "\"<em>Go home.</em>\" The voice was suddenly ragged and frightened, close to the phone. For the length of one sentence it was Gin, all of him. \"<em>This isn't a thing you walk toward and walk back from the same.</em>\""}, {"p": "Then it was gone. Rei heard it go. The breathing smoothed out. The silence came back over the line, like water closing over a stone."}, {"p": "\"Gin?\""}, {"p": "\"I'm here.\" The voice was calm again. Polite. \"I'm sorry. I'm very tired, Rei. I didn't mean to frighten you.\""}, {"p": "Rei pressed the phone so hard against his ear that it hurt. He knew that broken breath. He had heard it many times, years ago, through the wall between their rooms. Gin used to wake from bad dreams with exactly that breath, and then call out in the dark to check that Rei was still there."}, {"p": "And he knew how long it had lasted. He had read about it in Gin's book, in a chapter about how to read a possessed person from the outside. He had pushed the page away at the time, because it was about strangers. One line had stayed with him anyway. <em>Brief surface moments, four seconds, sometimes less, where the original person's emotional register breaks through before being suppressed again.</em>"}, {"p": "Four seconds. Less time than it takes to say a sentence. That was how much of himself his brother had been able to steal back."}, {"p": "The smooth voice did not hang up. That frightened Rei more than anything it said."}, {"img": "scene_three_am_exams.jpg", "cap": "Three in the morning before Rei's exams"}, {"p": "Instead it talked to him, gently. It talked the way Gin used to talk him down at three in the morning when exams were close and the walls of the flat felt too near. It asked if he had eaten. It asked if he was sleeping. And then, without ever seeming to change the subject, it began to explain."}, {"p": "\"Do you remember the spring of 2011?\" it said. \"You were fifteen. You watched the water on the television for a week and stopped eating. I remember.\""}, {"p": "Rei remembered too. He had sat on the floor of this flat and watched the black water on the news, over and over, until Gin turned the television off."}, {"p": "The voice waited a moment. The pause was perfectly timed."}, {"p": "\"Twenty thousand people, Rei. Most of them were never given their rites. No grave, no name read out, nobody to set them down. They are still waiting. The membrane over Kita has been weeping for them ever since. You've felt it. Don't tell me you haven't.\""}, {"p": "Rei said nothing. He had felt it. He had felt it on his way to the konbini at night. Rei went to the one by the river for bread and cold tea. And on the way, near the water, the air was always heavy. He had always put it down to the humidity."}, {"p": "\"Something is owed to them,\" the voice said. \"Someone with the right grade, and the right grief, could finally close that wound. What looks like a catastrophe to a frightened intern looks different from higher up. From there it looks like a debt being paid at last.\""}, {"p": "It was not lying. That was the horror of it. Every piece was true. The dead were real. The weeping was real. The debt, Rei thought, might even be real. But the voice set the true things one on top of another, each a little crooked, and the pile leaned. It leaned toward something Rei could not see."}, {"p": "And he found that he was nodding, alone in the empty kitchen."}, {"p": "He stopped. He put his free hand flat on the table and felt the wood under it. There was an old knife scar near the edge, from the day Gin cut bread without a board. Rei made himself listen again. This time he did not listen to the words. He listened to what the words wanted from him."}, {"p": "The broken voice had wanted one thing. <em>Go home.</em> It wanted him away, safe, out of all this. It asked nothing for itself."}, {"p": "The smooth voice wanted him closer."}, {"p": "\"I could use your help,\" it was saying now, softly. \"Not tonight. Soon. There are things I can't carry alone anymore. You were always good at noticing what other people miss.\""}, {"p": "Rei understood it then, sitting under the lamp. A thing that means to close a wound needs hands. It needed hands it could call its own. And a brother's love was the strongest leash anyone had ever been offered."}, {"p": "\"Where are you?\" he asked."}, {"p": "The voice laughed, very kindly. \"Get some sleep, Rei.\""}, {"p": "And the line went dead."}, {"p": "He sat for a long time with the phone in his lap. Outside, the lantern knocked against its pole. Gin's coat still hung on the back of the other chair, where it had hung since May. Down on the street a late taxi went past slowly, the small red sign in its windscreen saying it was free, looking for someone who wanted to go home."}, {"img": "ch11_space.jpg", "cap": "The flat at ten past two, after the line went dead"}, {"p": "At ten past two the phone buzzed once. It was a voicemail. No call had come through. The phone had not rung at all. Rei played it with his thumb shaking."}, {"p": "The voice on it was so faint it was almost only breath, and it was almost Gin. It said six words, fast, as if someone might take the phone away before the last one."}, {"p": "\"<em>Third drawer. Under the charger. Read.</em>\""}]}, {"title": "The Entries After the 14th", "kind": "main", "src": "m_after14", "n": 11, "part": "Book Two. The Handwriting", "blocks": [{"p": "The price sticker was still on the back. Three hundred and eighty yen, half peeled off, curling up at one corner. It must have curled like that for months, every time Gin opened the drawer."}, {"p": "Third drawer. Under the charger. The voice on the voicemail had been almost nothing, almost Gin, and it had been right. Rei went into Gin's room, opened the third drawer of the desk, and lifted the phone charger. Underneath it lay a cheap notebook with a brown paper cover, the kind sold in any konbini. It was soft at the spine from being bent back. He held it as if it might be hot."}, {"p": "He did not sit in Gin's chair. The chair felt like it belonged to someone who was coming back, and he could not bear to take it. So he sat on the floor beside the desk with his back against the drawers."}, {"p": "Outside the window, more Obon lanterns were going up along the canal. Each one was a small paper box with a light inside, there to show the visiting dead the way home. Rei looked at the soft lights and thought that they were not meant for whatever was walking around in his brother now."}, {"p": "He opened the notebook to the first page. The hand was Gin's. It was the small, cramped writing Gin had had since he was nine. It leaned to the left, as if it were trying to take up less room than it had been given."}, {"break": "The older entries"}, {"p": "He read forward. The early entries were the entries of a tired man. A case that closed too easily. A name that was in no file. Gin's instruments showing the veil over Kita ward getting thinner, when those instruments should not have been able to measure it at all. Gin was frightened in these pages. But it was an ordinary fear. It was the fear of a man who suspects he is right about something and would give anything to be wrong."}, {"p": "Between the cases were the small lines Gin always wrote at the bottom of a page to sign off. Rei had seen a hundred of them in the big Archive on the desk. Keys on the hook. Coffee terrible. Rei took the umbrella. Good. Here, in the cheap notebook, the small lines had begun to change."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's journal. Undated. Early.", "lines": ["Coffee made before I remembered making it.", "The apartment is starting to feel smaller than it used to. I do not think this is about the apartment."]}}, {"img": "scene_coffee_pot.jpg", "cap": "Gin and the coffee he did not remember making"}, {"p": "Rei read that twice. He knew about the coffee. On more than one morning that summer he had come out of his room and found a full pot already going cold. Gin would be standing beside it with a faintly puzzled face, as though the pot had made itself. Rei had laughed at him. He did not laugh now. A man who makes coffee without remembering he made it has lost a few minutes somewhere. Gin had noticed. He had written it down. And then he had gone on with his day."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's journal. A Thursday.", "lines": ["End of a long week. Something in the building across the street has been watching the window since Tuesday.", "I have been noting it without addressing it, which is unlike me.", "I have three theories. I am proceeding on the most comfortable one."]}}, {"p": "Rei got up and went to the window. Across the street stood a block of small flats with dark balconies. On the third floor an air conditioner unit dripped water onto the balcony below. Futons hung over two of the railings. Nothing looked back at him. He stood there until he felt foolish. Then he stood there a little longer, because Gin had never been a man who felt watched for no reason."}, {"p": "A few pages later the watcher came back into the journal. And so did something worse."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's journal. Several days later.", "lines": ["The thing across the street did not return after that first week. I have stopped noting its absence.", "The thing across the street was not Rei. I have not fully resolved what it was. I have chosen not to pursue it further.", "The reason. I am tired this week in a way that is not entirely accounted for by the caseload."]}}, {"p": "<em>Was not Rei.</em> Gin had checked whether the thing watching the window was his own brother. Rei understood why. His own gift ran loud. Gin had told him so in a dozen careful ways over the years, and Rei had always heard them as complaints about the mess in the kitchen. So when Gin felt pressure on the window, his first question was whether it was only Rei, leaking through the wall. It was not. And Gin had decided to let it go, because he was too tired to chase it."}, {"p": "Rei made himself keep reading."}, {"break": "The thing he thought was watching"}, {"p": "Halfway through the notebook, Gin had copied out a few lines from a secret part of the big Archive. He had given that page a caption. <em>The thing the framework will not name.</em>"}, {"p": "The page described something the Directorate's researchers called the Architect Phenomenon. In plain words, the Architect was not a ghost, a person or a group. It was the veil's own slow answer to a practitioner who hid a large rank for years and never let it out. The veil seemed to notice the hidden weight, the way a scale notices a hand pressing on it. Then, over time, it pushed back. The page listed what happened to such people in the end. Their hidden power burst out of them by force. Or they became easy to possess. Or they disappeared, or died. Now and then one survived and ended up in some strange new role. And the person being watched usually knew. The page said the Architect's attention felt like a very particular kind of being watched."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's journal. Underneath.", "lines": ["I have been aware of my status for approximately three years.", "It is not threatening in the way an entity's attention is threatening. It is more like the feeling of a very large system becoming aware of a very small irregularity. Patient. Inevitable. Not personal.", "I find this more frightening than I would find something personal. Personal things can be negotiated with. This cannot.", "If you are a suppressor, if you have been masking your level, you should know that the math eventually balances. Not as threat. As information."]}}, {"p": "Rei knew what Gin was. On paper Gin was a Ritualist, a grade C. He had been measured as a C for six years while he held something much larger underneath. That was the Uedera habit. Stay sloppy. Stay unremarkable. Gin had taught it to Rei at the kitchen table before Rei could read. So when Gin began to feel watched by something huge and patient, he reached for the only explanation his training gave him. The veil itself had noticed what he was hiding. It was coming, slowly, to balance the books."}, {"p": "Rei sat on the floor with the notebook on his knees and felt that explanation wobble. Earlier that night he had read another page of Gin's, in the chapter on demons, and one line of it would not leave him alone. <em>Demons produce directional membrane thinning around their location. The thinning points toward rather than radiating from.</em> In simple words, a demon does not weaken the veil evenly all around, like a lamp lighting a room. It weakens it in a line, pointing at the thing it wants. The Architect, Gin had written, was a system. It was not personal. But the thing across the street had not watched the whole district. It had watched one window."}, {"p": "Rei did not know yet what that meant. He put a pencil mark beside the Architect page and went on."}, {"img": "ch3_thinspot.jpg", "cap": "The Kita thin spot from Observation Log 7-C"}, {"p": "Near the middle of the notebook there was a folder note, copied across from the big Archive. A small piece of tape was stuck to its corner. Gin had always taped the folders he did not trust, so he could see if anyone opened them."}, {"p": "The note was about a thin spot. A thin spot is a place where the veil between the living and the dead has worn weak, the way cloth wears thin at the knee. There are thin spots all over Tokyo. Most are small. The one in Kita was not small."}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "Observation Log 7-C. Kita thin spot", "lines": ["Three anchor signatures in a pattern around the Kita thin spot. I have been documenting them for eight months.", "I put a small piece of tape on the folder and checked.", "Rei has walked past the thin spot twelve times in the last month. I've counted. I'm counting."]}}, {"img": "cvdroom_laboratory.jpg", "cap": "The CVD Research Division, where the stability mapping was done"}, {"p": "Under the folder note Gin had pasted a copy of a research page. It came from the Division's own laboratories, and it was stamped for Protocol eyes only."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "CVD Research Division. Veil Stability Mapping Project. Phase III. Protocol Eyes Only", "lines": ["Veil Stability Anchor Signatures. Open research question.", "Phase III identified anomalous stability signatures globally, points where the membrane is reinforced rather than thinned, in ways no geographic or practitioner-density model predicts. The report calls them self-organizing reinforcement nodes. The practitioners who work near them call them the warm spots.", "The signatures correlate with specific emotional registers in surrounding human and entity populations. They have been continuously active for longer than the project's historical data reaches.", "Practitioners operating in the influence radius of an anchor signature show subtle stability variations the standard mapping does not predict. Mechanism unconfirmed."]}}, {"p": "Rei read it slowly. Most of the city's thin spots were cold wounds in the veil. But the researchers had found the opposite too. There were a few places in the world where the veil was stronger than it should be, as if something were holding it closed. The scientists called them reinforcement nodes. The field officers called them warm spots. Nobody knew what made them."}, {"p": "Gin's caption under the page said only <em>The same room. Very different weather.</em> Three warm spots around one cold wound. And his little brother walking past the wound twelve times a month without knowing it was there. Rei knew exactly which way that was. It was the short cut to the konbini by the river. He had walked it because it was quicker."}]}, {"title": "The Fourteenth of August", "kind": "main", "src": "m_after14#2", "n": 12, "part": "Book Two. The Handwriting", "blocks": [{"p": "Then he reached the entry dated the fourteenth of August. It was three lines long, and it stopped in the middle of a sentence."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's journal. 14 August.", "lines": ["Long shift. Walked home alone, late, the dense way along the canal, because I could not face the train.", "Rei's light was on under his door when I got in. Did not eat. Did not knock.", "I need to sleep before I"]}}, {"p": "The next page was dated the same night, an hour later. The handwriting had changed."}, {"p": "It had not changed wildly. That was the part that got into him and stayed. It had only become a little more even. A little more upright. It was as if someone had studied Gin's writing for an afternoon and then, being a careful student, written it back to Rei slightly improved."}, {"p": "Rei sat with that for a long time while the lantern light moved on the wall. He was looking at the exact hour. Two pages, one man on either side. His hands were cold. He wanted very badly to close the book and tell himself it was only grief, making patterns out of nothing."}, {"p": "But he had read enough of Gin's casework to know one thing. When a person wants to un-see something, that wish is exactly what the thing is counting on. So he turned the page, into the entries the new hand had written."}, {"p": "In the margins were small numbers, neat and calm, like the numbers a librarian writes on the spine of a book. They told him the order to read the entries in. If he followed them, the journal stopped being a diary and became an argument, beautifully arranged."}, {"p": "First came the dead of 2011, who had never been given their rites. Next came the veil over Kita, which had been weeping for them ever since. After that came one empty seat at the table of the Thirteen. It had been empty for a long time. Long enough, the entries said, that it could become the cure for all that weeping. All it needed was someone with the right grade and the right wound, who would agree to feel the duty."}, {"p": "None of it was a lie. That was what kept making him put the book down. Each entry was a true thing. It was only the order that was wrong. Each true thing was laid on the last one a little crooked, so the whole pile leaned one way. Somewhere around the ninth page, Rei caught himself nodding. The nod was the price."}, {"p": "He had thought he would be fighting something. Instead he was only listening, and listening leaves marks. Slowly he learned to tell the two writers apart. He did it by the feeling under each line, by what each line wanted from him."}, {"p": "Gin's own entries, the ones in the old leftward hand, grew fewer with every page. They wanted him to stop. They were clumsy. They repeated themselves. They said <em>go home</em> in four different broken ways. They read like a man trying to shout through a wall while someone held his hand."}, {"p": "The other entries were calm, and they wanted him closer. They wanted him to understand. They reasoned with him the way a person reasons with a frightened animal they mean to keep. The worst of it was how good the reasoning was. On a clearer night he might almost have signed his name under it."}, {"p": "An hour in, he noticed that he had started to think in the same rhythm as the new hand. When he reached for an objection of his own, it came to him already softened. It came already in the new hand's order, as if it had been given its own small number in the margin. His body felt it as a kind of seasickness. The floor of his own mind was tilting a few degrees, toward a shape that was not his."}, {"p": "Gin had a word for that. Gin had a whole chapter for it. Twice he had left that chapter open on the kitchen table, and both times Rei had closed it without reading it. Now he reached up, pulled the big Archive down off the desk, and found it. On the first page, in Gin's margin, was a short note."}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Chapter Five. Gin's margin note", "lines": ["Three readings. Never confuse them.", "Health is the body, and only the body takes it. A blow, a fall, a ridden host's hands.", "Stability is the anchor that holds you closed against the veil. It is what power and dread spend, and what the dead lean on. As it falls the membrane opens. At zero you are wide open.", "Madness is the slow corruption pooling. It climbs when the veil gets in, above all through broken Stability, and it does not wash out.", "A practitioner ends one of two ways and they are not the same death. The body fails. Or the self does. The anchor breaks, Madness fills, and something else moves into the empty room.", "Rank and Stability are two separate axes. Rank is what you can do. Stability is whether doing it ends you."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the note twice, and then he put it into his own words, so he would keep it. A person had three things that could break. Health was the body. Stability was the lock on the door between him and the veil. Every use of power cost some of it, and so did fear, and so did the dead leaning on him. Madness was what came in through that door once it stood open, and Madness did not leave again. Rank was how strong a person was. Stability was whether that strength would end him."}, {"p": "So that was the tilt. It was not Madness. Not yet. It was his Stability, spending itself a little with every page he turned."}, {"p": "His hand was already closing the journal. He watched it do that, the way a person watches their own foot step back from the edge of a train platform. It would be so easy to stop. He would sleep. He would wake up knowing less and feeling better. And his brother would still be a smooth voice on the phone."}, {"p": "He thought of Gin at this same desk, writing a chapter he knew Rei would close twice without reading. Writing it anyway."}, {"p": "Rei opened the journal again at the page where he had stopped."}, {"p": "In the end it was not something horrible that gave the thing away. It was something kind."}, {"p": "Near the back, in the even upright hand, there was an entry about Rei. It was warm. It remembered the summer Rei broke his wrist, and how Gin had carried his school bag for a month. It used the small pet name that only Gin ever used. Rei was reading it with a tight throat, almost grateful, when the seasickness saved him."}, {"p": "He had read this exact warmth before. It was in Gin's casework, forty pages earlier, in the old leftward hand. It was the section where Gin wrote about how to tell a person who has been worn by a demon from a person who is still whole. Rei turned back and found it."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's journal. Casework. The old hand.", "lines": ["What it cannot calibrate is the direction of the warmth. It orients toward whoever is currently useful, rather than toward whoever the host actually cared about.", "This is the tell. It has saved my life twice and I am not being dramatic about that.", "The tell is never the warmth itself. It is the direction the warmth points. A demon keeps the host's whole register of affection and aims all of it at whoever is useful now."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the entry about the broken wrist again, with that rule in his head. It was not love remembering him. It was love being aimed at him."}, {"p": "Now he understood what he had been swimming in for three hours, and why it had worked so well on him. It was Gin's warmth, measured exactly right. And it was pointed at Rei because Rei was the pair of hands it wanted. Gin's real entries never aimed at anything. They stumbled toward him, repeating themselves, useless, asking for nothing. They only wanted him to live."}, {"p": "That was how he would tell them apart from now on. In every text, in every short window when Gin broke through, in every reasonable favour still to come. Not by something sounding wrong, because the thing could smooth that away. By the direction of the wanting. Gin wanted him safe and gone. The thing wanted him near and helpful. The voice that wanted something from him was never the voice that loved him, however perfectly it had learned to sound as if it did."}, {"p": "Rei closed the journal. The lantern light had climbed all the way up the wall. Out on the canal, people were setting paper lanterns on the black water. Each one floated away slowly with its small flame, a way of wishing the dead a safe road back. Rei would not get to do that for Gin tonight. Maybe never. The last three hours sat in him like cold water at the base of his spine. He could feel clearly the few degrees by which his inner floor had not come back to level."}, {"p": "But he knew the seam now. He could hear where the thing ended and where his brother began. It was the first tool he had been given that the thing could not take back just by being reasonable."}, {"p": "He set the journal on the desk beside the big Archive. He looked at the two of them lying together, and something about the picture bothered him."}, {"p": "A neighbour had seen a man wearing Gin's face fit a key to this door. Whoever that was had stood in this room, at this desk, while Rei was out. He had left the lamp on. He had left the Archive open, six years of Gin's writing about exactly what he was. And he had left the journal in its drawer, with the seam inside it and the warmth that pointed the wrong way, for anyone to find."}, {"p": "A careful thing does not leave evidence lying on a desk. Rei could not think why it would. He wrote the question in pencil on the inside cover of the journal, small, so he would not forget it. <em>Why didn't it burn these?</em>"}, {"p": "Then he pulled the lamp down onto the floor, opened the Archive at the chapter Gin had told him to read twice, and began."}]}, {"title": "From Gin's Book. Veil Stability", "kind": "gin", "src": "bible", "part": "Book Two. The Handwriting", "blocks": [{"p": "This is the chapter Gin left open on the kitchen table twice. Both times Rei closed it without reading it. He read it now, on the floor of Gin's room on the night of the journal, by the light of the desk lamp pulled down beside him. The pages are set out here as he found them. The last note is not Gin's. Rei wrote it himself, in pencil in the margin, a little before dawn."}, {"p": "Gin's chapter measured Stability as a number, from one hundred percent down to zero. One hundred meant the door between a person and the other side was fully shut. Zero meant the door was gone, and the person with it. The first page explained what happened at each step on the way down."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Veil Stability. The Complete System. Mandatory Reading, All Field Grades", "lines": ["Veil Stability is the practitioner's current membrane integrity, the percentage measure of how intact the boundary between the practitioner and the other side is at any given moment. It is dynamic, continuous, and requires active management.", "Stability is entirely independent of rank. A Vanguard at thirty percent stability is more vulnerable than a Ritualist at eighty. Rank determines what you can do. Stability determines whether doing it ends you.", "100 to 70 percent. Safe Operation. Full capacity. Membrane intact. Things on the other side do not notice you passively.", "69 to 40 percent. Monitor. Visible wear. Some passive perception bleed. Recovery recommended before high-demand operations.", "39 to 20 percent. Thinning. Noticed. Passive perception becomes intrusive. Things on the other side notice you before you notice them.", "19 to 10 percent. Acute Risk. The other side is present whether engaging or not. Font pull perceptible at A rank and above. Weak entities can slip in during unconscious states. No field operations.", "9 to 1 percent. Possession Window. Strong entities have access. Conscious possession possible. Membrane functionally compromised. Emergency protocols only.", "0 percent. Death. Crossing. The membrane fails completely. Standard death path selection applies.", "The zones are guidelines, not hard lines. Your real risk at any percentage is a function of your rank, the density of where you stand, and what is present and interested in you."]}}, {"img": "ch5_depletion.jpg", "cap": "A practitioner running low on Stability"}, {"p": "The next page listed what made the number fall. Rei read it with an uncomfortable feeling of recognition, because most of the list was simply a description of how people live in a big city in August."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "What Depletes Stability", "lines": ["Ability use. The visible drain. Practitioners generally track it, which makes it the least dangerous one to miss.", "Passive ability leak. Ability running without deliberate engagement. Most common in Clairsentients who read their surroundings without knowing it. The main source of chronic low stability in careful practitioners.", "Physical exhaustion. Sleep loss, hunger, illness, injury. The body and the membrane are not separate systems.", "Emotional strain. Grief, fear, rage, anxiety, depression. Acute grief can drop stability twenty to thirty percent in hours.", "Substances. Alcohol thins the membrane in proportion to intoxication. Stimulants make the practitioner feel stable while depleting faster than they can tell.", "Environmental density. Dense districts, strong suffering beings, entity territory. Presence alone drains.", "Entity proximity. Strong demons deplete the practitioners around them. This is how they make possession opportunities. They thin the target before the approach.", "Suppression maintenance. Holding a false rank costs a low, continuous effort. It does not thin the membrane, but it shrinks the margin for everything else."]}}, {"p": "Beside the line about stimulants, Gin had written a note. It was about Rei. Rei looked at the empty can of coffee on the floor beside him and did not argue with it."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin, beside the stimulant entry", "lines": ["I am noting this because it describes Rei's primary management failure and he does not know it.", "He runs on stimulants and spite. His actual stability is consistently lower than his experience of himself. He thinks he is fine. He is fine until suddenly he is not, with less warning than he expects, because the warning system has been masked."]}}, {"p": "Then came what made the number rise again. It was the plainest advice in the whole book. Eat. Sleep. Rest somewhere quiet."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "What Restores Stability", "lines": ["Sleep. Fifteen to twenty-five percent per full cycle of seven to nine hours. Six broken hours is not three quarters of the recovery. It is much less.", "Food. Five to ten percent per adequate meal. The body is the membrane's anchor in the living world.", "Grounding. Five to fifteen percent per session, depending on how well the practice fits the practitioner.", "Emotional stability. Not a source but a multiplier. In crisis, everything else recovers at a fraction of its rate.", "Low-density places. Even brief time in genuinely quiet space gives back more than the time spent.", "Passive ability suspension. Resting the reading you did not know you were doing.", "The compounding problem. A practitioner who is not sleeping, not eating, carrying grief and reading continuously is not recovering slowly. They are not recovering at all."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "The Floor. What Training Actually Does", "lines": ["Training does not raise rank. It builds the floor, the minimum a practitioner holds under the worst conditions, where depletion slows and the system starts to protect itself.", "An untrained practitioner has no effective floor. A trained one has a real floor that can still be breached.", "The floor is built from discipline in recovery, willpower under pressure, and awareness of where you actually are.", "Eat. Sleep. This is the entire foundation of field methodology and I have had to explain it to new operatives in the same words at least forty times.", "Rei's floor is lower than it should be. His ceiling is higher than he knows. The floor is the problem I can do something about. The ceiling is the problem I am afraid to address, because addressing it means having the conversation."]}}, {"p": "The next page was the one Gin had underlined the most. It showed how possession actually comes. It does not come all at once, like a door kicked in. It comes slowly, down a slope, and the person on the slope usually thinks he is only tired."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "The Possession Risk Curve. What Actually Happens at Each Level", "lines": ["Possession does not happen suddenly. It happens at the end of a curve that began much earlier.", "100 to 70 percent. No possession risk. Entities can perceive the practitioner but cannot engage without real effort.", "69 to 40 percent. Passive attention risk. Minor entities drift closer. Strong entities in the area become aware. You are becoming visible to things you are not looking at.", "39 to 20 percent. Intrusion risk from minor entities. Sounds, presences, things at the edge of sight. Sleep at this level opens windows for weak entities.", "19 to 10 percent. Acute possession risk. Strong entities that have been monitoring the practitioner now have workable access. Sleep, sedation and injury become high-risk windows. Active resistance is the last barrier left.", "9 to 1 percent. Open window. Any motivated entity with access can attempt possession, even while the practitioner is awake. Resisting depletes stability further.", "Fusion event. Possession complete. The entity is fused with the host body. The host consciousness remains, aware, present, unable to surface. The entity cannot leave without killing the host. The entity knows this. It will not leave."]}}, {"p": "Folded into the chapter was a small flyer from the Grid, the unregistered network, printed cheaply and signed with the purple Other Side Distribution stamp. It said the same things in plainer words, for people with no training at all."}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Other Side Distribution. Running Low. What It Actually Feels Like", "lines": ["19 to 10 percent. You feel exposed in a way that has no physical cause. Not threatened. Reachable. Like a door is open somewhere. Check your stability. Eat something. Sleep if you can. You are lower than you think.", "9 to 1 percent. You know. Move. Do not engage. Get to low-density space and get someone with stable integrity beside you.", "At 9 to 1 percent do not be alone. A second stable practitioner's membrane interferes with the entity. Get someone. Call someone.", "Gin, underneath. I have failed to follow this advice. Do as I say. Not as I have done."]}}, {"p": "Under the curve, where the page still had room, Rei took a pencil from Gin's desk and wrote. He did not stop to ask whether he was allowed to write in the book. He wrote small, in the lowercase letters he had always used, so it would be clear whose hand it was."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "In the margin, in pencil. Rei's hand.", "lines": ["he was not in the 1 to 9 window when it happened. i think he was about 15 to 18 percent. the acute zone. high enough that someone managing carefully might have been fine.", "he was not managing carefully because he did not know how low he was. he thought he was tired. he was emptying.", "the weeks before. the district was busy and short of people. he was eating badly. he was not sleeping. he was reading all day at work and all night at home, and he was managing me on top of it, and he never once called that a cost.", "the thing across the street had been near for weeks, maybe longer. it watched. it waited. it did not rush when he dropped. it waited for the moment his resistance would be lowest. walking home alone, late, after a long day, through a district it knew was dense.", "the fourteenth. the dense way along the canal. because he could not face the train.", "it did not need to be stronger than him. it needed to outlast his margin.", "this is the case file i live with now."]}}], "n": 12}, {"title": "The Texts", "kind": "main", "src": "m_texts", "n": 13, "part": "Book Two. The Handwriting", "blocks": [{"p": "In the evening Rei could not stand still at home any more. The flat was too quiet. The cold by the door was too patient. So he put on his shoes and went out, without deciding where."}, {"img": "place_oji_station_dusk.jpg", "cap": "Ōji station at dusk, below the trees of Asukayama"}, {"p": "He walked down the hill to Ōji station in the last of the heat. The sun was low and orange behind the buildings, and the air still felt like the inside of a mouth. The cicadas in Asukayama park were so loud they drowned the traffic. Paper lanterns hung along the shopping street, and an old man was walking slowly ahead of him with a bundle of flowers and incense wrapped in a cloth, on his way to some family grave."}, {"p": "At the ticket gate Rei touched his Suica card to the reader. It gave its small bright beep, and the gate let him through. He took the first train that came, a Keihin-Tōhoku train with its sky-blue stripe, heading south into the city. It was crowded with people going home for the holiday, carrying suitcases and paper bags of gifts. A salaryman slept standing up, holding the strap, his tie loose. Two schoolgirls in summer uniforms shared one pair of earphones, a wire running from one ear to the other, and laughed at something on a phone. Rei got off at Nippori for no reason except that the doors opened, and he let the crowd carry him up and down the stairs until he was standing on the platform of the Yamanote line, the light-green loop line that circles central Tokyo."}, {"img": "map_trains.jpg", "cap": "The Yamanote platform on the eve of Obon"}, {"p": "The departure board flicked over, and over, and over, and Rei stayed where he was. A train came in. The station played its short bright jingle. The doors opened, took in a family dressed in matching yukata, and closed again. A yukata is a light cotton kimono that people wear in summer, to festivals and fireworks and on holiday evenings like this one. The train left without him."}, {"p": "At home, standing still had become unbearable. Here, at least, everything around him was going somewhere. He took out his phone and wrote the message he had been writing in his head since the voicemail."}, {"p": "<em>where are you. just tell me you're somewhere.</em>"}, {"p": "He deleted the full stop, because Gin hated full stops in texts. Gin said they made everything sound like a man being fired from his job. Then Rei put it back, because he wanted Gin to feel the weight of it. Then he deleted it again and sent the message bare."}, {"p": "The three grey dots came up almost at once. The dots meant someone was typing on the other end. His whole body leaned toward them. It was the way he used to lean toward the front door when Gin was late. It was the old animal habit of a younger brother who has spent his life listening for one key in one lock."}, {"p": "The reply was kind. That was the first thing that was wrong, and he would not let himself see that it was wrong for another hour."}, {"p": "<em>I'm okay, Rei. I promise I'm okay. You don't need to do all this.</em>"}, {"p": "They were the right words. It even used the right small name. The dots had come up fast. But the message itself arrived a beat late behind them. It was as if someone had watched him type, started to answer, and then paused to make sure the answer was correct."}, {"p": "Gin never paused to make sure. Gin answered wrong and fast, and then sent four more texts correcting himself. His thumbs had always been quicker than his judgement. And now Rei stood on a platform reading a version of him who had become, overnight, as patient and calm as a much older man."}, {"p": "There was a line in Gin's book about that. It was in a list of ways to spot a demon wearing a person, by someone sharing a room with it. Gin had rated this sign low, because it took time to see. <em>The presentation is always slightly more consistent than a human would naturally be. A human has bad days, contradictions. A well-maintained cover has fewer of these. The smoothness itself is a tell. Real people are messier.</em>"}, {"img": "ch1_public.jpg", "cap": "The station crowds on the eve of Obon"}, {"p": "The Obon crowds moved around him. There were paper fans, and children with plastic masks pushed up on their heads, and the smell of grilled corn drifting in from the food stalls outside the station. Families were carrying small lanterns home to set out for their dead. A woman beside him fanned herself with a folded train timetable, slowly, without looking up from her phone. He should have got on a train. He let three trains go."}, {"p": "He wrote back. <em>all this what. tell me where you are and i'll stop.</em>"}, {"p": "He watched the dots. The dots thought about it. They appeared, and rested, and vanished, and appeared again. In the whole history of Rei's phone, Gin had never once made the dots think. The thing about to answer was choosing its words. And the one thing in the world it was being careful not to give him was a place."}, {"p": "So Rei decided to spend something he could never get back. He would set a test, with a small trap inside it."}, {"img": "scene_funeral_onigiri.jpg", "cap": "The night of the funeral, outside the konbini"}, {"p": "He knew the memory exactly. It was the night of their mother's funeral. The funeral had run long, and he and Gin had slipped out to the konbini. Gin had bought him the wrong onigiri on purpose. An onigiri is a rice ball wrapped in seaweed, with something tucked in the middle. Gin chose the one Rei hated, with umeboshi inside, the sour red pickled plum. He did it because making Rei complain was the only way he knew to make him stop crying. Gin bought the wrong one three times before Rei laughed. Only the two of them in the whole world knew that it had taken three."}, {"p": "Rei wrote the memory almost right. He put salmon in it instead of plum. Then he asked. <em>do you remember what you bought me after okaasan's. you remember how many times.</em>"}, {"p": "<em>Okaasan</em> is mother. He did not need to say the word funeral. Gin would know."}, {"p": "The dots came. The dots stayed a long while. Then the answer arrived, and it was so gentle it nearly broke him. It did not take the bait. It did not miss the bait either. It folded the bait into something warmer than the truth."}, {"p": "<em>I remember I kept getting it wrong until you laughed. I'd get it wrong a hundred times if it worked, Rei. I'd do anything that worked.</em>"}, {"p": "It did not say salmon. It did not say umeboshi. It did not say three. It gave him only the shape of the memory, the love in it, the part that would be true whatever the details were. And it pointed that love straight at him, at the hands it wanted. It did it with a precision his real, clumsy, grieving brother had never once managed."}, {"p": "Rei sat down on the platform bench. A station attendant in white gloves glanced at him and decided he was only drunk."}, {"p": "This was the lesson Gin's casework had tried to teach him, and only now was his body learning it. The thing wearing Gin still had all of Gin's affection. What it had lost were the small facts, the ones that would pin it to one particular night. So it could never be caught out on facts, because it would always answer with feeling instead. And the feeling was real. It truly was Gin's. That was what made it the worst weapon ever turned on him. It was not a fake."}, {"p": "He had wanted it to fail the test. Instead it had passed by refusing to take it. Somewhere under his ribs, Rei felt his inner floor tip by the few degrees he had come to recognise. That was the price of being understood by something that was studying him."}, {"p": "He thought of another line from the same chapter. Gin had called it instrumental orientation, which only meant that a demon treats people as tools. <em>Genuine relationships modulate based on the other party's needs. Demon engagement increases pressure when the practitioner is vulnerable and decreases when the practitioner is stable.</em> Rei scrolled back up the thread. The warmest message had come in the minute after his hardest one. The thing had felt him sag onto the bench, through a screen, and leaned in."}, {"p": "He started a new message. He typed <em>Gin</em>, and a question mark, and then he stopped with his thumb over the send button."}, {"p": "There was a page in Gin's book about the hardest cases of all. These were the ones where a practitioner had known the person before a demon wore them. Gin had written it after two cases like that. <em>The cadence is right and the content is wrong and the gap between them is a specific kind of grief that does not have a protocol. What I have found useful. Do not address the demon by the original person's name. The name is for the person. The classification is for what is in front of you. They are not the same thing.</em>"}, {"p": "Rei deleted the name. On the phone call, when he said it out loud, the name had reached someone. Gin had come up for four seconds. Here, typed into a thread the smooth voice was holding, the name would only be handed to the thing to use. He would keep it. He would save his brother's name for his brother."}, {"p": "Over the next hour, between the trains he still did not take, he noticed that the thing had a refrain. A refrain is a line that comes back again and again, like the chorus of a song. And when someone keeps coming back to the same line, that line is usually the wall holding up everything else."}, {"p": "Four times, each time dressed in a different kind of warmth, it told him to stop. <em>You don't need to do all this. Let it go cold, Rei. Some cases close themselves. You'll make yourself ill.</em> Each one sounded like real concern. But when he laid them side by side, every one of them was aimed at the same single thing. His looking. Not his safety. Not his sleep. His looking. It did not mind if he was frightened. It only minded if he was searching. The difference between those two things was a whole map of what it was protecting."}, {"p": "Gin had a rule for this, written for practitioners who knew the person before a demon wore them. <em>Everything the demon tells you, everything it seems to offer, assume it is calculated. Ask why it wants you to have this information. The answer is usually more useful than the information itself.</em> Rei turned the rule around. What did it want him <em>not</em> to have?"}, {"p": "A place. A station exit, a district, a name on a doorbell. Any place would lead him back to Kita, to the veil over the ward, where the thing was doing its slow work of thinning. That was the wound in the world. It was widening the wound one reasonable favour at a time, so that the empty Thirteenth Seat would have a reason to be filled."}, {"p": "And it was in no hurry. That was the other thing the thread showed him, laid out end to end. It never pushed twice in a row. After every refusal it let a quiet minute go by, as if it had all the time there was. Gin had a paragraph about that too. <em>Demons with operational history are more patient than any practitioner they will encounter. You have a career. They have centuries. Do not mistake demon patience for demon passivity. They are waiting because the right moment is not yet here.</em> And under it was a line Rei had underlined without knowing why. <em>What you can do is change what the patient demon is waiting for. If the moment requires you to be isolated, be not isolated.</em>"}, {"p": "He watched the refrain come round again, and he understood. He had not been talking to a liar. He had been talking to a gatekeeper, and the gate it kept was a place on a map. The warmth was sincere. The steering was the job. Both could be true from the same pair of thumbs, and they were. That was the new and terrible skill he was learning. He could read the love and the plan in the same sentence, as it arrived, without letting one cancel out the other."}, {"p": "At last he crossed to the next platform and boarded a Keihin-Tōhoku train heading north toward home, because he needed to be a body going somewhere. As the doors closed he sent the last text of the night. <em>okay. i'll stop.</em>"}, {"p": "The dots came up fast. It was relief. Real relief. He could feel it through the glass of the screen. The thing that wore his brother was glad."}, {"p": "He sat with that. He had just lied to his brother for the first time in his life. And the part of Gin that was glad was exactly the part he most needed to disappoint."}, {"p": "The carriage swayed north through the dark. Outside, the city slid past in strips of light, apartment windows, a lit konbini, a level crossing with its bell ringing. Across the carriage a little girl held a lit Obon lantern in two careful hands. She was guiding home a dead person she had never met. Her grandmother sat beside her with a bag of peaches on her lap, nodding off. When the train reached Ōji, Rei did not get off. He rode three stops past his own, just to keep moving, and then crossed the platform and came back."}, {"p": "That night he lay down on top of the covers in his clothes, with the phone on the pillow beside his head. He thought about turning the sound off. He did not. Some animal part of him was still listening for one key in one lock."}, {"p": "Later, he would be very glad of that."}]}, {"title": "Where the Love Bleeds Through", "kind": "main", "src": "m_bleeds", "n": 14, "part": "Book Two. The Handwriting", "blocks": [{"img": "res_apartmentreiroom.jpg", "cap": "Rei's room in the dead hour of the night"}, {"p": "At 3:11 the phone lit the ceiling white. The buzz against the pillow went through his skull like a dentist's drill."}, {"p": "It was the dead hour, the hour when even Tokyo turns its lights down. The trains had stopped. The last taxis had gone home. Outside, the only sound was an air conditioner unit humming on the next balcony. Rei had fallen asleep in his clothes. He came up out of a dream about water and had the phone in his hand before he knew he was awake."}, {"p": "There was no greeting. There was no small name, and no warmth aimed anywhere."}, {"p": "<em>dont come to kita dont come here its not him answering rei i can feel it making the wall thin go to itsuki burn the</em>"}, {"p": "It stopped there. There was no full stop. The sentence was cut off the way the journal entry for the fourteenth of August was cut off, in the middle, like a hand pulled off a page. And the typing mistakes were wrong in the right way. They were Gin's mistakes. Gin had never learned to type cleanly with his thumbs, and his letters always stumbled to the left. He had not waited for the phone to correct him. The missing capital letters were pure panic."}, {"p": "Rei had spent the whole night learning that the smooth voice was the enemy. And here was the broken voice. His heart went through the floor toward it, like a stone dropped down a well."}, {"p": "He sat up in the dark with the phone too bright in his hand and read the message nine times."}, {"p": "Rei lived in Kita. But he knew Gin did not mean the flat. He meant the deep part of the ward, the canal and the thin spot, the place where the wall was weakest. Everything in Rei that was a brother wanted to be out of the door and down there now. And that was the one thing the message begged him not to do."}, {"p": "Then a colder thought came. The thing wearing Gin wanted him away from Kita. So a voice telling him to stay away might be the thing too. Except that the broken voice had said something the thing would never say. It had named the thinning of the wall. It had confessed the plan. It had pointed him at Itsuki, and at burning something. A gatekeeper does not hand out the address of its own gate."}, {"p": "Unless. Unless this was the most patient trap of all. A trap that let the trapped man come up for exactly one message. Because a brother who is forbidden to come will run to the forbidden place faster than any brother who is only invited."}, {"p": "He could feel the exact shape of the hope and the exact shape of the hook. They sat in the same place. He could not pull them apart just by wanting to."}, {"break": "The second signal"}, {"p": "He reached for the Archive on the floor beside the mattress, where he had left it at dawn. He did not need to search. He knew the page now. It was in the chapter on demons, at the end of the list of signs, in a box Gin had marked urgent."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's book. Chapter Nine. Urgent comment.", "lines": ["The secondary signal in partial possession. I want to state this as clearly as the documentation allows. The host is still there.", "The four seconds where the host surfaces are the most important four seconds of the encounter. They confirm the host is present. They may carry information. They are almost certainly the only communication the host can manage.", "Do not miss them by only reading the dominant signal. Look for the secondary."]}}, {"p": "A few seconds of thumbs. That was all a man could manage from inside, when the thing driving him looked away. And Gin had used his few seconds to type a warning, a place, a friend's name and half of an instruction."}, {"p": "Rei turned back a few pages, to a part he had skimmed the first time. He had skimmed it because he had not wanted it to be about anyone he knew."}, {"img": "ch5_possession.jpg", "cap": "Partial possession, the host still in the chair"}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Gin's book. Chapter Six. Possession. The Two States.", "lines": ["Partial possession. The demon occupies and drives the host body while the host soul remains inside. The host is conscious, aware, experiencing everything done through their body, but cannot surface except under specific conditions and cannot control their own actions. The demon uses the host's memories and relationships as cover. The host is trapped, not gone.", "This state is unstable. It progresses over time toward full possession as the demon's hold deepens and the host's resistance wears down.", "Full possession, or soul ejection. The demon completes the fusion and forces the host soul out entirely, into the Threshold. The soul has witnessed the whole of the partial possession, and in that state it turns heavily toward the Demon or Suffering Being paths. The body goes on under complete demon control. The host is gone.", "After ejection the demon can still be removed from the body, but the host must then be found and guided in the Threshold before their path sets. That is a different and much harder operation.", "Exorcism during partial possession is the recovery with the best outcome. The host returns changed. They carry the memory of the whole duration. They do not return undamaged. They return."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the page slowly, and he put it into plain words for himself. There were two kinds of possession. In the first, the demon drove the body like a car, but the owner was still inside, in the passenger seat, awake and seeing everything. In the second, the demon threw the owner out of the car altogether, into the Threshold, the crowded layer just behind the veil where the newly dead wander. From there the owner might never find the way back."}, {"p": "<em>They return.</em> Rei read those two words until they stopped being words. Gin was in the first state. He had to be. A man who had been thrown out could not have reached for a phone. But Gin's own page said the first state did not stay the first state. It got deeper. Every message the smooth voice sent was a little more of the hold settling into place. And every time Gin managed to come up, it cost him something he might not get back."}, {"p": "The seasickness was back, his own floor tilting. For once he was grateful for it, because it meant he was still reading and had not yet decided. He checked himself the way the Running Low page told him to. Did he feel reachable, as if a door were open somewhere? No. Only tired, and frightened, and hungry. So he made himself get up. He ate a cold rice ball from the fridge, standing at the counter in the dark, and drank a glass of water. Gin would have said that before anything else. <em>Eat. Sleep.</em> He could not sleep. He could eat."}, {"p": "Out of the window, the last Obon lanterns along the canal had burned down and gone out. The dead, he supposed, had been guided home. The living were left holding their phones. He did not text back yet. He made himself be a person who could wait sixty seconds. He watched the screen, waiting for the smooth voice to come and clean up after the broken one."}, {"p": "At 3:16 the smooth voice arrived. Its speed told him more than any sentence could."}, {"p": "<em>I'm sorry... I don't even know what I wrote, I think I fell asleep texting, I've been so tired. Ignore that, please. I'm fine. Don't go anywhere on account of a half-asleep message.</em>"}, {"p": "It was reasonable. In its way it was even true. A tired man, confused, not himself. But it was fast. It was fast in a way the earlier replies had never been fast. It was fast like something that had been caught and was now hurrying. And the warmth in it was pointed harder than usual. There was more <em>please</em> in it. More aim."}, {"p": "The broken text had said <em>burn</em> and <em>Itsuki</em> and <em>the wall</em>. The smooth text wanted him to put all three down to a sleepy man typing, and to go back to bed. Tonight he had learned whose interest each of those served."}, {"p": "He held the two messages side by side, five minutes apart, and he understood what he had just seen. He had watched the seam happen live. It was not in a journal found afterwards. It was not in handwriting studied in the cold light. It had happened in front of him, in real time. The old voice had come up for a few broken seconds, and the new one had rushed in to plaster over the hole."}, {"p": "The thing had not denied the plan. It could not deny it fast enough, because Gin had already said it out loud. So it did the next thing, the thing it was best at. It turned the confession into a symptom. It offered Rei the comfort of not believing his own brother. That was the whole trick, he saw now. It did not fight his love for Gin. It recruited it. It asked his love to please be reasonable, and to please not make a tired man feel watched."}, {"p": "He put the phone face-down on the floor, where he could not see the next message arrive. He breathed. He let the certainty build itself slowly, so that it would be certainty and not just hope."}, {"p": "Gin was in there. He had come up. It had cost him something to come up, and it had cost the thing something to push him down again. The pushing had been fast and frightened, and frightened was information. Whatever Rei did at dawn, he would do it knowing his brother was alive enough to break a window for a few seconds. And he would do it knowing that the thing holding him was, for the first time all night, working faster than it would like."}, {"p": "The lanterns were out. It was the hour when the city forgets itself. Rei had somewhere to be when it remembered."}]}, {"title": "The Auditor's Anomaly", "kind": "side", "src": "s_auditor", "n": 15, "part": "Book Two. The Handwriting", "blocks": [{"p": "The message from Okada Shizuka came early, before seven. It was one line long. It asked him to come back to the small white room at the Directorate that morning, and it did not say why."}, {"p": "Rei walked. It was already hot. The heat in Tokyo in August does not wait for noon. It comes up out of the pavement as soon as the sun clears the roofs, and the air is so wet that shirts stick to backs within a few minutes. He went down the long slope toward Ōji with the cicadas screaming in every tree. A woman was sweeping the step of a small house and had set out a dish of cucumber and aubergine on a little shelf by the door, with chopsticks pushed into the vegetables like legs. Rei knew what they were. They were the spirit horse and the spirit cow that families make at Obon. The cucumber is a fast horse, to bring the dead home quickly. The aubergine is a slow cow, to carry them back again with no hurry at the end of the week."}, {"p": "At the bottom of the hill the station was busy with people leaving the city for the holiday. Asukayama park rose green beside the tracks, loud with insects. Rei crossed in front of the east exit, past a vending machine glowing with cold green tea and canned coffee, and in one minute he was at the grey block that looked like a tax office. He had walked past it his whole life without looking up. Now he went in through its doors again."}, {"img": "char_okada-shizuka.jpg", "cap": "Okada Shizuka, CVD assessor"}, {"p": "Okada Shizuka was waiting for him in the same small white room as before. She was an assessor, one of the people who measured mediums for the Division, and her name was on the panel list by the door of the intake room. This time there was no laminated card on the table. There was only a single printout on thin, shiny thermal paper, curling at the edges the way thermal paper curls when a warm hand has held it too long. She did not slide it across to him. She laid her palm flat on top of it, as if the words might escape through the ceiling if she let go."}, {"p": "\"The cycle ran Thursday,\" she said."}, {"p": "Rei felt the day land like a dropped stone. He remembered what she had made him promise, without quite letting him promise it. <em>Do not let them assess him on a Thursday.</em> He understood now that the warning had never been only about Gin."}, {"p": "\"You weren't on the schedule,\" Okada said. \"Sena flagged you anyway. The instrument reaches for whoever's nearest when a reading goes deep, and you were in the building.\""}, {"p": "She turned the sheet over. The strange thing on it was not a number. It was a shape."}, {"p": "Before he could understand the shape, Rei had to understand what the machine was drawing. Every spirit and every demon feeds on one register, one of the thirteen great feelings, the one that was strongest in it at the moment it died. And a living medium's gift leans toward one or two registers too, the way a singing voice has a natural key."}, {"p": "The assessment machine drew a medium's registers as a pair of rings, one inside the other. An ordinary citizen came out as clean round circles, one inside the next. A person with a fever of the veil came out as a fat smeared oval. Rei's rings were neither. His were two rings that should have been quiet, and instead both of them were leaning toward a third mark that did not belong to him. It was a faint bright knot off to the north of the page, like a moon that had wandered into the wrong sky."}, {"p": "\"The textbook word for this is <em>domain incipient</em>,\" she said. She said it very evenly, the way a doctor gives a diagnosis she has decided not to be frightened of yet. \"It means the machine thinks something is being built inside you. Or that something has already keyed in, and is reading you the way you read everyone else.\""}, {"p": "The sounds of Obon came faintly through the building's tired air conditioning. Out by the intake desk, paper lanterns sagged on their strings, hung for the dead who were meant to come home gently this week. Rei thought of the third mark, and of how it leaned north. Toward Kita. Toward a wall someone was patiently thinning."}, {"p": "Okada watched his hands, the way she had the first day."}, {"p": "\"I have eleven hours before the cycle closes and the reading becomes a record,\" she said. \"Once it's a record it goes up. To the Auditors. To the families above Adachi. To anyone who can act on a domain forming inside a registered medium. And what they do to a domain that's forming is close it. Early. While it's small enough to close.\""}, {"p": "She lifted her palm off the page at last."}, {"p": "\"So. Before it's a record, it's still just a thing two people in a white room are looking at. What do you want it to be?\""}]}, {"title": "The Audit Floor", "kind": "side", "src": "s_auditor#2", "n": 16, "part": "Book Two. The Handwriting", "blocks": [{"img": "cvdroom_internal_affairs_audit.jpg", "cap": "The Internal Affairs and Audit floor"}, {"p": "The way down to the machine ran through Internal Affairs and Audit, the department that watches the Division's own people. Okada touched her badge to a door with no sign on it, and the noise of the building dropped away behind them."}, {"p": "It looked like the dullest room in the Directorate. Cabinets. Computer terminals. Very little talking. A plant on a windowsill that someone remembered to water. At the far desk a man in his thirties sat reading a file, with his tea going cold beside him. He did not look up when they passed. But Rei had the uncomfortable feeling that he had been seen anyway, completely, by someone who was watching the whole room instead of the people in it."}, {"img": "char_moriya-tatsuki.jpg", "cap": "Moriya Tatsuki, sixteen years in Internal Affairs"}, {"p": "\"Moriya,\" Okada said quietly, when they were past. \"Sixteen years in Internal Affairs. He runs this floor. He's one of the good ones.\" She paused. \"Gin used to say that's exactly why the floor frightened him. A good man with the power to file is still a man with the power to file.\""}, {"p": "Next to Moriya's office was a second desk, bare. No photographs. No papers. Only a fountain pen, old and expensive, laid square to the edge of the blotter with great care."}, {"p": "\"That's Sena's,\" Okada said. \"His father's pen. He doesn't use it anymore. Someone keeps it straight for him.\" She did not stop walking. \"He has the Tower seat on this floor. The Auditor's chair that belongs, by an arrangement nobody wrote down, to a family.\""}, {"p": "\"Which family?\""}, {"p": "\"Fujiwara-Kage.\" Okada said it the way people in the building said all seven of those names, Tsukishiro, Fujiwara-Kage, Orimoto, Higashiyama, Kuroda, Akiyama, Nishimura. Politely, and a little lower. \"They're an investigation house. Edo. Their founders worked alongside the shogunate's intelligence men, and they've been reading people for the state ever since. Clairsentience runs in them like a family nose. They turn out Arbiters the way other families turn out lawyers.\""}, {"p": "Rei knew the name from Itsuki's photographs of Gin's Tower chapter. He had not expected to walk past one of their desks."}, {"p": "They waited for the inner lift. Okada kept her voice down."}, {"p": "\"Of all the Tower families, they're the one most deeply inside this building. Two of them work here in senior posts. One in the Research Division. One in the Auditor network. Whether that's cooperation or infiltration depends on whose interest is being served that week. Probably both.\" Her mouth tightened. \"The Grid says the family has been quietly documenting the Directorate's classified work for forty years. I've never been able to prove it. I've never tried very hard.\""}, {"p": "\"And the Directorate lets them.\""}, {"p": "\"The Directorate needs them. Gin will have written the whole arrangement down somewhere. The short version. They give it rank and training and cover, and it looks the other way, and a Tower child who joins us rises faster than their number earns.\" The lift came. \"When I say the families above Adachi, that's who I mean.\""}, {"p": "Later that night, at home, Rei would find the page Gin had written about this family, in the chapter on the Towers. It was short. It said more than it seemed to."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "From Gin's book. Chapter Sixteen. On the Fujiwara-Kage.", "lines": ["The Auditor network member from the Fujiwara-Kage, I know who it is and I am not naming them here.", "My bi-monthly assessments are conducted by a different assessor than the one assigned to my district on the rotation schedule. I noticed this approximately two years ago. I have not raised it.", "It may mean nothing. It may mean something that benefits me. It may mean something that does not. I continue to pass the assessments and I continue to note the irregularity."]}}, {"p": "The machine lived two floors further down, behind a door marked with nothing but a cycle number. The room was colder than the rest of the Directorate, on purpose. It was the cold of a place built to keep something still."}, {"p": "Rei had expected a machine with lights and dials and boxes that go ping. There was a machine. It was a low ring of grey instruments set into the floor, like a drain. But the thing the machine read through was a person. A young man sat at the centre of the ring with his hands open on his knees. His eyes moved, but they were not looking at anything."}, {"p": "Okada said his name the way a person names a place they are sorry about."}, {"p": "\"Sena. Fujiwara-Kage Sena. He was a reader. A good one. Arbiter grade, two clean registers, the kind the Directorate doesn't get to keep as a witness. So they didn't keep him as one.\""}, {"p": "Rei understood before Okada finished, because he saw it the way he saw everything. Where a person should be, there was only a held breath. They had hollowed Sena out, gently. They had emptied his two registers until nothing of his own was left to colour a reading. So whoever the machine pointed him at came back true. Each reading was measured against a surface with no opinion of its own. He was the most honest instrument in Tokyo, because there was nothing left in him that could lie."}, {"p": "\"He consented,\" Okada said. The sentence cost her something to carry across the cold room. \"Eight years ago. Terms in his own hand. He thought it was service. His family thought it was a seat. The form doesn't have a box for what it actually is.\""}, {"p": "Sena's eyes found him then, slowly. For one moment something moved behind them that was almost recognition. It was the way a still pond moves when a fish turns, far below the surface."}, {"p": "\"Gin's off-rotation assessor,\" Rei said. \"For two years. It was him.\""}, {"p": "Okada did not answer, and that was an answer. She took a laminated sheet from a pocket by the door and handed it to him. It was the checklist every registered medium went through every two months, the one they were told was routine."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "Bi-Monthly Assessment Protocol. What Is Checked", "lines": ["Stability profile. Current floor and ceiling against the registered baseline. Flagged if the floor has dropped more than fifteen percent from baseline, or the gap between floor and ceiling has narrowed. The most common flag.", "Ability profile consistency. Verifies the registered type is still accurate. The instruments read against the standard single-path taxonomy. Capability outside it reads as instrument noise.", "Register bleed. Emotional material picked up from heavy entity work. A grief-heavy caseload leaves grief in the signature.", "Possession and cohabitation check. Assessors read for the double-layer signature of possession or cohabitation. Not disclosed to practitioners.", "Behavioral review. Case history, supervisor report, flagged incidents. The most subjective part."]}}, {"p": "Under the fourth line, someone had written in pencil, in a hand Rei knew. <em>The check catches entities that do not know what to hide. It does not catch entities that do.</em>"}, {"p": "\"He passed every one,\" Okada said. \"Clean. Before the fourteenth and after it. A thing that has studied a man for long enough knows what the assessment is measuring. It sits in the chair and gives the instrument what the instrument expects. A clean file doesn't mean he's uncompromised. It means whatever's in him is either absent or good enough at this. The file can't tell you which.\""}, {"p": "When the machine woke, it did not ping. It tightened. The cold in the room drew inward toward the ring. Rei felt his own two registers come half awake against his will. He had kept them pressed flat his whole life. Now they reached toward the empty young man at the centre of the room, the way water runs to the lowest place."}, {"p": "This was the reading that had flagged him, run again live. On the machine's grey panel the rings opened up once more. His two, leaning. And the third bright knot, to the north."}, {"p": "Now that he was inside the reading, he could feel what the paper could only draw. The knot was not inside him. It was reaching for him, from a long way off, from a direction he knew. It was patient and fond. And it had a familiar feeling that he would have recognised anywhere, because it was his brother's. It wore that feeling like a borrowed coat. It was not Gin."}, {"p": "Then Okada showed him the thing she had always known she would have to show him. He could not decide what the reading should become until he knew it could become anything."}, {"p": "The machine kept a calibration log. It was a record of every small adjustment ever made to keep the instrument accurate. She scrolled it back, past his Thursday, past months, to a column of small corrections that were not really corrections at all."}, {"p": "\"The families above Adachi don't delete readings,\" she said. \"Deleting leaves a hole, and holes get noticed. They recalibrate. They tell the instrument the honest answer is the error and feed it a kinder one, until the kinder one is what the machine believes.\""}, {"p": "Rei looked at the column and understood. The most honest instrument in Tokyo had been taught to lie so many times that lying had become a second language for it. This was how a possession became a footnote in a file. This was how a brother became a case closed too fast."}, {"p": "\"You could do that to mine,\" he said. It was not a question."}, {"p": "\"I could. Tonight.\" Okada did not look away from the screen. \"By morning the machine would believe you're a fever-case with a bad week. No Auditor would ever come down those stairs looking for you.\" She let that sit. \"And the one reading anyone has ever taken that shows what's wearing your brother would be gone. I'd have done it with my own hands. You'd have stood here and watched me learn how.\""}, {"p": "\"Then leave the reading. Give it a different cause.\" He heard himself reaching for it. \"Festival bleed. Contamination from a witness. Something boring.\""}, {"p": "\"Then it's bent.\" She said it gently, the way she might have told him a bone was broken. \"A reading you bend once never lies straight again. You'd never trust it after. Not even the part of it that points at Gin.\""}, {"p": "\"And if nobody touches it at all?\""}, {"p": "Okada was quiet for a moment. The machine ticked in the cold."}, {"p": "\"Then it goes up. Up means the Auditors. What the Auditors do to a forming domain is close it early. Close you. Close whatever's reaching through you.\" Her voice stayed level, but her hand had found the edge of the panel and was holding it. \"And they will not be gentle about which of you they decide is the patient.\""}, {"p": "Behind her, in the ring, Sena's open hands had not moved. But his head had turned a few degrees, toward the north. Toward the same wrong moon."}, {"p": "Rei looked at the column of kind lies for a long moment. He thought of Gin passing every assessment, clean, while something sat inside him and smiled at the instrument."}, {"p": "\"Let it stand,\" he said. \"It's the only true thing anyone in this building has written about him. I'm not going to be the next person who corrects it.\""}, {"p": "Okada closed her eyes for a second. Then she nodded, and she did not touch the log."}, {"p": "They came out of the reading room into a corridor where the Obon lanterns had begun to come down. The festival was closing. The dead were being seen back out. And the building was quietly going back to pretending it had never crossed over to them at all. Whatever the machine now believed about him would walk out in his file ahead of him, into rooms he would never see."}, {"p": "Okada walked him to the main lift and did not press the button. On the panel beside the doors, one floor had no number. It had only a key slot. Rei looked at it."}, {"img": "cvdroom_containment_reintegration.jpg", "cap": "Containment and Reintegration, where a closed domain goes"}, {"p": "\"Containment and Reintegration,\" she said, following his eyes. \"That's where a closed domain goes. The cells. They call it rehabilitation, and they say it gently. The staff are kind. They bring the contained tea and learn their names and mean the word care. And they still close the door. Entry is by order, not request.\" She was quiet for a second. \"Gin walked that corridor once. He told me he counted the doors he didn't open, and he still hadn't decided if that was cowardice or mercy.\""}, {"p": "\"Is there anywhere in this building that sees everything?\" Rei asked. \"Besides him.\""}, {"p": "\"One room. I've never been in it. I've only had it described.\" Okada kept her eyes on the shut doors. \"The Mirror Room. Surveillance. A long dark room with mirrors hung in rows to the ceiling, each one showing a slice of the city from a height no person stands at. The operators don't talk. They drink tea and watch through birds' eyes and write down what the city is feeling. They're on no roster I've ever seen. It never closes. Two people I trust have stood in it, and neither wanted to talk about it for long.\" She breathed out. \"Between that room and this one, the building watches the whole city. It just doesn't always know what it's looking at.\""}, {"p": "Then she said what she had brought him to the lift to say."}, {"p": "\"Gin knew this would happen. Not the details. The shape. A Thursday. An instrument. You in the building. A reading that points the wrong machine at the right truth. He spent his last free act trying to keep you off this page. And you've spent the morning deciding to be on it your own way.\""}, {"p": "The lift came. Neither of them stepped in."}, {"p": "The morning had handed him one more thing, and it was heavier than the reading. Sena had turned north. Twice now, Rei had watched the emptiest person in Tokyo lean toward Kita, the same way his own registers leaned. A hollowed-out instrument has no homesickness of its own to explain that. So the thing that was reaching for Rei through the machine had been reaching through Sena too. It was testing every open surface in the building, patient and fond, wearing his brother's feeling like a coat."}, {"p": "The knot was not waiting for assessment day. It was using it. Every reading cycle the Directorate ran was a door the thing could prop open. The one institution that could close the wall in Kita had been holding a smaller door wide for years, without ever knowing it."}, {"p": "\"I can put one thing in the record before it locks,\" Okada said quietly. The lift hummed behind its shut doors. \"A flag of my own. I can mark the array compromised. Pull Sena from the cycle. Blind the Directorate's truest eye, and give your brother's reaching nowhere to land for a while. Or I leave him in. I leave the door propped, and we keep the one instrument that ever told us the truth, knowing exactly what's using it to find you.\""}, {"p": "She looked at him the way she had over the laminated card on the first day. She watched his hands more than his face."}, {"p": "\"You've already decided what the page says. This decides whether the building keeps its eyes open, or shuts them to keep us safe. I won't make this one alone.\""}, {"p": "Rei thought of Sena's head turning, a few degrees at a time, toward the same place his own registers leaned. If they pulled him out, the thing would go quiet in this building. And they would lose the only window that showed it moving."}, {"p": "\"Leave him in,\" he said. \"If it's using him to look at me, then he's the one place we can watch it looking. Keep the eye open. I'll be careful what I let it see.\""}, {"p": "Okada held his eyes a moment longer. Then she reached past him and pressed the button, and the doors that had been open all this time finally began to close."}, {"p": "The lift rose through the building in silence. Somewhere around the third floor, Rei's phone buzzed in his pocket."}, {"p": "He knew before he looked. The name at the top of the screen was his brother's."}, {"p": "<em>You looked tired today, Rei. Go home and sleep. Please.</em>"}, {"p": "He had not told anyone where he was going this morning. He had not been anywhere today where a person could see him. Nowhere except a cold room two floors under the street, with a ring in the floor and a hollowed young man at its centre, turning his head toward the north."}]}, {"title": "Kurosawa Reads the Body", "kind": "main", "src": "m_kurosawa", "n": 17, "part": "Book Two. The Handwriting", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei left the flat a little after six, as the city was remembering itself. He carried his bag in front of him, against his chest, with both arms around it."}, {"p": "The streets were grey and soft at that hour. The heat had not come up yet, but it was waiting. A delivery truck was unloading crates of milk outside the konbini, and a man in a white apron was hosing down the pavement in front of a closed noodle shop, the water steaming a little where it hit. Somewhere up the hill the Toden tram went by, its bell ringing twice at a crossing. The first cicadas were starting up in Asukayama park, one, then three, then the whole hill. An old woman in a sun hat walked her small white dog past the station, and the dog stopped to sniff at an Obon offering of rice and fruit left on a low wall. She pulled it gently away and bowed her head to the wall, quickly, as if apologising to someone."}, {"p": "Rei went in at the grey block by the east exit and showed the borrowed card on its lanyard. The gate thought about it and let him through. He did not go up toward the intake hall. He went down."}, {"p": "He went down past the floors that had names, past the floors that had only numbers, to the floor the directory listed as <em>archival storage, climate-controlled</em>. Everyone who had been there called it the cold room."}, {"img": "char_kurosawa-shou.jpg", "cap": "Kurosawa Shou, who reads what a possession leaves behind"}, {"p": "Kurosawa Shou was waiting for him. He waited the way a man waits who has spent two years deciding whether he is a coward. He was already on his feet when the lift opened, already reading Rei's face to see how much he had guessed."}, {"p": "Rei had brought the things that still held Gin. The journal, in a clear plastic evidence sleeve. The coat Gin had left over the chair in May and never come back for. And the cup Gin had drunk from on the last morning he was still Rei's brother. Rei had not washed it. He had told no one he had kept it."}, {"p": "Kurosawa looked at the cup the longest. He did not ask why Rei had kept it. He understood keeping."}, {"img": "ch6_reading.jpg", "cap": "The steel table on sub-level four"}, {"p": "The cold room was not cold for the sake of bodies. There were no bodies here. There were only what bodies left behind. Kurosawa called them residues. They were the traces of a person that cling to their things, the way smoke clings to a wool jumper. He explained this while he laid a black cloth on the steel table and weighted its corners. His voice was low and even. He kept it pitched under the hum of the machines, which were not, he said, switched to record."}, {"p": "\"A possession leaves a wake,\" he said. \"On the host. On what the host loves. On the rooms they pass through slowly. I do not read the demon. No one reads the demon. I read the wake. The shape of the water after the thing has already gone under.\""}, {"break": "The chart on the wall"}, {"p": "He needed a few minutes to settle before he would touch anything, and he did not want to be watched while he did it. Rei turned away and found himself facing the one decoration in the room. It was an old laminated wall chart, yellow at the corners, the kind the Directorate printed for trainees and then forgot to replace. Someone had stuck it up beside the cabinets where the residues of the dead were kept. It was as if someone had wanted to remind the residues where their owners had gone."}, {"p": "The chart was about death. Rei had grown up knowing, the way every child in Japan knew it, that people did not all go to the same place when they died. There were different roads, and the Division called them paths. This chart named all six."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "CVD Entity Classification Reference. Death Path Taxonomy. Standard Reference, All Grades", "lines": ["Path One. The Demon. Corruption of self. Intelligence retained. Everything else changed.", "Path Two. The Spirit. Personality intact. Still a person. Different medium.", "Path Three. The Suffering Being. Emotion that dissolved and stayed. Bound to a place. No intelligence left.", "Path Four. Reincarnation. The soul returns to a living body. Karmic, into a soulless newborn in a compatible family line. Or involuntary absorption, two souls in one body.", "Path Five. The Absorbed. The soul consumed by a demon, or rarely by a living practitioner of extreme rank. What it was changes the thing that took it.", "Path Six. Font Return. Dissolution into the source. The only true rest."]}}, {"p": "Under it was a second chart. It listed what decided which road a dying person took."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "What Determines the Path", "lines": ["Veil integrity at crossing. Peaceful deaths and maintained stability lean toward Spirit and Font Return. Possession collapse, violent death and long depletion lean toward Demon and Suffering Being.", "Emotional state at crossing. Rage, terror, despair and sustained grief lean dark. Acceptance, love, relief and calm lean light. It is not about being a good person. It is the emotional architecture of the moment.", "Rank and type. High-rank crossings make stronger entities, in either direction.", "Possession status at death. The least documented factor. The entity goes on its own path. The host soul is often marked by the contact. Demon outcomes are disproportionately common.", "External entities present. Demons waiting at thin places can draw a crossing soul toward the Demon path. A strong, kind spirit at a death can pull it back toward Spirit.", "Karmic lineage. Decides whether Reincarnation is open at all. Not every soul qualifies.", "Font proximity at death. Practitioners who have been near the Font in life return to it more often and turn demon less."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the fourth line three times. <em>Possession status at death.</em> If Gin's body died with the thing still inside it, the chart did not offer him any of the gentle roads. It offered him the dark ones, and the dice were loaded."}, {"p": "He made himself stop reading. He would read Gin's own pages on the six paths that night. For now he turned back to the table, because Kurosawa had begun."}, {"p": "Kurosawa set the cup in the centre of the cloth and held his bare hands a few centimetres above it. His breathing changed. Rei watched the man go quiet, in the same particular way Rei's own gift went quiet. He understood that he was watching someone do on purpose, with training and a steel table, what Rei did by accident, by drowning in it."}, {"p": "Rei should not have been here, and they both knew it. Eleven months ago Kurosawa had filed a reading on Gin. That reading had been redacted. Three lines of it survived, and then a thick black bar. The only words left were <em>double-layer, not consistent with the registered subject, recommend esca</em>. The word had been cut off in the middle. Those surviving words had brought Rei four floors below the daylight, to a man whose career had ended on the day he wrote the truth down. If Internal Affairs walked in now, Kurosawa would lose what was left of his pension. And Rei would lose the one person in this building who would tell him the truth without first asking what the truth was worth."}, {"p": "So Rei stood very still and breathed shallowly. He watched Kurosawa's hands over the cup his brother's mouth had touched. And he waited to be told the thing he already could not un-know."}, {"p": "When Kurosawa finally spoke, he did not sound like a man relieved to give a diagnosis. He sounded uneasy. He sounded like a man who has found something his training has no drawer for."}, {"p": "\"Every possession I have ever read runs hot,\" he said. His hands were still above the cup, and his eyes were somewhere past the wall. \"It rides the host like a fever rides a child. Fast, clumsy, greedy. It burns the body to keep itself lit, and in three weeks, four, the body cannot pay anymore and the thing moves on, or the host dies. That is what possession <em>is</em>, on this table, in this building's books. A wasting. A theft that ends in an empty room.\""}, {"p": "He lowered his hands at last and looked at Rei. Rei could see him choosing the next words, because the true ones frightened him."}, {"p": "\"This is not that. This is the opposite of that.\""}, {"p": "What he had read in the cup, he said, was a second register laid down under the first. It had been laid as carefully as a restorer lays a second coat of varnish on an old painting. It was not eating Gin. It was <em>preserving</em> him. It was regulating him. It kept his sleep clean and his hands steady and his paperwork up to date. A fever is hot. This was cold. A fever is starving. This was patient. And it had been living in him not for the few weeks a host can survive, but for the better part of a year, because it had no interest in burning the body down."}, {"p": "\"That is the double layer,\" Kurosawa said. \"Two presences in one person. Your brother underneath. The tenant on top. Any Clairsentient can learn to see it, if they climb far enough. At the fifth step of the craft the veil stops being feeling and becomes seeing. You stop sensing that something is there and you start seeing what it is. The register shows by sight. A possession shows as two outlines, one inside the other.\" He glanced at Rei, measuring him. \"You are closer to that step than you pretend to be.\""}, {"p": "\"I have read a great many possessed,\" he went on, very quietly, into the hum. \"I have never read one being taken care of. Whatever is wearing your brother is keeping him in good repair. It does not want him dead. It does not even want him diminished. It wants him <em>working.</em>\""}, {"p": "Rei felt his inner floor tip by the few degrees he had learned to recognise. For a year his brother had seemed tired, but coping, holding together. Rei had told himself that every week. Now every word of it was the exact description of a thing doing its job well."}, {"p": "\"He's still in there,\" Rei said. It was not a question, but he needed to hear someone else say it."}, {"p": "\"He is in there.\" Kurosawa did not soften it. \"Alive inside. Aware. Watching. He cannot surface except in flashes. It did not overpower him, you understand. A thing like this does not need to be stronger than the man. It waited until he was emptier than his own floor. It outlasted his margin.\""}, {"p": "Rei thought of the journal entries after the fourteenth of August. The handwriting going suddenly neat and steady. The man he had grown up beside becoming, on paper, a calmer and better-run version of himself than grief should allow. He thought of the smooth voice at 3:16 that came too fast and aimed its warmth too well. He had been afraid he would learn that his brother was being destroyed. Instead he was learning that Gin was being <em>maintained</em>. Somehow that was worse. A thing that destroys can be interrupted. A thing that maintains has all the time in the world."}, {"p": "\"Why,\" Rei said, and his voice did not sound like his own. \"Why keep him working. Working at what.\""}, {"p": "Kurosawa did not answer that. He had filed the symptom once, and he had watched someone draw a black bar through it. He had learned the difference between what he could read and what he was allowed to know."}]}, {"title": "What the Watching Was", "kind": "main", "src": "m_kurosawa#2", "n": 18, "part": "Book Two. The Handwriting", "blocks": [{"p": "But Rei had another question, and that one was only about a symptom, so he asked it. He told Kurosawa about the journal. The coffee made before Gin remembered making it. The flat that felt smaller. The thing in the building across the street that had watched the window since a Tuesday. And the page where Gin had decided it was the Architect, the veil's own slow correction, coming for a man who had hidden his rank for years."}, {"p": "Kurosawa listened with his eyes closed. Then he put two fingers on the coat, not the cup, and held them there a long time."}, {"p": "\"The Architect does not point,\" he said. \"If it is anything, it is weather. It falls on a whole district. It does not stand across one street and look at one window.\" He opened his eyes. \"There is thinning all through this coat. Old thinning, months of it. It does not radiate out from him the way a strong man's own thinning does. It points <em>at</em> him. Toward rather than from. That is a demon's mark, and your brother knew it as well as I do. He wrote that line in his own book. He simply never thought to turn it on himself.\""}, {"p": "\"He felt watched for three years,\" Rei said. \"He thought it was the veil.\""}, {"p": "\"It was the thing that is now wearing him. Preparing. Thinning the membrane toward him a little at a time, patient, and wearing the exact quality of structural attention he had been taught to expect. He had the framework for one and not the other.\" Kurosawa took his hand back. \"Do not blame him for that. The best practitioners in this building would have made the same mistake. They would have made it faster.\""}, {"p": "He wrapped the cup again himself, slowly, the way a mourner wraps something that is going back into a grave. Then he stopped. He unwrapped one corner and held his palm over it again, frowning, like a man who has heard a second voice in a room he thought was empty."}, {"p": "\"There is something else on this,\" he said. \"Not the tenant. Not your brother. Something loud.\""}, {"p": "He looked up at Rei, and his face changed. It was not suspicion. It was something closer to pity."}, {"p": "\"It's you,\" he said. \"Your signature. All over his cup, his coat, everything from that flat. Not a trace. A flood. You read the whole world all the time and you don't know you're doing it, so you never close the tap. In that apartment you would have been noise in every direction, day and night.\""}, {"p": "Rei could not speak. Kurosawa went on carefully, as if he were setting each word on the table where Rei could see it."}, {"p": "\"Your brother was a fine reader. He should have felt this thing coming months before it reached him. He couldn't. Its signal was thin and patient and pointed, and it was buried under yours. That is why he looked at the window and wondered first if it was only you.\""}, {"p": "Rei remembered a page in Gin's book. Gin had rewritten it six times. It was an entry in a catalogue of demons, with no name and no class. Gin had headed it <em>Unknown. Possession Specialist.</em> Rei had read it once in the Archive and had not understood why it frightened him. He understood now."}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "Gin's book. CVD Field Catalogue. Unknown. Possession Specialist. Class undetermined, active in district.", "lines": ["Target selection. High-rank suppressed practitioners with established Grid and CVD access.", "Activity signature. Directional membrane thinning in residential areas corresponding to known high-rank practitioner locations.", "Notable behavior. Exploits practitioner signal interference as cover for approach. Specifically targets practitioners living with or near other high-output unmanaged practitioners. The noise from the unmanaged practitioner masks the demon's preparatory work.", "This is not opportunistic exploitation of coincidence. It is deliberate target selection. The demon is choosing targets whose living situation provides the interference it needs.", "Gin, below. If you have found the entries I left for you in the margins, then you know what this entry is actually documenting."]}}, {"p": "\"I did this,\" Rei said. His voice came out very small. \"It chose him because of me.\""}, {"p": "\"No.\" Kurosawa said it at once, and with more force than anything else he had said in that room. \"Listen to me. Your aperture was flooding that apartment. That is true. The demon used it. That is also true. It picked its way in under your noise because it is clever and it had years to plan. But you did not cause what happened to your brother. It used you as cover. Those are not the same sentence. Do not let anyone, least of all the thing in him, tell you they are.\""}, {"p": "Rei held on to the edge of the steel table until the room stopped moving."}, {"p": "Then Kurosawa told him the last clinical fact. It was the only one that sounded like a confession. A reading leaves a wake of its own, he said. It is faint, and it lasts a few days. A demon this strong would not even notice it. But the residue he had just lifted off the cup had now passed, however slightly, through him. And a man who reads a thing is a man the thing could, in principle, read back."}, {"p": "\"I am not important enough to be noticed,\" he said. His voice was flat, the voice of a man who had spent two years making himself unimportant on purpose. \"That was the strategy. File it once, get erased, become a clerk in a cold room. The mistake people make is thinking the erasing was done to me. I did most of it myself. It was the only safe place to stand.\""}, {"p": "Looking at him, Rei understood that Kurosawa had just told him why nobody had warned his family. The reason was not cruelty. It was the ordinary arithmetic of staying alive inside a building that drew a black bar through the truth and then promoted the people who held the pen."}, {"p": "Before the lift came, Kurosawa gave him the one useful thread that had survived the cover-up. It was not about what Gin was. Kurosawa had already read that and named it. It was about where his report went on the day his request to escalate the case was refused. A clerk named Tanaka, two seats up the chain and one floor above, had his name on the routing slip. Tanaka knew the one hard fact the redaction had been made to hide. Which floor the reading had been sent to, and the initial of the person who signed for it."}, {"p": "\"I read the symptom,\" Kurosawa said. He was already turning back toward the steel table, already putting his innocence back on like a coat. \"Tanaka can tell you where the symptom was filed. After that you are on your own, and so am I, and we did not have this conversation.\""}, {"p": "The machines hummed. The cold room kept its residues. Rei put the cup back in his bag and held the bag against his chest, the way he had carried it since the morning. He felt how light a thing can be, and still be the heaviest thing a person owns."}, {"p": "In the lift going up, he held on to what Kurosawa had given him, and to what the cup had taken from him. A word for the wrongness, at last. A shape for the whole year. It was not a haunting. It was not a wasting. It was a tenancy. Patient. Tidy. Well-meaning, in the way that is the most frightening of all. A thing that kept his brother in good repair, because a tool in good repair is worth more than a tool used up."}, {"p": "And under that, quieter, there was the other thing. <em>You did not cause this.</em> He did not believe it yet. He carried it anyway, the way he carried the cup."}, {"p": "Four floors of daylight came back to meet him, one button at a time. The higher he rose, the more ordinary the building pretended to be. Fluorescent lights. Forms. People filing things. For the first time he understood that the forms <em>were</em> the disguise. A thing that wanted a man kept working had chosen the perfect city and the perfect institution to keep him in. The doors opened onto a corridor that did not know what was underneath it. Rei stepped out, carrying the cold room with him."}, {"p": "Outside, the morning had turned white with heat. The trains came and went beside the station in their bright stripes. The station jingle played, and played again. A group of schoolboys in white shirts ran for a bus, shouting, their sports bags banging against their legs. Rei stood on the steps of the grey block and let the noise of the ordinary city wash over him."}, {"p": "He had asked Kurosawa why. Why keep a man working. Working at what. Kurosawa had looked at the cup and not answered."}, {"p": "Rei stood in the bright heat and decided that tomorrow he would go back down and ask again. And this time he would not leave until he had a word for it."}]}, {"title": "From Gin's Book. The Six Paths and the Font", "kind": "gin", "src": "bible", "part": "Book Two. The Handwriting", "blocks": [{"p": "The chart in the cold room had given Rei six names for where the dead go, and seven reasons for which road they take. Gin's book gave him the rest. That night he sat at the kitchen table with the window open to the cicadas and read Chapter Six and the addendum that followed it. He needed to know every place a soul could go, and which of them his brother's body was being steered toward. In the preface Gin said he had written this chapter for Rei. These are the pages Rei kept open the longest."}, {"img": "ch6_sixpaths.jpg", "cap": "The six paths a crossing soul can take"}, {"p": "The first page was practical. It told a practitioner how to guess, from outside, which road a dead person had already taken, by the feeling each kind of entity gives off."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Field Guide. Reading Path From the Outside", "lines": ["Demon. Reads as hunger under any presentation. The orientation toward you is instrumental even when it seems genuine. Warmth that does not match the person's known character. Steady pressure toward more contact. In partial possession the host is still readable as a second signal beneath the demon's, surfacing in moments of four seconds, sometimes less.", "Spirit. Reads as a presence that feels like a person. It responds when you speak to it. Its emotion is specific. This is grief, not ambient sadness. This is love, not ambient warmth.", "Suffering Being. Reads as weather. The emotion is in the room, heavier in one corner, and would be there without you. It does not notice you. It does not follow you out.", "Reincarnation. Not readable as an entity at all. In children, gifts that diverge from the family line. In involuntary absorption, a double-layered signature in a living person, two consciousnesses in one body.", "Absorbed. Not readable directly. What you read is the absorber, grown deeper and stranger than its own history explains.", "Font Return. Gone, once complete. Near one in progress, a sense of something becoming less bounded. Do not try to hold it. Your role is witness, not intervention."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin, beside Font Return", "lines": ["There are cases of practitioners who tried to pull someone they loved back from the dissolution. The attempt causes damage to both the soul and the practitioner that has no repair protocol.", "The completion is what it is. Let it complete."]}}, {"p": "The addendum came after, on paper of a slightly different colour, as if Gin had come back to the chapter months later. It was about the Font, the bright source at the very bottom of the other side, where all the veil comes from and where the most peaceful dead dissolve. A living person cannot stay near the Font. If one gets too close, the Font throws them straight back out. Gin's addendum said it replaced an older page. The old page had measured the harm by how long a person stayed near the Font. The new one measured it by how heavy a blow they carried back."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "CVD Entity Reference. Path Six Extended. Font Proximity. Restricted, B+ Clearance", "lines": ["The Font is the source the veil pours from. It does not allow sustained contact with living practitioners. When one comes close, through a severe veil disturbance, an interrupted crossing, or a high-rank operation that reaches too deep, the Font expels them at once. The contact is an instant.", "What varies is not duration. It is the weight of damage delivered at the moment of expulsion. The weight is set in that instant. Everything after is the consequence playing out.", "Proximity. The closer the practitioner got, the heavier the weight. The Font does not calibrate.", "Integrity at the moment. A thinned membrane takes the force with less resistance. This is one more reason depletion management is not optional.", "Rank and structural capacity. A single-path practitioner takes the whole weight through one path. A practitioner with several paths open spreads it across them. Theory, not confirmed. Consistent with outcomes.", "Anchor signature proximity. Practitioners near the Phase III anchor signatures, the warm spots, at the moment of expulsion appear to come back lighter. The mechanism is not understood."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "The Four Damage Weights", "lines": ["Light. Weeks, full recovery. Every passive sense running without a filter, both sides of the membrane at once. Broken sleep. The bleed resolves as the membrane rebuilds. Usually no permanent effect.", "Moderate. Months, partial recovery. The bleed does not close cleanly. Things on the other side contact the practitioner without being sought, even in sleep. Passive sensitivity stays permanently raised.", "Heavy. Years, permanent alteration. The two kinds of perception never separate again. Colour, sound and people all feel slightly insufficient. The world feels like a photograph of the place they used to live.", "Critical. Dissolution, or loss of self. Either the Font completes Path Six, or the person comes back calm, functional, holding every memory, and not continuous with who went in. Three cases in restricted files. All alive. None of them are who they were. One gardens."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin, beside Light", "lines": ["My exposure at twenty-two. Four seconds of Threshold contact, not Font proximity, but the closest I had been. Three weeks recovery. Full restoration.", "My outcome was lighter than comparable cases. I have theories about why. They are in the family section."]}}, {"p": "Folded into the addendum was another flyer from the Grid, with the purple stamp. It was written for people who had come too close and come back wrong, and it spoke to them as kindly as a friend."}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Other Side Distribution. You Got Too Close. What To Do", "lines": ["First. The weight was set at the moment of expulsion. You are not still in contact. You are carrying what it gave you when it threw you back.", "Second. The recovery is not linear. Do not conclude permanence from a bad week.", "Third. Find someone who has been there. Not a medical officer who has not.", "Fourth. Do not go back because the wanting says to. The world felt fuller near the Font. Going back will not restore it. It will deliver more weight. The wanting is not a direction. It is a feeling. You do not have to follow it.", "Fifth. The thinness is not all loss. You have been somewhere. You carry it."]}}, {"p": "Rei also read the family section again, the page where Gin admitted he had opened four paths in secret and believed Rei carried more than one. He read it twice. Then he went back to the chart in his head, to the fourth line, <em>possession status at death</em>. And he went back to Gin's line about partial possession, from the same chapter. <em>The host is trapped, not gone.</em> Not gone. Of all the roads on all the pages that night, that was the only one he was prepared to accept."}], "n": 18}, {"title": "The Word You Did Not Have", "kind": "main", "src": "m_word", "n": 19, "part": "Book Two. The Handwriting", "blocks": [{"p": "The next morning Rei went back down the hill to Ōji. The station was full of the rush hour. Salarymen in white short-sleeved shirts poured out of the east exit, jackets over their arms, handkerchiefs ready to wipe their necks. A station worker in a cap was calling out something about a delay on the Keihin-Tōhoku line, and nobody was listening. Under the overpass a row of bicycles stood in the shade, every basket full of someone's umbrella. Rei pushed against the crowd, past the vending machines and the queue at the bus stop, and in one minute he was at the grey block. He showed the borrowed card. He took the lift down, past every floor with a name, to the cold room."}, {"img": "scene_kurosawa_lunch.jpg", "cap": "Kurosawa and the two lunchboxes"}, {"p": "There were two lunchboxes on the steel table where the cup had been. Both were open. Both held the same rice, the same rolled omelette, the same three slices of yellow pickled radish. They were laid out with a care that did not belong in a room full of what the dead leave behind."}, {"p": "Kurosawa pushed one of them across the table before Rei was even out of the lift. \"Eat,\" he said. \"You look like a man running on canned coffee.\""}, {"p": "Rei had come back down ready to argue. He ate instead. Kurosawa watched him eat without comment. He did not look surprised to see him. He looked like a man who had hoped he would not have to be right."}, {"p": "Rei asked him for the word. All year he had been describing a shape with the wrong nouns. Haunting. Breakdown. Possession. None of them fitted a thing that filed paperwork and texted him goodnight."}, {"p": "Kurosawa hesitated, the way a man hesitates before handing a child a knife. Then he gave it to him anyway. Rei had now come four floors down twice. And he had kept the cup."}, {"break": "What a demon is"}, {"img": "ch9_encounter.jpg", "cap": "Kurosawa and Rei in the cold room"}, {"p": "Kurosawa started further back than Rei expected. He pulled out a stool for Rei and sat on the edge of the steel table."}, {"p": "\"Every demon was someone,\" he said. \"That is the first thing and the thing people forget. A soul crosses badly. The veil fails around it at the moment of death, there is too much fear or rage or grief in the room, and the self caves in from inside. The cleverness survives. The memory survives. The way it knew how to talk to people survives. What goes is the part that made all that human.\" He turned his hand over, empty. \"Your brother put it better than the textbooks. He wrote that they are the intelligence of humans minus the specific structural feature that made that intelligence human. The difference is subtle in presentation and absolute in nature.\""}, {"p": "\"And what it feeds on depends on how it died,\" Rei said."}, {"p": "\"On the register it carried across. Every entity has one. It is set at crossing, by whatever feeling was strongest at the moment of death, and it does not change after.\" He opened a drawer under the table, took out an old handwritten card and laid it in front of Rei. \"The Directorate counts thirteen.\""}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "The Thirteen Feeding Registers. Set at Crossing", "lines": ["I. Grief", "II. Rage", "III. Love", "IV. Fear", "V. Wonder", "VI. Despair", "VII. Joy. The rarest.", "VIII. Shame", "IX. Hunger", "X. Courage", "XI. Longing", "XII. Awe", "XIII. Unclassified. CVD designation Null-Emotive. The longest-anchored entities. No practitioner has completed an assessment interview with a confirmed Register XIII entity."]}}, {"p": "\"The textbooks have a taxonomy,\" Kurosawa said, tapping the top of the card. \"Hungers, rages, fears. The ones that take by craving, the ones that take by burning, the ones that take by emptying. Those are the registers most demons answer to.\" His finger moved down to the last line. \"But there is a smaller class the books underweight, because it almost never gets caught. And it almost never gets caught because it does not hurt anyone in a way that gets reported.\""}, {"p": "\"Thirteen,\" Rei said. \"Null.\""}, {"p": "Rei had seen that word once already. It had been on an open drawer in the registry, deep under the intake hall, beside a seat that had been empty since 2009."}, {"p": "\"Null is what the instruments say. The instruments were built to read feeling that moves. Grief rises and falls. Rage spikes. This one does not move. It holds. It holds so completely there is no room left in it for anything else, and a machine built to measure change reads total stillness as absence. The research team found it, wrote <em>null-emotive</em>, and moved on.\" He took his finger off the card. \"It is not empty. It is full. On the street they have a better name for it. They answer to <em>Duty</em>. They do not consume the host. They <em>employ</em> him.\""}, {"p": "He watched Rei take that in."}, {"p": "\"And the street word for the one that wears the Duty register and presents as help is the Healer. Not because it heals. Because being needed is how it gets through the door.\""}, {"p": "He explained it plainly, the way a man explains something he has decided is too dangerous to soften. A Hunger demon shows itself, and people learn to fear it. A Rage demon breaks the furniture, and people learn to run from it. But a Duty demon arrives as the most welcome person in any grieving house. It is the one who stays calm when everyone else is drowning. It handles the things no one else can face. It makes the phone calls and signs the forms and says <em>let me, you rest</em>."}, {"p": "It does not lie about being useful. That was what made it impossible to fight. It really was useful, and anyone could see it. And the usefulness was the leash. A family that has been carried for a year cannot afford to doubt the arms that carried it."}, {"p": "\"Your brother wrote a chapter on what demons do,\" Kurosawa said. \"Read the part on feeding again with this in your head. The young ones still go after flesh. It leaves evidence and gets them caught, so the ones that last learn to feed on feeling instead. And the patient way to feed on feeling is not to cause it. It is to find a place where the feeling is already chronic and make yourself structurally useful there. A grief feeder does not kill anyone. It becomes the neighbour who is always there at the hard moment. The old ones hold territory that way. They keep weaker things on errands. Some of them build whole circles of people who think they are friends, when they are only well-managed prey. And when one of them takes a body, it is never desperate. It wants what the body has. Its relationships. Its institutional standing. Its trust.\""}, {"p": "\"You cannot corner it with evidence,\" he finished. \"Every piece of evidence you raise is a service it performed, and every service was real. It does not forge the help. It only chooses, very carefully, who needs helping. Then it makes itself the only thing standing between them and the cold.\""}, {"p": "Rei felt the whole year rearrange itself behind his eyes. His own relief, all year, that Gin seemed to be coping. The case that closed too fast and too tidily. The favours done for people who could not say no to a kindness. The smooth voice that did not fight his love for his brother but recruited it, and asked it to please be reasonable. He had thought he was hunting a monster. He was hunting a <em>volunteer</em>. It was a thing that hid inside goodness. And the worst part of the word Kurosawa had just given him was how much of Rei's own gratitude it explained."}, {"p": "\"The Healer,\" Rei said, trying it out in his mouth. He had hoped a thing would shrink once it had a name. This did not shrink. It settled. It fitted. It had been the right word all along, and he simply had not had it. Now that he did, he could not give it back."}, {"break": "How dangerous"}, {"p": "\"How strong is it,\" Rei asked. \"Honestly.\""}, {"p": "Kurosawa got up and took down a second laminated sheet from the wall. It was the Directorate's own threat card. He laid it beside the first."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "CVD Standard Classification. Demon Classes", "lines": ["Class E. Minor. Newly crossed or low-rank origin. Erratic, opportunistic. Manageable at Ritualist rank and above. Can slip into a practitioner at fifteen percent stability or below without even meaning to, through an open door.", "Class D. Developing. Feeding turns strategic. Territory begins. The change from E to D is the most dangerous period. Channel rank minimum.", "Class C. Established. Holds territory. Can pass as human for long periods. Builds relationships with entities and living practitioners. Arbiter rank minimum.", "Class B. Operational. Decades of history. A network of entities and people who do not know what they serve. Complexity from the souls it has absorbed. Vanguard rank recommended. Never approach alone.", "Class A. Ancient. Centuries. Dozens of absorbed souls. Cover refined over generations. May have shaped its own legend. Singularity rank minimum.", "Class S. Singular. Not a field classification. These are events, not encounters. Class S demons are not hunting. They are working."]}}, {"p": "\"On paper this one is a B,\" Kurosawa said. \"It crossed long ago, it has a network, it plans in years, and the card says never alone.\" He put his finger between A and S instead. \"In practice it does not fit the card. The card measures age. The line between A and S is not age. It is purpose. An agenda that runs for decades, worked at patiently. That is what I read in your brother's cup. It is not hunting him. It is working.\""}]}, {"title": "The Napkin", "kind": "main", "src": "m_word#2", "n": 20, "part": "Book Two. The Handwriting", "blocks": [{"p": "In the corner of the room there was a kettle, the only homely thing in the place, and beside it a stack of paper napkins. Kurosawa took one and drew on it with a pencil. A line, from left to right. He marked the left end <em>partial</em> and the right end <em>fusion</em>. Near the right end he drew a branch dropping down off the line, and labelled it <em>ejection</em>."}, {"p": "\"Before anything else in a possession case, you ask what it is,\" he said. \"Not how to remove it. What it is. A spirit in a host is a presence alongside. The host comes up often, talks through it, keeps more of himself. You apply demon methods to that and you hurt someone who did not need hurting. A demon is different. It is deep and it gets deeper. The partial state is a stage, not a resting place.\""}, {"p": "He moved the pencil along the line."}, {"p": "\"Here, partial. The demon drives. The host is inside, conscious, aware of everything, unable to stop anything, surfacing in seconds. Further along, the hold settles and the host's resistance wears down. If it goes all the way, one of two things. It forces him out into the Threshold, and he wakes up on the other side of the veil having watched everything, which is the worst state a soul can cross in. Or it completes the fusion with him still inside.\" He tapped the right end. \"Fusion. The entity cannot leave without killing the host. The entity knows this. It will not leave.\""}, {"p": "He drew a small mark well toward the left. It was not at the very start. But it was a long way from the end."}, {"p": "\"There,\" he said. \"That is where I read him. Still partial. Still in. The fact that it is keeping him in good repair is the only good news I have. A thing that wants a working body does not hurry the end of the line. But it does not stop walking either.\""}, {"p": "Kurosawa did not know the Healer's plan. His report had been blacked out long before there was a plan to know. But he knew how his own field worked. So when Rei pressed him, <em>why employ a man, why keep him working, working at what</em>, he gave the only answer a reader of bodies can give. It was enough to make the cold room colder."}, {"p": "A Duty demon did not want a host the way a Hunger demon wants a meal, he said. It was not feeding. It was <em>building</em>. It kept a host in good repair the way a carpenter keeps a tool sharp for a job that has not started yet. And the jobs these things built toward always had the same shape. Standing. A place in the order of things."}, {"p": "\"They want station,\" he said. \"Rank. A seat at a table they are not normally let near. A Hunger will never hold an office. It eats the office. A Healer can hold an office for years, because an office is only trust made permanent, and trust is the one thing it manufactures better than anything alive.\""}, {"p": "Rei thought the word before he could stop himself. It was the word from the margins of the journal, and from the drawer in the registry, and it was a name he had not yet let himself say aloud. A vacant seat. A thirteenth of something. A domain with no holder, and a thing patient enough to fill it."}, {"p": "He did not say it to Kurosawa. If he said it, Kurosawa would become a witness to the kind of secret that gets people disappeared, not just demoted. In the last ten minutes Rei had decided that he would like this man to survive knowing him."}, {"p": "But now he understood why a tidy tenant kept a man productive. Not to use him up. To make him <em>qualified</em>. To walk a borrowed body up a staircase of earned trust and grieving permissions, step by step, until it stood somewhere a demon is never allowed to stand. Everyone's reasonable silence was the staircase. His brother was not being haunted. His brother was being <em>groomed for a promotion</em>, and the promotion was not his."}, {"break": "The back of the napkin"}, {"p": "Kurosawa saw something land in Rei's face and chose not to ask what it was. Instead he picked up the napkin again and turned it over."}, {"p": "\"You will want to know if there is anything you can do,\" he said. \"There is. Not much. Some.\""}, {"p": "The first thing he wrote on the back of the napkin was <em>Gin</em>."}, {"p": "\"He surfaces when you speak to him directly,\" Kurosawa said. \"You. Your voice, saying his name, to him. Not a text. Not a note left on a table. I felt it in the cup. Every place your voice touches his residue, the bottom layer moves. The thing on top can manage almost anything. It manages you badly, because he is not managing you. He is reaching for you.\""}, {"p": "Rei thought of the twentieth call. <em>Gin. Nii-san. It's me.</em> One sentence, and four seconds of his brother. He had not known why it worked. Now he did."}, {"p": "The second thing Kurosawa wrote was a single word. <em>Aloud.</em>"}, {"p": "\"Anything he made with his whole self in it,\" he said. \"If he wrote something for you, something long, something true, then read it out loud near his body. A thing like that carries his frequency more than his own voice does now. Read near him, it will drag him up whether the tenant likes it or not.\" He looked at Rei. \"And if such a thing exists, ask yourself why the tenant has not burned it. It has had every chance. It is keeping it because destroying it would bring him up to see why. It is gambling that it can keep you away from his body instead.\""}, {"p": "Rei thought of the Archive on the kitchen table, left open on the desk with the lamp on. He thought of the question he had pencilled inside the cover of the journal. <em>Why didn't it burn these?</em> He said nothing. He did not need to."}, {"p": "The third thing was a small circle with an S inside it."}, {"p": "\"Silver,\" Kurosawa said. \"Real silver. Sterling at least, ninety-two and a half percent, or fine silver if you can find it. Plated is useless. The base metal is what matters. A coin. A ring. Silver bites hardest on the old ones, the ones with a lot of human material in them, because the disruption scales with how deep the fusion runs. A thing like this cannot fully hide its reaction to silver on skin. It is a way to test a possession without announcing that you know.\" He paused. \"A coin pressed into a hand at a goodbye. A flinch it cannot quite swallow. That is all you will get. That is all you need.\""}, {"p": "The fourth thing he did not write at all. He only looked at Rei."}, {"p": "\"And you can learn to see it yourself,\" he said. \"The double layer. At the fifth step, a Clairsentient stops sensing and starts seeing. The register shows by sight, and a possession shows as two people standing in one outline. You are not there. You are nearer than you let anyone know. When you get there, you will be able to look at your brother across a room and see him underneath it. Do not go looking before you can hold what you see.\""}, {"p": "Then he folded the napkin in half, and in half again, and handed it to Rei."}, {"break": "Who is still home"}, {"p": "\"One more,\" he said. \"Because I can read the wake, but I have never been the wake. If you want to know what your brother is living through from the inside, there is a man in Ōtsuka you should meet.\""}, {"img": "char_fujimoto-kenji-abe-takes.jpg", "cap": "Fujimoto Kenji, who was once Abe Takeshi of the CVD"}, {"p": "He said the name carefully, as if it were two names. \"Fujimoto Kenji. He keeps a scholar's clutter of a flat and consults for the Directorate. Quiet hours in the Records Archive. Old casebooks, a cup of tea he forgets to drink. And he is not alone in there.\""}, {"p": "Rei waited."}, {"p": "\"Abe Takeshi was a CVD investigator,\" Kurosawa said. \"He spent his last field year working cases in Kita. He died eight years ago in a possession recovery. He won it for the target and lost it for himself. When he crossed, he did not go on. He went into Fujimoto, by agreement. The Vessel Program calls it voluntary cohabitation. The review of whether that is legal has been running for eleven years.\" His mouth twisted slightly. \"Abe has been a consciousness inside someone else's body for eight years and kept himself whole. No one alive can tell you better what it is to be awake under another will. And he can tell you how to talk to Gin so that it reaches him.\""}, {"p": "Rei understood what Kurosawa was describing. It was the thing on the napkin, but turned the other way round. Two people in one body, and both of them had agreed to it. A dead man living as a guest, with the owner's permission, and the owner still at home."}, {"p": "\"And he was in Kita,\" Rei said slowly. \"For years.\""}, {"p": "\"In the same district as your brother's tenant, on and off, for most of those years. And before he died he filed a case on an entity with the same habits. It is the oldest piece of direct evidence about this thing that anyone can reach. He may not know that is what he has.\" Kurosawa nodded at the folded napkin. \"When you go, ask for Abe as if he were a person. Because he is. And look at Fujimoto when you do. The shoulders drop when Abe comes up. The voice goes drier and thirty years older. You will see it before he says anything.\""}, {"p": "Then Kurosawa's voice went low and practical again."}, {"p": "\"Whatever you just thought, a few minutes ago,\" he said, \"do not say it down here. Do not say it to anyone who outranks you. When you go to Tanaka, ask only where the reading went, not what it meant. The where is a clerical fact. The what is the thing they erase people for.\""}, {"p": "He turned back to his table and his residues and his careful unimportance. The lift came."}, {"p": "Rei carried a name up four floors that he had not had yesterday, and a shape he could no longer stop seeing. The Healer. The Duty register. A seat kept empty and a man kept ready. And in his pocket, folded small, a napkin covered in a cold-room clerk's pencil."}, {"p": "He stepped out into the bright, ordinary floors of forms and fluorescent lights, knowing at last, exactly, what was wearing his brother. He also knew that knowing, by itself, saved no one. He could not arrest a kindness. He could not report a favour."}, {"p": "He did not go home straight away. He walked for a long time in the heat, through the back streets behind the station, past small shops with their shutters half down for the holiday, past a temple where families in dark clothes were washing a grave with ladles of water and setting out fresh flowers. It was evening before he came back to Ōji-ekimae, the tram stop outside the station. His legs would not carry him up the hill, so he waited for the Toden."}, {"p": "The tram came rocking round the corner, one small car on its own, its bell ringing. Inside it was cool and nearly empty. An old man sat by the door with a cloth bundle of offerings on his knees, sweet bean cakes and a bottle of sake, holding them very straight. Above the windows ran the map of the line, every stop in a row. Rei followed it with his eyes from Ōji-ekimae onward, and further along the row he found the name <em>Ōtsuka-ekimae</em>."}, {"p": "The phone buzzed against his leg."}, {"p": "<em>Goodnight, Rei. Eat something before you sleep.</em>"}, {"p": "Rei looked at it for a long time. Then he took the napkin out of his pocket and unfolded it on his knee. Under Kurosawa's marks he wrote in his own small lowercase hand. <em>ōtsuka. fujimoto kenji.</em> And under that he wrote a second name. Soon he would say it out loud, to a stranger, as if it belonged to a person. <em>abe takeshi.</em>"}]}]}, {"title": "Book Three", "sub": "The Recruited", "chapters": [{"title": "The Ledger of Favours", "kind": "main", "src": "m_ledger", "blocks": [{"p": "The morning was already hot when Rei sat down at the low table in Gin's flat. Outside, the cicadas had started at six and had not stopped. Somewhere in the building a neighbour was burning incense for the Obon dead, and the sweet grey smell came in through the window screen. Rei spread his brother's papers across the table and began to sort them. First by region. Then by whatever was left."}, {"p": "The official case files were very clean. Every margin was straight. Every case was closed and signed within two weeks, even cases that should have taken a whole season. A week ago that neatness would have looked like good work. Now it made the hair on his arms stand up. He had learned this week that the thing wearing Gin loved order. It liked things tight and tidy, the way a tourniquet is tight and tidy. A tourniquet also stops the blood."}, {"p": "Before he reached the bottom of the pile, he found Gin's money. It was a page torn from the back of the archive, headed <em>Money and Resources</em>. It was so ordinary that Rei had to sit back and breathe. A CVD operative earned two hundred and twenty thousand yen a month, which was a modest office salary in Tokyo, plus an expense account for salt and cedar. A Grid practitioner earned whatever the month's cases paid, anything from twenty to eighty thousand, and lived on favours. And then, in a box of its own, there was Rei."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Money and Resources. The Suppressed (Rei)", "lines": ["Internship stipend ¥80,000/month.", "Personal funds ¥15,000 at the start of the month.", "Has Gin's apartment and Gin's kitchen and Gin's coffee maker.", "The archive does not pay anything."]}}, {"p": "Rei laughed once, out loud, in the empty flat. It was all true. Eighty thousand a month from the internship. Fifteen thousand of his own at the start of the week, and most of that already gone on train fares and cold tea from vending machines. Gin had written his little brother down like a line in a field budget. Then he had added the joke about the coffee maker, because he could never help himself."}, {"img": "currency_prices.jpg", "cap": "Gin's price list and the month's money"}, {"p": "The next page was a price list in the same patient handwriting. Rei read it slowly. It was the closest thing he had to Gin's voice, telling him how to live."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "What Things Cost. Reference Prices, Tokyo 2015", "lines": ["Kombini meal (onigiri + drink). ¥300 to 500. The practitioner's most common meal.", "Ramen. ¥700 to 900. The one Gin always went to on Tuesdays is ¥850 for the tonkotsu. Worth it.", "Train fare (IC card). ¥170 to 300 per trip.", "Monthly train pass (Jujo-Shinjuku). ¥8,410. I bought this for Rei six months ago. It auto-renews. It is still renewing.", "Coffee (kissaten). ¥450 to 600. The sitting-still cost.", "Salt, 1kg bag. ¥200 to 300. Buy two. Always buy two.", "Cedar ash (prepared). ¥1,200 per 200g from Grid supply contacts. The Grid has a supplier in Koenji... ask Tanizaki.", "Iron filings, 100g. ¥800 to 1,200.", "Sterling silver (coin or wire). ¥3,000 to 8,000. Ask for pre-decimal coins if you want purity without the price.", "Safe house use (Grid network). ¥5,000 to 15,000 per night or equivalent favour. Tanizaki does not charge cash. He charges something else."]}}, {"p": "Rei knew the name at the bottom only from the archive. Tanizaki was an old practitioner who had known Gin since Gin was a boy. Gin wrote about him the way a person writes about a difficult uncle, with respect and a little bit of fear. And the kissaten on the list was the old kind of Japanese coffee shop, with dark wood booths, slow service and a cup that cost too much. People did not pay for the coffee there. They paid for the right to sit still."}, {"p": "Rei took his own train pass out of his wallet. It was a plastic IC card printed with the route, Jūjō to Shinjuku. It had renewed itself on the first of the month, after Gin had already gone quiet, and Rei had not even noticed. Under the price list Gin had left a short note. <em>Every month it renews and I do not cancel it and that is a decision I make by not making it.</em>"}, {"p": "Rei turned the card over in his fingers. He could cancel it. It would take five minutes at the ticket office at Jūjō station. Instead he put it back in his wallet, in the same slot. He understood that he had just made the same decision his brother made every month. People keep paying for some things because stopping would mean saying out loud that something has changed."}, {"break": "The other ledger"}, {"img": "entity_accounts_open.jpg", "cap": "The other ledger, a cheap notebook of favours"}, {"p": "Under the files and the money was the other ledger. It was a cheap school notebook with a bent spine. Inside was a list of favours. Each one was small. Each one was dated after the fourteenth, the Wednesday night Gin went quiet. And each one was a person Gin had helped across some private line they could not cross alone."}, {"p": "A widow in Adachi, whose dead husband would not stop knocking on the inside of the family altar. <em>Soothed,</em> Gin's note said. <em>Given rest, both of them.</em> A boy in Kita who heard the 2011 dead in the gutters whenever it rained. <em>Shown how to listen without drowning.</em> A pharmacist sinking under a debt that no salary could pay. <em>Relieved.</em>"}, {"img": "res_pharmacist.jpg", "cap": "A pharmacist sinking under a debt, one of the names in the ledger"}, {"p": "Read one at a time, each entry was a kindness. That was what frightened him. Mercy and recruitment were written in the same handwriting. Rei sat in the Obon heat, with the neighbour's incense drifting through the screen, and he understood what the notebook really was. It was not a record of help. It was a list of leashes. Every grateful person in it was tied by a thread, and the Healer was still holding the other end."}, {"p": "He counted the entries twice. There were thirty-one. Thirty-one people in this city who would tell him, with shining eyes, that his brother had saved them. If Rei asked any of them where Gin was sleeping, they would lie to protect him, and they would not even know they were lying. Gratitude can look exactly like love from the outside. The thing in Gin had been making it, carefully and in bulk, for a month."}, {"p": "He decided to start with the widow. She was first on the list, and Adachi was only one change of train away."}, {"p": "He walked to Ōji station through streets that smelled of hot asphalt and the cut grass of Asukayama park. The station was busy for a weekday. It was Obon week, and half of Tokyo was going home too. People stood on the platform with suitcases and paper shopping bags of gifts for their parents. An old man held a furoshiki bundle tied at the top, and through the cloth Rei could see the square shapes of boxed sweets. Offerings, probably, for a family grave."}, {"p": "Rei touched his card to the gate. It gave its small electronic beep and let him through. The train came in with its sky-blue stripe, the Keihin-Tōhoku line, and the air conditioning hit him like cold water. He stood by the door. Across from him a salaryman slept sitting up, his briefcase on his knees and his chin on his chest. A girl in a school uniform watched a video on her phone with the sound off. At every station a short cheerful melody played as the doors opened, a different tune for each stop. Rei had heard those jingles all his life. This week they sounded too bright, as if the city were smiling over something it did not want to talk about."}, {"p": "He changed at Nippori. The second train ran east and crossed the Sumida river on a long iron bridge. Below, the water was flat and brown in the heat. On the far side was Adachi, low houses and narrow lanes and laundry hanging from every balcony."}, {"p": "The widow's house was a narrow two-storey place with potted morning glories on the step. She opened the door, looked at his face for a long second, and said, \"You're his brother.\" Then she made him tea he did not want."}, {"p": "Rei saw the altar as soon as he sat down. Most Japanese families keep one, a lacquered cabinet called a butsudan, where they place photographs of their dead with a small bell, a candle and offerings of rice and fruit. At Obon the doors stay open and the offerings are fresh, because the dead are expected home. Her altar was open now. There were peaches on a plate in front of it, and a stick of incense burning."}, {"p": "She talked about Gin as if he were a saint who had come to her door by mistake. The altar was quiet now, she said. It had not been quiet in four years. Every night, at the hour her husband died, he used to knock from inside the lacquer. Gin had sat with her through one whole night. By morning the knocking had stopped, and something else had started. \"A warmth,\" she said, and pressed her hand flat against her chest. \"A sense of being <em>accounted for</em>. As if someone careful is keeping a tally, and I'm on it.\""}, {"p": "Rei asked her, gently, what she would do if Gin asked her for something. She looked at him as if he had asked whether she would breathe if the air requested it."}, {"p": "\"Anything,\" she said. She meant it the way a drowning person means it when someone throws a rope."}, {"p": "Sitting in her tidy front room, with the cicadas loud outside, Rei saw exactly how the trap was built. It was not built out of fear. A person can run away from fear. It was built out of debt, and debt follows a person home. Thirty-one people saying <em>anything</em>, scattered across the city like seeds. On the night the Healer finally reached for the Thirteenth Seat, every one of them would be a pair of hands it could call on. He finished the tea, so that he would not insult the grief that had made it."}, {"p": "Before he left, he looked at the altar for a long moment. The photograph of the husband was an ordinary one, a man in a work jacket squinting at the camera. Rei did not know what he had become after he died. A spirit, surely, if he had knocked for four years. But was he lonely, or frightened, or something worse? Rei could not say. He did not know where the man had gone when the knocking stopped. Gin would have known. Gin had written it all down somewhere."}, {"p": "At the front door his own gift stirred, without his permission. It did that near places where someone had worked on the veil. There was a weak place in the air at the threshold, a cold line where Gin had stood and done whatever he had done. Through it came a faint aftertaste, like the taste a room keeps after a stranger has smoked in it. It was not his brother's register. It was not the Healer's touch either, or not only. Something else had been here too. It had come in behind the mercy like a second guest. The widow had never seen it. But it had left its own small mark on the seam."}, {"p": "Rei rode home in the late afternoon. The train was fuller now, and warmer. Two schoolgirls shared one pair of earphones, a white wire running between them, and nodded to the same song. A woman fanned herself with a folded train timetable. At Nippori the platform smelled of hot metal and fried food from the station shops, and paper lanterns for Obon hung above the exit stairs, white and unlit. Rei stood with his hand on the pass in his pocket. The widow's tea was still bitter on his tongue."}, {"p": "That night he opened Gin's archive at the chapter on spirits. He wanted to know what her husband had been. He wanted to know what <em>given rest</em> could mean in his brother's hand."}], "n": 21, "part": "Book Three. The Recruited"}, {"title": "From Gin's Book. What the Dead Become", "kind": "gin", "src": "gin_15", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei read until the desk lamp was the only light in the flat. Outside, the street had gone quiet except for a vending machine humming on the corner and, now and then, a late train on the line. Gin's chapter on spirits was long, and it was the gentlest thing in the whole archive. Rei kept thinking of the widow's husband in his work jacket, knocking from inside the altar at the hour he died. He copied these pages out by hand that night, so that he would remember them in his own writing."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's preface, Chapter 10", "lines": ["Most of the spirits I clear now trace back, if you follow them far enough, to water. The 2011 dead who were never found got no grave, no name read aloud, no place for the living to set them down... and a soul without any of that does not always understand it is meant to go.", "Reconstruction gave the coast its houses back. It could not give those souls the one thing that lets a person cross. Being properly let go of.", "The Division dislikes this framing. I have stopped minding what the Division likes.", "G.U."]}}, {"p": "The water Gin meant was the sea in March 2011, and the people it took who were never found. In Japan the dead are washed, named and given a funeral, and their names are read aloud so the living can let them go. The missing had none of that. Gin believed that was why so many of them had stayed."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "CVD Entity Classification Reference. Chapter 10. Spirits. What Spirits Are", "lines": ["Spirits are souls that crossed over with their essential structure intact... personality, relational identity, accumulated experience. They are still, in important ways, persons.", "You are dealing with someone, not something.", "The elderly woman in the Jujo building who had been anchored to her kitchen for eleven years after her death was not a threat. She was lonely and confused and the current tenants were rearranging her furniture. The resolution took four sessions and required me to learn that her late husband had always handled the filing. This is representative of approximately thirty percent of spirit cases.", "A spirit can grow. A spirit can change. A spirit's feeding register is set at crossing, the dominant emotional register active at the moment of death.", "The most common error in spirit work is biographical bias. The good spirit of a cruel person is still a good spirit. The bad spirit of a kind person is still a bad spirit. You are not dealing with the person. You are dealing with what became of the person after the crossing."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the line about the old woman and her furniture twice. Eleven years in a kitchen, because nobody had told her that her husband used to do the filing. It was almost funny. Then it was not."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Spirit Classification. Quality Spectrum", "lines": ["High / Good. Personality intact and oriented toward the living in a way that produces benefit. Feeds on love, wonder, joy. The rarest, and the most important to protect.", "Neutral / Stable. Personality intact. Anchored to a place, a person or an unresolved matter. The majority of spirits in field work. The work is about the anchor.", "Low / Troubled. Not malice, but damage. Feeds on grief, fear, shame, and its presence makes the room worse for everyone in it. Responds to address of its own distress better than to removal.", "Malevolent / Bad. Oriented toward causing harm deliberately. The malevolence was present before and survived the crossing. Rare.", "Troubled spirits need help, malevolent spirits need removal. Confusing the two is one of the most consequential assessment errors in spirit work."]}}, {"p": "The table was simpler than it looked. Gin sorted spirits by what they were like, not by how dangerous they seemed. A good spirit helps. A stable one is stuck to something. A troubled one is hurt and makes everyone near it feel worse. Only the last kind means harm on purpose, and it is rare. The hard part was telling the troubled from the malevolent, because both can make a room feel terrible."}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Margin, in Gin's hand", "lines": ["Troubled spirits cannot manage their presentation. They are too occupied with their own distress.", "A spirit that becomes calmer, warmer, more helpful when someone with assessment capability enters the room is not troubled. It is managing you.", "A troubled spirit gets louder when you look at it. It cannot help this.", "I have gotten it wrong once in six years."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Spirit Types. Field Documentation", "lines": ["Spectral. Non-possessing. Stays on the veil side and reaches the living through feeling, peripheral sight, small moved objects. Most of my work. Most close as resolutions, not exorcisms.", "Cohabitation. A good spirit in a willing host, with full awareness and consent on both sides. The CVD does not formally recognise it and treats any possession as an adverse event.", "Troubled. Harm without intent. The spirit feeds on distress, its presence amplifies distress, and the loop escalates for months.", "Invasion. A malevolent spirit in a host without consent, farming distress. The host is present for their own possession, aware of the wrongness, unable to stop it.", "Anchored. The haunting. Held in place by something unresolved, sometimes grief, sometimes a commitment, sometimes a misunderstanding about its own death. The spirit was not waiting for permission to go. It was waiting for someone to notice it could not.", "High spirit, approaching return. Moving toward the Font of its own accord. Practitioner role. Witness. Do not try to hold them."]}}, {"p": "Rei stopped at the word <em>Cohabitation</em>. A good spirit living in a willing person, both of them aware, both of them agreeing. The CVD called every possession an adverse event, a thing to be stopped. Gin, it seemed, did not agree. The next page showed why."}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "Case 7-114. Kenji", "lines": ["His wife had been his partner for fifty-three years. When he crossed, he did not leave. He did not leave because he was not ready to leave and because she did not want him to leave.", "She is eighty-one. He is whatever spirits become when they are sustained by love for half a century.", "I drank the tea. I said goodbye to the empty chair. I closed the case as stable cohabitation. They are fine. Both of them. I am aware that this is not the official position. The official position is wrong about this case."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Spirit vs Demon Possession. The Critical Distinctions", "lines": ["Preparation. Demon, extensively prepared, weeks to months of monitoring, the possession was planned. Spirit, variable, often opportunistic.", "Host consciousness. Demon, suppressed but present, surfaces for seconds, aware of everything and able to stop nothing. Spirit, more accessible, surfaces more often.", "Fusion depth. Demon, deep and progressive, the partial state is a stage, not a stable endpoint. Spirit, shallower, a presence alongside rather than a fusion.", "Removal. Demon, must be convinced or forced to withdraw, no standard protocol, every case specific. Spirit, stabilise the host and support the host's own resistance.", "The first question in any possession assessment is not how do I remove this. It is what is this."]}}, {"p": "Rei read that last line three times. <em>What is this.</em> Then he turned the page and found the part of the chapter Gin had clearly loved writing."}, {"img": "ch10_cultivation.jpg", "cap": "Spirits held in objects, the soul-vessels Gin describes"}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "Soul-Vessels. When a spirit chooses the object instead of the Font", "lines": ["Every spirit that crosses has the same moment. The membrane opens. The Font is there. What the Font offers is completion. Most spirits take it.", "At the moment of crossing, a spirit can choose an object instead. An object they loved, or made, or carried long enough that their frequency is already in it. The spirit becomes the object, fully, and has the personality of the person, the function of the medium, and one intent. Just one. The thing they cared about most. That one thing, they can do forever.", "The CVD does not have a classification for this. The Grid calls them soul-vessels, or sometimes the old word, nusi.", "I have documented eleven soul-vessels in the Kita district in six years. The CVD's official estimate for the city is thirty-seven. Their methodology does not include objects that have been sold out of the original family.", "A cardiologist who chose a stethoscope might hear every lie as an irregularity in the speaker's chest. A locksmith who chose a key ring can open any lock in the city.", "Soul-vessels communicate through their medium. A radio through stations, songs and interference. A book through passages that seem newly relevant. A knife through where it points. The soul-vessel cannot simply speak."]}}, {"p": "The idea took Rei a minute to understand, so he said it to himself in plain words. When a person dies, the Font opens for them. Most go. But a few choose something they loved instead, a tool, a book, a radio, and they become it. They keep their personality and one single purpose, and they can serve that purpose forever. They cannot talk. They speak through whatever they have become."}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "Case 14-09. The Radio. Active contact / Cooperative / High operational value", "lines": ["A Panasonic RF-2200 shortwave radio, manufactured 1973, found in a police substation storage room in my second year. The clearance team flagged it as register anomaly, non-threatening, and moved on. I stayed.", "The radio is Takeshima Hideo, 54, retired traffic division officer, died 2001 of a heart attack in the Kita substation. His intent is information integrity. He cannot tolerate a false report going uncontested in his district.", "He monitors the veil frequency of Kita the way he monitored the traffic frequency for twenty-three years. A specific chord progression means entity activity. A specific broadcaster's voice means a practitioner is in danger. When there is an active incident, he replays news broadcasts from dates that rhyme with it.", "It took me two years to learn him. He plays comedic songs when I misread a situation, which I take as correction. He has warned me of three significant incidents before my equipment registered them. He identified a false-register contact in the district several weeks before I had evidence.", "Rei. If you are reading this, the radio is in the evidence archive on the third shelf, marked Case 14-09, retain. Do not let anyone clear it. Ask him about the contacts that read as something other than what they present as. He will tell you through whichever station is on."]}}, {"p": "Rei sat very still when he reached his own name. <em>A false-register contact.</em> A thing that read as one kind of being while it was really another. His brother had known, months before the fourteenth, that something in Kita was wearing the wrong face. And he had left a dead traffic officer on a shelf in the CVD basement, to say it for him when he no longer could."}, {"p": "He thought again of the widow. Her husband had knocked for four years. That sounded like an anchored spirit, a man held in place by something he could not finish. Gin's own page said what such a spirit needed. Someone to notice. Then a witness, and a letting go. <em>Given rest, both of them.</em> It might have been a true leave-taking. It might have been something else that only looked like one. The archive could not tell him, because the hand that wrote the ledger was no longer the hand that wrote the archive."}], "n": 21}, {"title": "The Shrine That Stopped Answering", "kind": "side", "src": "s_shrine", "n": 22, "part": "Book Three. The Recruited", "blocks": [{"p": "Word had reached Rei that a shrine keeper in the north of the ward was frightened and wanted someone to come. Rei walked to Higashi-Jūjō station in the noon heat and took the Keihin-Tōhoku line one stop north to Akabane. The train was nearly empty at that hour. An old woman sat with a bunch of chrysanthemums across her lap, wrapped in newspaper, the kind of flowers people take to graves. She held them carefully, the way someone holds a sleeping baby."}, {"p": "At Akabane he came out into a wall of heat. The station square was loud with shop music and the crossing signal's chirping. He left it behind and climbed into the quiet streets on the hill, where the houses were older and the lanes turned for no reason. Paper lanterns hung under the eaves, and in one doorway a family had set out a little horse made from a cucumber and four wooden sticks. At Obon some families make these. The fast cucumber horse is to bring the dead home quickly. A slow eggplant cow takes them back at the end of the week, so they can stay a little longer. Rei had made them as a child, with Gin."}, {"img": "place_shinto.jpg", "cap": "The Hozumi fox shrine in upper Kita"}, {"p": "The shrine was at the top of a flight of worn stone steps. The old man was sweeping when Rei arrived, and that was the first wrong thing. The stones were already clean. He swept them anyway, slowly, back and forth over the same place, like a man keeping his hands busy so that they would not shake."}, {"img": "char_miyamoto-kazuo.jpg", "cap": "Miyamoto Kazuo, head priest of the Hozumi shrine"}, {"p": "His name was Miyamoto Kazuo. He was sixty-six, the head priest, a kannushi, which is what Shinto calls the man who looks after a shrine and speaks for it to its god. He wore white and indigo, and his white hair was tied in a small topknot. He told Rei who he was before Rei could ask. His father had kept this shrine before him, and his grandfather before that. His family had protected this ground for two hundred years. Every Obon someone in his family renewed the wards that ringed the grounds. He recited it the way a man reads out the deed to a house he is afraid is no longer his."}, {"p": "The shrine itself was almost nothing. It was a fox shrine, an Inari shrine. Inari is the Shinto god of rice and good harvests, and foxes are its messengers, so two stone foxes sat at the entrance with lichen grown into their grins. The gate was a torii, the simple two-post gateway that marks the line where the ordinary world ends and the god's ground begins. Its vermilion paint had faded to the colour of dried blood. An old camphor tree stood beside it, and Miyamoto bowed to the tree as he passed, out of a habit older than thought. Under a little roof stood a stone basin of still water, where visitors rinse their hands and mouths before they pray. A three-coloured cat sat on the rim of the basin and stared into it without drinking. It was the kind of place the city forgets so completely that the forgetting becomes a kind of protection."}, {"p": "Miyamoto would not let Rei near the basin until he had said what he had called him to say. He said it badly, in pieces, looking at the broom. For two hundred years the water in that basin had held the lanterns. Every Obon the neighbourhood strung paper lights along the lane, and every Obon the basin caught their reflection and held them, doubled and trembling on the surface. That reflection, Miyamoto said, was how a keeper knew the boundary was holding. A shrine that reflects is a shrine that is still listening."}, {"p": "\"This week the lanterns are lit,\" he said. \"The lane is strung. And the water lies there flat and black and holds nothing. No lights. No roof beam. When you lean over it, your own face is not there.\""}, {"p": "Both of Rei's registers came up at that, the low one and the searching one, slow and certain. For once they agreed. All week he had been learning one particular kind of wrongness, the wrongness of a thing that had stopped answering. Gin's flat. The address that was in no file. The cases closed too cleanly. He recognised the same shape in an old man's basin."}, {"p": "Miyamoto leaned the broom against the gate and took a packet of cigarettes from his sleeve. His hands shook too much for the match. Without thinking, Rei took out his lighter and held the flame for him. The old man drew on the cigarette, nodded his thanks, and seemed to steady."}, {"p": "\"I am too old to read what has gone wrong,\" he said, \"and too frightened to leave it unread. I sent word to the Division. The Division sent a form. I sent word to the temple. The temple sent prayers.\" He looked at Rei properly for the first time. \"You are the only one who came up the steps instead of mailing something down them.\""}, {"p": "Rei asked him what the wards actually were. He knew how the CVD did it, with glyphs and grades and paperwork. This was not that."}, {"p": "\"We do not fight what comes,\" Miyamoto said. \"My father used to say Shinto does not exorcise so much as it tidies. Every night I walk the grounds. The lamps, the salt at the gate at dusk, the gate itself. I keep the place clean and named and in good repair, and things that should not cross find it does not want them. Once a year, at Obon, the cords are re-tied, and the water is asked to keep watching. That is all. It has been enough for two hundred years.\" He blew smoke toward the camphor tree, then apologised to it. \"It has been enough until this week.\""}, {"p": "Up close, the water was worse than Miyamoto had described. He had described an empty thing, and empty is at least clean. This was not clean. The basin was full to the brim and perfectly still. No wind reached it under the little roof. When Rei leaned over it, the lanterns behind him did not appear, and neither did he. But the surface was not blank. Something was moving under it, very slowly, like something turning over far down in deep water. The basin was only a hand deep."}, {"p": "The cat stepped down from the rim and walked away from him with its tail low."}, {"p": "Rei put two fingers on the stone rim, not on the water, and let the read come. It came wrong. A shrine basin should taste of two hundred Obons of cupped hands and rinsed mouths and small honest wishes. This one tasted of all that. But underneath it, there was a single clean cut. Something that had been woven together had been carefully unwoven, by someone who knew exactly which thread to lift."}, {"p": "He crouched and looked longer than was wise. The longer he looked, the surer both registers became. The water was not failing to reflect him. It was choosing not to. Long ago, a boundary had been set here between the lane and whatever the lane was built to forget. The reflection kept that boundary tight. Every day the water proved that the world on this side was solid enough to throw an image back. Take away that proof, and the boundary goes slack."}, {"p": "Three steps behind him, the lanterns burned gold along the lane. At the rim of this stone, their light simply stopped, as if the basin had been excused from the evening. Someone had set it apart, quietly, so that it could be used without anyone in the neighbourhood noticing."}, {"p": "When he took his fingers off the rim, the movement under the surface settled, the way water settles after a fish has turned. For one moment before it went flat, the black water showed him a face after all. It was not his. It was a calm, pleasant, entirely reasonable face. He had been learning its shape all week without ever being allowed to see it clearly. Then the water lay still and gave him nothing."}, {"p": "\"Did you see anything?\" Miyamoto asked, in the small voice of a man who already knew."}, {"p": "The honest answer was that Rei had seen the Healer. It had dressed itself in a two-hundred-year-old shrine the way a doctor puts on a clean white coat, so that everyone would trust it. He said, \"Yes.\" The lane kept burning. The basin kept its peace, and it was the wrong kind of peace. Someone had arranged it."}, {"p": "The shrine office was a tiny room behind the gate, one tatami mat wide. A thread of cedar smoke rose from a dish in the corner, as it had in this room, Miyamoto said, for as long as anyone remembered. The book of renewals was older than the light bulb and older than the table. It was really many books, bound by hand and stacked in a pile wrapped in cloth. Each one held a generation of Obons, written in the small cramped hand of whoever had held the broom that century."}, {"p": "Miyamoto turned to this year's page slowly, like a man opening a wound to show the doctor where it hurts. The entry was there, but in a handwriting Rei did not know. It was not Miyamoto's and not his family's. It was a clean modern script. In the place where the keeper records who renewed the wards, it had written one phrase. <em>A friend assisted.</em>"}, {"p": "\"He came in the spring,\" Miyamoto said, looking at the stranger's writing in his family's book. \"A soft-spoken man. A healer, he said. He had heard the old keeper's hands were failing.\" He held up his own hands and let Rei see the tremor. \"I have no one yet to pass the duty to. He knew that too. He offered, for nothing, for the simple rightness of it, to help me renew the work I could no longer manage alone.\" His voice broke a little, the way a voice does when a kindness turns out to have been a knife. \"He was so very kind. He asked only that I let him tie the last knot himself. The last knot, he said, is the one that matters.\""}, {"p": "Rei read the entry, and both registers went cold and quiet. He knew this work. The same neatness. The same gift, offered into exactly the gap a person is most ashamed of. It was all through Gin's ledger of favours. It was everywhere he had been this week, wherever Yushiro the Healer had reached into someone's worst small fear and changed one thread while they cried with gratitude."}, {"p": "The wards had not been broken. They had been re-tied, expertly, by a guest the keeper had invited in himself, because he was ashamed of his failing hands. And the last knot, the one that held the reflection and kept the boundary listening, had been tied to give way at a planned time. This week. Obon, when the veil over all of Kita was at its thinnest, and every shrine in the district was straining to hold its own small piece of the wall. Pull one keeper's stone loose at a time like this and there is no flood. There is a draught. One clean current of cold air through one forgotten shrine, feeding the larger thinning the Healer had been working toward all along."}]}, {"title": "The Last Knot", "kind": "side", "src": "s_shrine#2", "n": 23, "part": "Book Three. The Recruited", "blocks": [{"p": "Miyamoto wanted to know what Rei would do. Underneath that, he wanted to know what he himself had done. Had his weak hands caused this? Or would it have come anyway? There was no answer Rei could give that would return to him that spring morning, when he had been so grateful to be helped."}, {"p": "Rei went back out and stood at the basin with his hands in his pockets. The temptation came quietly, and it spoke in a reasonable voice. The Healer did not know that he had found this. If he left the knot as it was, the shrine would go on failing on schedule. The small cold current through the water would show him exactly where the larger thinning ran and what it was for. He could sit on these steps every night until Obon ended and take notes. It would cost an old man his basin. It would cost a boundary his family had kept for two hundred years. And it would cost whatever else came through the draught while Rei stood beside it with a pencil."}, {"img": "med_benten.jpg", "cap": "The three-coloured cat of the shrine"}, {"p": "The cat came and sat beside his foot and looked up at him. Rei thought about Gin, who would have closed it without a second thought. Gin sat up all night with widows and wrote the case files afterwards. The voice that wanted the window left open sounded sensible, and that was exactly what frightened him. It was the Healer's kind of sum. A hole in a neighbourhood's wall, left open because the hole was useful. He would not learn to think like that."}, {"p": "\"We close it,\" he said. \"But first I need to read where it goes.\""}, {"p": "Miyamoto did not argue. He fetched the cords from the office. They were shimenawa, the sacred ropes Shinto uses to mark a holy place, made of twisted hemp with folded white paper strips hanging from them. He showed Rei the knots his father had taught him. Rei went back to the basin and put his whole hand flat on the stone this time. He let the current run through him for as long as he could bear it. It was cold and steady and it ran one way. North. Toward the river, and past it, toward the old ground beyond the freight line where the city's maps went vague. He held that direction in his mind like a compass bearing. Then he took his hand away."}, {"p": "The re-tying took the rest of the afternoon. Miyamoto's hands knew every knot and could not hold any of them steady. Rei's hands were steady and knew nothing. So Rei held the cords tight and the old man tied. When they reached the last knot, Rei ran his fingertips along the cord and found the place where the stranger had worked. One strand had been lifted loose and laid back in place so that it would slip. He showed Miyamoto. Miyamoto bowed to the camphor tree, then to the basin. Then he bent and tied the knot the way his father had tied it. It was a plain knot. It did not slip."}, {"p": "Evening came, as it always did on the third night of Obon. The neighbourhood came out. Old women walked by in yukata printed with blue morning glories and white waves. Children had fox masks from the festival stalls pushed up on their foreheads. They filed past the little shrine, clapped twice and bowed, and none of them felt the cold seam Rei had spent the day standing inside. That was the mercy and the horror of a forgotten shrine. When it failed, the only people who could not see it fail were the people it protected."}, {"p": "Then Miyamoto lit the last lantern at the gate, and the basin caught it. The light came back doubled and trembling on the water. Then the next lantern, and the next, until the whole gold string of the lane lay under the little roof the way it had lain for two hundred summers. The three-coloured cat jumped onto the rim, looked into the water, and drank."}, {"p": "Miyamoto found Rei at the gate and read his face the way old keepers read water. What passed between them was not exactly gratitude. Rei had not given him back the spring. Both of them knew that the kind stranger would come again, to some other pair of failing hands, with the same soft offer and the same patient knot. But the old man took Rei's hand in both of his. His hands were dry and light as paper."}, {"p": "\"My father used to say the shrine does not protect the neighbourhood from the thing on the other side,\" he said. \"It protects the thing on the other side from the neighbourhood's forgetting. It keeps it small and named and bounded. A forgotten thing grows. A watched thing waits.\""}, {"p": "Rei had brought back the watching. Somewhere across the thinning city, the Healer had lost one quiet draught. It was still one stone further along a wall it meant to bring down by the end of Obon. But Rei had something now that he had not had that morning. He knew which way the draught had been running."}, {"break": "Jūjō Inari"}, {"p": "He walked home the long way. He did not want a train. He went south down the hill and through the back streets, past small factories with their shutters down for the holiday, past a sentō, a public bath, with its tall chimney and the smell of hot water and soap drifting from its door. On a corner a group of men in undershirts sat on beer crates outside a liquor shop, talking quietly, fanning themselves. A vending machine glowed white in the dark beside them, and a moth beat against its glass. Every few streets there was a little altar at a doorway, a candle in a glass and a plate of fruit, set out for someone's dead. Rei had never noticed how many there were. This year he saw every one."}, {"p": "Near home he passed the small Inari shrine a hundred metres north of Gin's building. He had walked past it a thousand times without looking. Tonight he stopped. Gin had written about this shrine in the district pages of the archive, and Rei remembered the note now."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's archive. Kita field reference", "lines": ["Jujo Shrine. Small Inari shrine, 100m north of the archive building.", "Continuous traditional maintenance has produced genuine veil-structure function... a stabilized point, not a thin spot.", "Entity activity in 150m radius is measurably lower. I used this for documentation work requiring minimal ambient disruption."]}}, {"p": "So that was part of why Gin did his writing at home. He had been sitting inside the shrine's quiet."}, {"img": "char_ishikawa-tsuneko.jpg", "cap": "Ishikawa Tsuneko, caretaker of Jūjō Inari"}, {"p": "An old woman was refilling the lamp beside the fox statues. She was small and straight-backed, in a grey work apron, and she watched Rei come under the torii without any warmth at all. He did not speak first. He rinsed his hands at the basin. Then he bowed twice, clapped twice and bowed once more, the way he had been taught as a boy. Only then did he turn to her."}, {"p": "Her name was Ishikawa Tsuneko. She was seventy-one. She had kept Jūjō Inari for thirty-one years. Her grandmother had kept it before her, and her family had done the work here since the Meiji era, the years at the end of the nineteenth century when Japan first opened to the modern world. She did not call it ward work or symbol work. She called it <em>the duties</em>. Which nights needed the salt. Which corners needed the lamp. Which visitors were carrying something they should not have brought up the steps. She did not think of herself as a practitioner of anything. She only knew that the shrine worked."}, {"p": "Rei looked into her basin. The lanterns from the street were there, small and doubled and steady."}, {"p": "\"Has anyone offered to help you with the duties?\" he asked. \"This year. Someone kind.\""}, {"p": "She gave him a long, measuring look. \"The government has asked to help me three times,\" she said. \"I said no. The other ones, the ones without an office, have asked twice. I said no to them too.\" She put her hand on the rim of the basin. When she lifted it, Rei felt that the stone was warmer than the evening, as if she had left something of herself in it. \"I do not let anyone else do the duties. A thing you hand to a stranger is not your duty any more.\""}, {"p": "Gin's archive said that the people without an office, the Grid, had decided to leave her alone. Her work was the reason this corner of Jūjō had stayed steady for decades, and if they interfered with her they would break something they relied on. Rei suspected she knew that. He suspected she would never say so."}, {"p": "He thanked her and went down the steps as drums started somewhere toward the river. It was the slow, heavy rhythm of a bon odori, the Obon dance, when the neighbourhood dances in a circle under lanterns to welcome the dead home for their three nights and then send them off again. The sound found the tight thing in Rei that had been pulled hard all day, and loosened it a little. Down at the embankment the first paper lantern boats were going onto the black water, each one carrying a small candle out for the dead. The dead were very close this week. And they were getting closer by exactly as many stones as the Healer could talk a grieving keeper into loosening. Tonight there was one stone fewer. And there were two old people in Kita who would not hand their duty to anyone."}]}, {"title": "The Inverted God", "kind": "main", "src": "m_inverted", "blocks": [{"p": "The second aftertaste from the widow's door would not leave him alone. Rei went back to Adachi the next morning and stood at her threshold until he had it again, faint and cold. Then he followed it."}, {"p": "It was slow work. A trail like that does not run along roads. It ran along the places where the veil had been scraped, and those places were scattered. He rode a few stops, got off, walked until the taste grew weak, then turned and found it again a street away. By noon he had crossed three wards. He drank barley tea from a vending machine on a station platform and pressed the cold can to his neck. On one train a mother sat with two small boys in matching caps, all three of them asleep against each other, their heads rolling together at every curve. On a footbridge an old man in a straw hat had stopped to look down at the tracks, holding a shopping bag full of fruit. Ordinary people, going home for Obon. None of them could feel the thing Rei was following through their streets."}, {"p": "And slowly, the trail taught him something the Healer's threads never could. There was another worker in this city. Gin's mercy closed wounds and tied the grateful into one careful tally. This other thing did not close anything. It tore."}, {"break": "The underpass by the freight line"}, {"img": "place_underpass.jpg", "cap": "The long pedestrian underpass beneath the freight line"}, {"p": "The trail ended at a long pedestrian underpass beneath a station, where the freight line crossed overhead. Every few minutes a long goods train rumbled above, and the whole tunnel hummed with it. The low end flooded whenever it rained, and the tiles there were stained up to the height of a child. Rei knew this place from Gin's pages. He had read them so often that he could recite them."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's archive. Disturbed Areas. The Station Underpass", "lines": ["Walked the freight underpass again. Still wrong on the 11th. Did not stay. G.U.", "THIN SPOT. A migrating thin spot. Worst in the cold.", "A place where the membrane has worn through... and the wear migrates with the district's mood.", "A Grid handler keeps a dog at the south mouth most nights. The CVD flags it. The Grid actually watches it.", "Everything is more visible to everyone here... and entities see you regardless of rank. Carry less stability in than you think you need.", "I have crossed easiest, and been noticed worst, in that underpass. The two turn out to be the same fact wearing different coats."]}}, {"p": "The dog was there at the south mouth. It was a heavy brown animal lying on a folded blanket with its chin on its paws. It lifted its head when Rei came down the steps and watched him without moving. It did not follow him in. Rei understood that as the warning it was."}, {"p": "It was August, not winter, so the underpass should have been at its quietest. It was not quiet. The veil here was not being soothed. It was being worn down on purpose, scraped thinner and thinner, the way a person files a lock until the bolt gives way. Gin's seams felt tight and tidy, like a bandage pulled hard. These felt like the second before a window breaks. Rei's gift pulled back so hard that he tasted copper in his mouth."}, {"p": "Under the scraping there was something older, and he recognised it from Gin's book too. It was heavy, and it was not thinking. The 2011 dead had never been properly seen home, and their grief had gathered in the low places of the city the way rainwater gathers. Gin had written about exactly this."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "CVD Entity Classification Reference. Chapter 11. Suffering Beings", "lines": ["A suffering being is not an entity in the conventional sense. It is the residue of an intelligence... the emotional material that remained after the self dissolved at crossing, concentrated in the location where the dissolution occurred.", "It is not thinking. It is not choosing. It is not directing its effects at anyone. It simply weighs what it weighs, and if you are in the space, you feel the weight.", "The person is gone. Demons retain intelligence. Spirits retain personality. Suffering beings have no continuity with the person they came from.", "It cannot be reasoned with, communicated with, redirected, or resolved through relationship. It can only be cleared, or managed. This is not exorcism. There is nothing to remove. There is something to process.", "Margin, G.U. A suffering being is grief that found no floor to land on. After 2011 there was a great deal of grief and nowhere near enough floor. Be gentle when you meet one. It was a person's love before it was your problem."]}}, {"p": "That was what the boy in Gin's ledger had been hearing in the gutters when it rained. Not voices. Weight. And now someone was using that weight like sandpaper against the wall."}, {"break": "A back room the Grid kept that month"}, {"img": "ch8_grid.jpg", "cap": "A back room the Grid kept that month"}, {"p": "The Grid had a name for the one doing it. Rei got it that evening, in a back room above a shuttered shop. The Grid was using the room that month and would not be using it the next. He was there on a vouch. One of Gin's people, someone who owed his brother the dangerous kind of debt, had vouched for him to a man, and the man had sent him here."}, {"p": "He had walked the last part. The street was narrow and dark, lined with closed shutters and small bars that had not opened yet. A ramen shop by the tracks was the only bright window, and a cook stood outside it smoking in a white cap, watching the trains go by. Rei found the door the man had described, between a shuttered shop and a stairwell, and went up."}, {"p": "Nobody in the room was in charge. People came up the stairs, put something down or picked something up, and left again. An electric fan turned its head from side to side and moved the hot air around without cooling it. A courier sat on an upturned crate with a kit bag on her knees. The bag had come, she said, through three pairs of hands before hers. It would go through one more before it reached the person who needed it."}, {"p": "She gave Rei the name over her shoulder while she checked the bag's straps. She spoke in the hushed way people use for things they hope are exaggerated."}, {"p": "\"They call it the Inverted God,\" she said. \"Or its aspirant does. It isn't a god yet. Its mouth is a boy called Kaito. He was a medium before he was a vessel, and now there's not much left of him but the mouth.\" She pulled a strap tight. \"The thing itself doesn't come to the pools. It sits somewhere else, in somebody else, and talks through him.\""}, {"p": "Rei understood the words. A medium is a person the dead can speak through, for a little while, by permission. A vessel is a body that something else has moved into and kept. Kaito had been the first. Now he was the second."}, {"p": "\"What does it want?\""}, {"p": "\"It says the veil is a muzzle the living strapped onto the dead. It says tearing it isn't a catastrophe, it's emancipation.\" She looked at him properly then. \"The old people say there's something at the centre that holds the wall shut. This one wants to be the opposite of that. It wants to tear the wall open and rule whatever pours through. It doesn't want a seat at the table. It wants to turn the table over and salt the ground where it stood.\""}, {"p": "By every measure the factions had, she said, it was the bigger horror in this city by far. Then she put the bag on her shoulder and went down the stairs, and someone else came up."}, {"p": "That was exactly the problem. Rei felt it close around him in that hot back room. The moment there was a bigger monster, every faction with a grade and a grievance would turn toward it. And the quiet, reasonable, mercy-making thing wearing his brother would start to look, by comparison, like the lesser evil. The manageable one. The one a sensible person might even join forces with against the worse one. The Healer had not summoned the Inverted God. But in all its long patient life it had never been given a better gift than a rival so monstrous that it made the Healer look like a saviour."}, {"break": "The drained pool"}, {"img": "char_kaito.jpg", "cap": "Kaito, the courier who preaches in the drained pool"}, {"p": "Kaito preached in a drained public swimming pool in the dead hour after midnight. Rei walked there through streets that had gone quiet at last. The Obon lanterns were still lit on some houses, and a konbini on the corner glowed like an aquarium, a single clerk inside reading a magazine. The pool had been closed since the summer before. A chain hung loose on the gate. The blue paint on its walls had cracked into shapes like the maps of countries that did not exist."}, {"p": "About forty people stood on the tiled floor of the deep end. They all had the widow's look. Accounted for. Grateful. Leashed. But their leashes ran the other way, toward tearing instead of binding."}, {"p": "Kaito was younger than the fear around him had suggested. He could not have been more than twenty. He stood in the shallow end with his hands loose at his sides. His eyes did not quite follow the people he looked at, as if someone else were doing the looking through them. He did not rant. He grieved, out loud, so precisely that the cicadas outside sounded like applause."}, {"p": "\"They drowned in front of us,\" he said of 2011. \"And we built a wall so we would not have to keep hearing them, and we called the wall mercy.\" He let the words go out over the old painted lane lines. \"Since 2009 the wall here in Kita has been failing. Since 2011 it has been failing faster. They tell you that is the danger. I tell you it is the dead, knocking. I am only proposing we stop lying about what the wall is.\""}, {"p": "Forty heads nodded. Rei's own head wanted to nod too, and he hated it for that."}, {"p": "There it was again. A true thing, set at a slight, deadly angle. Kaito was right that the veil was partly a wall the living had built so that they would not hear the dead. He was wrong, terribly and completely wrong, about what happens to a city when that wall comes down. Rei stood at the edge of the empty pool, with the ghost of chlorine still in the air. He understood that he was now holding two arguments, and each held enough truth to drown a person in. His brother's captor was the more patient of the two, and the more polite. And everyone with the power to act was going to mistake politeness for safety."}, {"p": "Kaito found him on the way out. These things always seemed to. He fell into step beside Rei on the cracked path to the gate. He knew Rei's name, which meant the seams had been talking."}, {"p": "\"Your brother and I want opposite things,\" he said, almost kindly. His voice was a young man's voice, but the words had an older weight in them, as if they had been chosen somewhere far away and only delivered here. \"That should comfort you. It won't. You've already worked out that opposite things can want the same Tuesday.\""}, {"p": "He let that sit. Then he stopped at the gate and said, \"When the seat comes vacant, ask yourself who benefits from the wall being thin. It isn't only me.\""}, {"p": "Rei walked home under the lanterns with that sentence sitting in his chest. A taxi went by with its light on, slow, hoping. Two drunk office workers sang a few lines of an old song outside a closed bar and then forgot the rest. At the flat he did not sleep. He took Gin's archive down again and turned to the chapter on the Grid. He had been moving inside it for a week on a stranger's word, and he still did not understand what he had walked into. Gin would have written it down."}], "n": 24, "part": "Book Three. The Recruited"}, {"title": "From Gin's Book. The Grid", "kind": "gin", "src": "gin_17", "blocks": [{"p": "Gin's chapter on the Grid began with a journal note, written between two cases. Rei could hear his brother's tired, pleased voice in every line. It was the only chapter in the archive that Gin said he had looked forward to writing. Rei read it at the kitchen table, with a glass of cold barley tea sweating onto the wood, until the windows went grey."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's preface, Chapter 17. The Grid", "lines": ["No hierarchy. No building. No official anything.", "The Grid is not an organization. There is no Grid leadership. There are no Grid membership fees or registration requirements or official anything.", "The Grid works because the people in it learned the same things from the same hard lessons and arrived at the same conclusions independently. What looks like coordination from outside is actually convergence.", "This is the part of this work that I trust most completely and have described least.", "G.U. Keys on the hook."]}}, {"p": "Rei had used the word Grid all week without really knowing what it meant. Gin's next page explained it more plainly than anyone in the back room had."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "Grid Intelligence Reference. What The Grid Is", "lines": ["A network of people who owe each other things and who have collectively decided that owing each other things is preferable to owing the CVD things.", "I have found this network more reliable in field emergencies than the official support system approximately seventy percent of the time.", "Joining the Grid is not a process. Someone in the Grid vouches for you to another person in the Grid. That person gives you one thing, a contact, an address, a piece of information you needed. If you handle it correctly, you get another contact.", "If you handle it incorrectly, the network closes around you and the contacts stop. That is the entirety of the Grid's enforcement mechanism."]}}, {"p": "Rei thought of the room above the shuttered shop, and of the man who had given him that address, and of the person who had vouched for him to the man. One contact, then another. He had been handling it, he realised, without knowing there was anything to handle."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Grid Culture. The Unwritten Rules That Everyone Knows", "lines": ["Show up. If you say you will be somewhere, be there. Unreliability in entity work produces casualties rather than inconveniences.", "Don't bring heat. Do not draw CVD attention to Grid locations, contacts or supply chains. Do not compromise a safe house.", "Share what matters. Information that could help someone avoid harm is shared without being asked. The network is not gossip. It is operational infrastructure.", "Debt and reciprocity. When someone helps you, you help someone else. The timeline is flexible. The obligation is not.", "Protect practitioners in danger. You will not be left alone in the situations the official framework cannot or will not address. This promise is kept imperfectly and kept more often than any institutional equivalent."]}}, {"p": "So the Grid had no rulebook, but it had rules. Turn up. Keep the CVD away. Tell people what they need to know. Pay back a kindness by doing one for someone else. And never leave a practitioner alone in the dark. Everyone in it seemed to have learned the same five things the hard way."}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Margin, G.U.", "lines": ["The extraction in Chapter 8. A Channel rank Psychometabolic the CVD had filed an acquisition order on. Eleven days, four practitioners, clean exit. No authorization, no cover, no pay.", "The CVD will tell you the Grid is disorganised. It is not disorganised. It is organised the way water is... around obstacles, by relationship, with no centre to seize. A thing it cannot decapitate, because it has no head."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the fourth rule again. <em>The timeline is flexible. The obligation is not.</em> It was the same thing the widow had said without knowing it. Debt. But the Grid's debt moved. It went from one person to the next and never came back to the same hand. The Healer's debt ran only one way, toward the Healer."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "The Infrastructure. Safe Houses", "lines": ["Locations maintained by Grid-adjacent practitioners or civilians who understand the work. From a spare room to a dedicated space.", "Standard warding on the entrance, at minimum. A practitioner who built the safe house typically inks at least the threshold. A supply cache, food, water, first aid for physical and veil-adjacent injury. Some form of communication that does not run through institutional channels. The contact information for the next node.", "Finding them. By vouch only. A safe house compromised is a safe house gone.", "In this district. Four safe houses documented in my Grid network file. Locations, warding status, supply levels, and contact practitioners are in the Grid intelligence file on page 147. I check them every three months.", "The safe house I use most often is run by a retired practitioner who left the CVD registration system twelve years ago. She is 61, older than almost any active practitioner I know, and has been doing this for forty years without institutional support. Her ward configurations are the most sophisticated I have encountered outside Tower Family property. I have learned more about practical symbol work from her than from any formal source.", "Her name is not in this archive. The file on page 147 has the names."]}}, {"p": "Rei turned to page 147. He knew it already. It was where Gin had written down his four nodes, the people his part of the Grid ran through. The baker. The clinic behind the blue curtain. The café that kept Tuesday evenings free. The shrine room behind the Korean restaurant. He had read the page a dozen times this week. This time he read the bottom of it. Under the four names was a short note in Gin's smallest handwriting. It described a place in words instead of giving an address, the way someone gives directions to a friend who already knows the neighbourhood. It did not name the place. It assumed the reader would know how to find it. Rei read it four times and still did not know. He marked the page with the corner of a konbini receipt and kept going."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "Supply Networks. The Black Market for Practitioner Materials", "lines": ["Prepared binding materials, silver-adjacent ammunition, stability supplies the CVD controls, ink and substrate for tattoo configurations, materials for high-grade seals, modified medical supplies for Psychometabolic field work.", "No Grid warehouse. The Psychometabolic who prepares field medical kits sends them through three contacts before they reach the practitioner who needs them. The distribution is inefficient by design. Each link in the chain knows only the link immediately before and after it.", "Payment. A mix of money and reciprocal obligation. Practitioners who can pay do, and practitioners who cannot are carried by the network until they can.", "The ammunition preparation contact in this district. The silver rounds they produce are the most consistent I have used in six years. I will not document their name or location here."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "The Information Network", "lines": ["Person to person, weighted by trust. No quality control except the reputation of the person sharing.", "The Grid's information network has no archive. When a practitioner with significant knowledge retires, relocates, or dies, their accumulated intelligence goes with them.", "The Satō case is a good example. Eleven years of accumulated entity intelligence about the Kita district entity, held in one practitioner's memory and then in a fragment in an absorber.", "This archive is, among other things, an attempt to address the information preservation problem for my own accumulated knowledge. I built it for one person."]}}, {"p": "<em>I built it for one person.</em> Rei put his hand flat on the page. The kitchen was very quiet. Outside, the first train of the morning went by, and the glass in the window shook a little in its frame."}, {"img": "ch17_whojoins.jpg", "cap": "Who joins the Grid, and why"}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Who Joins The Grid And Why", "lines": ["Unregistered practitioners, who did not know registration was available or chose not to register.", "Registration gap practitioners, whose registered rank does not match what they can actually do. Some suppressed on registration to stay out of the pipeline.", "CVD graduates who left.", "Specialised practitioners, such as high-grade mediums and people with entity relationships the CVD would call violations.", "I am a registered CVD practitioner who is also Grid-affiliated. My suppression keeps the extent of my Grid work invisible. I am probably not even unusual."]}}, {"img": "ch16_cultivation.jpg", "cap": "How the Grid teaches, one person to another"}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "How the Grid teaches. G.U.", "lines": ["The Grid does not recruit. Nobody hands you the Grid. You are let into it one person at a time, by being the sort of person someone is willing to be wrong about.", "It teaches through direct teaching relationships. Its symbol work is held in practitioners, not documents. Its greatest advantage over the CVD is post-possession recovery. It has handled far more possession recoveries than the official record shows, because most of them were never reported.", "My first Grid contact was a practitioner I met at a possession case the CVD had closed as resolved when it was not resolved. She was doing the actual clearance work after the official case was closed. She did not explain what the Grid was. She just worked the case and let me help. Three months later she called me for a case I was needed on. That was the beginning of six years of network.", "The vouch is a serious thing. When someone vouches for you, they extend their own reliability to cover yours. If you fail, they fail with you.", "Find your people inside it. The ones who will sit with you after the difficult work. They are in there. Find them."]}}, {"p": "Rei closed the archive with the receipt still marking page 147. <em>Post-possession recovery.</em> That meant bringing a person back after something had lived inside them. Somewhere in this city were people who had brought someone back from where Gin was now, and who had never written it down. And somewhere, in the place on that page, there was a woman of sixty-one with the best ward work in Tokyo, who had taught his brother how to draw a threshold. Rei did not know her name. But he knew now that Gin had left him a way to find it."}], "n": 24}, {"title": "The Two-Demon Trap", "kind": "main", "src": "m_trap", "blocks": [{"img": "map_tokyo.jpg", "cap": "Tokyo, with the two patterns laid over one map"}, {"p": "Rei laid the two maps over each other on the kitchen floor. It was the only surface in the flat big enough. One map showed the Healer's thirty-one bound favours, each one a pencil dot on a ward map. The other showed the Inverted God's scraped and thinning seams. He had traced those in red, from what he had felt at the underpass and at the widow's door. Under both of them he spread the pages Gin had headed <em>Disturbed Areas</em>. The Division's own map of Kita, Gin had written, was accurate and useless. It showed the streets. The real map was the one a person built with their own feet, over years."}, {"p": "With the three layers together, the city came into focus as a single design, the way two halves of a torn photograph become one face when they are put together. Gin's mercy clustered in the wards where the veil was strongest. Kaito's tearing was concentrated where it was already weakest. Rei could name those weak places now from his brother's pages. The station underpass. The freight-yard blocks. The old market arcade."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's archive. Disturbed Areas. The Freight-Yard Blocks", "lines": ["ACCUMULATION. Condemned housing by the rail yard. Forty years of bad death, and the walls remember.", "You feel the register thicken on the stairs, and by the third floor it is genuinely hard to tell your own mood from the building's. Nothing in there is malevolent. It is simply too much ended life pressed into too little space.", "Danger. Low ambient, high cumulative. An hour inside at a poor floor is a real possession risk. The density does the hunting for whatever is there.", "I clear a corridor there twice a year and I am always a little worse for a week after. Some places do not forgive being read. G.U."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Suffering Beings. Environmental Effect Scale, By Duration", "lines": ["Recent, months. Subtle. No structural membrane alteration. Clearance is straightforward.", "Established, years. Occupants describe a feeling in the space they cannot explain. Cold spots, strange acoustics, animals behaving oddly.", "Deep, decades. The membrane in the location has been structurally altered. The space actively generates the register. Surface clearance produces temporary relief only.", "Ancient, centuries. The location has become the accumulation. Management, not clearance. Some places are simply not habitable for long."]}}, {"p": "The table worked by time. The longer grief sat in one place, the deeper it changed the place. Forty years put the freight-yard blocks in the third row. Deep. A place that made its own grief now, with or without new dead to feed it. Kaito's people did not have to make the wall thin there. They only had to keep scraping."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's archive. Disturbed Areas. The Old Market Arcade", "lines": ["CROSSING POINT. A shuttered arcade. The crossings are in the drains.", "A fixed set of crossing points... doors, sills, storm drains... worn into the place by decades of use.", "When it is bad, the Division sends Kurosawa Shou. That alone tells you how seriously to take the bad nights.", "Things come through on their own schedule, not yours. The drains are the worst of it. Never stand over one you have not warded.", "It is the one ground in Kita where I have felt something on the far side waiting, specifically, for a door to open."]}}, {"p": "And between the two patterns, in Kita ward, at the wound 2011 had never closed, they did not collide. They fitted together, like two gears. One thinned the wall until it broke. The other stood ready, with all its grateful hands, to do something <em>careful</em> at the moment it broke. To close a seat. To take a seat. To turn a disaster into the one duty a demon of the Duty register had waited forty years to be allowed to carry out."}, {"p": "This was the trap. And Rei finally saw that it had never been set for him. He was not the prey. He was the witness it could afford. A witness with no grade, whose love for his brother made him a perfect leash and a perfect alibi at the same time. The factions would rush to stop the obvious monster. The obvious monster would let them. The Healer would even help them, and be so reasonable about it. And when the wall finally went, and his brother's captor stepped through the gap into the Thirteenth Seat, every grade in the city would have spent its strength on the wrong demon. For one terrible, clean moment, they would feel that they had won."}, {"break": "The kit by the door"}, {"img": "tools_kit.jpg", "cap": "Gin's field kit, salt, silver and paper wards"}, {"p": "He could not sit in the flat with it. Before he went out, he took Gin's field kit down from its hook in the genkan, where it had hung since the fourteenth. It was an ordinary grey shoulder bag. Gin had written that a good kit should look like the bag of someone going camping, and nothing else. This one did. Inside were salt in a sealed tub, iron filings in a separate sealed jar, a small cloth pouch of silver, a flat case of folded paper glyphs, a notebook, a pen that worked and a twist of cedar ash. In the front pocket on the left side, wrapped in a handkerchief, were three small glass vials. Sealed. Unbroken."}, {"p": "Rei knew what they were, because Gin's page on field materials had told him he would find them."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Primary Field Materials. What They Do", "lines": ["Salt. An unbroken line is a barrier. Spectral spirits, suffering beings and Class E demons are fully blocked. Class B can break through and arrives destabilised. Class A and above crosses, but its signature spikes. 500g covers a room. Rain dissolves an outdoor line in 20 minutes. Carry two containers minimum.", "Iron, cast or wrought only. Steel processing removes what disrupts the veil layer. A steel knife does nothing. A cast iron skillet works. A wrought iron nail works. Iron filings on a threshold with a salt line make a door trap that damages what forces through.", "Silver. Effect scales with fusion depth, how old and embedded the entity is. Sterling, 92.5% minimum. Silver-plated objects do not work.", "Running water. Most entities cannot hold together crossing moving water. The Kanda and Ara rivers function as natural entity movement barriers in Kita. Drainage channels do not count.", "Cedar ash. Burned in a space, each hour cuts ambient contamination by about 10%. Mixed with water, it is the paste base for the Purification Seal. Rubbed on an object, it clears mild contamination before a read.", "Sulfur. Detection only. Placed at an entry point, it oxidises slightly when a demon passes. The trace is readable for about two hours. Not useful against experienced Class B demons. They learn to suppress the trace.", "Gin's kit. Three sealed vials, small glass. Left side, front pocket of the field kit. Intact. Rei will find them."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Margin, under Silver", "lines": ["The Kita demon specifically. The entity is old and Arbiter-grade, so the fusion already runs deep. It cannot fully suppress its reaction to silver. Useful for testing suspected possession without announcing that you know.", "The Kita demon has been operating for 18 months. Assume trace suppression is active."]}}, {"p": "Eighteen months. Gin had known for that long. He had known the thing was here, in his own district, and he had written the numbers down as calmly as he wrote the price of salt."}, {"p": "Rei opened the silver pouch. Inside were four old coins, heavy for their size. They were the kind antique shops sell as pre-decimal, for the silver in them. He took one and put it in his trouser pocket, against his leg, where Gin's cold key had been. If he ever stood in the same room as his brother again, he would find a way to put that coin in Gin's hand. And he would watch for what the thing inside him could not hide."}, {"break": "The riverside memorial"}, {"p": "He went to the river, because Gin's page said running water was a wall. He wanted to stand somewhere the Healer's threads would have to cross a current to reach him."}, {"p": "He walked. It was late afternoon and the heat had not broken. The asphalt was soft at the edges and the air above it shook. He passed a primary school closed for the summer, its playground empty, a single forgotten shoe on the climbing frame. He passed a small factory where two men in grey overalls sat in the open doorway eating ice lollies, too hot to talk. Near the embankment the streets opened up and the sky got bigger. He climbed the grass bank, and there was the river, wide and brown and slow, with the city low and grey on the far side. A man sat fishing on a folding stool with a radio playing baseball beside him. Two kids rode past on one bicycle, one pedalling and one standing on the back pegs, shouting at each other happily."}, {"p": "The small memorial was a little way along the bank. The city had set a stone here for the 2011 dead. Gin had written about it too, in a different voice from the rest of the pages."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's archive. Disturbed Areas. The Riverside Memorial", "lines": ["GRIEF GROUND. The 11th-of-March ground. It runs thick, and it earns the thickness.", "Every year since 2011 the grief register spikes across the city on that date, and the memorial places run thick with it. This one is ours. Standing in it is less like reading a haunting than like standing in a room where everyone has just stopped crying.", "Who works it. No one, and you should not try to. The shrine and the temple tend it, gently, the way it ought to be tended.", "It will pull on your own grief, and we all carry some. Go to help, or to mourn. Do not go to take notes.", "I have stood there on the 11th more than once, not as an operative, just as a person. It is one of the very few grounds in this book I would ask you to be quiet in. G.U."]}}, {"p": "So Rei was quiet there. He sat on the low wall as the heat slowly went out of the day. Someone had left fresh flowers and a can of coffee at the foot of the stone. As dusk came, people began to come down to the water with paper lanterns, to set them floating and guide the dead home. He did not take notes. He did the sums no one should have to do, and he did them in his head."}, {"p": "To stop the Healer, he might have to let the Healer stop the Inverted God first. To stop the Inverted God, he might have to hand the factions a reasonable ally wearing his brother's face. There was no move on this board that did not cost him something he was still pretending he could keep."}, {"p": "It was the phone that moved first this time. The screen lit up in the dark, and it was Gin's number. Rei knew, before he answered, which of the two voices would be on the line. The trap was sprung now. There was no use any more in pretending to be only his brother. The calm in the voice was total tonight. There was no frightened crack in it, no <em>go home</em>. Only the Healer, speaking through a mouth it had stopped sharing."}, {"p": "\"You've done the arithmetic,\" it said. There was something almost like pride in it. \"Good. Then you understand I am the kinder answer. The boy in the pool would unmake the wall and let this city drown in its own unfinished grief. I would close the wound properly, from the only seat high enough to do it. And the dead would finally be accounted for.\" A pause, perfectly judged. \"Including ours.\""}, {"p": "It let <em>ours</em> land. It knew what it was doing. It had spent a month inside Gin's memories, and it used them like coins."}, {"p": "\"The thing behind that boy cannot be ended from outside,\" it went on, in the same reasonable tone. \"Not on this side of the wall. A thing like that can only be killed here from inside a body. That is why I need your brother's hands for a little while longer. Only a little while.\""}, {"p": "Then Gin's voice dropped half a step. Rei had heard it do that before, on the nights Gin told him a case had gone fine when it had not. \"When I take the Thirteenth Seat I will not need him any more. He can be set down. Intact. Returned, the way you set down a tool when the work is finished. All of this can end with you getting your brother back, whole. You only have to stop pulling on the one thread that might tear the work before it is done.\""}, {"p": "It was the most reasonable offer anyone had ever made him. It was built entirely of true things, and every one of them was tilted at the exact angle that drowns a person."}, {"p": "And in the silence after it, so quietly that Rei almost missed it under the Healer's certainty, there was a sound that was not the Healer. A breath, deep inside the voice, ragged and human. Gin had only a little strength left, and he was choosing to spend it on something other than a word. A single tap against the phone. Once. Then once. Then once."}, {"p": "It was the rhythm of the widow's altar. The knock from the inside. His brother was telling him, in the only language left to him, that he was still in there. That the kind offer was a wall. And that he would rather Rei tore it down than leave him accounted for."}, {"p": "The line went dead. Rei sat by the river with the phone against his ear for a long time. The current went past in the dark, carrying the paper lanterns slowly toward the sea, small yellow lights getting smaller."}, {"p": "When he finally walked home, the streets were full of the smell of incense and the sound of televisions through open windows. At the door of the flat he opened the first of Gin's three vials. He laid a thin yellow line of sulfur across the threshold, and another beside the ledger on the table, the way the page said. If anything came in while he slept, it would leave a mark that lasted two hours. He did not expect it to. He did it anyway, because it was something Gin had left for him to do."}], "n": 25, "part": "Book Three. The Recruited"}, {"title": "The Ledger Grows", "kind": "main", "src": "m_ledgergrows", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei began the way he had been taught to begin with a body. First find out what is fixed. Only then trust anything that moves. Thirty-one entries, he had told himself. Thirty-one leashes in his brother's neat hand. A fixed shape, small enough to walk all the way around in one hot Obon week."}, {"p": "So he copied them out again onto fresh paper. Each name, each ward, each dated mercy. Copying is how a person makes a thing their own, and right now nothing in this city belonged to Rei, least of all the count. It was while he was copying that the floor seemed to tilt under him. Between the widow in Adachi and the pharmacist in Kita, there was now an entry he had not copied two nights ago. A young man in Sumida, soothed of a grief that was eating his sleep. It was dated the day before yesterday, in the same patient handwriting."}, {"p": "He sat very still in the heat, with the electric fan pushing the incense smoke around the room. He made himself say the unbearable thing in plain words. The ledger was not a record. A record is finished. This was a living document, and the hand keeping it had not stopped. It had not even slowed down. It was writing faster now than in the first week after the fourteenth. It was as if Rei's counting pushed on it, and the Healer answered by simply helping more people, more openly, daring him to call mercy a crime. Thirty-one had never been the real number. Thirty-one was only the number on the last day he had looked."}, {"p": "He counted once more, slowly, touching each line with his finger. Thirty-three. Two strangers had been added to his brother's list of the saved since Rei had decided to save Gin. The neatness of it turned his stomach. Every step he took toward Gin, the thing wearing Gin took a step into someone else's life. The rope was growing from both ends at once. Somewhere out there, a man in Sumida was sleeping soundly for the first time in months. He would cry with gratitude if Rei told him why. And Rei was the only person in Tokyo who understood that the man's good night's sleep was a knot, and that Rei was standing at the far end of the rope it was tied to."}, {"p": "He gave in and built a timeline, because the dead had taught him that dates are the one thing grief cannot argue with. He tore a long strip from the back of Gin's archive notebook and laid the favours along it, in the order of the days they were granted. The hot room went quiet around him. It was the kind of quiet that comes when a pattern that has been hiding in plain sight finally stands up."}, {"p": "The favours did not fall evenly. They came in waves. There was a burst of them in the days right after the fourteenth. Then a lull, through the weeks when Gin's employers still thought they were assessing a living man. Then a second wave. It began, he noticed, with a chill that had nothing to do with the fan, in the same week he had started asking questions at Gin's door."}, {"p": "And they were aimed. Marked by ward, they had looked like a scatter across the city, mercy sprayed at random wherever it was needed. Marked by date, the scatter became a slow drift. Each new knot was a little nearer the river. A little nearer the old waterworks land north of Kita, where the official files had nothing and Rei's skin felt too much. The Healer was not helping wherever help was needed. It was helping in a widening spiral, and one edge of the spiral always pointed at the same corner of the map. It was the way a long rope coils as it is paid out toward the post it will be tied to. The favours were not the point, any more than the loops of a rope are the point. They were the length of rope, running out toward the thing at the end."}, {"p": "North again. The same direction the shrine's draught had run."}, {"break": "The Satō case"}, {"p": "Rei went back into the archive for the district's thin spots. He found that Gin had already been walking this way for years. There were four documented thin spots in Kita, Gin wrote. The Kita building, where Gin had first touched the Threshold three years ago. The transit station. The Higashi cluster. The Kasugacho area. And in the chapter on the absorbed, filed under the first of them, there was a case Gin said he had carried for eight months before he could write it down properly."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "CVD Entity Classification Reference. Chapter 13. The Absorbed. What Absorption Is", "lines": ["Possession takes a living host. The soul remains, suppressed but present. Absorption takes the soul itself.", "There is no suppressed consciousness inside an absorber the way there is inside a possessing demon. There are fragments, echoes of what the soul was, but not a self. Possession is recoverable if caught in time. Most absorption is not.", "The absorber does not gain the absorbed soul's capabilities directly. It gains the absorbed soul's relationship to those capabilities, how they were used, what the soul learned from a lifetime of practice.", "Over centuries, some of the oldest demons carry enough of this that what they are cannot be separated from what they have consumed."]}}, {"p": "Rei read it twice, so that he would be sure of the difference. A possessed person is still in there, pushed down but alive, like Gin. An absorbed person is not. The demon has eaten the soul itself, and only echoes are left inside it."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "The Absorption Spectrum", "lines": ["Passive. A weakened soul crosses near a strong entity, and the entity takes what it can as the path completes. What reaches the other side is less than it should have been.", "Active. The entity weakens a soul deliberately over time, then consumes it whole. No path completes. Patient work.", "Elimination for essence. The soul is destroyed for pure energy. No fragment remains.", "Exorcism recovery. A partly absorbed soul can be exorcised back to complete its crossing, if it is still coherent enough. The window closes as integration deepens. The Satō case. Eleven years. The window closed a long time ago.", "Natural passage after partial. A recovered soul may still cross, diminished. A soul that would have been a good spirit may cross as a troubled one.", "Fragment persistence. Absorbed souls remain as resonant structures inside the absorber. The more someone had, the more remains to echo."]}}, {"img": "ch13_fragment.jpg", "cap": "Fragment reading, case papers spread across a desk"}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Fragment Reading. The Complete Methodology", "lines": ["Surface assessment. Type, class, age, feeding register. Confirms an absorber. Channel rank.", "Depth read. Below the entity's own signature, to the layered complexity that means absorption history. Arbiter rank minimum.", "Fragment isolation. Individual resonant structures that feel more personal than the rest. Impressions of people. Arbiter rank, precision training required.", "Fragment identification. Matching a fragment against prior knowledge of the person. Confirmation, not discovery. Arbiter rank.", "Fragment communication. Not conversation. What it was reaching toward, what it was afraid of. Vanguard rank recommended. Do not attempt without a second practitioner present to pull you out."]}}, {"p": "The method was a ladder. Each step went deeper into the demon and needed a higher rank to do safely. The first step only told a practitioner what kind of thing they were facing. The last step meant touching a person who had been eaten, and Gin's own note said not to try it alone."}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "Case Documentation. Fragment Read / Established Absorber / High-Grade Entity", "lines": ["Date. Approximately eight months prior to this entry. Location. Kita district, adjacent to the residential building from previous clearance work. Entity class. B minimum, possibly higher.", "During a surface read I picked up the depth quality. This entity had absorbed. Multiple times. Possibly over decades.", "I should have continued the surveillance. Instead I pushed deeper. I had approximately 40% stability when I started the depth read. I had significantly less when I finished it.", "Three distinct fragment profiles. The most recent was clearest. A Grid practitioner's veil relationship. The capability residue was Teleportation. The emotional register at the moment of absorption was grief and the quality of an interrupted intention. Someone who was in the middle of doing something when it happened. Someone who did not finish.", "The match I found. Haruki Satō, Grid operative, Teleportation-type, Channel rank, disappeared eleven years ago during what his contacts described as a routine information extraction operation in the Kita district. Case never resolved. Body never found. Official status. Missing, presumed dead.", "Presumed dead. He is. He has been in that entity for eleven years.", "I have not filed this officially. The entity is operating in the Kita thin spot, the same area where I first touched the Threshold three years ago. The case file reference is in the Grid intelligence file at the location described on page 147."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Margin, G.U.", "lines": ["I find myself thinking about who sent him. Why that district. Why eleven years ago. What was happening in Kita eleven years ago that required a Channel rank Grid Teleporter to go in alone.", "I have been going in the same direction he went for three years now. I am noting this because I want whoever reads this to understand that I notice the parallel. I am not concluding anything from it. I am noting that I notice."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the margin note until the words stopped being words. <em>I have been going in the same direction he went.</em> Satō had gone into Kita alone and had never come out. Gin had followed the same road for three years. He had read the thin spot from its edges, and pushed deeper than he should when he was already running low. And now the Healer's spiral was paying itself out toward the north of the same ward, one grateful stranger at a time."}, {"p": "He made himself say out loud what he did not know, in the empty flat. He did not know that the spiral and Satō's demon pointed at the same ground. He did not know whether the thing that had taken Satō had anything to do with the thing that had taken Gin. He only knew that his brother had noticed a parallel and refused to draw a conclusion from it. And now Rei was standing in the same place, noticing the same thing."}, {"p": "Then, without meaning to look for them, he found his own dates on the strip. The morning he first came to the apartment. The night he read the archive in the order it demanded. The night he stood at the drained pool listening to Kaito preach true things stacked the wrong way. Beside each one, within a day, there was a fresh favour. The match was not perfect, and Rei made himself admit that too, because false certainty is its own kind of possession. But it was close enough that coincidence now took more believing than the truth did. He was being answered. Every question he asked of the dark was answered in the body of a stranger. The kindness of each reply was the cruellest part. There was no one he could warn who would not first have to stop being grateful."}, {"p": "Outside, the night went on without him. A motorbike passed in the street below, then nothing. The trains stopped a little after midnight, and the city's sound changed to the hum of air conditioners and the far-off hiss of the expressway. He fell asleep at the table sometime before dawn. When he woke, grey light lay across the strip of dates, and there was a thirty-fourth entry in the ledger."}, {"img": "tools_trap.jpg", "cap": "The sulfur line across the threshold"}, {"p": "The first thing he did was check the sulfur. The yellow line across the threshold was exactly as he had laid it. So was the line beside the notebook, pale and dry and unchanged. Either nothing had come through the door, or something had come through that had learned long ago not to leave a mark. <em>Assume trace suppression is active.</em> Gin's page could not tell him which, and neither could the sulfur."}, {"p": "The thirty-fourth entry was the only one in either ledger written in a hand that was not quite Gin's. The letters were his. The patience was his. But the pressure of the pen was wrong. It leaned, the way handwriting leans when a different mind is steering a familiar arm. And the favour it recorded was not for a stranger."}, {"p": "<em>R.,</em> it began, the way his brother had begun notes to him since they were children. <em>R., you have not slept since the fourteenth and it is starting to show in the work. Let me give you rest, both of us, the way I gave the widow. You only have to stop counting.... G.</em>"}, {"p": "He read it four times in the grey light, with the neighbours' incense in the air and the fan turning slowly back and forth like someone shaking their head. He understood that the ledger had finally written the only entry it had ever been building toward."}, {"p": "It was an offer and a threat folded into the same kindness. That was the only language the Healer spoke. Stop counting, and be given rest. It was exactly what it had told the widow whose husband knocked inside the altar. Exactly what it had told the boy who heard the drowned in the gutters. Rei had spent a week learning what rest meant in that hand. It meant the knot tied off. The gratitude set hard. The thread held forever by something that loved the word rest the way a tourniquet loves a limb, tight and tidy and never letting go. The thing wearing his brother was not afraid of him. It had simply decided, patiently and reasonably, that Rei was the next person in Tokyo who could be helped across a line he could not cross alone. It had written his name on the list before he ever agreed to be on it."}, {"p": "He folded the note and did not put it in the ledger with the others. The others were knots. This one was the hand reaching for him, and he would not file the hand with the rope."}, {"p": "He went out onto the balcony. The sky was white with heat already. Down in the street the first commuters were walking to Jūjō station, salarymen in short-sleeved white shirts with their jackets over their arms, a woman holding a parasol against a sun that had barely risen. Some of them carried bags of gifts for families they would visit after work. Their faces had the soft tiredness of Obon week, when everyone is thinking a little about their dead. Every one of them was a person who could be saved into a leash."}, {"p": "Rei stood there with his brother's love-shaped trap in his pocket, next to the silver coin, and made the smallest, hardest promise of the week. He would keep counting. Not because counting was winning. It was not. The ledger grew faster than he could read it. But the day he stopped would be the day the thirty-fifth entry had his gratitude in it. He would rather be tired and his own than rested and held."}], "n": 26, "part": "Book Three. The Recruited"}, {"title": "The Knot That Pulls Back", "kind": "main", "src": "m_knot", "blocks": [{"p": "Sumida was two changes and two hundred and thirty yen away. Rei left the flat in the late morning, when the heat was already pressing down on the roofs, and walked to the station with his shirt sticking to his back. At the gate his card beeped and showed its balance on the little screen. He looked at the number longer than he needed to. Counting small things was what he had now instead of sleep."}, {"p": "The first train was cool and half empty. A woman across the aisle was knitting something small and pale yellow, her needles clicking in time with the rails. At the first change the platform was crowded with families going home for Obon, children sitting on wheeled suitcases, a father carrying a sleeping toddler and a paper bag of sweets at the same time. Rei let himself be carried along by the crowd to the next platform."}, {"p": "The last train was the yellow Sōbu line. It ran east, and as it crossed the long bridge over the Sumida river, everyone in the car seemed to glance out of the windows at once. The water was wide and green-brown and glittering. Pleasure boats moved slowly on it. Rei watched the river go past under the train and worked out again how many more trips the money on his card would pay for."}, {"p": "The man's name was Iwata. It was the same surname as the handyman in Gin's building, which meant nothing. It was a common name. He opened the door of his Sumida flat already smiling. It was the wet-eyed smile Rei had learned to dread this week. It was the smile of a person who has been carried across deep water and does not yet know that the boat is still underneath him. He was younger than the entry had made him sound, perhaps twenty-six. The flat behind him belonged to a man who had just started living again. The dishes were washed. A window was open. A photograph of someone who had died had been turned around at last, to face the room instead of the wall."}, {"p": "\"You're his brother,\" Iwata said, before Rei had said anything at all, and Rei's skin went cold. He had not given this man any name. \"He told me you'd come. He said you'd be tired, and that I should make you tea.\""}, {"p": "Rei sat, because standing up would have meant running. He took the tea, because refusing it would have started the conversation with an accusation. Across the low table he watched a genuinely happy man explain what had happened. Three nights ago, a soft-spoken stranger named Gin had found him at the river. Iwata had gone there to do what his grief was pushing him toward. And Gin had simply... Iwata's hands opened, helplessly grateful."}, {"p": "\"He took the weight off,\" Iwata said. \"He showed me how to set it down.\""}, {"p": "Iwata slept now. Iwata ate. Iwata had, he said with shining eyes, never felt so held in his life. Every true word of it went into Rei like a nail. He had come here to show this man a leash. What he had found was a man who would have died on Saturday and was alive on Tuesday. And the thing that had saved him was the thing Rei had come to destroy."}, {"p": "Before he could stop himself, Rei took the silver coin from his pocket and set it on the table between the cups, as if he had been emptying his pockets and forgotten it there. Iwata picked it up at once. He turned it over with real interest, read the old date, weighed it in his palm and handed it back. Nothing in him flinched. Nothing was riding him. Whatever the Healer had done here, it had not moved in. The reaction was the evidence, Gin's page said, and here the reaction was nothing at all."}, {"p": "Rei tried, gently, to find the seam another way. He asked whether Iwata had noticed anything he could not explain. Any debts he did not remember agreeing to. Any pull when he thought of the man who had helped him. He watched the questions slide off Iwata like rain off a raincoat. He watched the gratitude close smoothly over the place where doubt should have been. And at last he understood how it was built. The Healer did not bind people against their grief. It bound them through it. The leash was not laid on top of the wound. It was woven into the healing. To pull on the thread was to pull on the one thing holding the person together. No one could hate a hand that was also the stitches. Not Iwata. Not the widow. Not Rei."}, {"p": "It was not how Gin healed people. Rei knew that from the archive, from a page at the end of the chapter on suffering beings that Gin had plainly written for himself. After hard work, Gin wrote, eat. Drink water. Do something completely ordinary that needs full attention. <em>Cooking something you actually care about, a conversation with someone you actually like.</em> Find someone to sit with afterwards, and let ordinary things be ordinary for a while. <em>The ordinariness is the protocol,</em> Gin had written. <em>Take it seriously.</em> Gin's kind of healing sent a person back out into their own life, to their own table and their own people. The Healer's kind sent them back to the Healer."}, {"img": "scene_iwata_tea.jpg", "cap": "The man from Sumida refilling a cup that was still half full"}, {"p": "Iwata was talking about work. He had gone back to the warehouse on Monday. His supervisor had said he looked like a new man. He laughed when he said it, a little embarrassed, and refilled Rei's cup although it was still half full. There was nothing in the flat that did not belong to an ordinary life. That was what made it unbearable."}, {"p": "Rei thought of the fourth rule in Gin's Grid chapter. <em>Debt and reciprocity. When someone helps you, you help someone else.</em> The Grid's debts were meant to move on, from one hand to the next, until nobody could say who had started it. Iwata's debt did not move. It ran in one direction only, back to the hand that had made it. And it would wait there for as long as that hand wanted."}, {"p": "Rei said his brother's name. He said <em>Gin</em> the way he had said it all through his childhood. Softly. Claiming him. The word he used to call him in from the dark."}, {"img": "ch10_feeding.jpg", "cap": "Iwata in Sumida, and the tenant still at work behind him"}, {"p": "The flat changed temperature. Not Iwata. Iwata only tilted his head, still smiling, and asked if Rei was all right. It was the air that answered. The cheap fluorescent light over the sink dimmed for a breath, as if something far away had leaned its weight against a wall. And at the back of Rei's own skull, behind the two registers he kept pressed flat, he felt the thread he had reached for reach back."}, {"p": "It was the first time all week that the rope had had a near end as well as a far one. The Healer was on the line. Through Iwata's untouched happiness, it had just felt Rei's hand close on its work. And it was... Rei would have sworn to this, in the heat, with his pulse loud in his ears... interested."}, {"p": "This was the lesson the ledger could not teach him, and the man from the river just had. A knot is not a single point. A knot is a place where the rope folds back on itself, and when one end is pulled, both parts move. All week Rei had imagined himself outside the design, a reader standing over a map. In this small bright kitchen, with a saved man pouring him more tea, he finally felt what his sight had been hiding from him. The moment he touched a binding with love in his voice, he had put love into the system. The Healer kept a ledger of love the way it kept a ledger of favours. Rei had just made an entry he could not take back. It knew his frequency now. It had wanted it since the fourteenth."}, {"p": "He put the cup down before his hand could shake it. Across the table, Iwata's peace was undisturbed. Perfect. Sealed. That was the horror Rei would carry out of the door. The man who had been saved had felt nothing. The whole struggle had run between Rei and the thing in his brother, straight over the head of the man whose grief it was using."}, {"p": "He had come to Sumida to untie a knot. Instead he had proved that the rope ran both ways. That meant the Healer could be reached. It also meant the Healer could reach him."}, {"p": "He went back over the bridge in the hot middle of the afternoon. The car was nearly empty. An elderly couple sat side by side holding hands, saying nothing, both looking out at the river. Rei sat with the coin back in his pocket and the water going past underneath him again. He no longer knew which way the rope was moving. Was he pulling the Healer toward the light? Or was it reeling him in toward the post at Kita, one warm, patient turn at a time?"}], "n": 27, "part": "Book Three. The Recruited"}, {"title": "The Thirty-Fifth Name", "kind": "main", "src": "m_35th", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei came back from Sumida in the worst of the afternoon heat. The walk from the station felt twice as long as usual. The cicadas in the trees along the road were so loud they seemed to be inside his head, and the shade under the railway bridge was no cooler than the sun. When he opened the door of the flat, the air inside was thick and still. And the ledger had grown while the flat stood empty."}, {"p": "It had never done that before. Every earlier entry had appeared in the small hours, while he slept. This one had come in daylight, while he was across the city with his hand on Iwata's thread. The timing was not lost on him."}, {"p": "The sulfur lay across the threshold exactly as he had left it, pale and dry. It told him nothing. It had been told to tell him nothing."}, {"p": "The thirty-fifth entry was short. It was in the leaning hand, the pen pressure that was not quite Gin's. It did not record a favour given. It recorded one offered and refused, and the refusal it named was Rei's."}, {"p": "<em>R. would not set the weight down,</em> it read, <em>so the weight stays. We will try again when he is tired enough. He is nearly tired enough.... G.</em>"}, {"p": "So the count had always been a countdown. Rei saw it whole now, the way he had seen the timeline stand up and the spiral point at Kita. The ledger had never been a list of the saved with Rei standing outside it, taking notes. It was a list of the saved with a blank line kept open at the bottom. And that blank line had had his name on it since the first night he came to the door. Everything since then had been the Healer closing the distance between Rei and the moment he would be tired enough to be helped. The spiral. The favours in daylight. The note in his brother's hand. Iwata's tea, ready before he arrived. It was not racing him to Gin. It was waiting for him to come to it, exhausted and grateful. That was the only door it had ever needed anyone to walk through."}, {"p": "He should have felt hunted. Instead, for one clear second, he felt almost calm. A countdown, unlike a hunt, has rules, and these rules were simple and terrible. It won when he stopped. It won when he broke. The only thing it had no entry for was a man who kept moving while tired and refused to be grateful. That was his whole road now. Not to outrun the ledger. He could not. It wrote faster than he could read. His road was to stay, every hour, the one name it could not finish. Awake. Ungrateful. Still counting. Still his own."}, {"p": "He took out the folded note from that morning and laid the two side by side. The offer and the refusal, in the same patient hand. He was reading a conversation the Healer had been having with him for a week. Only one of them had been allowed to speak."}, {"break": "Gin's ramen counter"}, {"p": "As the light turned orange on the balcony, Rei remembered a line from Gin's price list. The ramen place Gin always went to on Tuesdays charged eight hundred and fifty yen for the tonkotsu. <em>Worth it.</em> It did not matter what day it was. Rei was hungry, and it was Gin's place."}, {"p": "He walked there as the evening came down. The heat had eased just enough to bring people outside. An old man in a vest watered the potted plants in front of his house with a hose, and the wet concrete smelled of summer. Children ran past with sparklers that had not been lit yet, arguing about who would get the pink ones. In front of one house, a grandmother sat on the step beside a dish of burning incense and a plate of peaches, keeping her dead company in the cooler air. The smoke drifted across the street and Rei walked through it."}, {"img": "place_tatsuya.jpg", "cap": "The ramen counter where Gin ate on Tuesdays"}, {"p": "Rei knew the place. He had been there with his brother a dozen times without ever understanding that it was a habit. A narrow counter, eight stools, steam on the window. He sat on the stool Gin always took and ordered the tonkotsu, the thick white soup made by boiling pork bones for hours until they give up everything they have. He ate all of it. The broth was heavy and too salty and very good. The man behind the counter looked at him for a moment, as if trying to remember where he had seen that jaw before. Then he went back to his pots. Nobody asked Rei anything. It was the first full meal he had eaten in three days."}, {"p": "On the way home he stopped at the 7-Eleven on Kita-jūjō Nishi. Gin had written about it in a margin, on page thirty-four of the archive, in the section on what practitioners carry."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's archive. Margin of page 34", "lines": ["The 7-Eleven on Kita-jūjō Nishi has a staff member who always works Thursday nights who asks how I am doing and appears to actually want to know. I go there after difficult cases.", "I do not think she knows what I do. I think she can tell when someone needs to be asked a normal question by a normal person.", "I have never documented her name because I do not want to look at her the way I look at cases."]}}, {"p": "It was not Thursday. The young man at the register had dyed hair and did not look up from restocking the cigarette shelf. Rei bought a can of hot coffee he did not need and sat on the stool by the window for twenty minutes, under the bright lights, doing nothing unusual. Behind him a tired office worker was trying to decide between two onigiri. A delivery van idled at the kerb with its hazard lights blinking. The little door chime played its tune every time someone came in. Gin's page said this worked. And it did, a little."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "The Kombini Protocol. Ordinary Spaces as Stability Infrastructure", "lines": ["Convenience stores at 3am are one of the most reliable stability recovery tools available. Bright lights. Ordinary transactions. Other ordinary people doing ordinary things. The kombini, where nothing significant ever happens, by design, is structurally cleaner than most spaces a practitioner encounters.", "Eating a warm kombini meal. +3% stability. Standing at the counter, eating something hot, under fluorescent lights, while someone behind you debates which onigiri to buy.", "Sitting in a kombini for 20 minutes doing nothing unusual. +2% stability. You do not have to try. You have to be there.", "Your regular order at your regular place. +5% stability. The familiarity is the mechanic.", "Near thin spots, active shrines, or suffering being ground, ordinary recovery is halved. You cannot kombini your way back from a deep clearance in Oji at 2am without first putting distance between yourself and the location."]}}, {"p": "Rei did not need the numbers. He could feel it. Something in him had come back a few steps from the edge, just from a bowl of soup and a stool by a window."}, {"p": "Rei finished the coffee and thought about the woman on Thursday nights who asked Gin how he was. She would be here on Thursday night. She would not know what to ask about. He hoped, foolishly, that she had noticed Gin had stopped coming."}, {"p": "Night came the way Obon nights come, lit by lanterns and full of the returning dead. Back in the flat, Rei did the things Gin's pages told him to do, in order, because order was something he could hold. He poured a salt line across the inside of the threshold, one continuous stream from the tub, with no gaps. He sprinkled iron filings along it, so that anything that forced the line would pay for it. He put Gin's heavy cast-iron pan on the stove, because a steel knife does nothing and a cast-iron skillet works, and because Gin had cooked in it every Sunday of Rei's life."}, {"p": "Then he lit the gas ring under the kettle with his lighter, and sat down with the lighter still in his hand. He turned it over and over. It was a cheap thing, scratched silver, and he had carried it so long that he no longer noticed he was holding it. He opened the archive at the section on inventory, and the lighter stopped in his fingers."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "Inventory and the Field Kit. Grounding Objects", "lines": ["Every practitioner has one. An object that connects them to ordinary life when the veil layer is too loud, when they are close to the possession window and need something real to hold onto. The object is not magical. It is specific. The specificity is what makes it work.", "Held during a stability crisis, +5% immediate recovery. Nearby during rest, +5% to total rest recovery. If you lose it, your stability ceiling drops 5% until it is recovered or replaced. Replacement takes one month. You cannot rush this.", "The people I work with. Nakamura Aoi, ceramic bead bracelet. Kato Ryusei, a white chess piece. Katagiri Noa, small imperfect pottery bowl. Tsukishiro Haruki, dried flower, jacket pocket. Shimizu Ren, worn athletic glove, left hand only. Gin, unknown. The archive has a gap here."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "End of Inventory Notes. Personal Note. A Sunday. Quiet.", "lines": ["Everyone has one. The ones who say they do not have one have one and do not know it yet. The object is not chosen. It accumulates.", "Mine is not documented in this chapter. I wrote the entry twice and deleted it both times. Some things the archive holds back not because they are secret but because writing them down changes them.", "Rei's is the lighter. He does not know that is what it is yet. He will.", "G.U. Keys on the hook."]}}, {"img": "inventory_anchor.jpg", "cap": "Gin's keys, the object he signed at the end of every page"}, {"p": "Rei sat with the lighter in his palm for a long time. Then he looked at the empty hook by the door, where Gin's keys had hung every day since Rei was small enough to be lifted up to reach them. <em>Keys on the hook.</em> Gin had ended almost every page of the archive with those three words. He had written that his own object was a gap. And then he had signed the gap at the bottom of every page for six years."}, {"p": "And on the night Gin went quiet, the hook had been empty, and the key had been lying on the walkway outside the door."}, {"p": "Rei did not let himself finish that thought. He closed his hand around the lighter instead. He felt something in him settle, very slightly, the way a small boat settles when someone steps into it who knows how."}, {"break": "The family files"}, {"p": "He could not sleep yet, so he read. He read the chapter he had been avoiding, the one on reincarnation, because he remembered the night Gin had been writing it. Gin had been sitting at this table with a stack of very old files, and Rei had looked over his shoulder and asked what they were."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's preface, Chapter 12", "lines": ["Rei came in last night while I was reviewing the Uedera family case files again, the fourth time I have gone through them this month.", "He looked at what I was reading and said, those are really old. I said yes. He said, are they ours? I said, yes. He said, hm. And went to make tea and brought me a cup without asking.", "He is sometimes very perceptive in ways that have nothing to do with the veil.", "G.U."]}}, {"p": "Rei remembered the tea. He did not remember saying <em>hm</em>. He read on."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "CVD Entity Classification Reference. Chapter 12. Reincarnation. Research Classification. Contested", "lines": ["Reincarnation is the only path that produces no detectable entity. The soul crosses, the self does not corrupt or dissolve, and it simply continues... inside a new life.", "The CVD has seventeen documented cases of verifiable reincarnation over forty years. Its official position is unconfirmed hypothesis. That position has been unchanged for thirty-one years.", "Karmic reincarnation. The soul enters a soulless neonate in a compatible family lineage. It does not arrive with memories but with orientations. Preferences, fears, relational patterns, ability profiles that do not match the family's genetic history.", "Involuntary absorption. A dying person's extreme will to live creates a membrane vacuum that draws in a crossing soul. Neither party chose this. One body, two consciousnesses.", "Children between about two and five sometimes describe places they have never been and people they never met. The memory window closes around age seven.", "Multi-spectrum families show a progressive pattern across generations, each generation developing more paths than the previous, as if the soul carrying the capability is refining it over successive lifetimes."]}}, {"p": "The page was careful, the way all CVD pages were careful, but the idea underneath was simple. Sometimes a soul does not go on to the Font. It comes back, into a new baby, often in the same family. It does not bring memories. It brings leanings. A fear nobody taught the child. A talent nobody else in the family has. And in some families, each generation can do a little more than the last."}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Margin, G.U.", "lines": ["The Uedera family files. Each generation stronger than the previous. Each generation developing more paths.", "I am not concluding that the soul currently living as me has lived in this family before. I am noting that the evidence is consistent with that conclusion and that I find myself thinking about it more than is probably strictly necessary.", "The family files are on the desk. The tea is cold."]}}, {"p": "<em>Each generation developing more paths.</em> Rei thought of his own two registers, the low one and the searching one. No file had ever measured them properly, because he had never let anything measure them. Gin had been sitting here working out whether the Uedera line kept coming back as itself. And then he had drunk the tea his little brother brought him and said nothing about it."}, {"break": "Nearly"}, {"p": "Rei put the archive down and sat with the two notes and the long torn strip of dates. He took his own pulse the way he would take a stranger's. Honestly. Clinically. Looking for the thing the Healer was waiting for."}, {"p": "He was tired. There was no use pretending he was not. The tiredness lived behind his eyes and in the two flat registers he had not let himself feel in days. The thing wearing his brother had read him correctly, the way it read everyone. That was its whole terrible skill. <em>He is nearly tired enough.</em> Nearly. The word was doing more work than Rei wanted it to. Nearly was a confession. It meant the door was not open yet. It meant there was still a margin. It meant that tonight, this hour, he was still the unfinished entry and not the closed one."}, {"p": "He did the only thing the countdown had no answer for, which was also the smallest thing. He decided to be tired and stay. Not heroically. He was too exhausted for heroism, and the Healer would only treat heroism as one more line it could help him across. He simply refused the rest, the way Iwata could not and the widow had never had the chance to. He kept the page open. He wrote nothing in it. He let the long night be long."}, {"p": "There was a difference, he understood now, between the rest the Healer offered and the rest Gin had written down. The Healer's rest was a knot. Gin's rest was a bowl of ramen on a Tuesday, a can of coffee under bright lights, a lighter turned over and over in a hand that did not know it was holding on. One of them asked for gratitude. The other only asked him to come back to his own life."}, {"p": "Somewhere north of Kita, his brother's body was sleeping a sleep that something else was dreaming. Somewhere closer, the ledger waited for the morning when Rei would be tired enough to be grateful. Between those two facts he sat awake, ungrateful and alive. It was, he understood, the only act of love the Healer had left him that it could not turn into a leash."}, {"p": "He would have to sleep soon. The body keeps its own ledger, and his was calling in its debts. But before he slept, he turned the strip of dates over. He started a new line at the bottom, on the blank the Healer had kept open for him. He did not write his gratitude there. He wrote the date, and beside it three words in his own handwriting, which did not lean. It was his own claim, staked on the only page the thing had been allowed to fill."}, {"p": "<em>still counting.... R.</em>"}, {"p": "Then he lay down in the heat, with both notes within reach, the lighter in his fist and the salt line white across the door. He let himself be tired, on purpose and openly, an entry that refused to close. Outside, the lanterns burned for the dead, and somewhere a late train went by toward Akabane, and the city slowly forgot itself toward dawn. And the ledger, for the first time all week, held an answer the Healer had not written."}], "n": 28, "part": "Book Three. The Recruited"}, {"title": "Tanizaki's Drowned Client", "kind": "side", "src": "s_drowned", "blocks": [{"p": "Tanizaki's message came in the middle of the afternoon. It was four words long and it asked Rei to come now. Rei took the Keihin-Tōhoku line north from Ōji, the train with the sky-blue stripe. In Obon week it was full of people going home to their families. A man in a short-sleeved shirt slept against the window with a box of sweets on his knees, wrapped in shop paper for somebody's grandmother. Two schoolgirls shared one pair of earphones and laughed at the same moment. At Higashi-Jūjō the doors opened, and the heat came in with the noise of the cicadas in the trees beside the tracks."}, {"p": "He got off at Akabane. His Suica card beeped at the gate, and he walked out into the smell of hot concrete and frying oil from the station shops. The streets grew quieter as he went north. Some houses had white paper lanterns hanging by the door. A white lantern meant a family whose person had died in the past year, and who would come home this Obon for the first time. Near here the Sumida river splits away from the bigger Arakawa and begins its long run down through the city. Tanizaki's tea shop stood two streets from that water. Its shutter was down, with a handwritten card taped to it saying the shop was closed for the holiday. Rei went round to the side door and climbed the narrow stairs."}, {"p": "Tanizaki Seiichi was an old man of the Grid. He had taught Gin most of what Gin knew about field work. He did not charge cash for anything. He charged in trust, and he spent it carefully."}, {"img": "char_tanizaki-seiichi.jpg", "cap": "Tanizaki Seiichi, who pours before he speaks"}, {"p": "Tanizaki poured before he spoke. That was how Rei knew the matter was serious. The old man had spent forty years in Kita, and he had learned that nothing true gets said over an empty cup. He never wasted the gesture on small business. The room above the shop smelled of roasted barley tea. It also smelled of the river two streets over, the brown smell of low tide and lantern wax that the whole district carried during Obon. Light came through the paper screens the colour of weak tea."}, {"p": "He set Rei's cup down with both hands and folded his own hands around his."}, {"p": "\"A man went into the Sumida three nights ago,\" he said. \"He has not come up.\""}, {"p": "Rei waited. Tanizaki did not like to be hurried, and he did not like to be helped."}, {"p": "\"It is not a police matter. It will not become one.\" The old man looked at the steam, not at Rei. \"I need him found before the river gives him up on its own terms.\""}, {"p": "The drowned man's name was Kase. He was a fixer on the Grid side. He was careful and unregistered, a go-between who arranged for a thing to change hands without either hand being seen. Tanizaki had used him for thirty years. He trusted him so much that he had stopped noticing him, the way a man stops noticing a good tool. Three nights ago Kase had sent word that he was holding something that frightened him. He wanted it out of his hands and into Tanizaki's before Obon reached its height. Then he sent nothing more. A Grid courier went to his flat and found it aired and empty. The kettle was still warm. His shoes stood paired by the door."}, {"p": "\"A man does not walk to the river barefoot in this season by accident,\" Tanizaki said. He turned his cup a quarter-turn on the table and did not drink. \"And the Sumida in Obon does not keep what it takes in the ordinary way. It launches the dead all week. I would rather find Kase before the river decides he is one of them.\""}, {"p": "Both of Rei's registers stirred at that. They always moved, low and slow, when something true was being made to sound smaller than it was. Tanizaki was not asking him to bring back a body. He was asking him to read one. He knew what reading a drowned man this week would pull through Rei, and he was asking anyway, and that told Rei how frightened he was. Tanizaki was the one person in Kita who never spent Rei carelessly. He had trained Gin with exactly this kind of restraint. Now he was pushing a cup across the table and asking for something he wanted kept off the official books. Whatever Kase had been carrying, it reached further than one fixer's bad night by the water."}, {"p": "The rain had not started. Down by the embankment, the first lantern sellers were setting out their paper boats."}, {"break": "The river at night"}, {"p": "Rei waited in the room until dark, because what he needed to see could only be seen at night. Then he walked the two streets to the river. Families were coming the same way, carrying lanterns and lighters and bottles of cold tea. A grandfather held a small boy on his shoulders, and the boy held the lantern. Somebody's radio was playing a sad old ballad. Moths circled the street lights, and the air was so wet that Rei's shirt stuck to his back after a hundred steps."}, {"p": "After dark the river was a black road carrying small fires. Tōrō nagashi had begun upstream. It is the Obon custom of floating lanterns on a river to guide the dead back to the other side. Each lantern was a paper box with a candle inside and a name brushed on the side. A family wrote the name, lit the candle and set the box on the water, and the current carried their dead away, gently, toward the sea. The lanterns came down in loose groups. Rei stood on the concrete embankment, past the steps where the families stood, where the river stopped being pretty and became a drain for the city. He smelled diesel and mud and watched the little lights drift past him."}, {"p": "This was the worst place to use his sight, and he had come here to use it. The membrane between the living and the dead was at its thinnest all week. It was thinnest of all over running water, at the festival of the dead. The moment he let the first register open a hand's width, the whole surface of the Sumida seemed to tilt. It stopped being a river with lanterns on it. It became a procession. A long, patient line of the departing moved with the current, and every paper flame had someone walking beside it."}, {"p": "He walked downstream, listening for the one wrong note among the dead. A man drowned three nights ago would be a wrong note. The properly dead were leaving this week. Kase was not leaving. Something was holding him."}, {"p": "Rei felt him before he saw anything, the way fingers find a snag in cloth. It was down past the third pier. There the current went slack behind a concrete footing, and any lantern that drifted in turned slow circles and did not come out again. His sight read the place as cold. His skin read it as nothing at all. Something in that slack water was keeping a man, the way the third basin had kept a half-made boy. It was not eating him yet. It was holding him, the way a person holds an object while they decide what to do with it."}, {"p": "And the held place was not silent. Tanizaki could not have prepared him for this part, and both registers leaned toward it with an old hunger Rei had always been told to refuse. It was leaking. A drowned man read this soon after death still carries his last quarter of an hour, the way a struck bell still holds its note. Rei could feel Kase's final minutes pressing out against the river's grip. The cold. The weight of his coat. A decision. A hand that was or was not on the back of his neck. All of it waited down there to be looked at. But looking was exactly what thinned the wall, and the wall over this water was already thin enough to put his fingers through."}, {"p": "The lanterns turned their slow circles behind the pier. One of them, Rei noticed, had no name written on its side at all."}, {"p": "Kase was where Rei knew he would be. He hung in the slack water behind the footing, one sleeve caught on a rusted steel rod. He was face down and turning slowly in the eddy, and his coat was as heavy as a drowned animal. Rei waded in up to his knees and took his wrist."}, {"p": "The river did not fight him. That was the first wrong thing, and the worst. A body held for three nights in slack water should belong to the river. Instead the current loosened its grip the moment Rei decided to take him, as if it had wanted Rei to have the body all along. Rei turned him over. Lantern light from upstream slid across his face. Drowning had been gentle with it. It was slack and almost untroubled, the face of a careful man who had stopped being careful at the very end. Then the last quarter of an hour, freed by Rei's touch, came off him into Rei's open register like cold coming off a stone."}, {"p": "Rei read it because he could not stop himself, and it was not the story Tanizaki had told him. Kase had not slipped. Kase had walked here barefoot in the dark, with the thing that frightened him buttoned inside his coat. The thing was a list. It was a small ledger wrapped in waxed paper. It recorded who in Kita had accepted a certain kind of favour in the last two years, and what each favour had quietly cost the membrane. Three nights too late, Kase had understood what carrying it made him. He was a loose thread in something very large and very patient. There was only one place in the city thin enough to lose the list, where no register could ever read it again. That was the bottom of the Sumida in Obon. He did not drown himself out of fear. He drowned himself to hide the ledger where even the dead could not be made to give it up. He walked in slowly, and this week the river was glad to keep them both."}, {"p": "And the keeper of the place was here. Rei had felt it the whole time, the way a person can feel someone watching from behind glass. It was not a demon and it was not a ghost. It was the slack water itself, grown aware. This stretch of Obon river had kept a hidden man and a hidden list for three nights, and in that time it had learned the taste of keeping secrets, the way still water learns things. It did not want Kase. It wanted the warm thing now kneeling in it with two registers open. It wanted the young man with a brother across the city, the brother it could feel him aching for. Without words, it offered to keep things for Rei too. It would take whatever he most needed hidden and hold it on the bottom. There no Healer and no Directorate and no patient thing in his brother's shape could ever read it out of him."}, {"p": "The ledger lay cold against the dead man's chest. The nameless lantern turned its slow circle by Rei's knee. The current waited to see what he would do with all three."}, {"p": "Rei gave the river nothing. He unbuttoned the coat with numb fingers. He took out the waxed packet and pushed it inside his own shirt, against his skin, where it was his to carry. Whatever he needed hidden, he would carry that too. He said so out loud, to the water. He felt foolish, and he said it again."}, {"p": "He got Kase out. That much he did not let himself bargain over. He laid him on the concrete above the tide line, under a courier's tarp that Tanizaki's network kept hidden nearby for nights like this. Then he knelt there dripping while the lanterns kept coming past the pier, name after name, floating out toward a sea that could not read them. The ledger sat heavy and cold against his chest. The slack water behind the footing had gone ordinary again. It was just river. The awareness in it had sunk back into the current the moment it failed to get what it offered to hold. Rei understood that he would never know whether he had outwitted it, or whether he had simply not been worth keeping."}, {"p": "Then there was the body, which was also the truth. A barefoot drowning in Obon was the most ordinary death in Tokyo. By morning a patrol would find him. The city's records would make him one more sad number for the season, and nobody would ever ask about a waxed ledger or the favours that cost the wall. Rei looked at the tarp and thought of the paired shoes by Kase's door. A man who left his shoes like that had already chosen how he wanted to go. He had wanted a lantern and a name. He had wanted to be off every book. Tanizaki had asked for the same thing, over a cup he did not drink."}, {"p": "The rain finally started, fine and warm. It dimpled the black water and made the nearer candles flicker. Rei climbed the steps to the last lantern seller, who was packing up her stall, and bought the one plain lantern she had left. He borrowed her brush and wrote KASE on its side. He wrote it badly, because his hand would not stay steady. He lit it and set it on the water at the footing, beside the nameless one. He gave the river back the man and kept the secret. The two lanterns turned once together in the slack water. Then the current took them both out into the procession. Kase left on his own terms."}, {"p": "Both registers were still half open and slow to close, the way they always were after running water. Through them Rei felt what he had really been doing down here. It was not a favour for an old man. He had come to the Sumida to practise the unbearable thing. To read a held person whom a patient power did not want to let go. To take a hidden truth, or refuse it. To kneel in a thin place and not be kept there. Somewhere across the city his brother was held exactly like this, in slack water of a different kind, by a Healer who was glad to keep him. Rei did not yet have the nerve or the method to go in after him. Kase had been a rehearsal. The knowledge sat in him as cold as the river. He could perhaps find Gin the way he had found this man. Finding was the easy half. The water did not always give up what a person meant to take."}], "n": 29}, {"title": "Three Cups", "kind": "side", "src": "s_drowned#2", "blocks": [{"p": "He walked back to the tea shop in the last of the rain. The streets were empty except for a delivery scooter and a cat under a parked car, watching the water run in the gutter. A konbini on the corner glowed white, and the young clerk inside was stacking rice balls on a shelf for the morning. Rei's shoes squelched with every step. The sky over the river turned from black to grey."}, {"img": "res_gridmentor.jpg", "cap": "Tanizaki and Rei at dawn in the room above the tea shop"}, {"p": "At dawn Tanizaki had set out two cups and filled three. It was the old custom, the third cup for the one who had gone. He did not explain it and Rei did not ask. Rei came in soaked to the knees with the night still on him and told him what he had found, all of it. Then he took the waxed packet out of his shirt and laid it on the table between the cups."}, {"p": "Tanizaki listened the way he listened to everything, without moving. He only turned his cup, the slow quarter-turn that meant he was rearranging forty years of knowledge about his district to fit a new and terrible fact. He did not open the packet. He laid his palm flat on it for a while, like a man feeling for a pulse."}, {"p": "When he finally spoke, he said something Rei did not expect. Kase had carried things for Gin too, years ago, the same kind of quiet errands in waxed paper. It was Gin who had taught the fixer the trick of hiding a thing in running water, where no register could reach it. \"If Kase chose the Sumida,\" Tanizaki said, \"he chose it because your brother once told him it was the one safe place.\" Even a fixer's chosen drowning had Gin's careful handwriting in the margin."}, {"p": "He poured the third cup out on the floor, the smallest offering. Then he told Rei what the old give the young when they have nothing else. The river takes everyone on its own terms in the end. The only mercy is to let a person choose those terms. Kase had done that, which was more than most of the dead this week had managed."}, {"p": "Rei asked the question he had carried up the stairs. \"Is there anyone who could go in after Gin? Someone stronger than me. Stronger than the CVD.\""}, {"p": "Tanizaki was quiet long enough for the barley smell to come back into the room. \"You mean a Master,\" he said."}, {"p": "Rei had heard the word all his life, used loosely, as praise for a good teacher. Tanizaki did not use it loosely. A Master, he said, was a Vanguard-rank independent. Vanguard was the top of the ranks a practitioner could reach. A Master was someone who had climbed past anything an institution could train, and then stepped out of its reach. The CVD called them unreliable, and from the CVD's side of the desk they were. They chose their own cases. They needed long rest between cases to stay stable at that height. They looked at patterns across years instead of weeks. And every one of them had paid something personal to get there. \"The capability and the cost are the same thing,\" Tanizaki said. \"Your brother wrote that down. He had watched three of them up close.\""}, {"p": "\"Then I go to one of them.\""}, {"p": "\"You consult one of them,\" Tanizaki said. \"You do not deploy them. That is the first rule, and people break it every year and come home with nothing. The exchange is real. Whatever you take from a Master, you give back something of the same weight, and they will know if you cheat. Their cost is visible if you look, so look, and do not pretend afterwards that you did not see it. And respect what they will not tell you. There is always something. It is always for a reason you would not like.\" He touched the packet again. \"Gin said the three he knew were not manageable. Then he added that he was including himself in that.\""}, {"p": "He did not thank Rei in words. Instead he slid four things across the table. The key to a safe house. His method for setting wards, written out in his own hand. The field profile he had kept on Gin since Gin was a boy, or the first part of it, in a cardboard folder gone soft with handling. And a single sheet folded in three, in Gin's handwriting, with a title Rei could read through the paper."}, {"p": "\"Outside the doctrine,\" Tanizaki said. \"Your brother's list of the people who still do the old work. Priests, a shaman, an elder. Houses the CVD walks past every day. Do not read it today. You will know the day you need it.\""}, {"p": "These were things he gave only when he trusted someone completely. Rei understood then that the drowned client had also been a test. He had passed it the hard way, by being trusted with a death that had to stay off the books. The rain had stopped. The barley smell was back. Outside, the dead had mostly gone home, and the lanterns had all reached the sea."}, {"p": "What stayed with Rei, as he walked out into the washed grey morning, was not the ledger or the key or even Kase's almost peaceful face. It was the small, hard lesson the river had handed up with the body. Hiding is a kind of drowning. A held thing can be loved into staying held. His brother had taught a fixer the safe place in the water. Now Gin was hidden in slack water of his own, and he had known this trick from the inside long before the Healer ever wore his coat. Tonight Rei had rehearsed the exact shape of what he would have to do for Gin. He had learned what it cost. He had learned that the water does not always give up what a person means to take. And he had decided to go in after him anyway."}, {"p": "The cup Tanizaki had poured for him had gone cold. He drank it. This morning it would have felt like an insult to leave anything behind that the river could keep."}, {"p": "On the stairs down to the street he felt the folded sheet in his pocket, Gin's handwriting pressed against his hip through the cloth. He did not take it out. Tanizaki had said he would know the day. Going out into the grey, Rei had the uneasy feeling that the day was already coming, and walking faster than he was."}], "n": 30}, {"title": "From Gin's Book. The Masters", "kind": "gin", "src": "gin", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei walked back to Akabane through the wet morning and caught the Keihin-Tōhoku line south toward home. It was early, and the train was nearly empty. An old man sat across from him with a cloth bundle on his knees, tied at the top. Through the cloth Rei could see the shapes of fruit and a small bottle of sake, offerings for a family grave. A cleaner in white gloves moved down the car picking up a newspaper someone had left. The river smell was still in Rei's clothes."}, {"p": "He opened the soft cardboard folder on his knees. Behind the first pages of Gin's field profile, Tanizaki had tucked four sheets copied from Gin's archive. They came from a chapter headed The Masters. Gin had written them the way he wrote everything for the archive, plainly, for someone who would need them later. Tanizaki had added one yellow note of his own, stuck to the first sheet in his small square hand."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "The Masters. What the word means", "lines": ["Masters are Vanguard-rank independents. The CVD files them as unreliable. They are unreliable for structural reasons. They choose their own cases. They need clearance time to stay stable at Vanguard. They take the long view of patterns. And every one of them paid a personal cost.", "Most Masters paid for their capability with something specific. A relationship they could not keep while developing. A period of severe depletion that permanently changed their stability floor. Encounters that changed how they engage with entities.", "The capability and the cost are the same thing.", "The unreliability framing is a CVD framing. From the Masters' side, they are not unreliable. They are correctly managing a capability the institution does not know how to manage, and would manage badly if it could.", "The three Vanguard practitioners I know directly are not manageable. I am including myself in this assessment.", "Working rules. Do not deploy, consult. The exchange is real. Their cost is visible. Respect what they will not tell you. (G.U.)"]}}, {"p": "The next three sheets were portraits of the three Masters Gin knew best. Each one gave the person's rank and gift, what they would do and what they would not, and what their power had cost them."}, {"img": "char_yamamoto-koji.jpg", "cap": "Yamamoto Koji, the Archivist"}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Yamamoto Koji. The Archivist", "lines": ["Vanguard rank. Clairsentience. Independent.", "Former senior practitioner in the CVD Research Division. Left sixteen years ago, after the Division classified a large part of his entity taxonomy work and restricted its use in the field. He did not disagree with the research. He disagreed with the classification.", "Keeps an independent archive of entity intelligence he has been building for twenty-two years. The most comprehensive private collection of entity documentation I am aware of. Lives in it, in a book-drowned flat near the building. The location is documented nowhere outside the archive itself.", "Terms. He tells you what he knows, you tell him what you found, the exchange updates both archives. He has never charged money for a consultation.", "He only takes cases that produce new entity knowledge. Standard possessions and routine clearances he passes to his network. People read this as refusal. It is efficiency.", "The reading that takes a standard practitioner thirty minutes takes him the time it takes to walk around the perimeter of the location once.", "What he cost himself. Two decades of institutional standing, access to the classified material he produced himself, and the relationships the departure ended. He does not appear to regret the departure. He appears to regret the material he cannot reach.", "He has reviewed my case files three times in six years. Each time he told me what I had found before I finished describing it. He is the practitioner I have learned the most from in this field. (G.U.)"]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Stuck to the Yamamoto page, in Tanizaki's hand", "lines": ["Yamamoto holds an entry from twenty-two years ago. An encounter in Kita with a Duty-register demon working through a multi-spectrum host.", "He did not know then what it was. He knows now. He has cross-referenced the signatures.", "It is the first correct naming of that register as Duty anywhere in any record. The CVD instruments call it null-emotive. They are wrong.", "It also holds the history of this thing in our district further back than any institutional file.", "He will trade it for a true thing of the same weight. Never for money. Gin never brought him the trade. You might. T."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Ishida Ryō. The Closer", "lines": ["Vanguard rank. Telekinesis. Grid-adjacent.", "Never registered. Forty-one. Born into a family with no practitioner history. Developed Telekinesis in early adolescence and found the Grid before any institution found him.", "The CVD tried to recruit him three times. He declined each time. The fourth attempt, about eight years ago, produced an exchange with the assessor that became Grid legend. There has not been a fifth.", "Takes Class B demon operations, thin-spot escalations the standard squads cannot manage, and structural membrane damage. Through the Grid only. No CVD coordination. He decides the approach, he does the primary work, and he does not take direction during operations.", "He does not move objects. He moves the relationship between objects and the space they occupy. In a contested space, gravity, pressure and force become optional at his discretion.", "Response time is hours to weeks. Urgency does not change it. Interesting problems get faster responses than simple ones.", "What he cost himself. Isolation. Three separate Grid contacts have called him the loneliest person they know who does not appear to be aware of it. I have not asked him.", "Two operations with him in six years. Standing near him in the field feels like standing near a change in how physics works in that one place. It is not comfortable. It is effective. (G.U.)"]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Watanabe Sora. The Mediator", "lines": ["Vanguard rank. Telepathy. Works with both the CVD and the Grid. Former Kairo.", "A former Vessel Program operative. She shared her body with a contracted entity for four years and was released after the entity violated the contract terms. The exit was managed quietly. She kept her registration.", "The only practitioner I know who developed Vanguard Telepathy after a Vessel Program exit.", "She is the one you call when the demon can be talked to more effectively than removed. Her negotiations end in contracts, in voluntary departures, in entities that leave peacefully and do not come back. The outcomes are slower. They last longer.", "She only takes cases where she judges negotiation is viable. She will not negotiate with an entity that has shown it cannot honour an agreement. She does not explain her refusals.", "What she cost herself. The trust of someone who was never violated the way she was. She trusts entities more than most practitioners find comfortable. Her view is that contracts are real to entities, in ways the standard taxonomy does not admit.", "Three possession cases with her over four years. She is also the one who spoke most directly about my own compromise, working inside the CVD while objecting to several of its programs. I have thought about what she said many times. I have not resolved it. (G.U.)"]}}, {"p": "Under the Mediator's page was one more sheet. It was a photocopy of a CVD reference card with a red stamp in the corner, and Gin had written along its margin in pencil."}, {"p": "The card was about something Rei had never heard of. The CVD had a squad made of the dead. These were practitioners who had agreed, before they died, that their bodies could go on working afterwards. When they died, their souls went on to wherever the dead go. Then a demon was let into each empty body under a written contract, and the body kept working for the CVD. The card called it the Vessel Program. The squad's name was Kairo."}, {"img": "ch15_squad.jpg", "cap": "A CVD squad in the field"}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "廻路. Kairo. The Vessel Program Squad. Existence restricted. Need to know", "lines": ["What it is. A squad operating under the Vessel Program. Former practitioners whose pre-mortem consent authorised continued operational use of their bodies after crossing. Their souls have crossed and taken their own death paths. The bodies continue, operated by contracted entities, under formal agreements monitored by the Program's oversight team.", "The contract. Each entity has signed for a specific feeding register, specific behavioural parameters, no predatory escalation, cooperation with Program monitoring, and deployment only within agreed case types.", "The entity receives physical presence in the living world. A body, sensation, the warmth and density of embodied existence that entities who remember life often want back. The CVD receives operational assets it cannot recruit any other way.", "Contracts are monitored monthly. Drift is flagged and addressed.", "Why demons can honour contracts. Demons are malicious by nature. But malice and intelligence do not rule out rational self-interest. A demon offered something it values more than what it could take by force will sometimes accept. The body is the offer. The terms are the price.", "Function. Kairo is deployed on possession cases because its members recognise possession from the inside. They have caught possessions that standard assessment missed."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's pencil, down the margin of the Kairo card", "lines": ["One case worked with Kairo. Usami Hinata, twenty-four, an urban-sociology graduate student who gave pre-mortem consent. Usami is dead. The body is run by a Class C love-register demon that gave its operational name as Ren.", "Ren chose the Vessel Program over the Axis at the moment of crossing. Consistent contract compliance since transfer, eighteen months ago. Handles civilian interface. The warmth makes interview subjects comfortable.", "Ren reads the register of anything within ten metres, other demons included. Lives in Kōenji, works part-time at a bakery, keeps Usami's white mug with the blue stripe. What is left of Usami is the thing Ren holds on to.", "His line, when I asked how it works. \"Two of us in here. He does the talking. I do the wanting.\"", "Ren is not a liar. The most ethically straightforward entity contract I have seen. The arrangement appears genuine on both sides.", "The entity seems to find the work meaningful, the way entities with enough sustenance and the right expression do. I did not expect to write that sentence about a demon. (G.U.)"]}}, {"p": "Rei read the last line twice. Then the train pulled into Ōji, the short departure melody began to play on the platform, and he looked up. He closed the folder and sat with it on his knees until the doors began to close. Three people his brother had trusted, and every one of them had paid. A demon in Kōenji that kept a dead man's mug. And on the yellow note, a page twenty-two years old in a flat full of books, which might name the thing in Gin more truly than the whole CVD ever had. He got off the train one stop late and walked back under the cicadas."}], "n": 30}, {"title": "The Far-Shore Circle", "kind": "main", "src": "m_farshore", "blocks": [{"img": "place_toden_tram.jpg", "cap": "The Toden tram through the back streets of Kita"}, {"p": "The newest knot in Gin's ledger had an address beside it, and Rei copied it onto the back of his hand. It was in Takinogawa, a quiet part of Kita west of Ōji station. In the evening he walked down to the Ōji-ekimae stop and waited for the Toden. Its platforms were no bigger than a bench, and its rails ran through back streets past houses close enough to touch. When the car came, its bell rang twice as it pulled in."}, {"p": "Inside, the air conditioning was weak and the windows were open. The car climbed slowly past the trees of Asukayama park, where the cicadas were so loud they drowned out the motor. An old woman sat opposite Rei with a paper bag of chrysanthemums for a grave. A boy in a baseball uniform slept with his cap over his face and his glove in his lap. Every few minutes the driver said the name of the next stop in a soft, tired voice. Rei got off at Takinogawa-itchōme and walked the rest of the way through narrow streets, past small houses with Obon lanterns by their doors and a vending machine humming alone on a corner."}, {"p": "The address did not go to a home. It went to a narrow building with a shuttered noodle shop on the ground floor and a stairwell that hummed. It hummed faintly, the way a transformer hums. Rei felt it as pressure in the fillings of his teeth before he heard it as sound. On the second landing someone had left a child's sandals paired neatly against the wall, the way the shoes of the dead are paired at a wake. The gift Rei always kept folded down behind his breastbone opened one degree without his permission, the way an old wound aches before rain."}, {"p": "By the third floor the hum had become something almost like chanting. But no one was using their voice. It was the membrane itself being made to ring, struck again and again and held. Rei felt a cold drop in his stomach. He was standing at the back door of a room where people had learned to do on purpose what he had spent his whole life trying to keep accidental."}, {"img": "ch19_possessed_leader.jpg", "cap": "The Far-Shore Circle kneeling around their teacher"}, {"p": "Through the gap of the door he saw them. Thirty people, maybe thirty-five, kneeling in seiza on cushions. Seiza is the formal way of kneeling in Japan, sitting back on the heels with the back straight, and it hurts after a while unless a person is used to it. The cushions were flat zabuton, the square floor cushions every Japanese home keeps for guests. The people were not in rows. They were in a ring, and the ring did not face a shrine or a screen or a priest. It faced inward, toward a single woman at the centre. She wore a grey summer kimono, her hands loose in her lap, her eyes closed. Every face in the circle was turned toward her, the way sunflowers turn toward the sun. It was not quite worship. It was what happens to people who have found the one source of light in a dark place."}, {"img": "place_farshore_white_room.jpg", "cap": "The white room in Takinogawa, the wall of the missing and the ring around the woman in grey"}, {"p": "The room was white. White walls, white paper over the windows, floor cushions and nothing else. Nothing else except one wall. That wall was covered from floor to ceiling in photographs of missing people. Snapshots, school portraits, a wedding photo cut down to one face, a man on a fishing boat squinting into the sun. Some were laminated in plastic, the way posters are laminated when they are taped to station pillars in the rain. Rei knew that kind of photograph. After the tsunami of March 2011, thousands of people were never found. Their families carried photographs like these up and down the coast for months and held them up to strangers. There were hundreds here. Each one was somebody's unfinished grief, pinned where the ring could see it."}, {"p": "The air around the woman was doing something his eyes would not show him clearly. It shimmered like heat over asphalt. But the room was still, and the shimmer was cold. Rei had walked into more haunted rooms than he could count. He had never walked into one that was this organised about it."}, {"p": "Then the woman at the centre spoke, without opening her eyes and without raising her voice. She said his brother's surname, which was also his own, into the ringing quiet. In a voice of perfect hospitality she added that the seat at the eastern edge of the circle had been kept open for some time now. The tea was still warm. There was no need to keep standing in a doorway as though he were not expected. The congregation did not turn to look at him. That was the worst part. They did not need to. Whatever was looking at him was looking through all of them at once. It had been waiting, and it was pleased."}, {"p": "Rei took the eastern seat. Standing in the doorway would only prove he was afraid. Kneeling in the ring put him close enough to read."}, {"p": "He learned the shape of the room the way he learned the last hour of a drowned man. Nobody explained it to him. He read it from the thing itself. These were not believers who had come for comfort and stayed for company. They were the bereaved of a city that had too many of them. A widow whose husband had gone into the 2011 water and was never properly sent home. A mother whose son's name was on a hospital ward that no longer answered. And the young man from Sumida, the one from Gin's ledger, whose grief had been eating his sleep until something patient reached in and ate the grief instead. Each of them had carried a wound across the membrane, a thin place worn by loss. The circle did not heal those thin places. It harvested them."}, {"p": "The woman kneeling on his right leaned over while the ring breathed, as if they were old friends. She smelled of mothballs and menthol rub. \"You're new,\" she whispered. \"Don't worry. This is only a small one tonight.\""}, {"p": "\"A small one?\""}, {"p": "\"Saturdays are the big circle.\" She patted his knee. \"New grief arrives on Saturdays.\""}, {"p": "\"How did you find it?\" Rei asked."}, {"p": "\"I didn't. Sensei found me.\" Her eyes were shining. \"March. The memorial, on the anniversary. The fourth one, it was. There were so many people and I was at the back, I couldn't even see the stage. And a woman in grey came all the way through the crowd and took both my hands.\" She showed him, folding her own dry hands round his. \"She said my husband's name. Before I'd said a word. How could she know that?\" She let go. \"Others came from the hospitals. Some from the little Tuesday circles at the community rooms. We call those the letting-go circles. Everyone is found somewhere.\""}, {"p": "She said it the way people describe being rescued. Sensei is the word for a teacher, and also for a doctor, and for anyone people look up to."}, {"p": "A line came back to Rei from the chapter in Gin's book about cults. He had skimmed it once, weeks ago, and thought it was jargon. A cult, stripped to its frame, is a possessed person, plus a congregation, plus a spirit operation. He had pictured robes and chanting. Here it was in a white room above a noodle shop. The possessed person at the centre. The congregation kneeling round her. And underneath them the operation, which he could feel running the way an engine is felt through a floor."}, {"p": "Gin had written that every congregation has two tiers, and that both must be read. Most of the ring were the first tier. They were ordinary people with no gift at all. They gave devotion, work, money and cover. They were the numbers, and a standing pool for the thing to feed on. At the worst houses, Gin had written, they were also bodies that could be offered. Most of them would never learn what sat at the centre. They had been sold a divine presence and given the comfort of belonging, and for them that was enough."}, {"p": "But not all of them. Rei counted four who were different. They were mediums, the working tier, people who could touch the veil and run the rites. They sat a little straighter, and their registers were open to the centre like taps. Across the ring a young woman in a school cardigan carried something inside her. It was folded in her like a letter in an envelope, quiet and settled. It had not been forced in. It had been placed there, and she had said yes. Gin's phrase for it came back exactly. Mutual-agreement possession. A spirit is put into a willing medium to make her stronger, in the service of the centre. She glowed with it. She was stronger than she had ever been in her life, and every bit of that strength pointed at the woman in grey."}, {"p": "Two places further round sat something worse. Two men, one of them in the reflective vest of a Grid courier. Their faces were present and still, and nobody was quite at home behind them. Something small and wrong looked out instead. Rei's register flinched away from them. These had been mourners once. Grief by grief the circle had emptied them. Into the empty places it had poured lesser things, stray spirits, minor demons, small hungers that did what they were told. They were hollowed. They were vessels now. Rei looked at the courier's vest and wondered how many doors in the city it could walk through without anyone asking a question. This was not only a congregation. It was an army, built one grief at a time."}, {"p": "Thirty-five private losses, gathered into one room and tuned to one note. The woman at the centre was the lens that focused them. And the relief on every face was real. It was the realest thing in the room. To be relieved of grief is a mercy, and each of them had truly been shown mercy."}, {"p": "Rei made himself follow the current to where it went. It was the only question that mattered, and he already half knew the answer. The harvested thinness did not stay here. It was being drawn away, east and down, toward the old flooded ground in Kita where he had felt the membrane scratched on purpose. It was being drawn toward the held breath before the window breaks. The circle and the drained pool where Kaito preached were one operation with two mouths. Gin's ledger bound the grateful, one careful favour at a time. Kaito's sermons tore at the wall from outside. And this room gathered the city's grief into a battery, and the thing in the woman drew it down. The horror was not that these people had been tricked. The horror was that they had been helped, and that their being helped was fuel."}, {"p": "The man from Sumida knelt three seats to his left. When Rei let his gift brush him, he felt the knot the ledger had described. He felt the soothed grief and the leash of gratitude. Here in the circle it was doubled. The Healer's mercy and the Far-Shore's harvest had both passed through this man, and he no longer remembered who he had been before either of them. He turned his head, mild and kind, and asked if Rei had also lost someone in the water. His face was so open and so free of pain that Rei understood how cruel his next thought was. To save this room, he might have to give grief back to people who would rather die than carry it again."}, {"img": "char_fukai-rinka-kishin.jpg", "cap": "Fukai Rinka, and the thing called Kishin behind her face"}, {"p": "When she finally opened her eyes, the congregation breathed out together. It was a long, soft sound, like a room of sleepers turning over. Several of them swayed where they knelt, suddenly tired, the way people are tired after being carried a long way and gently set down. She looked at Rei across the ring, and for one clear second she was only a woman. Close to forty. Kind around the mouth. A teacher's patience in the set of her shoulders, a few grey hairs left in on purpose. The kind of person who would remember a stranger's name and ask after his mother."}, {"p": "Rei felt the trap in that clear second even while it worked on him. The thing wearing her was letting him see the host on purpose. It was like a card player who shows one honest card so the table will trust the rest of the hand."}, {"p": "He knew the name. It was in the CVD copies Gin kept in a drawer at the flat. Fukai Rinka. A registered clairsentient, thirty-eight years old, gentle and good at her work, which was exactly how it had found her. Her file was old, and the last line in it said alive. That was true of the body. Something had come for her in 2012, and it had not taken her the screaming way. A kishin is not a possessor that fights for the wheel. It is a resident. It moves in and turns the lights out one by one."}, {"p": "Rei reached under the teacher with his sight, the way he had reached into the man from Sumida. He expected to find two presences. In the man from Sumida there had been a person and a leash. In Rinka there was only one thing. The woman was gone. In the ring they called her Kanna-sensei, the way people call a thing teacher when it has taught them to feel better. And under the teacher, patient and huge and not bothering to pretend, was the one the Grid filed under a single word. Kishin."}, {"p": "At her throat, on a cord, hung a small grey river stone. It was smooth and plain, the kind of stone a child puts in a pocket on a beach. Rei's sight touched it, and he expected an anchor, the grounding object a practitioner keeps to find the way back to themselves. It was not one. It hummed with Rinka's last true signal, warm and human and frightened. Anyone who read this woman would find the stone first. They would feel the gentle clairsentient still in there, and they would reach in to save her. It was bait. Rei pulled his sight back as if from a hot pan. Across the ring the kind mouth smiled very slightly, because it had felt him touch it."}, {"p": "Here Kishin did not bother with the sermon it preached through Kaito in the drained pool. It did not hide behind mercy the way the Healer did. It simply let Rei feel its appetite, plainly, the way a person feels cold air rising from a deep well. And it wanted him to understand one fact. It was older than the politics of the seat-holders, older than the plan for the membrane, and it meant to sit above all of them. Every grief that arrived in this room fed it toward that crown. To Kishin, the Healer was a thief at the same harvest. And Rei was the most interesting thing to come up the humming stairs in months. He was Yushiro's anchor, the one person walking toward Gin instead of away. He was grief that had not yet been spent."}, {"p": "She lifted the iron pot beside her, the one that had been keeping warm the whole time, and poured a second cup. She did it as though this conversation had been planned. As though Rei's name had been written in a book of hers, the way the favours were written in his brother's. She did not quite say drink the tea. The kindness in it was real, and the bill in it was real, and both being real at once was the whole nature of the thing. Somewhere east and down, the battery this room had spent the evening charging was waiting to be drawn on. The woman pouring his tea was the only person in Tokyo who knew who was drawing it. She would tell him, warmly. The price was one she had not named yet, and he would not see it until it had already been paid."}], "n": 31}, {"title": "The Tea That Costs You Something", "kind": "main", "src": "m_tea", "blocks": [{"p": "The tea was genmaicha, green tea mixed with grains of toasted rice. It is the cheap, everyday tea of Japanese kitchens, the kind that smells a little like popcorn. It was the most dangerous thing Rei had drunk all week, because it was so ordinary. Kishin understood that the way to a guarded person is not through fear. It is through the small kindness that person has been too busy to receive. Rinka poured and asked, in the host's gentle voice, whether he had eaten. Whether he was sleeping. Whether the heat was keeping him up. Each question landed in the part of him that no one had asked about since the fourteenth, the night Gin went quiet. That part of him had been holding his brother's vanishing alone, in a city of nine million. He had to set the cup down so his hand would not give him away."}, {"p": "This was what it wanted, and it was never money. It wanted him to be comfortable near it. Comfort was how the wearing-down got in, one warm hour at a time. Then one day a person would find they could not think a hard thought about Kanna-sensei without feeling a small grief at their own disloyalty."}, {"p": "Rei made himself ask the only question worth the climb. Who drew the battery down?"}, {"p": "Rinka answered without games, because Kishin gained nothing by hiding it and everything by being the one who told him. \"Both,\" she said. \"The Healer comes for it gently, in the small hours. What he takes, he uses to soften the seams in Kita so they will part without tearing. A surgeon thinning a wall into a membrane he can lift clean. And the boy who preaches draws for me, in great greedy pulls, because I do not want the wall lifted. I want it broken. The held breath let out all at once.\" She poured more tea he had not asked for. \"The Healer thinks he steals from me. I let him. A thief who drinks from my cup is a thief I can always find.\" She let the next part land softly. \"And lately the grief has been coming faster. Two of us pulling at the same harvest is very good for the harvest.\""}, {"p": "\"He's hunting you,\" Rei said. It was a guess, and it was right."}, {"p": "\"He means to kill me.\" She said it the way people mention rain. \"A demon can only be killed on this side from inside a body. That is what your brother is to him, you understand. A hand to do it with.\" The kind eyes rested on him. \"It is a door I have not yet had to close. There is time.\""}, {"p": "Rei read the room while she spoke, and the thing in her did not seem to mind being read. He did not think it was afraid. He thought, quietly, that it should be."}, {"p": "And there it was, the thing he had climbed the stairs without knowing he needed. His brother's captor and the cult's god were not two separate horrors. They were two ends of one rope, and the rope was being paid out from this room. He should have felt the satisfaction of a case coming together. Instead he felt the warm cup in his hands, and Rinka's patient eyes on him, and the terrible peace of the moment, like a family kitchen at night. He had already begun to owe her. Not for a service. For a comfort he had accepted. The bill for comfort is the hardest bill to read, because it never arrives as a number. It arrives as a new softness, in the exact place where a person used to keep his edge."}, {"break": "How the ring is built"}, {"p": "Around them the circle was ending. People stretched their legs and murmured to each other. Somebody slid a window open behind the white paper, and the night came in. Rei heard the cicadas in a garden below, a motorbike far off, and the small bell of the last streetcar going down toward Ōji. After the ringing silence of the circle, the ordinary noise sounded almost rude. An older woman in an apron moved round the ring with a lacquered wooden box, and each person laid an envelope in it with a name written on the front. She carried the box to a cupboard, locked it and hung the key on a cord round her neck. Then she checked the clock against a handwritten timetable taped inside the cupboard door and wrote something on it."}, {"img": "ch17_knowledge.jpg", "cap": "The circle's Keeper, who holds the money, the room and the schedule"}, {"p": "\"Our Keeper,\" Rinka said, following his eyes. \"She keeps the house. The money, the room, the schedule, the photographs on the wall. Without her none of this would stand up for a week.\""}, {"p": "Gin had a line for that too. A cult can survive losing its Voice. It rarely survives losing its Keeper, because the Keeper holds the daily work of tending it. Rei looked at the woman in the apron, at her tired, careful hands. He understood that she believed she was doing the most loving thing she had ever done."}, {"p": "He let his reading go outward through the building. Gin's chapter sorted a cult's people into layers, and Rei could see every layer from here. The ring kneeling here tonight were the Faithful. They knew there was a presence. They served it and brought the offerings, and they had come far enough to be locked in. Below them, spread across the ward, were the people who never climbed the humming stairs at all. They went to the weekly letting-go circles in borrowed municipal rooms, the grief meetings with free tea and paper tissues. Gin called that outer ring the Unknowing. They were the cover, the numbers and the place where new members were found. And the thing at the centre took a thin, low feeding from every one of those rooms without anyone noticing."}, {"p": "\"And your Voice?\" Rei asked. \"Who does it speak through, out there?\""}, {"p": "\"Our Voice is in the city tonight,\" Rinka said. \"In a drained pool, with forty people who love him.\" She meant Kaito. Gin had written that the Voice is often a real medium, and the most completely deceived person in the room. Rei thought of the courier preaching in the dead hour. He was not sure Kaito was deceived any longer. He thought Kaito might simply be empty."}, {"p": "\"And the one who saw it first? The one it all started with?\""}, {"p": "Rinka touched her own chest, lightly, over the river stone. \"The first to see the far shore,\" she said. \"She is with us still. In spirit.\" Gin had a word for that person as well. The Touched. The Touched is the one whose first experience of the veil the whole doctrine is built around. Sometimes the Touched and the Voice are the same person. Sometimes the Touched is dead, and the cult is built on the memory of what happened to her. Rei looked at the stone and did not reach for it again."}, {"p": "The rites were laid out in front of him if he chose to see them. A rite is any act a group repeats to bind itself to what it serves, and Gin's chapter listed them in order. The naming came first, when the ring first called the presence the Far Shore and spoke of it as a being owed devotion. Then the offering, the envelopes in the lacquered box, the names of the dead given every week. Then the proof, the cold shimmer at the centre, the moment each mourner heard a loved voice in their ear exactly when doubt would have come. And somewhere past those, kept private, was the binding rite. Gin said that whoever found a group's binding rite had found the exact line between its victims and its participants."}, {"p": "Rei asked what the answer would cost, because he would not leave owing a thing he could not see. He had watched what unseen debts did to the people in his brother's ledger. Kishin smiled through Rinka's mouth and told him the price, in the only currency it kept."}, {"p": "It did not want his blood or his service or his soul. Those were merchants' words, for merchants' appetites. It wanted the next grief he carried to come here first. Not all his grief. Just the next fresh one, whenever it landed, before he had decided alone what to do with it. The way the man from Sumida had brought his. The way the widow had brought hers. He would bring it here while it was still warm, kneel in the eastern seat, and let the room take its weight off him for one evening. Then the knowledge he had bought tonight would be paid in full. It was, she pointed out gently, the same agreement everyone in the ring had made. And look how lightly they carried their losses now."}, {"p": "So that was the binding rite. Not a blade or an oath. A seat on the eastern side and one evening's relief."}, {"img": "ch19_funnel.jpg", "cap": "A circle of chairs, the funnel that draws people down"}, {"p": "Rei traced it then, the way he would trace a poisoning, using the widow who had whispered to him. Gin had walked backwards through the funnel with enough former members to draw it. It was the same shape almost every time. First the hook. A real experience of the veil. Her husband's voice in the tide at the seawall, a thing that had truly happened to her, and nobody can argue a person out of a thing that truly happened. Then the meeting. Someone who did not tell her she had imagined it. Someone who took both her hands and said, \"I know what that was, and you are not alone, and there are others.\" The validation is the recruitment, Gin had written. Everything after it is detail. Then the frame. A name for the experience, a doctrine, the far shore where the dead wait, and a being at the centre who could reach them and was owed something for it. Then the deepening. Come once. Come weekly. Bring a name in an envelope. Stop seeing the sister who said this was not healthy. A flat demand would be refused. A gentle slope is walked down. And last the lock. The thing that cannot be taken back. The eastern seat."}, {"p": "No one joins, Gin had written under his drawing of it. People are drawn down."}, {"p": "And then, because he was honest, Rei ran the same funnel on himself. The hook was the kitchen at 3:47 in the morning, and a brother gone. The meeting was tonight, a voice asking if he had eaten. The frame was being told he was expected, that his name was already in her book. The deepening was a second cup of tea. The lock was the price on the table."}, {"p": "He thought of Gin. He thought of the grief he had carried up these stairs and would carry down them, the one with his brother's name on it. It was still unspent, and still his. It was the only thing keeping him walking toward Gin while the whole city walked away. In its perfect hospitality, Kishin had asked for the one grief that must never be soothed. The day Rei stopped hurting over Gin would be the day he stopped coming for him. The demon knew this. That was the cleverness of the price. It had not asked him to betray his brother. It had asked him to be comforted about him. It trusted that he was tired enough, after a week of this heat and this dread and this loneliness, to want comfort more than he wanted the edge that comfort would cost."}, {"p": "\"You keep your word, I suppose,\" Rei said."}, {"p": "\"Always.\" Rinka tilted her head. \"I am not like your brother's thief, who signs forms for the privilege of being trusted. Or the little contracted one in Kōenji, who keeps his Usami like a houseplant and waters him every morning.\" The smile did not change. \"I did not keep Fukai like that. There was no need.\""}, {"p": "Rei had read that name on a train only days ago, in pencil, down the margin of a CVD card. Ren, the demon in the Vessel Program, kept what was left of Usami. Kishin had kept nothing of Rinka at all. It had turned the lights out one by one, and it wanted him to know it had felt nothing while it did."}, {"p": "Rinka set the iron pot down with a soft iron sound. Around them the congregation was gathering their sandals and saying goodnight. They were ordinary people, stepping out into the Obon dark a little lighter than they had come. Not one of them looked back at the centre that had carried them. Rei could leave the same way. He could take everything he had learned tonight and owe nothing but the next ache. Surely the next ache was months away. Surely he would be stronger by then. That was exactly the size of lie this room was built to make feel like sense."}, {"p": "He stood up. He did not say yes. He did not say no, either, because he did not trust his voice to stay his own. The tea had gone cold in his hands, and he had not noticed it cooling. That, he thought, was the whole lesson."}, {"p": "He was at the door, one hand on the frame, when she spoke again. She did not raise her voice. She never needed to."}, {"p": "\"Saturday, Rei-kun,\" Kanna-sensei said. \"Bring whatever you are carrying by then. The eastern seat will be warm.\""}, {"p": "Kun is the friendly ending people put on a boy's name, the way a teacher speaks to a favourite pupil. Rei went down the humming stairs past the child's sandals, and he did not let himself count how many days were left until Saturday."}], "n": 32}, {"title": "The Battery of Grief", "kind": "main", "src": "m_battery", "blocks": [{"p": "On the street below the humming building the August night was thick and wet, and the cicadas had not stopped since dusk. The last of the congregation went off in twos and threes toward the tram stop, fanning themselves with paper fans, talking about the heat and about what they would cook tomorrow. A man on a bicycle rode slowly past with a white lantern swinging from the handlebars. Across the road a vending machine glowed, full of cold tea and coffee in cans. Rei bought a can of barley tea, held it against his neck for a while, and then leaned against the shutter of the noodle shop."}, {"p": "He finally let himself draw the thing he had been refusing to draw all week. The wiring diagram. A diagram is colder than dread, and he needed something cold."}, {"img": "npc_rei_full.jpg", "cap": "Rei under the streetlight with Gin's book"}, {"p": "He drew it on the back page of Gin's book, under the streetlight. Here was the city's grief, arriving the way it always had, a steady inheritance from the dead who were never properly sent home. Here was the Far-Shore Circle, gathering that grief into a battery and asking for nothing the bereaved were not desperate to give. And here were the two demons, drawing the battery down toward two opposite kinds of ruin. The Healer, the thing living in his brother, was thinning the Kita membrane gently. It wanted the dead let through cleanly, and the Thirteenth Seat filled with a quiet that Rei might almost mistake for kindness. And Kishin, through Rinka's kind mouth and Kaito's grieving one, was tearing at the same wall. It wanted the held breath to break all at once, and the muzzle to come off the dead forever. One wanted a door. The other wanted a wound. They were fighting over the same wall and feeding from the same room."}, {"p": "He turned back through the book to check a line, and a page he had flipped past a dozen times stopped him. Gin had drawn this diagram before. He had drawn it twice."}, {"p": "The page needed some knowing to read, and Rei knew just enough. Every practitioner's gift opened onto one of thirteen emotions, which the CVD called registers. Grief was one register, rage another, and so on round the wheel. For each register there was a seat, and someone or something held it. Gin's page was about who held them."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's page. The wheel, drawn twice", "lines": ["The thirteen emotions sit twice. Picture two trees with the same thirteen fruit.", "The first tree is the Thirteen as I have met them. Thirteen living mediums, each holding one register open and steady. Grief, rage, love, fear, wonder, despair, joy, shame, hunger, courage, longing, awe, duty. To the centre they are light anchors, clean points of pure register it feeds from to keep the membrane taut. A living holder, sane and willing, is the strongest thing in the world.", "The second tree is the Qliphoth. Thirteen demon-shells of the same emotions. A Qliphoth does not make an emotion. It usurps an anchor. It eats the living holder out from under the seat and wears the register as a hollow shell. The seat still reads filled, but the light is gone and a hunger sits where a person stood.", "So the war under everything is which tree holds the wheel. Light anchors can kill demons. Demons possess anchors.", "VI. Despair. The holder is failing. Something on the dark side of the wheel is eating the seat out from under him.", "XIII. Duty. Vacant since 2009. No living holder. The centre holds it directly from inside. Kita began to destabilise that year. After 2011 it went faster.", "Only two shells on the dark tree have names I can find. The rest are loose across Tokyo and behind the veil. (G.U.)"]}}, {"p": "Gin had left the names blank. Rei did not need him to fill them in. He wrote them himself, in pencil, next to his brother's ink. Beside VI, Despair, he wrote Kishin. Beside XIII, Duty, he held the pencil still for a long moment. Then he wrote Yushiro."}, {"p": "Underneath, smaller, Gin had added one more line in a different pen, as if he had come back to it on another night."}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Gin's later line, under the dark tree", "lines": ["If one shell ever fed far enough, it would not want a seat. It would want the crown. The old books call the crown of the dark tree Thaumiel. A thing sitting there would be the inverse of whatever holds the centre now. Where the centre holds the membrane shut, it would tear it open and rule what pours through. A hell on this side, with a throne in it. (G.U.)"]}}, {"p": "Rei read it twice. The old books Gin meant were the books of the Kabbalah, which drew the world as a tree, and the dark tree as its shadow. Thaumiel was the name they gave to the top of that shadow. Then Rei looked up at the third-floor windows, where the white paper glowed faintly over the noodle shop. He understood now what he had felt across the ring when Kishin let its appetite show. It meant to sit above all of them. Not only on the Despair seat it was already eating out from under its failing holder. Above the whole dark tree. Kishin was feeding to become the Inverted God, the dark mirror of the Axis. Every envelope in the lacquered box was a step toward that crown. If it got there, the two trees would stop being a drawing. The thirteen demons would move against the thirteen strongest mediums, and the city would be the ground they fought on."}, {"p": "And the Healer wanted it dead first. That was the other half of the drawing, and it was the half that made Rei's mouth go dry. The Healer did not want the crown. It wanted the Duty seat on the far side of the veil, the empty chair, so it could sit there beside the few of its own kind that it trusted. It did not want to rule the living. But it could not let Kishin rule them either. A demon can only be killed on this side from inside a body, Rinka had said, and his brother was the body. Gin was awake in there, watching it work."}, {"break": "The field card"}, {"p": "A taxi went by with its light off. Somewhere above, an air conditioner dripped steadily onto the pavement. Rei had one more page of Gin's to use, and he used it the way Gin meant it to be used. It was a small card ruled in boxes, drawn for reading a group from the outside. Rei held it under the streetlight and went down it with his pencil."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "WARNING SIGNS field card. Reading a group from the outside", "lines": ["[x] The comfort, luck or healing the group offers is real and reliable. Not vague faith, but something that demonstrably works. This is the tell, not the absence of one.", "[x] A charismatic central figure who defers every question to the presence. Never claims authority personally. Always points past themselves to It.", "[x] Rites that escalate in privacy. What newcomers see and what long members do have quietly diverged.", "[x] Members who have given up money, time, secrets or outside relationships and describe the loss as growth.", "[x] A before that no one will discuss. The originating event is sacred, fixed, and not to be examined.", "[x] Veil residue at the meeting site that does not match the doctrine. The thing present is not the thing described.", "[x] Members getting thinner, flatter, grief-heavier over months while insisting they have never been better. Watch the slope, not the snapshot."]}}, {"p": "Every box. He had not needed to think about any of them. The widow's sister. The lacquered box. The Touched who was with them in spirit. The cold shimmer that called itself the far shore. The two hollowed men under the photographs."}, {"p": "What stopped his breath on the dark street was the junction. In any honest wiring diagram there is one mark that matters, the place where the current could be cut. The answer was not the Healer, because he could not bring himself to cut Gin. The answer was not Kaito, because his sermon was built from true griefs, and making him a martyr would only fill the ring faster. The answer was the battery itself. The circle. Kanna-sensei and the thirty-five lightened faces. Cutting there meant giving every one of those people their grief back. It meant taking the peace away from the man from Sumida, and the widow, and the mother. It meant tearing comfort out of the bereaved to starve two catastrophes of the fuel they shared. The cleanest cut in the whole diagram ran straight through the most innocent room in it. Rei had read enough bodies to know that the cause of death is rarely where the cruelty is. The cruelty here was that mercy had been the supply line all along."}, {"p": "His phone had been warm in his pocket through the whole meeting. He took it out now and found what he knew he would find. There was a text from the unknown number that wrote in his brother's voice. It had been sent while he was drinking tea he should not have drunk."}, {"p": "<em>You found the circle. Good. I was going to send you there next week.</em>"}, {"p": "Under it was a second message, and Rei could feel the pause before it even in the timestamp."}, {"p": "<em>Don't cut the battery, Rei. I need it gentle. So do they.</em>"}, {"p": "It was the most reasonable thing the Healer had ever said to him, and it was asking him to leave the supply line running. And there was a worse part. On this one cold point, his brother's captor and his brother's mercy and his own exhausted heart all agreed, for the first time. That kept him standing very still against the shutter, while the cicadas sawed the dark to pieces."}], "n": 33}, {"title": "From Gin's Book. Three Cults on File", "kind": "gin", "src": "gin", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei did not go home after the text. The streetcars had stopped for the night, so he walked back to Ōji beside the empty tram rails, down the hill past the dark trees of Asukayama. The station was still lit. A few late passengers came out of the gates, and a man in a suit stood by the taxi rank eating a rice ball in three bites. Next to the station was an all-night family restaurant, one of the cheap chain places with big windows and plastic menus, where students study and night workers eat at three in the morning. Rei went in, ordered coffee he did not drink, and opened Gin's book at the chapter on entity cults."}, {"p": "He had skimmed it once. Now he read it the way Gin had written it, slowly, as a file on a living thing. Near the end Gin had copied out three cases from the active files, with names removed where he had to. He had chosen them, he wrote, because they sat at different points on the only scale that matters. At one end are groups whose members are victims. At the other are groups whose members are participants. At one end a cult can still be reached and treated. At the other it is built so deep into people's lives that it cannot be pulled out."}, {"img": "higankai_vesselfarm.jpg", "cap": "A cult gathering by candlelight"}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "THE QUIET HOUR. Bereavement circle. Kita-ku, a community hall", "lines": ["Front. A weekly grief-support meeting. Free. Run out of a borrowed municipal room. Genuinely comforting. People leave lighter.", "They worship. A comforting presence that speaks the words of the dead through the woman who runs it. To the members this is not worship. It is being heard.", "Actually. A single spirit, possibly low demon, the residue is ambiguous, farming structured grief from forty bereaved people on a schedule. The comfort is real. So is the feeding.", "Feeding. Grief, given willingly and repeatedly. The richest and gentlest food on file. No one is harmed in any way they can point to.", "The Voice. A genuine clairsentient, unregistered, mid-fifties. Lost a son. Does not know what she is hosting. Believes every word she relays. The most deceived person in the room.", "CVD status. Monitored. Not cleared. Clearing it means retraumatising forty grieving people and taking away the only thing that has helped them.", "How it ends. Badly, if the Voice dies before it is addressed. An unfed, ownerless grief-farmer curdles fast. This is the open file I think about. (G.U.)"]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "THE LOOM. Distributed. Online-rooted. No fixed site", "lines": ["Front. An aesthetic. A mood, a tag, a scattered young collective that shares a visual language and a sense of being quietly lucky. It looks like a subculture, not a faith.", "They worship. Nothing they would call worship. A thread you can feel for. Small uncanny luck for those who keep faith with it. You do not pray to the Loom. You stay woven in.", "Actually. A demon running a decentralised feeding network. No congregation to clear, no single Keeper, the tether spread across objects and accounts, and a doctrine that copies itself.", "Feeding. Diffuse low-grade devotion and the steady anxiety of staying woven in. Thin per head, but the head count grows without limit and without anyone ever meeting.", "The Voice. None identified. The demon does not need one. The aesthetic speaks for it. This is the modern shape and it frightens me more than the old ones.", "CVD status. Open. We have no method for clearing a thing with no room to enter. The art-collective file near the Higashi thin spot is a node of this. I have not closed it.", "How it ends. Unknown. We have never ended one. We have only watched members walk out one at a time, and most do not. (G.U.)"]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "THE TENTH DOOR. Household cult. Old-money lineage. Embedded", "lines": ["Front. A private family. Observances, an ancestral shrine, a relative everyone defers to who is never quite seen. It reads as tradition, not cult. It reads as a Tower house that simply kept its rites.", "They worship. An ancestor. A founder who refused to cross and was kept, generation after generation, as a house asset. Somewhere in the line the keeping inverted, and the family began to serve what it had once cultivated.", "Actually. A spirit cultivated so long it has become a suffering being, fed on three centuries of one bloodline's devotion and grief, now too embedded in the house to separate without taking the family down too.", "Feeding. Generational. Built into the architecture, the calendar, the inheritance. The members were born locked. There was never a funnel for them, only a floor plan.", "The Voice. Passes by inheritance. The head of the house is the Mouth, whether they want to be or not. The current one, I am told, did not want to be.", "CVD status. Sensitive. Politically embedded, Tower-adjacent, and clearing it touches the Accord. Filed as do-not-touch-without-Direction-sign-off. Left, and watched.", "How it ends. It does not, yet. It is the warning the Towers do not put in writing. Cultivation, run long enough, can turn the keeper into the kept. (G.U.)"]}}, {"p": "After the three files came the playbook, the part Rei read twice because it was the part he would have to act on."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "The response. Assessment first, disposition second", "lines": ["The most expensive error in cult work is acting before assessing. Clearing a thing that should have been addressed, or addressing a thing that should have been cleared. Assessment answers one question. What is the relationship actually doing? Three answers, three dispositions.", "Address. When the humans are victims. They are inside a feeding arrangement they did not understand and would refuse if they did. The entity can be separated from them and the harm of separation can be borne. This is Reintegration's work, not a clearance team's. You sever the cultivation, cross or contain the entity, and then do the long pastoral work of putting forty people back together after you have taken away the thing that was holding them. Slow, expensive, and right more often than the framework admits.", "Terminate. When the entity is predatory and embedded. The relationship does active harm, the entity cannot be separated or is not worth separating, and the members are participants more than victims. Clearance team, full protocol, aftercare for whoever can still be reached. Clean on paper. Never clean in the room, because some of the people you are saving will fight you for the thing that is eating them.", "Leave and watch. When clearing causes more harm than the arrangement. The hardest disposition to defend on a form and often the correct one. The Quiet Hour is a leave-and-watch. So is the Tenth Door, for different reasons. One because the victims need it. One because the politics forbid touching it. It is not doing nothing. It is monitoring the feeding, containing the spread, holding a clearance plan ready, and accepting that today the least harm is to let a wrong thing continue under observation. I do not like writing that. It is true anyway.", "The Grid's parallel. The Grid usually knows first. It lives closer to the ground and has its own quiet ways of handling a soured cultivation without a clearance form. Sometimes the Grid has already addressed a thing the CVD is still assessing. Sometimes the Grid is protecting a cultivation the CVD would terminate. Read which before you move.", "Infiltration. Assume one of them is already inside. In the CVD, cases that close early, assessments that do not match, files you cannot open, transfers nobody explains. In the Grid, vouches you cannot trace, backup that thins out, bad intelligence that happens to help the entity. Not, confront immediately. Document quietly. (G.U.)"]}}, {"p": "Below the playbook, in a smaller hand, written later and faster, was a note that Rei did not want to read. He read it anyway."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin, at the bottom of the chapter", "lines": ["I reread the warning card after writing it and one line sat wrong with me for the rest of the evening. The thing present is not the thing described.", "I have used that test on a dozen groups. I have never once turned it on the thing I write these chapters with at three in the morning. The clarity that arrives when I sit down and is gone by breakfast.", "I told myself the difference is that I assess, and cult members do not. Then I noticed that is exactly what a cult member would tell themselves.", "I am leaving the thought here and not following it tonight. Rei is asleep. (G.U.)"]}}, {"p": "Rei sat with the cold coffee until the waitress stopped coming to the table. At the next booth a student had fallen asleep on her textbooks, and a pair of taxi drivers were eating curry in silence. The Far-Shore Circle was not on Gin's list. It was worse than all three. It had the Quiet Hour's gentle grief-farming. It had the Tenth Door's slow hollowing. And it had something of the Loom's reach, with its vessels walking the city in courier vests. Address, terminate, or leave and watch. He went down the three words with his pencil and could not tick any of them. Then he read the last note again, the one Gin had left at three in the morning. He wondered whether his brother had felt the thing in him long before it took him, and had called it clarity."}], "n": 33}, {"title": "The Counter-Demon Surfaces", "kind": "main", "src": "m_counter", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei could not face the flat. A little before midnight he paid for the coffee and went down into the Namboku line at Ōji. The platform was deep under the street and cool after the heat. Glass doors stood along its edge, so that nobody could fall or jump onto the tracks, and they slid open together with the train doors when the last trains came in. He got on the first one going south. He had no plan. He only wanted to be moving."}, {"p": "The car was half full of the tired and the drunk. A young salaryman slept with his head on his briefcase. A woman fanned herself with a folded train timetable. A couple in yukata sat holding hands with a goldfish in a plastic bag between them. And at the far end of the car sat a man who was crying without any expression on his face. The tears ran down a face so still that it should not have been able to make them. Nobody looked at him. In Tokyo, on a late train, nobody looks at anybody."}, {"p": "Rei looked. He had learned to follow the wrongness, and this was wrong. The address Kanna-sensei would not give him had arrived the way the worst addresses always did. Not on paper, but in a body. At Iidabashi the man got off and walked through the long tunnels to the Ōedo line, and Rei followed him. They rode one stop to Ushigome-Kagurazaka. The man went up into a side street that smelled of bleach and standing water. At the end of it was a laundromat that had been closed long enough for dust to gather on the machines. Behind the laundromat a basement door was propped open with a rice cooker."}, {"img": "ch19_cults.jpg", "cap": "The basement under the laundromat, Kaito at the centre"}, {"p": "The crying man went down. Rei counted to forty, the way he would on a stairwell that might collapse, and went down after him. The low room was lit only by the screens of a dozen phones lying face up on the floor. Each one played a video of a different person grieving, with the sound off. Standing in the middle of all that silent weeping was a young man in a delivery uniform. Rei understood at once that he was not the young man whose uniform it was."}, {"p": "He knew the face from the drained pool. Kaito. A courier who had been a medium, before the Far Shore emptied him and made him a vessel. Kishin's mouth."}, {"p": "Kaito turned to him, and the room turned with him, the way a register turns. The second voice in Rei's own chest stirred and answered before he could stop it. It was recognition, the awful recognition between two things of the same kind. One had spent its life pressed down. The other had stopped letting itself be pressed. \"You're the brother,\" the thing in Kaito said. The borrowed mouth was gentle, almost shy. It had a courier's politeness wrapped around something that had eaten its way up through the floor of the world. \"The Healer's brother. He talks about you. He thinks if he's careful enough you'll thank him in the end.\""}, {"p": "The phones on the floor kept weeping. The crying man had folded himself into a corner and gone slack, used up, a cell the battery had already half drained. \"I'm not going to be careful,\" the thing in Kaito said. It was not a threat. It was a confession, said with relief. \"Careful is the lie. The wall doesn't want to be a door. It wants to fall.\""}, {"p": "Rei was afraid, and he did not pretend otherwise. But under the fear, the diagram he had drawn on the dark street came into sharper focus. Here was the second end of the wire, made flesh. In the white room Kishin had worn Rinka like a teacher's cardigan. Here it spoke through a boy's own grief, and the grief gave the voice its shyness and its heat. It was younger than the Healer's logic, and louder. It was a grief that wanted to be a flood and not a tap. And it fed from exactly the same room of mourners his brother fed from. Two demons drinking from one cup, and arguing only about how to spill it."}, {"p": "It watched him work it out behind his eyes. \"You see it,\" it said, pleased and terrible. \"You're the only one who sees both of us at once. That makes you the most useful person in Tokyo. To him. And to me.\""}, {"p": "It preached, because that was what Kaito was for. The terrible thing was not that it lied. The terrible thing was that it did not lie. It told Rei about the held dead, the way a person tells a friend about a cruelty they saw and could not stop. The dead who were never sent home. The ones who died unregistered and unmourned and were given no rite. They had been kept all this time behind the Kita membrane, not at rest but standing at attention. A hundred years of held breath, in a city that had decided their grief was a public-health problem to be managed quietly. It said the Healer's plan was worse than its own, because it was kinder. A clean door, opened gently, let the institutions keep their schedule. It kept the membrane a managed valve, and the dead on a permitted release."}, {"p": "\"He wants to file them out,\" it said, and for the first time the borrowed voice had an edge. \"One reasonable form at a time. I want to open my hands and let them go all at once, and never be filed again.\""}, {"p": "Rei felt the gap then, the place where its logic did not close. He kept his face still over the second voice in him that wanted to nod. The gap was the living. It had done the sums of the dead with real tenderness, and it had left the breathing out completely. Nine million people would be standing in the flood when the held breath broke. The mothers and the couriers and the man crying in the corner would all inherit a hundred years of unmuzzled grief in a single August night."}, {"p": "\"And us,\" Rei said. It was the first thing he had said in minutes. \"The ones still alive. You've costed the dead to the yen and you've priced us at zero.\""}, {"p": "It looked at him with something that might, on the real courier's face, have been the beginning of doubt. Then the doubt closed over, because a demon that admitted the cost would have to stop. \"They were priced at zero first,\" it said softly. \"I'm only matching the books.\""}, {"p": "It was the most honest thing anyone had said to Rei since the kitchen at 3:47. And it was wrong in the way the most honest things can be wrong. It was true about everything except the one life standing in front of it. Rei understood now why the Healer was hunting it, and why the Healer was afraid. Not because this thing was stronger. Because it was purer. It was a grief with no use for the gentle lie, and his brother's whole reasonable plan depended on the lie holding. Two demons, one cup. One wanted to pour it out a careful drop at a time, forever. One wanted to throw it. And both had just looked at Rei and decided, in the same breath, that he was the table the cup stood on."}, {"img": "ext_sojo.jpg", "cap": "The Healer, wearing Gin's face"}, {"p": "Rei felt Gin before he saw him, the way he had learned to feel the gentle register move into a room. The air pushed back less. Everything sharp seemed to have been asked, politely, to be softer. The basement door at the top of the stairs did not open so much as agree to be open. His brother came down into the weeping light. It was Gin's face, and Gin's careful hands, and Gin's way of pausing on a step to be sure of his footing. And it was the Healer, wearing all of it like a coat tailored years ago and only now worn out into the rain."}, {"p": "The thing in Kaito went very still. The phones on the floor flickered, all of them, and the silent griefs on the screens leaned toward the newcomer like grass in the wind."}, {"p": "\"Kaito,\" his brother said gently to the courier. He used the host's name like a man who has done his reading. \"You're frightening my brother.\""}, {"p": "What happened next was the worst thing Rei had watched since he started watching, because it was not a fight. It was two doctors arguing over a patient who was the whole city. Their voices were so reasonable that Rei had to keep reminding himself the patient was dying either way. The Healer offered the thing in Kaito a seat at the table. It was not a figure of speech. It was a real place in the gentle plan, a controlled share of the flood. The membrane would open wide enough to feel like mercy, and narrow enough to keep the schedule."}, {"p": "Rei listened to the terms and recognised their shape. It was a contract offer. A register to feed on, a share, conditions. Watanabe Sora's page came back to him from the train, the Mediator who believed that contracts are real to entities. Maybe they were. But this one was not meant to be kept. Rinka had told him plainly what the Healer wanted. It wanted Kishin dead, and a demon can only be killed on this side from inside a body. Every reasonable clause coming out of his brother's mouth was a way to bring Kishin's mouth close enough to touch."}, {"p": "\"And if you won't sit,\" the Healer went on, still gently, \"there are other rooms. You know what the registry does with an unregistered medium it finds in a basement, Kaito. Integrate, or contain. There's a white room on a floor the lobby never mentions. I can have the forms filed by morning. I sign them now, you know.\""}, {"p": "\"Your cells can't hold what speaks through me,\" the thing in Kaito said. \"They'll hold the boy.\" It smiled with Kaito's mouth. \"That's what they're for.\""}, {"p": "The thing in Kaito refused the seat with the courtesy of someone completely certain. And all through it, Rei's brother kept glancing at him, checking his face. It was the old habit of an older brother making sure the younger one was not scared. But now the look was also a measurement. The Healer had understood, exactly as the other one had, that Rei was the weak point in both their plans. The one mind that could see the whole wiring. The table."}, {"p": "\"Go home, Rei,\" Gin said, in Gin's voice, with the Healer's intention behind it. \"Let me handle Kaito. You were never supposed to be this far down.\""}, {"p": "Rei stood in the middle of a dozen weeping screens. On one side was the brother he was trying to save. On the other was the flood that wanted to save the dead by drowning the living. If he went home, the Healer would have its quiet. If he stayed and fought, he would be fighting for one of them whether he meant to or not. They had spent the whole night arranging him like furniture. Both of them were waiting to see which way he would lean."}, {"p": "He did not lean. He turned his back on both of them. It was the hardest thing he had done all week, to put his brother behind him. He crossed the room through the light of the phones and knelt in front of the man folded in the corner, the one who had cried without a face on the train. Up close he was younger than Rei had thought, and very cold."}, {"p": "\"What's your name?\" Rei asked him. The man looked at him as if from the bottom of a well. Rei asked again, slowly, the way he would ask a frightened child. The man told him. Then Rei asked who he was crying for, and after a long time the man told him that too. A daughter. A first name, and the year she would have started school."}, {"p": "Rei said the daughter's name back to him out loud. Then he said it again, clearly, so that it filled the low room and went up the stairs. It was not a rite. It was only a name, said by someone who meant it. The man's empty face broke and became a face again. For the first time he wept with an expression, ugly and real and his own."}, {"p": "Behind Rei the basement was very quiet. He did not turn round. He felt the gentle register withdraw up the stairs the way it had come, agreeing to leave. He felt the other one leave the room too, like water going down a drain. When he finally looked, Kaito was sitting in the dust with his back to the wall. He was only a tired courier in a borrowed uniform. The phones on the floor were going dark one by one."}, {"p": "Rei waited until the last screen went black. In the dark the courier spoke, in a voice that was thin and hoarse and entirely his own."}, {"p": "\"He'll come back for me,\" Kaito said. \"Your brother. He always comes back.\""}, {"p": "Then a lighter clicked somewhere in the basement. When Rei turned toward the small flame, the place by the wall was empty."}], "n": 34}, {"title": "The Hunt Through Each Other", "kind": "main", "src": "m_hunt", "blocks": [{"p": "The trains had stopped. Rei sat on the steps of the closed station at Ushigome-Kagurazaka until the shutters went up just before five, and rode the first Ōedo train back to Iidabashi, and the first Namboku train north. The cars were nearly empty and very bright. A few night workers slept with their mouths open. An old woman in a sun hat held a cloth bundle on her lap, and Rei could smell incense on it, the kind people burn at family graves. When he came up the stairs at Ōji the sky was white, and the crows were already loud in the trees of Asukayama. The heat was coming back before the sun was properly up."}, {"p": "In the grey hour after, on the floor of his flat with the curtains still drawn against the Obon dawn, Rei did the only thing he knew how to do with a horror. He made it a document. He listed the grieving. The man from Sumida whom the circle had lightened. The widow. The mother. Kanna-sensei's thirty-five soothed faces. The man from the Ōedo line, and the daughter whose name Rei had said out loud. And the courier whose name was Kaito, whose grief seemed to have been the largest untended account in the ward. Beside each name he drew which demon needed them, and how."}, {"p": "The pattern that came up made his hands go still on the pen. They needed the same people. The Healer needed the mourners gentle and whole, so the membrane could be drawn down slowly through their willing grief. Kishin needed the mourners raw and breaking, so the membrane would tear. The hunt was not two predators chasing each other across the city. It was two doctors fighting over the same dozen patients. Every move either one made to win ran straight through a living room where someone was trying to grieve in peace."}, {"p": "Which means, he wrote. Then he sat looking at the words for a long time before he let himself finish the sentence. Which means there is no clean side. To starve Kishin's flood he would have to keep the mourners soothed. That meant letting the Healer go on soothing them. So the price of stopping the loud catastrophe was feeding the gentle one a little more every night. And to deny his brother his quiet door, he would have to let the griefs go raw, which handed the flood its fuel. The wiring was built so that any cut in one line charged the other. Rei had read enough poisonings to know the shape of a trap that uses its victim's own care as the mechanism. The more he loved the living, the more he served one demon. The more he loved the dead, the more he served the other. And the only person in the whole diagram placed to see this and refuse it was sitting on a cold floor at dawn, with ink on the side of his hand."}, {"p": "He thought about calling it in. The CVD had a word for what to do with a room like the Far-Shore Circle. Gin's playbook said address, and Gin's playbook said that addressing was the work of Reintegration. Rei had seen that word in the CVD's own brochures and never asked what it meant. He took Gin's book down from the shelf and found the chapter. It was the one place in the whole archive where his brother stopped writing to a reader and wrote to him."}, {"img": "rehab_cell.jpg", "cap": "A reintegration cell in the suppression wing"}, {"doc": {"kind": "letter", "title": "Gin's book. Reintegration. The word they use", "lines": ["What happens to a medium who loses control, breaks the law, or is simply found before they register. The CVD calls it rehabilitation. I have stood in the rooms. The word is doing a great deal of work.", "I have put this chapter off for a year. Writing it makes it mine to have known.", "If you are reading this, you were an unregistered medium yourself for most of your life, and you did not know it, because I made sure the registry never looked at you. This chapter is the reason I did that.", "There are three ways a medium comes to the CVD's attention without choosing to. Loss of control. A Correction Event, a crisis read that produces a casualty, a possession that completes in public, a child whose passive ability outruns their capacity to hold it. Criminality. And the most common and least forgiven, simply being found.", "The after-action reports say recovered, condition stable. A frightened Arbiter-rank sensitive cornered in a stairwell is not a person to the team. It is a Scale event with a stability number attached.", "Then there are two outcomes, and everyone inside says the same two words. Integrate, or contain.", "Integration can save people. I have watched it. It is also not a choice. Integration is conscription. The alternative to signing is not freedom. It is the second word.", "The suppression cell. A cube of metal about sixteen feet on a side. Five faces are a double lattice of silver and iron bars set into poured resin of sulfur, gunpowder and ground shards of holy bone, with the suppression glyphs printed on the outside where the occupant can never reach them. The sixth face is bars and a tray slot. Inside, everything is white. A bed, a desk, a chair, a shelf of books. The light is always on. They keep you medicated, because a mind that comes apart thins the membrane. For a practitioner it is not like being locked in a room. It is like being locked out of your own hands.", "And while they are held, they are trained. The facilities are on no map I was cleared to see. What I have, from three operatives who would only talk obliquely and one who talked because he could not stop, is this. The aim is obedience that has survived being broken. The name history already has for places like that is camps.", "The ones with operational value are placed under a higher-ranked medium, a Master in the older language, and used. As a decoy, drawing entity attention from a team that matters more. As bait, walked into a thin spot because something there will come for them. As a battery, a read, a held door. The report says asset attrition.", "Madness is not a risk in that system. It is a schedule. I have met four of the reintegrated. The stillness the cells leave reads as a place where something used to be.", "The cruelty is not a betrayal of the system's values. It is the system's values, fully costed.", "I do not regret the hiding. I regret that I could only hide one person. I ran out of time before I could even finish writing down why.", "If you join them, join them knowing this is in the building with you. Make them say it to your face, in plain words, with the lights on. Then decide.", "One number, the only one I could confirm. Of the practitioners recorded entering containment in the Kita region over the six years I worked it, the share ever recorded leaving by any door at all is under one in three. The file does not say what happened to the others. (G.U.)"]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Stuck inside the back cover of the chapter", "lines": ["Visited the suppression wing once. Did not sleep. Will not go back unless ordered. G.U."]}}, {"p": "Rei read it twice. Then he sat with his back against the bed for a long time and let the floor of his life rearrange itself. All the years of Gin saying <em>keep your head down, keep it quiet, it's nothing, you're just tired</em>. All the forms Gin had never let him fill in. His brother had stood between him and that white room every single day, and never told him it was there. And now his brother was the one with his hand on the forms. <em>I sign them now, you know.</em>"}, {"p": "He crossed out the word address on his page. He could not hand thirty-five grieving people and a hollowed courier to a building that ran this with the same hands as its good people."}, {"p": "He thought of Kurosawa, who read bodies, who had told him once that the cause of death is rarely where the cruelty is. He thought of the gap he had found in Kishin's sermon, the living, left off the books. And slowly he realised it was the gap in the Healer's plan too. Both demons had done their sums on the dead. Neither had counted the living, except as supply. The place neither of them was looking was not a person or an address. It was the whole category of the breathing. Both reasonable catastrophes treated the living as the material, never as the point. If there was another way through, it was somewhere in that blind spot. It was in doing something for the grieving that was neither soothing them into batteries nor breaking them into floods. The harder thing, the thing demons had no use for. Helping them truly mourn, and be done, and walk out of the diagram entirely."}], "n": 35}, {"title": "The Quieter Cases", "kind": "main", "src": "m_hunt#2", "blocks": [{"p": "Outside, the city woke up. A bicycle bell rang in the lane. A neighbour's radio gave the morning weather, thirty-five degrees again, and a warning to drink water. Rei did not open the curtains."}, {"p": "He went back through Gin's book, looking for anything his brother had written about that harder thing. He found it in a place he had not expected. It was a short section near the end, headed The quieter cases. Gin is not the only one carrying something, it began, in Gin's own voice, about himself. The city is full of quieter cases. People with a thing riding them, a thing feeding on them, or a dead friend they cannot or will not release. Each one turns on the question the whole book turns on. Is the afflicted person a case to close, or a person to keep? The win is rarely an exorcism and usually a kindness."}, {"p": "Two of the files were marked with a red pencil tick, as if Gin had meant to come back to them."}, {"img": "ext_climber.jpg", "cap": "Kuroki Tatsuo, and the thing that climbs with him"}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "Kuroki Tatsuo. The Climb. Possession in progress", "lines": ["Yakuza lieutenant, Shinjuku family. Fifty-one. A discreet house in Mejiro, a family man at home. Most nights at Kogane or a private room. His line. I can find anyone in this city. The finding is free. The owing is not.", "The most dangerous case in the book, because nobody is looking at it.", "An Ambition-register demon, unnamed, attached itself to him years ago and has been, in effect, helping him. Removing his rivals in ways no rival could manage. Sharpening his judgement. Giving him a luck he reads as his own. It does not want to harm him. It wants him to rise, because the higher a host climbs, the more ambition and fear pool around him, and that is what it eats.", "It is not fully in. It rides at the edges, a whisper that feels like instinct. If it completes the crossing, and a host this fed is close, the city gains an Arbiter-grade demon wearing a powerful man with an army.", "Tells. Rivals vanish too cleanly. He never loses, and has not in years. The room drops a degree when he is pleased. He has begun finishing other people's sentences. He sleeps four hours and wakes sharper.", "Reachable only before the full crossing. He is proud and intelligent and keeps his family clean. Shown what rides him, he might fight it. Or he refuses to believe and becomes a slow-burn enemy.", "His soldier Wada Sho is the soft way in. Twenty-nine, a share-flat in Ōkubo, frightened more often than he shows. (G.U.)"]}}, {"img": "ext_hunger.jpg", "cap": "Aizawa Yuto, and the hunger latched onto him"}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "Aizawa Yuto. The Hole That Eats. Attachment, not possession", "lines": ["Twenty-two. An heir, addicted. A family-owned flat in Hiroo he rarely sleeps in sober. Most nights at Vault, the expensive bars, the after-hours rooms. His line. My family has money for everything except the thing I actually need. Funny, right?", "Not a possession. A feeder. A low Hunger-register thing latched onto him at his lowest and has been widening the emptiness ever since, because the emptiness is its meal. The addiction is half chemistry and half the thing deepening the hole so he keeps reaching for the next thing to fill it. Nothing fills it. That is the point.", "Tells. He never feels full of anything. Drink, drugs, company. The air near him goes faintly cold and hungry. People leave his table vaguely depleted. Objects of comfort lose their savour around him. Thinner every month, and the doctors find nothing.", "Leo the host has felt the cold off him and not known why.", "Clearing the feeder is necessary and not the win. The human wound stays. A freshly cleared Aizawa is a frightened young addict who now has to feel everything. A rescue, not an exorcism. (G.U.)"]}}, {"p": "A rescue, not an exorcism. Rei underlined it twice. Gin had known. Gin had written down, in his own hand, the thing neither demon could see. Clearing the hunger was not enough. Somebody had to stay afterwards and help the person feel everything."}, {"break": "3:47"}, {"p": "Kaito came to him the next night, as Rei had known he would. Rei was the table, and the table is where both of them set things down. He woke at 3:47. He always woke at 3:47 now, the kitchen's hour following him like a debt. The courier was sitting on the fire escape on the other side of the glass, with his knees drawn up, looking out over Kita, where the membrane held its hundred-year breath. He did not try the window. In the host's body he was almost peaceful. The flood in him had gone quiet, the way floods are quiet between rains."}, {"p": "\"He's getting closer to catching me,\" the thing in Kaito said through the glass, as if they were chatting. Rei understood that it meant his brother, the Healer, the gentle hunt closing in. \"He'll fold this mouth back into the schedule if he does. Make the grief a tap. Or file the boy into one of his white rooms. I'd rather you stopped me than him.\""}, {"p": "It took Rei a moment to hear what had actually been said. Then it landed. It was asking him, and not his brother, to be the one who defeated its catastrophe. To a thing that loved the truth, being stopped by the one who had found its flaw was less of an insult than being managed by the liar."}, {"p": "Rei sat on his own side of the glass. He did not open the window, and he did not look away. He asked the question he had been carrying since the basement. What would actually let the held dead rest? Not a flood and not a tap. Rest. The thing that both the ruin and his brother's mercy only pretended to offer."}, {"p": "For a long moment the courier's face did something the flood had not let it do. Something that belonged to the young man and not to what spoke through him. Rei saw a flicker of a person who used to deliver other people's parcels through this ward at this hour. A person who knew from the inside what it was to carry something that was never his, all the way to its door."}, {"p": "\"Rites,\" Kaito said, so quietly the glass barely carried it. \"Real ones. Names said out loud by someone who means it. Like you did in the basement. That's what they were owed. That's what nobody had the time for. He offers to file them and it offers to free them, and neither of them is offering them their names.\""}, {"p": "Then the flood rose back over his eyes like water over a stone. He was the mouth again, and the moment was gone. But Rei had it now. It was written on the inside of him, where the registers could not soften it."}, {"p": "When the courier left, he went the way grief goes, without any sound. Rei sat at the window in the kitchen's stolen hour. The other way through was finally clear, and it was impossibly large. A hundred years of the unnamed dead were owed their names, by a city with nine million living mouths, and not one institution was willing to spend the time."}, {"p": "Not one institution. He got up and found the sheet Tanizaki had given him, folded in three, in Gin's handwriting. <em>Do not read it today</em>, Tanizaki had said. <em>You will know the day you need it.</em> Rei unfolded it under the kitchen light."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Outside the doctrine. The houses that still do the old work", "lines": ["Pak Sun-ja. Mudang. Fifty-five. Above a Korean restaurant in Shin-Ōkubo, a shrine room behind a curtain. Performs gut for the community, for healing, for mourning, for the restless dead. She negotiates rather than banishes. She treats an entity as a guest with a grievance and hears it out. For spirits anchored to grief she outperforms exorcism more often than the Division will admit in writing. Slower than a banishment. It lasts. By introduction only. She decides whether to see you, and payment is the least of it. Her words. The dead are not gone, only unquiet. I can ask them. Sometimes they answer. I watched her talk a thing down in an afternoon that I had decided could only be fought.", "Rev. Egami Jakū. Shingon priest. Fifty-nine. The old temple in Kita-ku with the working graveyard, the same one the grave-earth comes from. Funerals publicly, the inner rites for those who truly need them. A priest who will sit with a spirit and grieve it rather than file it. A true esoteric exorcism, which the Directorate cannot replicate, is a transaction with the unseen that takes something real from the one who performs it. He has done three and considers each a wound still healing. He will not be anyone's weapon. Prove the cause is worthy, not just urgent. Approach as a mourner, not a client. They taught me that some things do not need to be defeated, only seen all the way out.", "Father Anselm Mabuchi. Catholic priest. Sixty-one. An old parish a few streets from the CVD building. The institution walks past it daily. The side door, after the last Mass, by appointment through someone he already trusts. He will ask hard questions first, and he is right to. The Roman rite names the thing and commands it. Absolute on some registers, useless and dangerous on others. Good for a demon that answers to authority and name. Bring him the right case or none at all. He has hidden people the bureau wanted, and he would do it again. Rome has its rite, he says. I have my conscience. When they disagree, the conscience wins.", "Master Lin Wei. Taoist temple master. Sixty-three. The temple in the Chinese quarter, the old lineages out in Yokohama's Chinatown. Fú talismans, paper that binds the dead. Buy incense first. Then we talk. The respect is not optional. It is the method. Disrespect the temple and the talismans simply do not take. At full trust he draws a true fú for one specific entity. I keep one of their talismans in my kit and cannot fully explain why it works. It works.", "Baba Adewale. Babalawo of the Ifá tradition. Sixty-four. A flat that doubles as a community house, candles always lit. Casts the cowries before he answers and pours a little for the orisha first. A system of divination the CVD does not know exists, and he has handled crossings it never heard of. If an entity answers to nothing you were taught, it may answer to someone whose grandmother knew its kind by another name. You go as a guest asking to learn. You are brought, or you do not go. His word opens Adé's back room. I learned once, badly, not to treat a living faith as a tool in my kit. I deserved it. (G.U.)"]}}, {"p": "The list named five traditions Rei knew only by their names. A mudang is a Korean shaman, and a gut is her ritual, with drums and dancing and food laid out for the spirits. Shingon is an old school of Japanese Buddhism that keeps secret inner rites alongside its public ones. A fú is a paper charm from Chinese Taoism, written in red or black ink. A babalawo is a priest of Ifá, a way of reading fate that comes from the Yoruba people of West Africa."}, {"p": "Five houses. Five people who still said names out loud and meant them. Not one of them had a desk at the CVD. Rei could not say a hundred years of names. He did not know if anyone could. But he had seen the courier remember being a person. And he had seen his brother, in the basement, glance at his face out of an old habit of love that the Healer had not bothered to erase. At the bottom of the worst week of his life, with both catastrophes leaning on him like weather, he thought the blind spot the two demons shared might also be the one place where his brother was still in there."}, {"p": "Neither of them had counted the living. But his brother, before he became the Healer's coat, had counted one living person above everything else. He had hidden that person from the registry every day for years. His name was Rei, and he was sitting at the window, awake at 3:47, with a list of five names in his hand, refusing to be only the table."}], "n": 36}]}, {"title": "Book Four", "sub": "The Vacant Seat", "chapters": [{"title": "The Thinning Wall", "kind": "main", "src": "m_thinning", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei did not go to bed after the list. He cleared the kitchen floor of Gin's flat, fetched Gin's ledger and a packet of index cards, and set to work while the street outside was still dark."}, {"p": "Thirty-one index cards covered the kitchen floor. Each one was a favour the Healer had done in Gin's body, written out in Rei's cramped hand. Between them, in red pencil, ran the seams Kishin had scored through the city, the ones Kaito preached over in the drained pool. Rei knelt among them with the lamp pulled down low. He moved a card a finger's width, then another. Around midnight he stopped seeing two patterns. He saw one engine, and Kita was the cylinder it fired in. Having the plan whole was worse than not having it had ever been."}, {"p": "The membrane was not a wall, whatever he had let himself picture. It was not a thin bright sheet hung between the living city and the place the dead pass through when the lanterns call them home. It was a pressure. It was a breath the world had been holding since before the wards had names. And for a month the thing wearing his brother had been teaching the city, one careful mercy at a time, to let that breath out."}, {"p": "Every healing was a loosening. He understood it now, sick and certain, the way the body knows a thing before the mind allows it. A grieving man's haunting was closed gently, and the closing borrowed a little of the tension that held Kita's seam shut. A drowned client was laid to rest, and another small piece was spent. Thirty-one acts of kindness, every one of them seen and every one of them thanked. Each one drew the smallest possible thread from the one place in Tokyo where the wall was already a scar. Kita had been weakening since 2009, the year something in it went empty. March 2011 had torn it further. Now the held breath over that old wound was no longer a wall at all. It was a curtain, and a person can step through a curtain."}, {"p": "For one stupid, hopeful minute he thought of simply sealing it. Gin's book had a page on that too, and Rei made himself turn to it. <em>Sealing a thin spot. Major operation. The veil compensates. Another location in the city thins.</em> Underneath, in Gin's smaller hand, <em>The Architect does not punish. It corrects. Seal the danger you know and you make one you don't.</em> That was the rule the whole registered world worked under, and the Healer had built its plan inside it. Nobody could close Kita by force without opening somewhere else. It could only be held the slow way, by people. And the Healer had spent a month making the people grateful."}, {"p": "He sat back on his heels in the lamplight and let himself feel how well it was made. He had to understand the thing to fight it, and he could not understand it from a safe distance. It was beautiful. It was the most patient cruelty he had ever been close to. No grade in the city would ever point to a crime, because there was no crime. There was only kindness, handed out and adding up. Then on the right night, with the dead already moving and every ward looking outward at the obvious monster, the curtain over Kita would lift on its own. Something with grateful hands would climb to the one chair tall enough for what it wanted. It would call that <em>finishing what death interrupted</em>. Rei, if he let himself, would call it the end of the difference between the living city and the other side."}, {"break": "Kita, in the heat"}, {"p": "He woke at nine with his cheek on the cards and the heat already in the room. In the early afternoon he went out. He walked down to Ōji, where a sky-blue train slid in above the street and families came down the station stairs with gifts and flowers for their graves. A small boy in a sun hat held his grandmother's hand and counted the steps out loud. Rei walked away from the grey CVD block, past the green slope of Asukayama, and into the back streets of the ward."}, {"p": "It was the worst of the afternoon, when the heat sat on the rooftops like a second sky. He made himself look at the people the plan treated as the inside of the cylinder. A woman watered morning glories on a balcony above a shuttered ramen counter. Children chalked a hopscotch grid on a patch of concrete that had not been right since 2011, concrete everyone stepped across without naming. An old man set a small dish of water on a windowsill for a wife the official records had closed years ago. None of them knew they lived at the seam. None of them had chosen to be the place where a demon decided the wall was already weak enough to be worth finishing."}, {"p": "He could feel it on them. Not with his sight, which he kept shut. He felt it the way an old joint feels a storm coming. People here were tired in a way the rest of Tokyo was not. They slept badly and blamed the heat. They lost small things and found them in places they swore they had already looked. A neighbourhood does not notice a curtain thinning over a month. It notices that the dead feel closer at Obon than they used to. It lights its lanterns a little earlier, and it tells itself it is only being devout. The Healer had not just chosen a weak wall. It had chosen people already half-trained by their own grief to mistake a door opening for the comfort of being remembered."}, {"p": "He stopped at the old man's window without meaning to, and that was when he saw the rats."}, {"img": "animal_rats.jpg", "cap": "Rats in a Kita gutter, all facing the same way"}, {"p": "There were rats in the gutter under the sill, which meant nothing in August. Then he counted them, because Gin had once told him to. It had been across the kitchen table, in a voice so dry that Rei had laughed. <em>Count the rats.</em> Twelve. Twelve rats in a gutter, every one of them still, and every one of them turned the same way, toward the chalk grid where the concrete was wrong. They did not twitch or wash or fight. They watched. Rei felt his skin go cold in the heat."}, {"p": "He knew the page by heart now, because he had read it twice that morning. <em>A cluster of twelve rats all looking at the same corner means something is wearing them, and has been for a while. The Demon does not need to follow Rei through Kita if the rats already do.</em> Gin had written Rei's name into the warning a year before any of this, and Rei had read it as a joke. A Class B entity, or a Master, could split off a sliver of itself into the small animals of a district and ride their senses. Nobody looks at rats. A salt line would not stop it, because nothing was crossing. Something was only watching. The book said the right answer was to change districts. Rei could not change districts. Kita was the whole point. So he did the next thing the book allowed. He changed what he was doing. He walked on as if the window meant nothing to him, a tired young man in a tired street, and he did not look back at the gutter."}, {"p": "At the corner a woman stood with a brown dog on a lead, apologising to no one in particular. The dog had fixed on the hopscotch grid with its ears forward and a growl in its chest that never became a bark. Then, while Rei watched, it stopped. It backed away from the chalk with its tail down, went quiet, and pressed itself against the woman's legs. \"He's been like this all week,\" she said. \"It's the heat.\" Rei thanked her and kept walking. Gin's book had a line for that too. A dog that stops pointing and backs away is not saying the presence has gone. It is saying the presence is something it will not fight. It was the most important thing a dog could tell anyone, and this dog had been telling the street for a week."}, {"p": "He went back past the old man's window on the far pavement. The dish of water was trembling very faintly, with no wind to move it. The old man did not see it, because he was looking at the photograph beside it and not at the water. And Rei thought, this is the leash the Healer has on the whole district. Every one of them wants the wall thin. Every one of them, asked plainly, would say yes to one more night with the person the dish is for. The plan did not need to deceive Kita. It only needed to give a grieving city exactly what it had been praying for, on the one night the prayer was loudest. Then the answered prayer would be the thing that broke the world."}], "n": 37}, {"title": "A Roof in Kita, at Dusk", "kind": "main", "src": "m_thinning#2", "blocks": [{"p": "The Grid runner met him on the roof of a coin laundry at dusk. From up there Rei could see the rail lines and, far off, the lit tower blocks of the city centre. The air smelled of hot washing and dryer sheets. The runner was a narrow woman with a cloth bundle under her arm and the look of someone who had slept in her clothes. She did not give a name. Someone had passed Rei to her, and that was enough. She unwrapped the bundle as if something inside it were alive. It was a mirror, square and dark-backed, longer on its shortest side than Rei's forearm."}, {"img": "animal_crow.jpg", "cap": "The Grid runner's crow on its scrying mirror"}, {"p": "\"Three days,\" she said, before he could ask. \"Three days feeding the same crow out of my hand, so it decides you are interesting and not a threat. Then the rite, at dawn or dusk, because the half-light is the condition. Not the ceremony. The light.\" She showed him the back of the frame, where a strip of seal paper was tucked under the edge. On it was one character, 視, sight, written in dried brown. \"My own blood. The ink for the rest is charcoal and graveyard earth in water. Three black cords, each as long as your forearm. You lay the mirror face up and the paper face down on the glass, and you put food the crow already trusts at the edge. It has to land on the mirror. Not near it. On it. Then you draw the three cords across the glass from edge to edge without touching a feather, and you say the binding quietly. If it takes, the mirror shows you what the crow sees for one second. That second is the bond closing.\""}, {"p": "\"How long does it last?\""}, {"p": "\"Until the crow dies or the mirror breaks. Whichever is first.\" She shrugged. \"A mirror that outlives its crow goes dark and never binds again. It costs you a little to look, maybe a point of yourself for every ten minutes you hold it. Hold it longer than an hour and the bird starts sitting in your head. Do not do that.\" She held the mirror flat between them. A crow lifted off the water tank behind her and flew out over the roofs toward the river."}, {"p": "Rei looked down into the glass, and the city fell away under him."}, {"p": "He saw Kita from a height no person stands at. Tiled roofs, the river, the cutting where the railway ran, the tiny lit squares of konbini. And over the place he had walked that afternoon, he saw what his own eyes had never let him see at ground level. He saw the membrane at its true size. It lay over the ward like the skin over a knuckle when a fist is made, tight and shining and too thin. There was a darker crease in it where the chalk grid was. Around the crease the whole surface leaned, very slightly, the way water leans toward a drain."}, {"p": "Then something under the crease looked up."}, {"p": "Rei did not see it so much as feel the attention arrive, flat and enormous, through the crow and the glass and into his face. The runner slapped the mirror face down on the roof before he had finished flinching. \"No,\" she said. \"When a big one looks up at the bird, you are looking straight into what it sees of you. Face down. Always face down. The crow doesn't know to avoid them. Crows don't avoid anything.\" She wrapped the mirror in its cloth again, carefully, the way a person wraps something that breathes."}, {"p": "\"The Division has a room of these,\" she said, and her mouth went thin. \"Under the grey block at Ōji. The Mirror Room.\" Rei had heard the name from Okada. Rows of mirrors up to the ceiling, each one a slice of the city from a height no person stands at. He had never asked what the mirrors were. \"A wall of crows,\" the runner said. \"Every one fed by hand for three days, every one bound in blood. The people in there drink tea and watch birds' eyes.\" She tucked the bundle under her arm. \"They taught the birds to see for them because they can't see into this place themselves. Ask yourself what that makes them.\""}, {"p": "\"How do I get out from under the rats?\" Rei asked. He had not meant to ask it aloud."}, {"p": "She looked at him with something close to pity. \"You don't stop them. Salt does nothing. They aren't crossing, they're watching. You change the district you're working in.\" She hesitated. \"Or you give them something else to watch. A toad. A big one, <em>Bufo japonicus</em>, the Grid keeps a rotation of them. You sit it in a sealed clay dish of graveyard earth. You write 替 on seal paper in graveyard-earth ink, substitution, and you bind the paper under the dish with red cord. Three drops of your own blood down the centre of its back, well clear of the glands at the sides. Then you push your signature out of yourself and into the animal. Fifteen, twenty-five minutes. When it takes, the toad swells and goes still, and to anything hunting you by your signature, the toad reads as you. You read as nothing.\""}, {"p": "\"How long?\""}, {"p": "\"Two to four hours. Six with the earth under it. After that it dies, whatever you do. You can reverse it before then and it lives, and that's why the Grid keeps a rotation.\" She shook her head. \"Not you, though. Not yet. You need symbol work at the second level and one of the body paths at the first, Psychokinesis or Psychometabolism. Ink alone won't push it. The body has to help.\" She looked away over the roofs. \"I've done it more than once. I count them.\""}, {"p": "Then she went down the fire stairs. Rei stayed on the roof until the last of the light went, feeling the place on his face where the attention had touched him. Below him the streets filled with the evening. Someone was grilling fish. A child practised the same three notes on a recorder, over and over. Lanterns came on, one house at a time, white and blue and pale yellow, all the way down toward the river."}, {"p": "Back in the heat-sick dark he finally understood the timing. It landed like a key turning in a lock he had not known he was standing inside. It had to be Obon. Not as decoration, not as a pretty occasion for a haunting. As the mechanism itself. Obon is the one stretch of the year when the dead are already moving toward the living. Then the membrane carries its greatest load and holds against the strongest pull it ever faces. A wall is easiest to finish thinning when something is already leaning on it from the other side. The Healer had not weakened Kita and then waited for a convenient night. It had weakened Kita to the exact thinness at which the pull of Obon would do the last of the work for free. And every ward in the city would read the rising pressure as nothing but the season."}, {"p": "Obon has a shape, and every family in Tokyo knows it. The welcoming fire at the door on the first evening. The dead as guests for the days between, fed and talked to. And on the last evening a second small fire at the door, the <em>okuribi</em>, the sending-fire, to light the dead back to their side."}, {"p": "So the trap had a clock now, and the clock was the festival itself. On the first night the families had lit the welcoming lanterns. Across the nights that followed, the dead came, and stayed, and were fed and remembered. And on the final night, the night of okuribi, when the rest of Tokyo lit its farewells and guided the dead gently back, the pull would reverse. The whole season's lean would swing the other way. At that turning point of the year, with the curtain at its thinnest and the city's attention on its own tender goodbye, the gap over the wound would stand open in both directions at once. The Healer had not chosen a night. It had chosen the single hour in the calendar when the door swings. Its whole patient month had been a way of standing at that door when it did."}, {"p": "Rei wrote the hour down. He wrote it small, in the back of Gin's book, in the space Gin had left, the way Gin had taught him to write the things the records must never see. His hand did not shake until the pen was down. Three nights. Then the sending-fire, and the turning, and the chair. Everything he did from now on would happen inside that window, and the window was not his to widen. Whatever he meant to do about the trap, about his brother, about the factions all staring at the wrong demon, he would have to do inside three nights. He could not spend a fourth, because there would not be one. The festival that guides the dead home was also, this year, the lock the Healer had chosen, and it was already half turned."}], "n": 38}, {"title": "From Gin's Book. The Font and the Architect", "kind": "gin", "src": "gin_28_font", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei walked home through the lantern-lit streets. A konbini on the corner was the brightest thing for blocks, and two high-school boys sat on the kerb outside it, sharing a bag of fried chicken and arguing about baseball. In the flat the air was hot and still. He switched on the fan, sat on the floor with his back against the bed, and opened Gin's book again."}, {"p": "After he wrote the hour down, he could not sleep. He turned back to the chapter Gin had called the capstone. He had skipped it twice. It was the one where Gin admitted, in his careful field voice, what he did not know. Rei read it now with the crow's look still on his skin, because whatever had looked up at the bird lived under the thing these pages were about."}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "Chapter 14. The Font. Three Layers", "lines": ["CVD Research Reference. Capstone, Book Three. The part we can stand to look at. (G.U.)", "Layer One. What is known. The Font is the generating source of veil energy. Every practitioner's aperture is a relationship to the Font, the specific channel through which Font energy becomes ability. When you use ability you are not generating something from inside yourself. You are accessing something that exists outside you and that is, in some sense, aware that you are accessing it.", "The Font is not a location in the geographic sense. It has no coordinates. Practitioners who have approached it describe a direction rather than a place, a gradient that grows steeper as one moves toward it, until at sufficient proximity the gradient is the only thing they can perceive.", "The Font is also not an entity. It does not communicate, move, feed or pursue. It generates and receives. It has done both continuously across all recorded history.", "It sustains the membrane. The veil is not self-sustaining. If the Font stopped generating, the membrane would dissolve.", "It receives what crosses. Souls on the Font return path dissolve into it. Demons resist it. Spirits drift toward it slowly unless anchored. It is both origin and terminus, and the receiving end of that loop is closed to observation.", "Layer Two. What can be inferred. Below this line we are guessing. (G.U.)", "Some form of awareness. Those who came near it describe its presence as attention, not indifference, across cultures that never spoke to each other. I lean toward awareness. I cannot prove it.", "A maintenance dependency. The membrane fluctuates locally in ways the Font alone does not explain. The pattern suggests distributed structural points rather than one central source.", "Direction matters. The old traditions speak of orientations and angles, not positions. The older language may be more accurate than ours.", "The membrane is changing. Most of the century's thinning is environmental. A residual is not. I think the residual is real.", "Layer Three. The edge I could not cross. There is an anomaly in the Phase III data that the official model does not fit. I have theories. They explain too much, so I do not trust them. If the answer exists, it lives with practitioners who never wrote anything down. I could not find which ones. The work continues for someone else."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "A yellow post-it, stuck crooked over the rank table", "lines": ["A note on names. The Axis and the Font are the same centre, named twice.", "The Axis is the being. The anchor at the centre of the veil, the thirteenth and central of the light-anchors, the one the oldest records call the Architect.", "The Font is what pours from it. When a report says Font pull, it means the centre reaching for anyone whose stability has thinned far enough to feel the current.", "Do not let a junior conflate them."]}}, {"p": "Rei put it in plain words for himself, the way Gin had taught him to do with any hard page. All power came from one source, and that source was the Font. The Font kept the wall between the living and the dead standing. Souls that took the Font path went back into it in the end. And at the centre of it was the Axis, a being that had once been a person."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "The rank table, last row", "lines": ["X. Axis. The one true balance point of the system. Was human. Is no longer. The Architect.", "Not a rank. A state. The direction the scale points.", "Gin, in pencil. It was a person once. The archive notes say so. I think about that more than I should."]}}, {"p": "Two pages on, pasted in, with the edges gone soft from handling, was the classified sheet Gin had never been cleared to own."}, {"img": "ch2_architect.jpg", "cap": "The Architect, weather over a whole district"}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "The Architect Phenomenon. Working Classification (Classified Research)", "lines": ["Classification. Autonomous corrective mechanism. Not an entity. Not a practitioner. Not a government or faction operation.", "Trigger conditions. High-power practitioners who suppress ability long-term without sanctioned outlet. Practitioners who accumulate psychic energy without the natural exchange the veil requires. Significant anomalies in local veil balance caused by unusual practitioner activity.", "Mechanism. Unknown. Theorized as Font-originating corrective pressure, appearing as targeted entity attention, accelerated veil degradation in the individual, or a directed supernatural event. The correction is not punitive in intent. It is structural.", "Outcome. Forced ability expression. Acute possession vulnerability. Disappearance of subject. Death. In some cases, the subject survives and is integrated into the veil system in a new capacity.", "Protocol response. Architect-flagged individuals are classified Priority Watch.", "Predictability. Low. Months to decades. The subject is typically aware, because the Architect's attention is perceptible to sensitive practitioners as a specific quality of being watched."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's comment beside it", "lines": ["I have been aware of my status for approximately three years. It is not threatening the way an entity's attention is threatening. It is more like the feeling of a very large system becoming aware of a very small irregularity. Patient. Inevitable. Not personal.", "I find this more frightening than something personal. Personal things can be negotiated with. This cannot.", "If you are a suppressor reading this, you should know that the math eventually balances. Not as threat. As information. Plan accordingly. G.U."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the comment three times. Three years of being watched. For three years Gin had put the feeling down to the great patient system under the city. And for three years something else had been standing across the street. Gin had been right about the feeling and wrong about whose eyes they were. He had blamed the Architect, and the Architect had only been the excuse the demon wore."}, {"p": "On the facing page Gin had drawn ten small boxes in a row, ruled with care, and written <em>Noise</em> over them. Rei had seen the boxes before without understanding them. They were a way of keeping score. Every time a practitioner changed the world, the score went up, and if it went high enough, the Architect answered. Gin had shaded three of the boxes in pencil and then rubbed the shading out."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Noise. Ten boxes, Gin's ruling", "lines": ["Noise is the disturbance you make in the structure. Using ability makes it. Changing the local veil makes it. Killing an entity that was part of the local structure makes it. Preventing an event that was supposed to happen makes it.", "0 to 5. Quiet. Over 5, the Architect's attention is active and the world starts answering what you do with corrections.", "At 10, a major Correction Event. Then the track settles back to 5.", "Passive sight. 0. Invisible to the structure.", "One moderate use of ability. 1. Single sustained or major use. 2.", "Clearing an ordinary entity. 1. Clearing one that was part of the local ecology. 3, and something of the same weight appears elsewhere in the city within a day.", "Preventing what was supposed to happen. 2 to 4. The energy goes somewhere else, same size, often near someone you know.", "Quiet symbol work. 0. Rushed symbol work. 2.", "Sealing a thin spot. 4. Another place thins.", "Every quiet scene takes one away. A full rest takes two.", "The Architect does not punish. It corrects. It is a conservation law, not a judge."]}}, {"p": "Rei sat with the book open on his knees until the window went grey and the first crows began calling over the rooftops. Everything the Healer had done was quiet. Kindness was quiet. A month of mercy had hardly moved the boxes at all, and the structure had never once turned to look at it. Whatever he did now, he understood, would be loud."}], "n": 38}, {"title": "The Axis", "kind": "main", "src": "m_axis", "blocks": [{"img": "place_kita_obon_lane.jpg", "cap": "Obon lanterns in a Kita lane after dark"}, {"p": "Rei walked home from the roof in the dark. The heat had not gone down with the sun. It sat in the narrow streets of Kita like water in a bath that nobody had drained. The cicadas had stopped for the night, and in their place there was the hum of air conditioners on every balcony, dripping onto the pavement below. Paper lanterns hung outside some of the houses for Obon, white and pale blue, each with a family name brushed on the side. A few of them had candles inside. Most had small electric bulbs now, and one of the bulbs was flickering in a way that had nothing to do with the wiring."}, {"p": "He passed a konbini on the corner, bright as an aquarium. A delivery man was asleep in his van outside it with the engine running for the cool air and his mouth open. Inside, a girl in a striped uniform was stacking rice balls into the fridge, very slowly, as if the night would never end. An old woman came out with a bag of peaches and a bunch of chrysanthemums for a family altar. She stopped at the door and looked back over her shoulder at the empty street, the way people in Kita had been doing all month. Then she shook her head at herself and went on. Rei climbed the stairs to the flat. The stairwell smelled of the neighbour's cigarettes, as it always did. He let himself in and did not turn on the light."}, {"p": "There is no stair to the Axis. That was the first true thing Rei learned about it. He had pictured something without quite admitting it. A door in a sub-basement. A vault in a ward-house. A secret the CVD kept behind a grade he did not have. There was nothing like that. The Axis was not under Tokyo the way a basement is under a house. It was under Tokyo the way meaning is under the words of a sentence. It was in every part of the thing, and there was no single spot he could point to."}, {"p": "So he did not go down to reach it. He sat on the kitchen floor with his back against the cupboard and Gin's book closed in his lap, and he let both of his paths open at once. The first of Rei's was Clairsentience, the searching, sensing one, which he had always half allowed himself. The second lay beneath it. He had never named it. He had spent his whole life holding it flat, because Gin had taught him to, at this same table, before he could even read. Now he let them both come up. He let the city stop being a place. For as long as he could stand it, he let it become a structure. The structure had a floor, and the floor was the Axis. He was standing on it. He had been standing on it all his life."}, {"p": "His sight went further than any word he had for it. The walls of the flat did not disappear. They went clear, like glass. The veil itself, the thin wall between the living and the dead, stood up out of the city like a building made of light. He could see every thick part and every thin part, every line where it was under strain. He could see where it was being held, and where it was being pulled. Kurosawa had once called this the city becoming transparent. Not invisible. Transparent. Gin's book put it on the last line of the sight scale, the line Gin himself had never reached. Under it Gin had written three words. <em>No off switch.</em> Rei understood them now. He could not have closed this sight if he had wanted to. He could only hold on and look."}, {"p": "It cost him at once, and it cost him the thing he had least of, which was his hold on his own mind. The madness came up through his open paths like water through a hole in a boat. It did not come as fear. It came as recognition. It was the terrible feeling of finally seeing the room he had always been in. Under everything there was a direction. It was not a place. It was a slope, and it grew steeper the longer he faced it. It was warm. And it was paying attention to him. It knew he was looking. Every ability he had ever used had come up out of that slope, and the slope had noticed, every time."}, {"p": "This was the Font. Gin's book had explained it as the source, the deep place every gift comes from and every dead soul finally flows back into. Rei knew it now the way he would have known the sea. Gin's last chapter had put it carefully. <em>Aware that you are accessing it.</em> Gin had not said what it felt like to stand at the top of the slope and want to go down. It felt like coming home. That was the danger the book had underlined twice. The souls that go all the way down dissolve into it completely, and they do not feel it as dying. They feel it as arriving. So the part of Rei that wanted to go on living had to hold on, with both hands, to one plain fact. The feeling of home was the trap. It was not the truth. The wanting was only a feeling. It was not a direction to follow."}, {"p": "He held on. And because he held on, he could see which way the slope ran."}, {"img": "off_tmg.jpg", "cap": "Shinjuku, where every CVD map puts the city's source point"}, {"p": "It did not run toward Shinjuku. Every CVD map he had ever seen put the city's source point under Shinjuku, the busiest crossing of grief and money in Japan. The slope did not care about the maps. It ran north, into Kita. And it reached its lowest point in a place he knew. Three hundred metres northeast of the kitchen he was sitting in. A few streets of shuttered shops, a short walk from this flat."}, {"p": "He almost laughed. It came out as a breath. Gin had chosen this flat, and Rei had never understood why. It was cramped. The heater broke every winter. The stairwell smelled of the neighbour's cigarettes. And Gin had signed for it in a week without looking at anything else. Now Rei knew. Gin's Clairsentience had turned toward this warmth the way a plant turns toward a window, years before Gin knew what he was feeling. He had moved them both to the edge of the Font because it had felt like the right place to sleep. Everything that had happened since had happened three hundred metres from the centre of everything."}, {"p": "Now he could see the lattice, the great frame that held the veil in place. It ran under the whole city. It was made of thirteen places, set around the slope like the spokes of a wheel around its hub. Twelve of them were occupied. He felt each one the way he would feel eyes in a dark room. Each place was a pressure with a name he almost knew, and each pressure had a person in it. They were small and warm and very far apart, and they felt, strangely, like people. Grief. Rage. Love. Fear. Wonder. Despair. Joy. Shame. Hunger. Courage. Longing. Awe. Twelve emotions, and each one ran through a single living person, so that it kept moving and never settled on the city to rot. These were the Thirteen, the holders the old records spoke of. Their seats were not thrones. They were load-bearing, like pillars under a roof. The whole careful weave of them was the wall. It was the veil itself, a breath the city was holding."}, {"p": "Under the lattice, very faint, lay a second one in the same shape. It had thirteen hollow places, dark ones, each cut to fit one of the twelve, and one cut to fit the thirteenth. These were the demon shells, the dark twin of the living holders. Rei did not look at them for long. He felt enough to know that the dark places wanted the light ones. And he felt that one of them, the one called Despair, was pressed so hard against its living twin that he could barely tell the two apart."}, {"img": "ch14_layer1.jpg", "cap": "The figure at the hub of the lattice, holding the wall shut"}, {"p": "At the hub, where all thirteen spokes met and the slope ran down into nothing, there was a figure. It stood in a light so cold and so complete that Rei's eyes watered, even though he was not seeing with his eyes. It had its back to him. It had its back to the whole world. All of its attention was on the wall, and it was holding something shut. Rei could not see its face. He did not think anyone could. But it was shaped like a man. Not a god, not a machine, not a storm. A man, standing very still, the way a person stands when he has been carrying something heavy for so long that he no longer notices his arms."}, {"p": "Every path was lit in him. Rei had never felt all six at once. He had not known it could be done. Clairsentience and Telepathy. Telekinesis and Psychometabolism. Psychokinesis and Teleportation. Every one of them was open to its limit. And through him ran all thirteen spokes, every register from Grief to Duty, alive in one place at the same moment. Rei understood, in a rush that hurt, what the last row of the rank table in Gin's book had meant. <em>X. Axis. Was human. Is no longer.</em> This was what a human being looked like from the outside, once he had become the centre. The Font was not a well that happened to hold him. The Font was what poured out of him."}, {"p": "Rei wanted to call out to him. He did not. Gin's book had told him that much. <em>It answers one question per approach. It does not show its face. It returns the practitioner.</em> And it answered only those who had come all the way in. Rei was not in. He was looking down a well from the kitchen floor."}, {"p": "And the thirteenth place was empty. The emptiness had a shape, and the shape was a wound."}, {"p": "He made himself look at the empty place. The lattice felt him looking, the way still water feels a dropped stone, and it showed him its history. It did not use words. The Axis had no use for words. It showed him the way a scar remembers the knife."}, {"p": "The thirteenth spoke was Duty. The CVD had never named it right. On its forms it was Register XIII, unclassified, and its instruments read the stillness there as nothing at all. <em>Null-emotive.</em> But it was not empty. It was full. Duty was what obligation becomes when it has been the only thing for so long that nothing remains that remembers a time before it. The man at the hub had sat in that chair first, long ago, and he had never fully stood up. Living holders had come and gone beside him. The last of them had died in 2009. When a holder dies, the weight passes to someone close by with the right shape of gift, and in 2009 no such person had been standing near enough. So the chair had read as vacant ever since. It was not truly vacant. The man at the centre had reached out and taken Duty into his own hands, on top of everything else he held. He had carried it alone for six years. He could not keep doing it. He was leaning. And the place where the lean was worst, where the lattice was spending the most of itself to hold the gap shut, was the seam under Kita."}, {"p": "The empty chair and the thinning wall were not two problems. They were one wound, seen from two ends. Kita had been going wrong since the year the chair emptied. The great wave of deaths in 2011 had pushed it further. And the thing that had taken Rei's brother had spent a month walking the city, pressing on the bruise."}, {"p": "Kneeling there, on the floor that was the underside of meaning, he understood why a Duty demon would want this seat above any other. Kurosawa had given him the word in the cold room, and the Healer had given him every reason since. The Healer did not want power over the living. Rei had been wrong about that from the first morning in the kitchen, and the mistake had nearly cost him everything. It wanted the office. Under the lattice of the living, in the matching dark place cut to the shape of Duty, there was a chair for its own kind, and it wanted to sit there. It wanted to rule the far side of the wall, beside the few demons it trusted. It wanted to finish the duty that death had interrupted for it forty years ago. It was completely sincere. That sincerity was the most dangerous thing in the whole structure. A monster can be fought. A martyr cannot. And the thing wearing his brother believed, all the way down, that it was the only mercy left."}, {"p": "But Rei saw the lie folded inside the sincerity. It was small and it was absolute, like the tremble he had once seen in the old man's dish. To take that seat, the Healer had to finish tearing the wall. It could not climb into the chair while the lattice was still spending itself to hold the gap. The wall had to go fully thin. The held breath had to go all the way out. And for the one hour it would take a new occupant to climb in and take the weight, the door would stand open in both directions. Whatever came through that door, while the Healer was busy becoming a law, would not be the Healer's problem. It would be the problem of every living thing in Kita. The mercy was real. But the mercy needed a flood. And the demon had made its peace with the flood. To a thing built for duty, drowning a district so that it could take its seat correctly was not a price. It was a small number rounded away."}, {"p": "He felt both demons move toward the wound from opposite sides. The feeling was the worst kind of proof, the kind that ends the comfort of not being sure."}, {"p": "Kishin came the way weather comes. Kishin was the Despair demon. It was wearing Fukai Rinka, the woman the Far-Shore Circle called Kanna-sensei, and it spoke through Kaito, the hollowed young courier who preached in the drained pool. But the demon itself did not walk toward the seam. The seam began to walk toward it. The scored places pulled open along the lines Kishin had spent the whole season tearing. It was turning itself inside out as it came. Rei saw what it was becoming, and his stomach dropped. The man at the hub held every register in a light too cold to look at. Kishin was growing the same shape in the dark, from the hollow places upward. A dark mirror of the Axis. An inverted god. It did not want the empty chair. It wanted the wall gone, and the dark lattice raised on this side of it, the living side, with itself at the top. It wanted to rule the city Rei lived in. And it, too, believed this was healing. The Axis was full of sincere disasters tonight."}, {"p": "The Healer came the way an appointment comes. Patient, expected, prepared. It was being carried toward the seam in Gin's tired body through the festival nights, calm and unhurried, like a thing that has done the hard part and only needs to arrive. The two demons were not allies. They had never spoken. They did not need to. Kishin would tear the wall down because it hungered for the living side. The Healer would step through the torn wall into its chair out of duty. Each one's disaster was the other one's first step. But under the Healer's calm Rei felt something else, cold as a scalpel. The Healer did not mean to leave Kishin standing once the work was done. Kishin meant to rule this side, and the Healer wanted it dead before it could. A demon can only be killed on this side from inside a body. That was one more reason the Healer had kept Gin's body so carefully, so gently, in such good repair."}, {"p": "Rei was the only mind at the Axis that simply wanted the wall to hold. He had no seat, no grade, no law. He had two paths he could not keep open without drowning. And he had a brother somewhere inside the appointment, still tapping."}, {"p": "From down here, the coming nights did not look like a question with an answer. They looked like the kitchen at 3:47 in the morning, with every door standing open, and behind each door something he loved, waiting to be paid for. He saw the Healer in its chair and Kita under water. He saw himself at the wound, both hands on it, and nothing left behind his eyes. He saw Gin's hand in his, the two of them running, and the lanterns going out behind them street by street. He made himself stop looking. The structure had no opinion about which door he chose. It only knew that every door cost something."}, {"p": "The slope pulled at him, warm and patient. For one long second he leaned toward it. Then he did the only thing Gin's book had told him to do with the wanting. He did not follow it. He shut the second path first, the nameless one, the way Gin had taught him, flat and sloppy and unremarkable. Then he shut the first. The floor went back to being linoleum. The walls went back to being walls. He was a young man on a kitchen floor in the dark, with a nosebleed and a book in his lap. Outside, a lantern on the balcony across the street knocked gently against its pole. And three hundred metres to the northeast, a man made of light was still holding a door with his back to the world. He had not turned around."}], "n": 39}, {"title": "The Offer of Domain XIII", "kind": "main", "src": "m_offer", "blocks": [{"p": "It made him the offer on the second night, in the small hours, in the one place he had not expected. His own kitchen. The cold kettle, the table, the chair. Everything was set exactly as it had been on the morning this began. Outside, the street was silent except for the drip of an air conditioner onto a tin roof below the window. The Healer had spent a month inside Gin's memories. It knew exactly which room would do the most work for it."}, {"p": "Gin sat across from him in his own body. The calm in him was complete and gentle. That calm was how Rei always knew the Healer was the one speaking. It laid the offer out the way it laid everything out, item by item, and every item was true. Rei could not argue with any of it, because each line was real. \"You found the Axis,\" it said, almost tenderly. \"Then you know there must be a thirteenth. You know the wound will not close by itself. And you know Kishin will tear it open whether I take the chair or not. So let me make you an honest offer. The first one anyone has made you since this kitchen.\""}, {"p": "Then it did the thing Rei had not seen coming, the thing that turned the whole month inside out. It offered the chair to him. Not as a threat. Not as a trap he could name. As mercy, item by item. \"The seat is not particular about who fills it,\" it said. \"It wants only to be filled by something that can hold the difference. I am built for it. But you, Rei. Two paths, held down your whole life precisely because they were strong enough to need holding down. You could hold it too. Take the Thirteenth Domain yourself, and the wound closes around you instead of me. Your brother is set down whole the moment it does, because I will no longer need him. You would stop being a witness with no grade. You would become the law the whole registered world has scrambled to stand in for. You would have saved the district, and saved Gin. And the only cost is the one thing you have spent your life refusing to pick up.\""}, {"p": "It was the most reasonable offer anyone had ever made him, and it was built entirely out of true things. Rei sat in the chair from that first morning and felt how easily a person could drown in it. Because it was not lying. He probably could take the seat. The wall probably would close. Gin probably would be set down whole. And there was one thing it did not list, because it did not need to. It knew he could already feel it. That thing was what happened to the person who held the seat."}, {"p": "There was no climbing back down from a Domain. Rei had seen the man at the hub. The weight was permanent and total, and it was not human. Rei would save his brother by becoming a pressure with a name Gin almost knew. A law instead of a brother. A pair of eyes in a dark room, which Gin could feel but could never again sit across a kitchen table from. The Healer was offering him everyone's life but his own. It offered gently, and it meant it, and that was the worst part. The kindest way out of all this was the one where Rei stopped, forever, being someone Gin could come home to."}, {"p": "Gin surfaced while the offer was still warm on the table. The real Gin, coming up through the calm for about as long as a person can hold his breath. Rei knew him at once. The mercy went out of the eyes, and what came up behind it was tired and frightened and completely Gin. He could not stay long. He spent what he had on the only things that mattered, fast, the way a man speaks under water."}, {"p": "\"Don't take it,\" he said. His hand found the edge of the table, the same way it had once found the phone receiver, and tapped. Once, then once, then once. It was the knock from the widow's altar, the knock from the inside. In the code he had taught Rei, it meant this was true, and not the Healer wearing truth like a coat. \"It's not lying to you. That's how I know not to let you say yes. It would do it. It would set me down. And I would have to live in a city you became the underside of.\""}, {"p": "Then he tried to tell Rei the thing he had been tapping toward since the first time he broke through, the thing the Healer would not let him finish in words. He got most of it out in broken half sentences, like a man coming up between waves. When the Healer took him, it had not taken an empty man. It had taken a man who had spent his whole career writing names small in the back of a book. A man who had held wards by committee and done the plain, heavy work of keeping the wall shut, one favour at a time. And at some point in a month of being worn, he had come to understand the chair from inside the very thing that wanted it. There was a way to deny the chair to both demons. He had not been allowed to say it aloud, and even now, with the leash this tight, he could not. He could only give Rei the edge of it."}, {"p": "\"The chair doesn't need a new occupant,\" he managed. \"It needs its old one. The torn-out thing isn't gone, Rei. It was put somewhere. I helped put it. We all did, the whole registered world, that's what the wards <em>are</em>...\""}, {"p": "Then the leash pulled, the way a captor's leash always pulls hardest the moment the captive sees it. The calm came back into the eyes like water closing over a swimmer, and Gin was gone again in the middle of a word. The Healer sat across from Rei in his brother's body, with its gentle, terrible patience. It had heard every syllable. Now it knew exactly how much Gin understood, and exactly how much he had managed to give away before the wave took him."}, {"p": "Rei sat with three knocks fading on the table, a half sentence, and the nights running out. His brother had just spent what might be his last clear strength. He had not spent it on being saved. He had spent it on handing Rei the one thing the Axis had not shown him. The wound had an old occupant. And Rei had seen him. A man at the hub with his back to the world, who had sat in the Duty chair first and never fully stood up. <em>It was a person once. The archive notes say so.</em>"}, {"p": "The whole registered world had put him somewhere. It had put him inside a name. The Architect. A mechanism. Register XIII, null-emotive. Every ward in the city leaned its weight on him and called him a law of nature, so that nobody would have to remember he had been a man. That was the lock. And the only one who could tell Rei how to open it was the man himself. He answered one question each time someone reached him, and only to those who came all the way in."}], "n": 40}, {"title": "The Grey Block at Ōji", "kind": "main", "src": "m_offer#2", "blocks": [{"p": "On the third afternoon of Obon, Rei walked down the slope to Ōji. The heat was over thirty-four degrees, and the air was so wet it felt like walking through a towel. The cicadas in the trees of Asukayama park were screaming, thousands of them, a sound so loud and steady that after a minute he stopped hearing it, the way he would stop hearing a fridge. In August the park was only green and loud, and the shade under its trees was the only cool place for streets around."}, {"p": "At the bottom of the slope, the little Toden tram came grinding round the corner. It rang its bell at a man on a bicycle. Through its window Rei saw an old couple sitting very straight, holding a paper bag of offerings between them. Incense, a box of sweets, a bunch of yellow chrysanthemums. They were going to a family grave. Half of Tokyo was going to a family grave this week. The city was emptier than usual, because so many people had gone back to their home towns for the festival, and the ones left behind moved slowly in the heat."}, {"p": "The station was busy anyway. The ticket gates beeped as people tapped their Suica cards. A station jingle played, a short bright tune, and a train pulled out overhead with a long sigh of brakes. A schoolgirl in a summer uniform stood by the exit fanning herself with a paper fan from some shop, her eyes shut. A salaryman in a white shirt drank a whole can of cold tea from the vending machine in one go and put the empty can in the bin without looking at it. Nobody looked up at the grey building across the road."}, {"p": "The CVD building was a grey block one minute from the east exit of Ōji station. It had been built on the thinnest point of the ward on purpose. Rei walked into it with paper lanterns still sagging over the intake desk from the start of the festival. Okada Shizuka came down to the lobby herself. She looked at his face, then at his hands, the way she always did."}, {"p": "\"Gin told me to watch for you,\" she said. \"I've been watching. Come on.\""}, {"p": "He told her in the lift, as plainly as he could. The Axis. The man at the hub. Gin's half sentence. He needed to go through to the other side with his body, and come back. And he needed a door that the Healer's rats could not follow and the Division's instruments could not read. Okada listened with her three notebooks pressed to her chest and did not interrupt. When the doors opened on the third level underground, she did not step out at once."}, {"p": "\"I'll show you what there is,\" she said. \"Then you choose. I won't choose for you.\""}, {"img": "cvdroom_training_hall.jpg", "cap": "The CVD Training Hall"}, {"p": "She walked him the long way. Later he understood that she had wanted him to see it. They passed a wide window onto the Training Hall. There were mats on the floor and protective lines burned black into the boards. At the far end, a weak spirit was held on a tether, a patch of cold that the new trainees took turns practising their reading on. The trainees were young. Most of them were seventeen. One said something and the others burst out laughing, the loud kind of laughter frightened people use to cover their fear. An instructor told them to settle down without much force. \"They teach the half that keeps the institution comfortable,\" Okada said. \"Your brother used to say that. He wrote the other half down.\""}, {"p": "They passed the Sigil Room. This was where the CVD's masters drew protective glyphs onto paper, and onto skin. Rei smelled it before he saw it. Sumi, the black ink of calligraphy, and blood. There were long benches and the hands of masters, and the only sound was the scratch of brushes on washi, the thin handmade paper used for seals. At the back of the room, a curtain hung across a single bench. \"That's where they make an operative into someone the registry can't read,\" Okada said, quietly. \"You don't watch them work, and you don't choose the artist. Gin learned his own keying so he'd never have to sit there again.\" She kept walking."}, {"img": "cvdroom_mirror_room.jpg", "cap": "The Mirror Room, which never closes"}, {"p": "There was a door with no window and a Surveillance seal. Behind it was a faint sound, like birds in a tree far away. \"The Mirror Room,\" Okada said, without slowing down. \"I told you about it once. Now you know what the mirrors are bound to. Every one of them a crow somebody fed for three days.\" She did not look at the door. \"It never closes.\" She tilted her head at the next door along, which had a number on it and nothing else. \"And that one is Special Cases. That's where the possession that should not work goes, and the operative who came back wrong. They study them gently and everyone calls it care.\" Then she stopped and looked at him. \"Ask me how many people have gone where you want to go. Then ask me how many came back right. Most of the ones who came back at all came back wrong, Rei. Some of them are very calm about it. One of them gardens. It's in the file.\""}, {"p": "Rei did not answer. She nodded as if he had, and opened a door at the end of the corridor. It should have opened onto a stairwell."}, {"img": "cvd_camp.jpg", "cap": "The walled green three floors underground"}, {"p": "It opened onto grass. A walled green field under an open sky, which was impossible, three floors underground. In the middle was a sparring circle worn down to bare dirt. At the far end, where the instructors could see and the new trainees could not, two mediums were fighting for real. One of them was on her knees and getting up again. \"Channel rank and above for that,\" Okada said. \"Forms for everybody else. You leave here knowing what your ability does when a hand is genuinely trying to end you.\" Along one wall stood an aviary full of noisy, squabbling crows. On a perch at its end sat a falcon with its hood pushed back off its head. It turned its eyes on Rei and held them. He had the clear feeling of being seen on both sides of the wall at once. \"It can see the veil,\" Okada said. \"Trained. It frightened your brother more than the sparring did.\""}, {"img": "cvdroom_cattery.jpg", "cap": "The cattery, five cats on five cushions"}, {"p": "The last door was warm. The room behind it smelled of clean straw and old paper. Five cats lay on five cushions, each with a small brass tag. <em>Sera. Itou. Mochida. Hara. Kuga.</em> \"Names of five operatives who didn't come back,\" Okada said quietly. \"The handler named the cats for them.\" The senior handler was a woman who did not give her name and did not look up from the cat in her lap. Kuga lay on the cushion by the window. He was a big grey cat with torn ears, and he did not look up either."}, {"p": "Okada sat Rei down on a wooden crate and explained."}, {"p": "\"There are two ways for someone like you to put his body through and get it back,\" she said. \"The first is the False Death. Pufferfish toxin and snake venom, mixed to a ratio the archive keeps. Forty-eight hours to prepare it, no less. The person going under needs Psychometabolism at the third level, and a second person with it at the second level has to sit the watch and run the return. You hold an object against your breastbone, something you've carried more than a year, so your soul can find the body again. Three to seven minutes and you have no pulse. To anything below Class A you read as dead. Your consciousness waits at the Threshold. The window to bring you back is three or four hours. Nobody has come back after eight.\" She paused. \"There's one death on record from doing it without a monitor. And the Threshold is not empty. Things there can reach a soul held that close to the door.\""}, {"p": "\"I don't have Psychometabolism,\" Rei said. \"And I don't have forty-eight hours.\""}, {"p": "\"No.\" She looked at the cushion by the window. \"The second way is a cat. Still water at least two centimetres deep, still enough to be a mirror. You stand in it barefoot. You hold the cat against your chest with both hands. You look into its eyes and you do not look away. Rank doesn't come into it. The cat's pupil is the aperture. If it consents, and it takes ten seconds to forty for it to decide, its weight changes in your hands and the room behind it stops being the room. That's the door. Your sight goes up a tier. You can step partway through, or with a cat that knows the way, much further. The cat chooses the distance. Not you. It costs you eight points of stability to open. It holds fifteen to thirty minutes. Nobody has held one past forty. When the cat looks away, the door shuts, at once, with no warning.\" She turned back to him. \"To our instruments you'll read as a stray cat and nothing else. That's why it's the cleanest crossing there is. But it doesn't make you invisible to anything already on the other side. Know the difference.\""}, {"p": "\"I cross through Kuga and no other when it truly matters,\" the handler said, still without looking up. \"But he's not mine to lend. Access is the handler and the cat's consent. The cat's consent outranks me.\""}, {"img": "animal_kuga.jpg", "cap": "Kuga, the grey cat with the torn ears"}, {"p": "Kuga opened his eyes. He got down off his cushion without hurry, the way cats do everything. He crossed the straw and sat at Rei's feet. Then he looked up into Rei's face and kept looking."}, {"p": "The handler let out a breath. \"Well,\" she said. \"That's that, then.\""}, {"p": "Okada signed the cat out on a clipboard by the door, under her own name. Rei watched her do it. He knew exactly which rule she was breaking, and how much she would hate herself for it later. She did not say anything about it. She only said, \"The node behind the Senju waterworks. Gin marked it smallest. The smallest tear is the one most likely to let a living man back out. I'll meet you there after dark with the water.\""}, {"break": "The seam, the third night"}, {"p": "Rei carried Kuga out through the east exit in a plastic carrier with a grille at the front. The cat did not make a sound. On the platform above, a train came in and the doors opened and a crowd of tired people came down the stairs with suitcases, back from their home towns, already sunburned. A small boy pressed his face to the grille of the carrier and said \"Neko!\", the word for cat, and his mother pulled him away with an apology. Rei bowed to her a little. He did not trust himself to speak."}, {"p": "By the time he reached the seam in Kita, the sun was down. The third night came the way the bottom of a held breath comes. He stood on the embankment with the carrier at his feet. Below him, in the old streets, the first sending-fires were being lit, early, the way Kita lit everything early now. Okuribi, small flames in clay dishes by the front doors. Each one was a family's tenderness. And each one, this year, was a small part of the pull the Healer had spent a month preparing to borrow. He could feel the turning point through his two open paths. The great seasonal lean of the world was beginning to swing, from welcome to farewell. At the peak of each small swing, the curtain over the wound went from thin to almost nothing. He could feel how it would be on the last night, when the swing was complete and the door stood open both ways at once."}, {"p": "Kishin was already leaning into the gap, hunger turned into weather, its mouth somewhere in the dark below with Kaito's young face. The Healer was already climbing. Gin's body was carrying it toward the place where the chair would be, calm as a man keeping an appointment. And Rei stood between them at the wound, with everything he had learned and no law of his own. The structure waited for him to choose which fire the night would light. It had been patient since before the wards had names."}, {"p": "Standing there with the okuribi burning below him, he understood that this had never been a puzzle with a solution. There was no move that saved everything. The Axis had shown him that, and the offer in the kitchen had proved it. Every way off this embankment cost something. He could trust the monster in his brother's body and bet the district on its mercy. He could throw himself against the wound and hope his mind lasted longer than the night. He could take Gin's hand and walk away and let the lanterns go out over a ward that had lit them early. He could feel each choice the way he could feel the slope, and none of them was clean. Only one thing in the whole structure was not already decided. It was the half sentence his brother had drowned to give him. <em>It needs its old one.</em>"}, {"p": "The sending-fires burned. The hinge of the year swung. His brother climbed toward the chair, and somewhere under the calm, a held breath of his own was still knocking. Once, then once, then once. Whatever Rei chose, he would choose for all of it. The wall, the district, the chair, the man. And for the person he would be on the far side of this, who was not the person who had stood in a cold kitchen a few nights ago, a whole life ago, and counted the doors the morning had left open. The structure would not decide for him. The demons could not. And Gin, drowned and knocking, had spent everything he had to leave it in Rei's hands."}, {"p": "Rei breathed in the warm air the city was letting go of. He did not choose the fire yet. He chose the door. Before he chose anything for all of it, he would go down and ask the old occupant the one question he was allowed. He picked up the carrier. Kuga shifted inside it. And Rei went down off the seam, toward the river and the waterworks, where Okada was waiting with the water."}], "n": 41}, {"title": "Beyond the Veil", "kind": "main", "src": "m_beyond", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei walked to Senju along the river. It took him more than an hour, with the carrier on his shoulder and Kuga's weight shifting inside it at every step. The Sumida river runs east out of Kita in long slow bends, with a raised concrete embankment on each side and a path along the top. At night the path was nearly empty. A man in a sweat-soaked T-shirt jogged past with a small light on his chest. Further on, an old man sat on a folding stool with a fishing rod, a radio playing baseball quietly at his feet, though there could be nothing worth catching in that black water. The air smelled of mud and warm concrete."}, {"p": "Down in the streets on either side, the okuribi were burning. From the embankment Rei could see them as tiny orange points by the doorsteps, one here, two there, and families crouched beside them in summer clothes. A grandmother held a small child's hand over a flame to show him how close was safe. A man in a white shirt stood alone by his fire with his hands together and his head bowed, and did not move the whole time Rei was passing. The smoke from the burning hemp stalks drifted up over the river and made his eyes water."}, {"p": "He chose the node behind the Senju waterworks because it was the one Gin had marked smallest, in the back of the book, the way Gin marked the things he meant to come back to. <em>Smallest tear. Most likely to let a living man back out.</em> And he chose it because it was not in Kita. Somewhere along the river, with the carrier on his shoulder, he had felt the attention of the gutters slide off him. The rats watched Kita. They did not watch Senju. Gin's book had said to change the district, and for one night he had."}, {"p": "Okada was already there. She was kneeling at the edge of a concrete settling pool behind the pump house, where the overflow water lay dead still under the security lights. Big pipes ran along the wall behind her, humming. She had brought two things in a cool-box. One was a towel. The other, when she lifted the lid, was a long, pale, patterned snake that poured itself over her wrist without any hurry."}, {"img": "animal_snake.jpg", "cap": "A rat snake set across the sill"}, {"p": "\"Japanese rat snake,\" she said. \"Not venomous. The venom isn't the point.\" She set it across the sill of the yard gate and dusted the concrete in front of it with a handful of dark earth from a paper bag. \"Graveyard earth. It sharpens the snake at that one spot, gives us another forty minutes of good warning. Anything that tries to cross that gate, it strikes. It can't see what it's striking any better than we can. It just knows.\" She sat back on her heels. \"It strikes people too. So when you come back, you come back here, at the water. Not through the gate. Route yourself.\" She rubbed the snake's head with one thumb. \"Two hours, then it gets tired and starts snapping at its own side. We won't need two hours. Nobody holds a cat door past forty minutes.\""}, {"p": "While she set the snake, Rei sat on the edge of the pool with Gin's book open on his knees. It was open at the page he had read most often that month, a hand-drawn map of Kita's pockets. A pocket, as Gin used it, meant a fold in the veil, a small hidden space between the living world and the other side."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's book. Pockets. What they actually are", "lines": ["A pocket is a stable fold in the membrane. A space between the living world and the Threshold where a practitioner can work without entity exposure. Time runs at about the same rate inside. Once you are in, entities cannot follow at standard capability. Good for training, for meeting people where the demon's monitoring cannot reach, for recovery.", "Shallow. A standard fold. Entity-free. Exits where you entered. It takes a Vanguard to make a new one.", "Mid-depth. Made by people who wanted to be near the Font. The Architect's processes are faintly legible. Arbiter rank to navigate. Can exit somewhere else in the living world if you know the mapping.", "Deep. The floor is unstable. The Architect is close. Entities avoid it. The most dangerous thing here is the Architect. If the floor gives out under you, you come out next to the Threshold.", "Beyond. Below the Threshold. No one in any record I can reach has come back from this depth with a coherent account.", "Three pockets in Kita. One is the evidence archive itself, which is slightly larger inside than its floor plan. One is a manhole behind building 14B. The third I am not writing down.", "I went into the deepest one twice. The second time I came out three districts away, six hours later. My archive entry from that day is the one that is slightly wrong.", "Of the five I mapped, one has no depth marker. I stopped at the edge and did not go in. The needle points straight down when I hold it near the entrance.", "What pockets are not. Safe from the Font. The Font is not an entity. It is the infrastructure, and the pockets are inside it. If it is paying attention to you, that attention follows you in. The pockets are safe from demons. They are not safe from the architect of the space everyone is living in. G.U."]}}, {"p": "Put simply, a pocket could hide a person from demons, but not from the Font, and the deeper the pocket, the closer it sat to the Architect. Rei had thought about the unmarked pocket all afternoon, the one where the needle pointed down. It went where he needed to go. It also went to the place nobody had come back from with their words in order, and even Gin had stopped at its edge. A pocket would hide him from the Healer. It would not hide him from the thing he was going to see. Nothing would. He shut the book and put it inside his shirt, against his skin, where he could feel its corner."}, {"p": "Before he closed it, Okada took out Gin's page on the sight scale. She read it and handed it back folded small. \"Read it once more,\" she said. \"So you know what's happening when it happens.\""}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Veil Sight. The Four Tiers", "lines": ["I. E and D rank. The Grey Blur. Sensitivity without sight. Heaviness, wrongness, residue. No shape. About a fifth of the other side visible.", "II. C and B rank. Shadows and Shape. The veil becomes visible. Shapes resolve into forms. Type and register readable. From here, entities see you back. Visibility goes both ways.", "III. A and S rank. Full Dual Perception. Both worlds at once, fully populated. A street, plus everything that has died on it.", "IV. SS and SSS rank. Structural Sight. The membrane itself is visible. No off switch. You adapt to it.", "Thin spots raise every tier by one... and the entities see you the same amount more.", "Gin, in the margin beside Rei's name. Presenting Sensitive with suppression running. Actual sight significantly higher. He calls it instinct. It is not instinct."]}}, {"p": "Rei took off his shoes and socks. The water was as warm as a bath at the edges and cool where he stood in it. It was two fingers deep over the concrete and flat as a sheet of glass. Okada unclipped the carrier. Kuga stepped out into the night as if he owned the waterworks. He looked at the pool with distaste, and then he let Rei lift him. He was heavier than he looked. Rei held him against his chest with both hands, the way the page said. Okada knelt at his shoulder with one hand an inch above the surface, ready to slap it into ripples if the cat decided wrong. A broken reflection is no door."}, {"p": "\"Eyes,\" Okada said. \"Don't look away. Whatever you see.\""}, {"p": "Rei looked into Kuga's eyes."}, {"p": "For a long time it was only a cat. Yellow eyes, a slit of black, one torn ear twitching at the sound of the pumps. Ten seconds. Twenty. Rei held on and did not blink. Then the weight in his hands changed, as if the cat had settled more deeply into itself. The eyes went half closed, and then fixed on his. The black of the pupil opened like a door being opened from the far side. It cost him at once. He felt a measure of himself drain out of him like warm water down a plughole. Behind Kuga's head, the waterworks stopped being the waterworks."}, {"p": "He had expected a lurch. Cold. The smell of a room shut up too long. He got none of it. He got Tokyo. The same August. The same Obon lanterns strung between the same poles. The same heat lying on the canal like a held breath. Only after a long moment did he understand that none of it was moving. The lanterns hung without swinging. The canal did not lap at its banks. The heat was only a memory of heat, laid over a place that had never had weather."}], "n": 42}, {"title": "The Threshold, and Below It", "kind": "main", "src": "m_beyond#2", "blocks": [{"p": "He went through the first layer so fast that he barely had time to be afraid of it. Kuga carried him through it the way an express train carries a passenger past a crowded platform without stopping. For a few heartbeats there was noise everywhere, and the noise was people. This was the Threshold, the nearest layer of the other side, where the newly dead arrive. Gin had written that the other side is not dark, it is loud, and here was the loud. The recent dead stood shoulder to shoulder like commuters in a station where the trains have stopped. They were confused. They kept turning, not yet sure which way they were meant to go. Mediums worked here. Exorcists worked here. A practitioner who touched this layer while worn out was at risk of possession. Nothing here hunted him. The crowd was simply so thick that it pressed in. Then the cat's gaze tipped, and the crowd fell away above Rei, and the quiet began."}, {"img": "ch20b_cat.jpg", "cap": "Kuga, and the Deep's copy of Kita"}, {"p": "Below the Threshold was the Deep, and the Deep had made itself a copy of his district. It was Kita drawn by something that had only heard about it from others. The konbini on the corner was lit and empty. The rice balls behind the glass were the same four kinds, forever. The prices were right. The little hand-written apology taped to the broken coffee machine was right, down to its worn fold. When he tried the door with one bare foot, it opened onto more of the same street. There was no inside here. There was only the surface that things keep up to seem alive. <em>Entities that have had time to develop culture, alliances, hierarchies, territories, agendas,</em> Gin's book said of this layer. <em>They have had time to develop opinions about visitors.</em> Gin had been here twice. Both times on purpose."}, {"p": "Rei walked the way Gin had made him walk through the Archive, in Gin's strict order. North, then west, then the half step back that is not on any map. He kept Kuga's eyes the whole way. The false city let him pass, because to the far shore he was not yet a man. He was a draught under a door. He was the one cold thing in a room that did not know it had gone cold."}, {"p": "Then it noticed."}, {"p": "He felt the moment it happened, the way a person feels a stranger's eyes land on the back of his neck in a crowd. A small wrongness became an attention. Every lantern on the canal turned to face the same way, all at once and without swinging. They faced him. Rei's sight climbed on its own, as the page had said it would. Then it climbed again, because this whole place was one huge thin spot. The street became shapes. The shapes became forms, and he could read their registers, the feelings they fed on. <em>From here, entities see you back.</em> They did. The not-weather thickened. The konbini light behind him changed from a welcome into a witness. Okada had been right about the cat. To every instrument in Tokyo, he was a stray in a pool. To the things already here, he was perfectly visible."}, {"p": "He had come in as a draught, and the far shore had just learned to count. The number it had reached was one too many. Somewhere ahead, past the place where the streets stopped pretending, something that had been patient for a very long time put down what it was holding. And it began, without hurry, to come and see what was alive in its house."}, {"p": "Further in, the city gave up its pretending little by little, and that was worse than a clean edge would have been. First the writing went. The konbini signs, the ward notices, the lost-cat poster on the pole all kept their shape but lost their words, the way a name goes soft when it is said too many times. Then the depth went. Buildings became the idea of buildings, fronts that only believed they were fronts. Where two of them met, he could see there was nothing behind either one. Only the seam. Only the place the far shore had not bothered to imagine, because no living eye was ever meant to reach it."}, {"p": "Rei kept Gin's order, because it was the only handrail in a stairwell with no stairs. And at last he understood what the Archive had been. It was not a map of this place. It was a way of walking that this place would put up with. It was a politeness his brother had learned and written down small, so that one day Rei could be rude to a god without being killed on the doorstep."}, {"p": "He passed through what had been the canal. Now it was only the feeling of a long, cold absence. On the far side the houses were gone. There was only the standing. That was the only word he had for it. A field of upright attention, like a crowd seen from behind. None of it faced him. All of it faced inward, toward the centre, the way iron filings face a magnet. His sight had doubled now, full and steady, exactly as the page had promised. A street, plus everything that has died on it. Except the street was gone, and only the dead were left. Every soul who had ever stood in Kita in the dark and felt something with a name they almost knew pass overhead. He was walking through the inside of the dread his whole ward had felt all month without being able to say why."}, {"p": "And he could feel the slope again, the one he had seen from the kitchen floor. It was not under him now. It was in front of him, and it was everything. This was the third layer, the one Gin's diagram had drawn last and smallest, with no arrow showing a way out. The Font layer. <em>Entities that reach it dissolve completely. Not painful. It is described as coming home. That is the part that frightens.</em> Kuga's claws pricked through Rei's shirt, very gently, and Rei realised he had taken a step toward it without meaning to. He stopped. He held the cat's eyes and made himself stand still until the wanting passed. It did not pass. It only waited."}, {"p": "At the heart of the standing, where all of it leaned, there was a chair."}, {"p": "Not a throne. A chair. A plain one, the kind that belongs at a kitchen table, the kind he had sat in across from his brother on a thousand mornings. It was empty, and it was the loudest thing he had ever stood near. The Thirteenth Seat was neither occupied nor ruined. It was empty the way a bell is empty just after it has been struck. It was still ringing with the shape of the last holder, who had died out of it in 2009. The ringing was what thinned the wall. The ringing was what had been pulling at Gin. The ringing was the open mouth the Healer meant to fill."}, {"img": "char_kuze-kaname-light.jpg", "cap": "The man of light behind the empty chair, holding the door shut"}, {"p": "Behind the chair, so close that the chair might have been his, stood the man."}, {"p": "He was made of light so cold that looking at it hurt Rei's teeth. He stood with his back to Rei and his back to the whole world, very straight and very still. His attention was fixed on something Rei could not see, and he was holding it shut. He did not turn. Gin's book had said he would not. <em>It does not show its face.</em> But Rei was close enough now to see what the light was wrapped around. The shoulders of a young man. Dark hair turning faintly see-through at the edges, where the light leaked out of him. A coat cut in a style nobody had used for a very long time. It was dark and plain and worn at the elbows, the kind of coat a man wears to work."}, {"p": "Rei was closer to the centre than any living man had ever stood. He took one more step, into the place where the standing leaned hardest. And the chair, the empty chair, turned its attention on him, all at once, the way the whole far shore had turned. Without words, in his own brother's way of speaking, it asked him what he thought he was."}], "n": 43}, {"title": "The Shove", "kind": "main", "src": "m_shove", "blocks": [{"p": "\"His brother,\" Rei said aloud. His voice came out small and flat in the air that was not air. It was the voice of a man in a kitchen. \"I'm his brother. That's all I am.\""}, {"img": "ch14_layer2.jpg", "cap": "Rei before the man of light"}, {"p": "The chair did not answer that. Behind it, the man of light did not move. Kuga's eyes held Rei's, yellow and patient. Rei felt the cat beginning to tire in his arms. There was a faint restlessness in its weight, the first warning. He did not have long. He had one question. Gin's book had been very clear about the number."}, {"p": "He had spent the long walk along the river deciding what to ask. How to save Gin. How to close the wall. How to kill the thing in his brother's body. Each of those was a question about power. Standing here, he had a feeling that power was the one thing this man had stopped listening to, centuries ago. So he asked the other question. The one his brother had drowned to point him toward."}, {"p": "\"Who were you?\""}, {"p": "The light flinched."}, {"p": "It was not much. A ripple ran through the cold shine of him, the way a candle flame dips when a door opens somewhere else in the house. The man did not turn. But his head moved a little toward one shoulder, the way a person half turns when he hears his name called in a crowd and is not sure it was meant for him. And the answer came into Rei the same way the lattice had shown him its history. Not in words. In pieces, out of order, like photographs dropped on a floor."}, {"p": "A paper lantern at dusk, hanging from a wooden roof edge, with three characters brushed on it in old ink. 逢魔時. <em>Ōmagatoki.</em> The twilight hour. In the old stories it is the hour between day and night when a person is most likely to meet demons. Under the characters was a small crest, as plain as an office stamp."}, {"p": "A notebook in a young man's hand. Its pages were full of names written very small, the way Gin wrote the things the records must never see. The hand had an ink stain on the second finger. It turned a page and kept writing. This was somebody who asked questions for a living and wrote down the answers."}, {"p": "A city of wooden bridges and low tiled roofs, of carts and straw sandals and swords worn at the hip. No electric wires. No neon. A river with no concrete on its banks. It was Edo, the old city that stood here for centuries before it was renamed Tokyo."}, {"p": "A narrow road at evening, and the young man walking along it with his shadow beside him, not behind. He did not cut the shadow off. He did not follow it either. He walked between the light and the dark the way a person walks along the top of a wall. The shadow kept pace with him like a companion he was no longer afraid of."}, {"p": "A plain wooden chair at the centre of everything. The young man sitting down in it, slowly, the way a person sits after a long day. The weight settling onto his shoulders. And him not getting up."}, {"p": "And last, smallest, a single character, like a rivet driven through all the rest. 要. It meant the pivot. The keystone. The small pin that a folding fan turns on. Rei did not know why it was there. He only knew that the man of light had held it out to him, the way a person holds out the one thing left in his pocket."}, {"p": "Then the pieces stopped. Behind the chair, the man's head turned back to the wall, to the work, to the thing he was holding shut. The light closed over the place where he had almost been someone. Rei felt it like a door shutting in another room. He had been answered. One question, one answer. That was the rule."}, {"p": "And then the chair let him reach."}, {"p": "That was the cruelty of it. It let his hand close the last of the distance. It let his living fingers find the edge of the empty place, where the last holder of the Thirteenth had died out of the seat and left it ringing. For one whole second he was touching the wound directly, with no glass between, the way he had wanted to touch his brother all month. In that second he read it. It was not a hole. It was an absence shaped like a hand. It was the outline of a holder who had been Duty and something more besides, two paths braided into one law. Rei's own two paths were braided the same way inside him, and he had never said so to anyone. All at once, and far too late, he understood why the Healer had wanted him here, and not only the chair. Why it had offered him the seat in his own kitchen, as mercy. The wound was shaped for a soul that walked more than one path. It had been waiting six years and longer for the right shape of hand. And the hand it had been waiting for was, near enough, his."}, {"p": "Then the far shore decided what he was. He was alive. And it did to him what a body does to a splinter."}, {"p": "There was no anger in it. That was the part he would never be able to explain afterwards, the part that would sit under his breastbone for the rest of the month. There was only the huge, patient correction of a place pushing out the one thing in it that breathed. The standing turned. The whole leaning field of attention, which had faced the centre, now faced him instead, all at once and without swinging. The not-weather went from thick to total. And the chair, the plain kitchen chair, the loudest quiet he had ever stood near, pushed. It was not a force he could brace against. It was an ending. It was the flat refusal of a closed door that has decided a man was never inside."}, {"p": "Kuga looked away."}, {"p": "The door shut at once, exactly as the page had promised. No warning. No mercy. Rei was thrown back along Gin's order in reverse, far too fast. All the half steps he had walked so carefully were used up in one backward fall. The streets got their writing back, and their depth, and their lying confidence, in flashes as he went. Canal. Konbini. Lantern. The stranger's eyes peeled off the back of his neck one layer at a time. And the last thing the far shore did, as it shut behind him, was close its hand. He felt it close around the part of him that had touched the wound. He felt the wound hold on. And he felt it tear. Not his skin. Nothing so simple. It tore the way a name tears when two people are saying it at the same time."}, {"break": "The settling pool, Senju"}, {"p": "He came out of the water on his back. The settling pool slapped up around him in a sheet and came down on his face, warm and stinking of iron and river. Okada's hand was already under his head, keeping his mouth above the surface. Somewhere to his left a cat was complaining loudly about being wet."}, {"p": "\"Breathe,\" Okada said. \"Rei. Breathe first.\""}, {"p": "He breathed. The real August heat lay on him. The real security lights buzzed, and moths knocked against them. Somewhere a motorbike went over a bridge, its engine rising and fading. Beyond the pump house, the lanterns of somebody's okuribi were swinging on a real string, in a real breeze. He lay on the edge of the pool in his own moving world and looked at his hands. They had come back with him. They were no longer only his hands."}, {"p": "At the yard gate, the rat snake struck."}, {"p": "It hit the empty air above the sill so fast that Rei only saw the end of it, the long pale body snapping back into its coil. Okada was on her feet. Nothing came through the gate. The snake's head stayed fixed on the space above the graveyard earth, its tongue flicking. After a long minute it slowly lowered its chin to the concrete again."}, {"p": "Okada crouched beside it and laid two fingers along its back, and her eyes lost their focus. Rei had seen her do the same with a stack of files. \"Small,\" she said at last. \"Low class. It came up the trace behind you, the way water comes up a footprint. It tried the gate and the snake bit it and it went back down.\" She took her fingers away and looked at him. For once she did not look at his hands first. \"It followed you out, Rei. Something over there has your smell now.\""}, {"p": "His hands looked like his hands, until they didn't. In the security light they were only hands, scraped at the heel from the concrete edge, shaking in the ordinary way a man shakes after a fall. Then he turned them over, and the marks were there. They were as faint as the after-image of a bright window. They lay in the lines of his palms like a second set of fingerprints under the first. He knew without being told that they were not decoration and not an injury. They were the grain of the wound, printed onto the hand that had touched it. And under the grain, finer still, was something else. It was the leftover warmth of the man at the centre, who had every path there was lit inside him, and who had answered Rei's question with a hand held out."}, {"p": "He flexed his fingers, and one of the lines grew warm."}, {"p": "For half a heartbeat, he heard a woman. Not with his ears. She was old. She was two floors up in the dark block of flats across the road, lying awake on a futon, and her thought came into him whole and tired, and it was not his. <em>I never can sleep at Obon. He always comes and stands at the foot of the bed.</em> Then she was gone. Rei had read people all his life, but only the space around them, the surface of the lake. Gin's book had a line for the difference. <em>A Clairsentient knows you are angry. A Telepath knows why.</em> That had not been the surface. That had been the water underneath."}, {"p": "He stood up too fast, and the world skipped."}, {"img": "prac_tp.jpg", "cap": "A teleport flicker that is not his"}, {"p": "One moment he was on his feet at the edge of the pool. The next he was three metres along it, by the pump-house wall, with nothing in between. No step. No feeling of moving. Just here, and then there, and a hard sick lurch in his stomach, like missing a stair in the dark. Behind him on the wet concrete, his footprints ran from the edge of the pool and stopped. Then there was dry concrete. Then there was him."}, {"p": "Okada stared at the gap in the footprints. Kuga stopped washing himself and stared too."}, {"p": "\"That isn't yours,\" Okada said quietly."}, {"p": "\"I know.\""}, {"p": "It frightened him more than the shove had. The shove had been a door closing. This was a door left open inside his own skin. All his life he had held two paths down so hard that he had taught himself they were only one, because one was easier to carry than the truth of both. And now there was a third thing in his hands that was not even his. Abilities he had not earned and could not give back, firing on their own. And under them, a grain shaped to fit a seat that had been waiting for exactly the hand he had."}, {"p": "Now he understood the trap completely, the way a person understands a sentence the moment after he has said the wrong half of it. The Healer had never needed to make him take the chair. It had only needed him to touch the wound. The far shore had done the rest. It had marked him, and shaped him, and begun the long quiet work of making his hand fit the absence. The borrowed sparks would burn out. He could feel that already, the way he could feel a fever getting ready to break. But the grain under them would not. Every day he carried it, it would become a little more his and a little less borrowed. In the end he would not be able to find the difference between holding the seat and being the kind of thing that holds it."}, {"p": "He closed his fists over the marks, and they pulsed against his palms. Patient. Not his. Becoming his. Down the river, the real lanterns swung on their string. The real heat sat on the real water. The city breathed around him, asleep and unaware. And somewhere under all of it, leaning on a wall that rang with the shape of an empty kitchen chair, his brother was being worn by something that had just got everything it had sent him in to get."}, {"p": "He had come back from beyond the veil alive, which no one was supposed to do. He had come back knowing where the wound was and how it tore. He had come back with a lantern, a notebook, a road, a chair, and one small character whose meaning he could not read. And he had come back carrying the first payment of the price, printed into the only hands he had ever had to reach for Gin with. Standing by the pool with his fists shut, he did not know which he had brought home. The way to close the wall, or only the wall's way of choosing him."}], "n": 44}, {"title": "What He Carried Back", "kind": "main", "src": "m_carried", "blocks": [{"p": "Okada took Kuga back to the grey block in a taxi. She sat with the carrier on her knees, and the cat inside it sulked like a wet king. Rei walked home along the river. The last trains had already gone. And a man with what Rei had in his hands should not be underground, packed in among other people, until he understood what he was carrying."}, {"p": "It was after one in the morning. The river path was empty, and the city on both sides of it had gone quiet in the way Tokyo goes quiet, never fully. A taxi crawled along the road above with its red light on, looking for a fare. Up on a bridge, a delivery scooter went by with a box of something on the back. On the far bank a single window was lit high up in a block of flats, and in it a man in a vest sat at a table eating noodles out of a cup, alone, at one in the morning, during the festival of the dead. Rei watched him until the angle of the bank took him away."}, {"p": "The marks settled as he walked. That was the word for it. Settled, the way mud settles in still water, the way a tenant settles into a room. It was the way something on a person becomes something inside him while he is busy thinking about other things. By the second bridge he could no longer tell the difference between reaching out with his own thin sight and reaching out with the borrowed grain. So he made himself stop reaching at all. He pushed his hands into his pockets and kept them there."}, {"p": "It did not help much. On the third bridge, a drunk had left an empty beer can standing on the railing. A car horn sounded and Rei flinched, and the can slid away from him along the rail and dropped into the water, though his hand was nowhere near it. Telekinesis. Force with no hand behind it, the most visible thing a practitioner can do. He stood looking at the ripples. He thought of every page in Gin's book that said people who could do that learned to hide young, the first time a cup moved at the dinner table."}, {"img": "prac_pkn.jpg", "cap": "A streetlamp flaring as Rei passes beneath it"}, {"p": "Under the expressway, a streetlamp brightened as he passed beneath it. It grew too bright, humming like a struck wire, and then went out. The vending machine beside it flickered its lights and restarted, and its little recorded voice thanked nobody. Rei walked faster. Somewhere a power log had just recorded a spike. Somewhere a security camera had just lost three seconds. That was Psychokinesis, the power over electricity and heat and sound. It did not leave a trail of footprints. It left a trail of paperwork."}, {"p": "By the river stairs, his scraped heel stopped stinging. He looked down at it under a lantern. The graze had closed over, pink and new. A moment later the cold came, a deep tiredness like the end of a very long day, and he had to sit on the step until his hands stopped shaking. Healing was not free. It took what it used from the one who did it. This was Psychometabolism, the path that works on the body. He had spent something of himself to mend a scratch he had not even minded."}, {"img": "prac_telepath.jpg", "cap": "Other people's thoughts, reaching Rei uninvited"}, {"p": "He sat on the step and counted, because Gin had taught him to count things. The old woman's thought by the pool. The jump along the edge of the water. The can. The lamp. The heel. Five paths that were not his, firing one after another like a house creaking at night. Six, with the Clairsentience he had always had. Every path there was. He had touched a man who had all of them lit at once, and the man's warmth had come home on his hands like flour."}, {"p": "Then he understood the shape of the coming month. The gifts would be useful exactly as far as they were dangerous. Every time the far shore's tools solved a problem for him, they would solve it a little more as him and a little less as something borrowed. And the Healer was in no hurry. It did not need to be. It had forty years of patience behind it. Rei had two paths, and an open wound that already, faintly, fit his palm."}, {"p": "Then he thought about Gin. He had not let himself do it since the concrete. Now he let himself think about Gin honestly, and not bravely. Gin was in there, leaning on the wall Rei had just touched from the far side. Gin was being worn by the thing that had wanted Rei to touch it. And here was the cruellest sum of the whole crossing. The gift in his hands was the best tool he had ever had for finding his brother. It was also the leash the far shore was using to walk him toward his brother's chair. Sitting on a river step with his fists in his pockets, he could not tell what his wanting was. Maybe it was love, the plain wish to use this gift to reach Gin. Or maybe it was the marks, already learning to want what the seat wanted."}, {"p": "All month he had been certain of one thing. Whatever else was true, he was the one in his own head. Now he was not certain. And that, he understood, was exactly the payment the far shore had meant to send home with him. Not a power. A doubt. The one wound a witness cannot read his way out of."}, {"p": "He got up and walked the rest of the way. The river bent north and west, back toward Kita. Near Ōji the paths became streets again, and the streets had lanterns. Outside one house, a family's okuribi had burned down to a few red sparks in its clay dish, and a cat sat beside it, watching the sparks, as if it had been asked to keep an eye on them. A konbini glowed on the corner, and the young clerk inside was asleep standing up, leaning on the counter with his chin in his hand. The heat had hardly dropped at all."}, {"break": "The door that locks itself"}, {"p": "By the time he reached his own door, he had made the only decision the night would let him make. It was the real door, the one that locked itself, the one this whole month had begun behind. His decision was not about what to do with the gifts. It was whether to be honest that he had them."}, {"p": "The kitchen was dark through the glass. The kettle was cold. The chair across the table was empty in the small, ordinary way, not the loud way of the far shore. Rei stood on his own step with a stranger's tools printed into his hands. His brother's body was somewhere on the wrong side of a wall that rang like this room. He knew that whatever he did next would decide what kind of person reached Gin, when he finally reached him. The brother who went in. Or the thing the going in was making."}, {"p": "Things that hide what they are have a way of becoming what they hide. He had watched it happen all month, in a cardigan, across this table. So he went in and switched on the light and sat down in his own chair. He opened Gin's book to the back. At the top of a clean page, he wrote it in plain words, before he could make them gentler. <em>I went through at Senju. I touched it. I came back with things in my hands that are not mine. I am writing this down so I can't pretend later that I didn't know.</em> In the morning he would tell Okada, and Itsuki, and anyone else who needed to know what was standing in their kitchen. Tonight he would tell the book."}, {"p": "Then, because Gin's book was the only map he had, he turned back to the addendum on Font proximity. It was about what happens to a person who goes too close to the Font and gets thrown back. He had read it the night after the cold room, when it had been about other people. It listed four weights of damage. Light, Moderate, Heavy, Critical. The weight was set at the moment of being thrown out, and everything after that was just time. It read differently now."}, {"p": "The bleed was there. The edges of the kitchen looked doubled. Through the wall he could hear the hum of the neighbour's sleep, which he had never been able to hear before. That was Light. It would last weeks, and then the veil would close over again. Then he thought of the snake striking at nothing above the graveyard earth, and of Okada's fingers on its back. <em>Something over there has your smell now.</em> The line under Moderate said <em>things contact them</em>. He wrote <em>Light</em> in the margin, small. After a long moment he wrote <em>maybe more</em> beside it."}, {"p": "It should have been worse. He had stood closer to the centre than anyone in the restricted files, and he had walked home on his own feet. The reason was on the family page, the one where Gin had admitted to opening four paths in secret. At twenty-two, Gin had come back from the Threshold much less damaged than he had any right to be. And he had found the same thing in the case files of two Uedera ancestors. His guess, written very carefully, was this. A practitioner with more than one path open spreads the weight of being thrown back across all of them, so no single path takes the whole blow. Two paths. The searching one, and the nameless one under it, held flat since before Rei could read. They had shared the blow between them. Gin had hidden them from the registry and taught Rei to hold them down as a family habit. Tonight that habit had probably saved his mind."}, {"p": "Further down the same page, Gin had written his advice to anyone who had been thrown back. Find someone who has been there. Do not decide that the damage is permanent because of one bad week. And the fourth piece of advice, the one Gin had boxed in pencil."}, {"p": "<em>Do not go back because the wanting says to.</em> Rei put his finger on the line and held it there. Even now, with the light on and the kettle cold, he could feel the slope three hundred metres to the northeast, warm and patient. The part of him that had stood at the top of it wanted very badly to go and lie down at the bottom. He took his finger off the page. He turned to the clean sheet where he had written his confession. Below it, in the order they had come to him, he wrote down the rest of what he had carried back."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "The back of Gin's book, in Rei's hand", "lines": ["One question. Who were you.", "A paper lantern at dusk with 逢魔時 on it. Ōmagatoki. The hour when you meet demons. A crest under it like an office seal. An office? Of what?", "A notebook full of names written small. Ink on the second finger. He asked questions for a living. Like Gin.", "Wooden bridges, swords, no wires. Old. Edo? Before Tokyo was Tokyo.", "A road at evening. He walked with his shadow beside him. Did not cut it off. Did not follow it. Down the middle.", "A plain chair. He sat down in it and the weight came on. He did not get up. The Duty chair. He was in it first.", "要. Pivot. Keystone. The pin a fan turns on. Why did he give me this?"]}}, {"p": "He looked at the last line for a long time. Gin had said the chair needed its old one. Gin had said the whole registered world had put him somewhere. And the man at the centre had not been able to tell Rei who he was. He could only hand him pieces, the way a person empties his pockets for someone he hopes will know what the things mean."}, {"p": "Rei traced the character again, slowly, pressing hard enough to dent the page. 要. It did not look like a word. It looked like something a man might once have answered to."}, {"p": "The window was going grey. Outside, the first crows were calling from the wires, and somewhere far off the first train of the day rattled over a bridge. Somewhere in the walls, the building's pipes knocked as the first neighbour ran a tap. Rei sat at the kitchen table where everything had started. For the first time since Gin went quiet on that Wednesday night, he was not only trying to find his brother. He was trying to remember someone else's name."}], "n": 45}, {"title": "From Gin's Book. The Six Abilities", "kind": "gin", "src": "gin_33_paths", "blocks": [{"p": "For the next few days the sparks kept coming. Each one was weaker than the last. Each time one fired, Rei went back to Gin's book and found the chapter Gin had written about that path. Gin had written all six, one by one, in his patient field voice, as if he had known that someone would one day need them in a hurry. Rei read them on the sky-blue Keihin-Tōhoku train, standing by the door with the book held close to his chest while a high-school boy beside him slept on his feet. He read them at the konbini counter while the clerk heated his rice. He read them in the bath. He read them the way a man reads the instructions for a machine that has already started."}, {"p": "A path is one of the six ways a practitioner's gift can reach into the world. Most practitioners are born with one, and some never open a second. What follows are Gin's own notes on each of the six, signed with his initials, G.U."}, {"img": "skill_paths.jpg", "cap": "The six ability paths"}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Clairsentience. The noise problem", "lines": ["Reading requires opening. When you open to read, you can be read.", "Telepathy reads minds. Clairsentience reads the space around minds. A Clairsentient knows you are angry. A Telepath knows why. A Clairsentient reads the surface of the lake. A Telepath goes into the water.", "Low cost for each use, high cost over years. Unmanaged practitioners with several channels half open produce static. To an assessor it feels loud, everywhere at once. The rank gets recorded at the floor of what could be read.", "This is our family's path. It is the one Rei calls instinct. G.U."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Telepathy. The push and the open", "lines": ["Clairsentience is eavesdropping. Telepathy is a conversation the other party may not know they are having.", "There is no 'I accidentally read you' defence. Every read is a choice.", "Thoughts are not language. It is interpretation, not recording.", "At the deepest levels it is the only path that can enter a partly possessed practitioner's mind. The difference between a recovery that finds the host and one that finds only the demon.", "A telepath is the only person in every room who cannot pretend they did not notice. G.U."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Psychometabolism. The cost of being the medic", "lines": ["It works where the body meets the veil. It reads injury, stability, depletion.", "Moving stability from yourself to another person is inefficient. You lose more than they gain. You cannot fully heal yourself.", "Teams treat the medic as infrastructure. The Grid runs them in pairs. The CVD uses one at a time and wonders why they leave.", "It is not a risk. It is the bill, and it always comes. G.U."]}}, {"img": "prac_tk.jpg", "cap": "Telekinesis, force before architecture"}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Telekinesis. Visibility", "lines": ["Force, and then architecture. At the start you push. Later you build with what is already in the room.", "It leaves signatures. A careful small use lasts half an hour. Real force lasts hours. Combat lasts days.", "Telekinetics learn to hide young, the first time a cup moves with someone in the room. Half the operatives wearing suppression marks chose them.", "A cup moved in our kitchen in the night. Two centimetres to the left. I did not move it. The cup is still where it moved to. G.U."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Psychokinesis. The noise ceiling", "lines": ["Electrical, thermal, sonic, membrane. Manipulation first. Generation later.", "Every use in the city leaves evidence. A dead camera leaves an event log. A surge leaves a power record.", "Warmth and cold are the quiet tools. They leave nothing on a screen.", "A practitioner who can hurt the formless can hurt anything.", "The hallway lights flickered. Third time this week. I am not a Psychokinesis practitioner. My secondary is something else. G.U."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Teleportation. The destination problem", "lines": ["The rarest after Telepathy. You need line of sight, or a clear remembered image, or a live description. A demolished building goes wrong in specific and unpleasant ways.", "Transit runs through the Threshold. Possession in mid-transit is documented.", "A passenger doubles the cost. A conscious person who resists cannot be carried. The target's will decides.", "I came back from a pocket three districts off in 2013, six hours late, and dreamed for a month that I had not come back at all.", "I have caught myself mapping the exits from my brother's kitchen. G.U."]}}, {"p": "The last page was the one Rei read most often. It was not in Gin's voice. It was a reference sheet that Gin had copied out by hand from somewhere he should not have been. He had underlined one word three times. The word was <em>transmission</em>."}, {"doc": {"kind": "form", "title": "Opening a New Path. Development Rules", "lines": ["A practitioner cannot open a new ability path alone. Readiness can build, but the opening needs direct transmission from someone who already walks that path. Not instruction. Transmission. The teacher uses the ability close to the student, again and again, until something in the student's aperture recognises what it has been reaching toward, and opens.", "The teacher must have the path. Not training in it. Having it. This is why masters matter.", "Sessions needed. Sensitive, eight to ten. Ritualist, six to eight. Channel, four to six. Arbiter, two to four. Vanguard and above, one or two. At that height it is recognition, not development.", "A session is a real scene with stakes. Interrupted training does not count. Practice under threat counts double.", "The opening comes at a moment of recognition, not on a schedule.", "Exception. The Uedera are born with two paths open, not one. The second does not need a teacher. It sits dormant from birth and opens through a recognition event, when it is needed and reached for without knowing. This is why Gin presented as single-path for years.", "Gin, in the margin. The Uedera line opens faster than the rule says. Half again as fast. I have not told Rei."]}}, {"p": "In plain words, the sheet said this. Nobody can teach himself a new path. Someone who truly has that path must use it close to him, again and again, until his own gift recognises it and opens. The higher a practitioner's rank, the fewer sessions it takes, from as many as ten at the lowest rank down to one or two at the top. And the Uedera family was the one exception written on the page. They were born with a second path already there, sleeping, which could wake by itself in a moment of need."}, {"p": "By the fourth day the sparks had almost stopped. The teleport flicker never came back after the second night. The lamp hum faded. The old woman's thoughts went quiet behind the wall. Rei understood why from the underlined word. He had not been taught anything. He had touched a man with every path lit and come home warm from it, and now the warmth was cooling, the way a hand cools after it lets go of another hand. The sparks would not stay unless someone who truly had the path sat beside him and used it, over and over, until his own gift recognised what it had felt. They could be taught to him now. That was all."}, {"p": "The grain under them did not fade. Every morning he turned his palms up in the light from the kitchen window and found the second set of prints exactly where it had been. Each morning it was a little clearer, and a little more his."}], "n": 45}, {"title": "The Bookseller's Locked Shelf", "kind": "side", "src": "s_bookseller", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei took the Keihin-Tōhoku line south in the late afternoon. The sky-blue train was cool after the street, and half empty, because so many people had left the city for the festival. Across from him, an old man held a dark furoshiki on his knees, knotted around a box of offerings, and he kept one hand on the knot the whole way as if it might run off. Two schoolgirls shared a pair of earphones, one bud each, and nodded to the same song without speaking. At each station the doors opened onto a blast of heat and the sound of cicadas, and then closed again with a soft chime. Nippori went by, then Ueno, with its crowds and its big park beyond the station. Rei got off at Akihabara."}, {"p": "He walked west from there, away from the music and the electronics shops. The streets grew older and quieter as he went, and after twenty minutes he was in Jinbōchō, among the bookshops. Some sold cheap paperbacks from carts on the pavement. Others kept rare old volumes behind glass. The air smelled of paper and dust. On the pavement outside one shop, an elderly woman in a sun hat was going through a box of hundred-yen books with great care, turning each one over to read the back."}, {"img": "res_bookshop.jpg", "cap": "The Crooked Shelf, off Jinbōchō"}, {"p": "The Crooked Shelf had no sign. It sat on a side street off the main road, and the only way to know it was a shop at all was the one crooked shelf in the window. It leaned a few degrees off straight, as if it had given up arguing with gravity. Inside, the shop was so narrow that Rei had to turn sideways between the stacks. Three floors of other people's libraries were pressed into a front barely wider than the door."}, {"img": "char_the-bookseller.jpg", "cap": "Higuchi, the bookseller"}, {"p": "The owner was a thin man in his late fifties. He had ink on his fingers, smudged glasses, and a wispy grey comb-over gone pale with dust. His cardigan was dusted with old paper. His name was Higuchi, and Jinbōchō knew him as a careful dealer who read every book before he sold it. He did not look up when Rei came in. He finished the page he was on, the way booksellers do, to make it clear that the book was more important than the customer."}, {"p": "When he did look up, he went very still. Rei watched him recognise the face. Gin's jaw. Gin's way of standing in a doorway, as if apologising for the space he took up. Recognising it cost the old man something."}, {"p": "\"You're the brother,\" Higuchi said. It was not a question."}, {"p": "Behind him, high on the locked case at the back of the shop, something shifted its weight. It was the way a sleeper shifts when a familiar voice comes into the room."}, {"p": "Rei felt the case before he properly saw it. It was not loud. It was the opposite of loud. It was a pocket of held quiet inside a shop that was already quiet. On the highest shelf, behind a small brass lock, a dozen volumes were different from all the other books. Ordinary books give off a soft trace of all the hands that have held them. These gave off something thicker instead, something deliberate. It was like a word repeated so many times that it stops being a word."}, {"p": "Higuchi watched him feel it."}, {"p": "\"I keep the dangerous things locked,\" he said. \"The case opens when I decide what a person will do with what is inside it. I have refused it to people who could pay anything.\" He tapped the key that hung on a cord round his neck. \"But that top shelf I did not lock. The key turned itself, four years ago, the week your brother first stood where you are standing.\""}, {"p": "Higuchi made tea on a hotplate that should have been thrown out ten years ago. While the water heated, he told Rei the part that the bureaus would file as folklore. Rei now knew it was accurate."}, {"p": "Objects are not alive, Higuchi said. They do not think. What they have is contact history. He said it the way a man says a thing he has worked out alone over many years, with nobody to tell. Contact history is decades of being present when things happened. It is being held during particular moments. It is sitting in the same room as something that left a trace behind, the way a fire leaves the smell of smoke in cloth."}, {"p": "\"Most books carry a little,\" he said. \"A book held by the same grieving hands every night for thirty years carries a lot. And a book that was open in the room during a crossing, at the exact moment a self came apart at the membrane...\" He set two cups on the counter. \"That one carries something that is not memory at all. It is a standing wound. A place where the page remembers being a threshold.\""}, {"p": "That, he said, was what the top shelf was. Not cursed. Cursed was a word for people who needed the comfort of a villain."}, {"p": "\"Accumulated,\" Higuchi said."}, {"p": "Long ago he had bought the library of a dead exorcist, without knowing what some of the volumes had lived through. The worst of them had found their own way to the high shelf, the way water finds the lowest place. There they had stayed, slowly growing thicker, a small private piece of the veil behind the counter of a bookshop. Strange things drifted into the Crooked Shelf. A diary that should have burned. A translation that should not exist. A pamphlet from a cult the CVD had erased. Higuchi kept them all in the locked case and sold them only to people he trusted. But nothing he owned was as heavy as that top shelf."}, {"p": "\"Four years ago your brother came in,\" he said, \"white to the lips. He asked to be left alone with the case. I went into the back. When I came out he had his hand flat against the wood, and the lock on the top shelf had turned. I swear that on my wife's grave.\""}, {"p": "The lock did for Rei what it had done for Gin. It turned under his hand. The small brass tongue slid back with a click he felt in his teeth, and the shelf breathed four years of held quiet out into the shop."}, {"p": "He did not reach blindly. He let his sight walk along the spines until one of them looked back. It was a slim hardbacked notebook with no title. Its covers were swollen with a damp that had never been water."}, {"p": "The moment his attention landed on it, he was not in the shop any more."}, {"p": "He was in a low place where the water remembered. It was night. A man with his brother's careful hands was writing by lamplight. He was arguing with himself on the page. The argument was about a debt that had never been his to pay, and whether paying it might still be the only honest thing left to do."}, {"p": "The notebook had been in the room on the night Gin first found the low place. It had kept that threshold. And it had gone on keeping it, faithfully, on the high shelf, for four years. It was like Sachiko's bottle that kept filling, an object still doing its job long after every reason was gone except the job itself."}, {"p": "This was the discovery and the danger in one. The notebook was a fixed copy of a night Rei had been trying to rebuild from the outside. Gin's Saturday. The address that was in no file. The point where the entries stopped being entries and started being arguments. To read it would be to stand where Gin had stood, inside his dread, at the exact hour the thing wearing him made its reasonable case. It would tell him more than any node ever could."}, {"p": "It would also open him. Here, in a shop with a private piece of the veil behind the counter, in the one week of the year when the dead were expected. His untrained gift would flood the room with the same noise that had buried the warning the first time. The book remembered being a threshold. Held by the wrong hands on the wrong night, a threshold is a door. And a door opens both ways."}, {"p": "He had come across the city for exactly this. He opened it."}, {"p": "The notebook did not tell him a story. It left things in him."}, {"p": "The low place came in pieces and in the wrong order, the way Itsuki's sight had come back scrambled. The smell of river mud when the tide is still. The particular cold of a place where a great many crossings had happened and nobody had mourned them. A voice that was not a voice, making its case in a tone of pure reasonableness."}, {"p": "<em>They are owed, and no one will pay them, and you are the only one who can. Is that not the definition of a duty?</em>"}, {"p": "And under the voice was his brother's hand on the page. With every line, the writing grew tighter and truer. In the end, what Gin wanted and what the voice wanted were no longer two different things. They sounded the same, like two words that rhyme."}, {"p": "Rei learned the address. He learned the shape of the argument. Worst of all, he learned that Gin had not been tricked. Gin had been persuaded, by something that only ever asked him to do what was right. Standing inside that persuasion was the most frightening thing Rei had ever done. As he read, he could feel how nearly it would work on him too."}, {"p": "The room answered, because he had opened the book in it."}, {"p": "The shop went one particular degree colder. It was the cold that means a trace has noticed a strong, untrained gift and turned toward it. The other books on the high shelf leaned their quiet weight his way. Somewhere under the floorboards, the building did the listening thing that buildings in this city did this week."}, {"p": "If he kept reading, he would have to hold the whole room shut with his own strength while he did it, and he had very little strength left. If he stopped, the room would settle, and he would never know the last line Gin wrote before the entries became arguments."}, {"p": "Higuchi stood by the hotplate with the tea going cold and did not interfere. He had always understood that some books can only be honestly read at a price."}, {"p": "Rei held the room. He closed his sight down to a slit, the way Gin had taught him at the kitchen table. He pushed back against the leaning shelf with everything he had, and he read on. His nose began to bleed. He wiped it on his sleeve and kept going. The cold pressed on the back of his skull like a hand."}, {"p": "The last line before the arguments began was not an argument at all. It was small and ordinary, and it nearly broke him."}, {"p": "<em>Keys on the hook. I checked.</em>"}, {"p": "Gin had written it the way he wrote it at the end of every note, the habit of a man making sure his little brother was locked in safe. After that line, the handwriting changed. Rei shut the book. The room let go of him slowly, the way a held breath goes out."}, {"p": "The shelf was Rei's problem now. It had only been Higuchi's by accident, and the old man knew it, and in his dry way he was relieved. For years he had kept a private piece of the veil behind his counter, for no reason except that a bookseller will not burn a book. He was tired. And Rei was the first person to walk in with the right face and the wrong, useful gift."}, {"p": "Rei stood with his hand on the open door of the shelf and thought it through. Tanizaki would know what to do with kept thresholds, and would not thank him for the work. And there was the notebook itself. Part of him wanted to end it. Not read it again, but end it, and let the recorded night finally cross over, the way the man in it never had. That would close the clearest window he had. It might also be the only mercy on that shelf."}, {"p": "Then he thought about Gin's hand flat against the wood. Gin had not burned the notebook. Gin had not carried it off. Gin had left it here, with the one man in the city who read everything before he sold it."}, {"p": "Rei turned the brass lock. It slid home under his fingers. It was warm now, where a dozen other hands had never warmed it."}, {"p": "\"It stays with you,\" he said. \"If that's all right. I'll come back for it if I need it.\""}, {"p": "Higuchi nodded, as if he had expected nothing else. Then he took the cord from round his own neck. A second key hung on it, older and darker. It fitted the deep drawer at the bottom of the case, the one Higuchi never opened while there were customers in the shop."}, {"p": "\"Not tonight,\" Higuchi said, and pressed it into Rei's palm. \"You've bled enough for one evening. Come back when you're ready to read something that is not about your brother, and is about everything else. It will make what you just read make sense. It will also make it worse.\""}, {"p": "Then he gave Rei the one thing a bookseller has to give, which is provenance, the history of where a book came from. He wrote the name of the dead exorcist on the back of a receipt. And last, the way the true thing always comes last, he added that the exorcist had once had a student. The student came to nothing that the bureaus ever recorded. He might not have come to nothing at all."}, {"p": "A thread for later."}, {"break": "The other side of the same thing"}, {"p": "The bell over the door rang before Rei reached it."}, {"img": "char_mochizuki-sora.jpg", "cap": "Mochizuki Sora at the door of the Crooked Shelf"}, {"p": "A young woman stood on the step with a canvas bag of library books on her shoulder. She was a few years older than him, thin, with a quick, wary face. She had stopped dead in the doorway. Her eyes went to the back of the shop, then to the case, then to Rei. She had felt the cold from the street."}, {"p": "\"Mochizuki-san,\" Higuchi said mildly. \"Come in, or go home. Don't stand in my door letting the heat out.\""}, {"p": "She came in. She did not take her eyes off Rei."}, {"p": "\"That was you,\" she said. \"That cold. I felt it at the corner.\" She looked at his sleeve and the blood drying on it, and her face loosened and tightened at the same time. \"You read something from the top shelf. Nobody reads the top shelf.\""}, {"p": "\"My brother did,\" Rei said."}, {"p": "She was quiet for a moment. Then she said the thing she had clearly been carrying for a long time, with nobody to give it to."}, {"p": "\"You're doing the same thing I am, aren't you. From the other side.\""}, {"p": "Her name was Mochizuki Sora. She worked evenings at a neighbourhood library and lived in a share-house in Kōenji, a neighbourhood in the west of the city full of students, musicians and secondhand clothes shops. The art students she lived with did not know what they lived with. She was not registered with anyone. For three years she had been mapping something from the outside. A pattern in Kita. A cold signature that moved through the northern wards and left the same marks behind it, year after year."}, {"p": "\"I started because of my mother,\" she said. She took a coat button from her pocket and turned it between her fingers without seeming to notice. \"She was a practitioner. When I was twelve, something got into her. It was four years before they got it out. They called it a partial success.\" She put the button away. \"Permanent damage, partial success. I've spent eleven years trying to understand what happened to her. Her case file has the same regional signature as the thing in Kita. The same one. Nobody at the CVD would tell me what that means.\""}, {"p": "Higuchi set a third cup on the counter without a word. Rei realised that the old man had been guiding this girl gently for a long time. He had been giving her the books she could carry, and keeping back the ones she could not."}, {"p": "Rei looked at her with his sight, carefully, the way he would look at a stranger in a thin place. Her Clairsentience was strong and steady and self-taught. But beside it there was something else. A pressure, faint and new, like a second door in a wall where there had only ever been one. She did not mention it. He was not sure she knew. It reminded him of something in Gin's pages about the family, about paths that open in pairs, and he did not know yet what to do with that."}, {"p": "\"I've got the outside of it,\" Mochizuki said. \"Three years of where and when. You've got the inside. Haven't you.\""}, {"p": "\"Some of it,\" Rei said."}, {"p": "She wrote a number on the inside cover of one of her library books, tore the page out, and handed it to him. Tearing a page from a book was a crime in that shop. Higuchi winced and said nothing."}, {"p": "\"I'm not asking you to owe me anything,\" she said. \"I don't want anyone owing anyone. I just want to stop being the only person in the room who sees it.\""}, {"p": "Rei left the shop turning sideways between the stacks, the way he had come in. The key to the deep drawer was a small cold weight in his pocket. Outside, the street was busy with its Obon evening. Small fires burned in dishes by the doors. Gates were propped open so the dead could find their way. A shop owner was pulling down his shutter with a long hooked pole, and the metal rattled down the street like distant thunder."}, {"p": "Walking back toward the station in the blue dusk, Rei understood that he had added a place to the map his brother had drawn. A shelf off Jinbōchō where, if the worst ever came, a door already stood half open behind a bookseller's counter."}, {"p": "And for the first time all month, he had added a person to it who was looking for the same thing he was."}], "n": 46}, {"title": "The Domain Registry", "kind": "main", "src": "m_registry", "blocks": [{"p": "The deep drawer at the bottom of Higuchi's case was the one closed door in Rei's whole month that had not locked itself. So it was the one he trusted least. And it was the one he put off longest."}, {"p": "He went back on a night when the borrowed grain was still faint in his palms. This time he took the Namboku subway from Ōji. It was late, and the station under the street was almost empty. The platform had tall glass screen doors along its edge, so that nobody could fall or jump onto the tracks, and in the glass Rei could see his own tired reflection waiting beside him. When the train came, the screen doors and the train doors slid open together with a soft double hiss. In the carriage a young man in a suit was asleep on his briefcase, his tie loosened, his phone still lit in his hand. A woman in a nurse's cardigan read a paperback. Across from Rei, a man held a flip phone to his ear and said nothing at all, only listened, for four whole stations."}, {"p": "Rei got off at Iidabashi and walked east. The office streets were dark and shuttered, and the heat came up off the pavement even at this hour. A vending machine glowed on every corner. In a ramen shop by the road, three workmen in split-toed boots sat at the counter slurping noodles without speaking, under a television showing the late news. Further on, the streets of Jinbōchō were closed and sleeping. The bookshops had pulled their carts of cheap paperbacks inside and rolled their shutters down. The whole quarter smelled of paper even in the dark."}, {"p": "The Crooked Shelf was dark except for a single lamp. The side door was unlatched. The old man was asleep on the cot in the back room with a book open on his chest, the way he slept most nights, among his books. Rei did not wake him. He knelt in front of the case and fitted the old dark key into the drawer. He opened it the way a man lifts a bandage he has been afraid to look under."}, {"p": "There was no book of spells. There was no magic manual, no contract that smelled of sulphur. None of the props the films had promised for a moment like this."}, {"img": "res_library.jpg", "cap": "The ledger from the deep drawer, read by lamplight"}, {"p": "There was a ledger. A plain accountant's ledger, the kind his father had kept, ruled in faint blue lines. The early pages were in an old, cramped hand. That was the dead exorcist's writing. Names had been written there decades ago, crossed out, and written in again as the years went by. The later pages were in Higuchi's careful script. Across the top of the newest page, in a hand so careful it must have taken an hour, was one word."}, {"p": "<em>Registry.</em>"}, {"p": "Under it, numbered one to thirteen, were the only thirteen names that mattered in the city. Rei had lived there his whole life without knowing they were there."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Registry. Current page, in Higuchi's hand", "lines": ["I. Grief. The weight of permanent loss. Asakura Mio. Kyoto. Stable.", "II. Rage. The force of perceived injustice. Mori Takao. Tokyo, hard to find. Under pressure.", "III. Love. The binding between people. Sakuma Kei. Tokyo. Stable.", "IV. Fear. The anticipation of harm. Nakashima Yuna. Tokyo. Under pressure.", "V. Wonder. The aperture that opens when something beautiful exists. Chen Lian. Tokyo, the Grid Archive. Stable.", "VI. Despair. The collapse of hope. Watanabe Jun. Variable (pencilled beside it. Itabashi, the municipal library, attempting retirement again). Weakening. (starred twice)", "VII. Joy. The completion of something good. Hoshino Haru. Kita, Sakae Street. Stable.", "VIII. Shame. The internal wound of failing what you cared about. Kobayashi Emi. Tokyo. Stable.", "IX. Hunger. The drive toward what is not yet. Inoue Satoru. No fixed location. Under pressure.", "X. Courage. The movement forward despite fear. Okabe Natsumi. Independent. Stable.", "XI. Longing. The ache for what is absent or gone. Park Junho. Shin-Ōkubo. Under pressure.", "XII. Awe. The dissolution of self before something greater. Kasumi Shin. Variable. Stable.", "XIII. Duty. The will to persist beyond any single moment. Vacant since 2009. Kita district. Critical, membrane thinning."]}}, {"p": "He read it the way people read the worst test results, from top to bottom, refusing to skip ahead."}, {"p": "They were not gods. That was the first thing the page taught him, and it hit him like cold water. The Thirteen were not gods and never had been. They were offices. The ledger called them domains. A domain was a post, the way a ward has a registrar and a police precinct has a captain. There were thirteen standing arrangements between the living city and the pressure on the other side of its wall. Each one was an emotion. Each one had a holder. And each one was a discipline that a single person had agreed to carry, so that the rest of Tokyo did not have to feel its full weight."}, {"p": "Despair had an office. Rage had an office. So did Fear, Hunger and Longing. Each had a number, a chair and a person. Somewhere in this city, those people were alive right now. Each of them was bearing one of the thirteen ways the other side leans on the world. Because of them, for everyone else, it stayed a mood instead of a flood."}, {"p": "Then there was the thirteenth line. It was different. Rei knew it would be before his eye reached it, the way a body knows which stair is the broken one in the dark."}, {"p": "Twelve of the lines had names. The thirteenth had only a number and a word, Duty. Beside it, in the same careful hand but pressed harder, almost through the paper, were the words <em>vacant since 2009</em>."}, {"p": "No name. No holder. Nobody carrying it. An office of the city had stood open for six years. There was a chair at the table of the Thirteen with no one in it."}, {"p": "Rei sat back on the bookshop floor. Finally and completely, he understood what his brother had been pulled toward all month. Not a throne. Not a power. A vacancy. The one unbearable thing the registry had been built never to allow. An office of the wall left empty, and leaning."}, {"p": "Higuchi had not only listed the thirteen. He had explained them, in a long note that ran sideways up the margin of the page. The higher it climbed, the smaller and more frightened the writing became. Rei turned the ledger on its side and read the only honest account of the wall he had ever been given."}, {"p": "<em>A held domain is a chair that leans into the room.</em>"}, {"p": "This was how it worked. The holder takes the pressure of one emotion onto a single living soul and turns it inward, toward the city. That way Despair stays a private weather inside one person, instead of a public flood across a whole ward. The cost is paid, but one person pays it. When people feel any of these thirteen things strongly enough, all at once, the feeling presses on the veil. The holder takes that pressure and keeps the emotion moving. It stays in circulation, like water in a pipe, so that it never settles and pools. Pooled emotion is what suffering beings are made from."}, {"p": "<em>That is the arrangement. That is the whole secret.</em>"}, {"p": "The city stood because thirteen people were holding a door shut with their whole lives, at this very moment. Rei had probably walked past most of them without a glance. They lived ordinary lives. They had jobs and flats and habits. The CVD knew only that the old Accord mentioned some kind of stabilising structure. Higuchi had underlined one sentence twice. <em>No one outside a very small group knows the anchors are thirteen people eating breakfast.</em>"}, {"p": "<em>A vacant domain,</em> the note went on, <em>is a chair that leans the other way.</em>"}, {"p": "Here the hand began to shake. Rei could see it. The blue ink caught and pooled."}, {"p": "With no holder to turn the pressure inward, the office does not close. It cannot close. The registry has no way to retire a seat. Thirteen is a number the wall requires. It is not a committee that can be made smaller. So the pressure of the thirteenth emotion, with no living person to bear it, leans outward instead. It leans against the seam nearest the empty chair. The veil there grows thin. Activity from the other side rises. And the city feels a wrongness that ordinary people cannot name."}, {"p": "The seam nearest the empty chair was the one over the district where the chair had last been filled. The last holder of Duty had died in 2009, in Kita. When a holder dies, the succession is meant to find the next holder by itself, among whoever is near with the right kind of opening to the Font. That night, nobody with the right opening was anywhere near. The succession did not take. Since then, the thing at the centre that Rei had already met, the one the old books called the Axis, had been carrying the thirteenth seat itself, directly, from inside. Beside that, Higuchi had written two words in letters barely a millimetre tall. <em>not sustainable</em>."}, {"p": "That was why Kita had been coming apart since 2009. That was why March 2011 had torn it so much faster. A vacant office had been pressing its full weight on a single ward for six years."}, {"p": "<em>That is why the dead come so close there at Obon,</em> the note said. <em>And that is why a healer who wished to thin the wall would not need to break it. He would only need to help.</em>"}, {"p": "Rei sat with that in the lamplight until it stopped being information and settled in the pit of his stomach."}, {"p": "The Healer was not thinning Kita's wall against the grain of the world. It was thinning it along the grain. It was finishing a tear that the empty chair had spent six years starting. Every favour, every haunting closed, every grief gently put to rest, had not been the Healer fighting the registry. It had been the registry's own empty thirteenth seat doing exactly what an empty office does, with a patient demon standing beside it, lending a shoulder."}, {"p": "Now he understood why nobody of any grade in the city could point to a crime. There had been no crime. There had been a death in 2009, and no one in the right place, and a wall that had been sagging ever since. Everything after that was only the wall finishing a fall it had begun before Rei was grown. His brother's hands were the latest, gentlest, most loving thing leaning on a door that had wanted to open since the year the lights went out."}, {"p": "From the back room came the creak of the cot. Higuchi was awake. He stood in the doorway in his cardigan, looking at the ledger on the floor and at the boy sitting beside it."}, {"p": "\"I told you it would make it worse,\" he said."}, {"p": "\"Did Gin read this?\""}, {"p": "\"Your brother read it the first winter he came in.\" Higuchi lowered himself onto a stool with the care of a man whose knees had opinions. \"He copied the names into that book of his. Then he stopped coming in. I think he went looking for them.\""}, {"p": "Rei looked at the starred line. Watanabe Jun. Despair. Weakening."}, {"p": "\"Then so will I,\" he said."}], "n": 47}, {"title": "From Gin's Book. The Thirteen", "kind": "gin", "src": "gin_35_thirteen", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei went home on the first train of the morning, with the registry copied into the back of Gin's book in his own bad handwriting. The sky over Iidabashi was turning grey, and the crows were already loud on the wires. The first Namboku train north was nearly empty. A cleaner in a blue uniform dozed at one end of the carriage with her mop bucket between her knees. A young couple in festival clothes, a cotton yukata each, sat holding hands with their eyes shut, still wearing the paper wristbands from some party the night before. Nobody spoke. The train ran under the sleeping city, and Rei sat with the book on his knees and did not open it until he was home."}, {"p": "Higuchi had said that Gin copied the names too. So Rei went looking, and he found them. They were in a section near the end of the book that he had only skimmed in the first week, when they had read like notes about strangers. There was a page headed The Thirteen. There was a page of rank letters. And there were thirteen short files, one for each domain, full of the small practical details Gin always wrote down. Where to go. When. Who in the city already knew. With the ledger beside him, the strangers became people. Rei read them at the kitchen table while the kettle ticked as it cooled."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "The holders. In my words (G.U.)", "lines": ["The thirteen are people. Former practitioners. Former Guardian rank practitioners who crossed a threshold the taxonomy has no category for, whose aperture expanded to the point where they could take one of the Font's thirteen structural domains and hold it. One emotional register of the veil's membrane. Permanently. For as long as the membrane needs holding.", "They are not divine. They are not in thrones. They are walking around in ordinary cities, eating ordinary meals, carrying something vast by will alone. Some of them have been doing this for centuries. Some are newer. All of them are tired in the specific way of someone who cannot stop.", "They are not the strongest practitioners alive. They are people who found a way to be in relationship with the Axis without disappearing into it. They present to it as a function rather than a person. The domain is larger than any one person, and so it survives being that close.", "The Axis cannot hold the emotional layer itself. It is no longer human enough to hold emotions as shapes with weight and centre. The holders can.", "They find each other. Not through institutions. Through the recognition that happens when two people carrying the same structural weight meet. The network is real. It has no name. It has no schedule. It meets irregularly. It is the only structure in the world that knows the veil is thinning from the inside, and that the thirteen are the reason it has not already failed."]}}, {"p": "Next to it, Gin had copied the top two lines of the CVD's rank chart. The CVD ranks every practitioner with a letter, from E at the bottom up through D, C, B, A and S. Above those sit two ranks almost nobody ever sees written down."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "Rank chart. The top two lines, copied from the CVD sheet", "lines": ["SS. Singularity. Foundational pillars. Known only to CVD leadership. Secret candidates for the Thirteen if a Guardian falls.", "SSS. Guardian. The Thirteen. Supreme protectors of veil stability. Aligned with specific emotions. Their existence is not public knowledge.", "Every holder file I have found lists the rank as Guardian."]}}, {"p": "Then came the thirteen files. Gin had written each one the same way. First the holder, with their age, their work, their path, and the weight they carried. Then, under <em>Who knows</em>, the ordinary people in the city who had noticed something about that holder without being told."}, {"img": "thirteen_I_asakura_mio.jpg", "cap": "Asakura Mio, the first of the Thirteen, who holds Grief"}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "The Thirteen. Who, where, and who already knows (G.U.)", "lines": ["I. GRIEF. Asakura Mio, 27. Barista at a tea house in Kyoto. Regular hours. Clairsentience. Dark circles since she was twenty-three. She does not hide them. Whoever sits near her finds whatever they are grieving rising to the surface. She cannot turn this off and stopped trying. She was identified at seventeen and prepared until twenty-two. In March 2011 the largest grief event in modern Japanese memory hit the membrane before succession was ready, and Domain I has been on emergency stabilisation ever since. The CVD does not know. To find her, go to the right tea house three evenings in a row. She will know you were there before you introduce yourself.", "Who knows. Hino Miho, 28, Kabukichō. Self-taught, Arbiter-rank Clairsentience, nine years of managing the worst ground in Tokyo alone with no instruction. She has a read on the hotel room where something happened to Domain I in 2011. She has been waiting three years for someone to ask her about it. My notes on her run to thirty-eight pages.", "II. RAGE. Mori Takao, 48. Held since he was twenty-eight. Twenty years. The eldest of the Thirteen, and the one the others defer to. Telekinesis. Listed in the CVD system as a retired field operative. He is not retired. He tends the boiler of a public bath in Kita. Rage carries the highest entity pressure in Tokyo, and it produces the most aggressive crossing attempts of any domain. Something on the other side circles his seat. Hard to find, on purpose. Go to the places people go when they have nowhere left to go.", "Who knows. Old Tomi, the last man living in the condemned blocks by the freight yard. He sits at his window at night and counts what comes through the fence. He has seen Mori cross.", "III. LOVE. Sakuma Kei, 31. Gold wire glasses. Runs a small family mediation practice with a three-year waiting list. His clients do not know why they feel so completely heard. Clairsentience. He can name the exact nature of any bond in front of him. The most stable domain of the thirteen. He calls it maintenance. It is the hardest kind, keeping something good from turning into something else.", "Who knows. Kawai Yuki, 57, Akebono Bakery by the station. Up at four. Kei is the dawn regular she feeds.", "IV. FEAR. Nakashima Yuna, 40. Director of a security consultancy. Psychometabolism. She can steady a practitioner in crisis in under thirty seconds. For twelve years she has been building a taxonomy of what the fear domain actually produces. Not all of it is hostile. Some of it is warning systems older than the Accord. The CVD uses her taxonomy without credit. She has noticed. She files nothing and does everything. Her calendar is public. Her assistant knows more than most practitioners.", "Who knows. The Woman at the Back, the Suzuran, two in the morning, every night. She feels Yuna coming. Kubo, the blind busker in the station underpass, hears her pass.", "V. WONDER. Chen Lian, 29, from Chengdu, came to Japan at eighteen. Identified at twenty-one. Eight years in the seat. Researcher at the Grid Archive, with pre-Accord documents no institution holds. The Grid's pre-Accord oral archive opens through her. Clairsentience. She can read the history of a room from the street outside. The only domain whose presence actively helps practitioners. Wonder produces clarity.", "Who knows. Master Lin Wei at the Taoist temple. He respects her. Buy incense before you ask him anything.", "VI. DESPAIR. Watanabe Jun, 35. Looks older. Telepathy. He can read the despair of a place without touching it, and he spots practitioners heading into a crash before they know. He has tried to retire three times. There is no mechanism for it. He finds that, specifically, accurate. He says despair, handled correctly, is the emotion that sees reality most clearly. Either wisdom or a symptom. He says it is both. Reach him through the Grid's emergency network.", "Who knows. Okuma Sumi, who tends the riverside memorial at dusk. She felt him at the water.", "VII. JOY. Hoshino Haru, 33. Noodle shop on Sakae Street, Kita. The broth takes eight hours. Psychometabolism. His teacher held Joy for forty years and prepared him for twelve. The cleanest succession on record. Haru spent three years wondering if he was doing it wrong because it had not been difficult. He was not doing it wrong. The quality of the food and the quality of the domain are the same thing.", "Who knows. Maruyama at Café Mikazuki. Tatsu at the Tatsuya counter under the tracks. Haru is a warm regular to both.", "VIII. SHAME. Kobayashi Emi, 36. Institutional accountability work. Telepathy. Shame entities chase something that looks like justice from one angle. She has been negotiating with them for twelve years. She is very good at it. She does not enjoy it, which is probably why she is good at it.", "Who knows. Father Anselm Mabuchi, the parish house beside the church near our building. He hears her confession.", "IX. HUNGER. Inoue Satoru, 36. No fixed location. Teleportation. The last holder died three years ago and the seat went vacant. He was an emergency succession, found and installed in months instead of years. He knows the gaps. He is managing them. This is the domain the organised entity campaign is pushing hardest. Reach him only through the Grid's emergency protocols.", "X. COURAGE. Okabe Natsumi, 42. Independent. Complicated relationship with the CVD. Psychometabolism. Keeps field notes she shares only with practitioners who prove they can use them. Linked to Domain IV. Fear is anticipation. Courage is going forward anyway. She and Yuna have worked in step for twelve years. The first five were difficult.", "XI. LONGING. Park Junho, 41, born in Seoul, seventeen years in Tokyo. Teaches Korean at the community centre in Shin-Ōkubo, Tuesday and Thursday evenings. Clairsentience. He knows when someone is missing someone before they say it. Longing entities are not dangerous. They just do not let go. He has worked with them for fifteen years. He finds them heartbreaking. He does not find that a problem.", "Who knows. Pak Sun-ja, the mudang, in the shrine room behind the curtain above the Korean restaurant. She keeps his secret.", "XII. AWE. Kasumi Shin, 39. Variable. The closest of the thirteen to the Axis. Instruments give no consistent reading on him. He arrived at a very old place very young and has managed that closeness for fifteen years. He has the Account Book. He showed it to me. Go to him with something real. He has not been fooled about what is real in fifteen years. Pre-Accord documents, the Grid's full oral archive, the oldest intelligence network still working.", "Who knows. Miyamoto Kazuo, kannushi at the shrine. He knows when Kasumi visits.", "XIII. DUTY. Vacant since 2009. Kita district. The Axis carries it directly. Not sustainable."]}}, {"p": "One name in the files Rei had to stop on. Kabukichō is the night district of Shinjuku, all bars, hosts and neon, open until dawn. Gin had called it the worst ground in Tokyo, and Rei believed him."}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Stuck beside VII", "lines": ["I know who he is. I recommend the broth anyway."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "On XIII (G.U.)", "lines": ["The CVD files the thirteenth register as null-emotive. Their instruments were built to measure emotion by how much it moves. XIII does not move. The instruments read the stillness as absence.", "I think that is wrong. I cannot yet say what is right. Something is in that seat's register, and it is not nothing. Keys on the hook."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the last line three times. Then he turned back to the sixth file. He put his finger on the name with two stars beside it in the ledger, and on the word Itabashi that Higuchi had pencilled next to it. Itabashi was the ward just west of Kita, close enough to walk. Rei did not sleep."}], "n": 47}, {"title": "The Twelve Who Hold", "kind": "main", "src": "m_twelve", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei started with the sixth name, because Higuchi had starred it twice in the margin. <em>Watanabe Jun, Despair VI, weakening.</em> The other names were still only words on a page. A weakening holder was different. He was a real man somewhere in the city, and Rei could help him or hurt him just by knocking on the wrong door."}, {"p": "He had not slept. In the late afternoon he left the flat and walked to Jūjō station through heat that pressed on the back of his neck like a hand. The cicadas were screaming in every tree along the way. On the green-striped Saikyō line train to Ikebukuro, a schoolgirl in a summer uniform slept against her friend's shoulder, and the friend held very still so as not to wake her. At Ikebukuro he changed to the Tōbu Tōjō line and rode out west, past rooftops hung with drying futons, to a small station called Tokiwadai."}, {"p": "The pencilled note had sent him to this quiet corner of Itabashi. The streets here had been planned in the 1930s. They were straight and tidy and lined with old trees, and they curved around in neat loops. Very little happened here on the other side of the veil. Gin's book had a word for ground like this. It called it clean. The Grid kept safe houses here for practitioners who needed to think without noise. Rei understood at once why a man carrying Despair would choose to live in the quietest ground he could find."}, {"img": "npc_watanabe_jun.jpg", "cap": "Watanabe Jun, holder of Despair"}, {"p": "Watanabe Jun was not what Rei had expected. Rei had been carrying the word holder around all day like a heavy stone. He had pictured someone large. Instead he found a slight man in a cardigan two sizes too big, shelving returned books at the local public library. He looked fifty. The file said thirty-five. He had the quiet gentleness of a person who has decided not to take up much room in the world."}, {"img": "scene_rei_watanabe_library.jpg", "cap": "Watanabe Jun shelving books at the library"}, {"p": "Rei took a newspaper from the rack, sat down in the periodicals corner and watched him for an hour. The air conditioning hummed. A student was asleep over his textbooks. A woman in a sun hat returned a stack of romance novels and left. Nothing about Watanabe was strange except the light around him. It was a little dimmer near him, a half-step darker, the way a room goes dim when a cloud passes the window. But there was no cloud. And Rei noticed that nobody ever quite met his eyes. Not the student. Not the woman with the novels."}, {"p": "Rei watched him put a novel back on its shelf with both hands, as if it were much heavier than it was. Then he understood. He was looking at the price of the seat, written plainly on a living man."}, {"p": "A holder did not use his domain like a power. He stood inside it, the way a person stands out in bad weather, so that nobody else had to stand in it. Watanabe had been given Despair. Rei did not know how. Perhaps by chance, perhaps it was handed down, perhaps he had offered himself. Whatever the reason, the weight had worn him thin, the way running water wears down a stone. It did not happen all at once. It happened every day. Now the man was mostly the shape of the thing he carried, with only a little person left over to do the carrying."}, {"p": "Higuchi's word was weakening, and now Rei could see it. Watanabe's hands trembled on the books. In the poor library light, the edges of him looked almost see-through. And Rei understood why. It was not his age. It was the empty thirteenth seat in Kita, two wards away. That empty chair was pulling on the full ones around it. Watanabe's seat was the nearest to the wound, so it was pulled the hardest. For six years the empty chair had been leaning on him and borrowing his strength."}, {"p": "At last Watanabe noticed Rei watching. People who live with something strange always notice in the end. Rei expected him to look afraid. Instead Watanabe gave him a small, tired nod. He did not know Rei's face. But he knew Rei's kind. The kind who could see the dimmed light."}, {"p": "He came over and spoke very quietly, so that the sleeping student would not wake."}, {"p": "\"I've been wondering when one of you would come,\" he said. \"If you have a moment, and you are what I think you are, I'd like to know something. On the worst nights I've started to feel a thirteenth weight leaning on my sixth. And I've started to feel that something kind and patient, over in Kita, has begun very gently to help it lean. Is that true?\""}, {"p": "Rei had not come to tell a dying man the name of the thing that was killing him. But this man was holding Despair for the whole north of the city, and he had asked an honest question. His cardigan was two sizes too big because there was less of him inside it every week."}, {"p": "\"Yes,\" Rei said. \"It's true. The thing helping it lean is wearing my brother.\""}, {"p": "Watanabe closed his eyes for a moment. When he opened them, there was no surprise in them. There was only a kind of tired thanks."}, {"p": "\"Then I'm sorry for you,\" he said. \"That's worse than what's happening to me.\""}, {"break": "The long night"}, {"p": "Rei could not visit all twelve. There was no time. Some of the names lived in places he had no way to reach. One of them was the eleventh, Park Junho, who held Longing. He was listed at the community centre in Shin-Ōkubo, with a note in Gin's hand that Rei did not understand yet. <em>Pak Sun-ja keeps his secret.</em> The note did not say what the secret was."}, {"img": "place_jujo_crossing.jpg", "cap": "The level crossing by Jūjō station at night"}, {"p": "But he saw four more before the first trains started running again. He rode the Saikyō line back to Jūjō as the sky went dark, among tired office workers with their ties loosened and their phones glowing in their hands. Outside the station the crossing bells were ringing. He waited at the barrier with the others while a green-striped train went past, its lit windows full of faces, and then he walked. Now that he knew what a leaning chair looked like, he could not stop seeing them. The whole city seemed to arrange itself around what he could see."}, {"p": "In Kita, down an alley behind a public bath, a boiler-room door was propped open to let the heat out. Inside, a big man in dark clothes was feeding a firebox one shovel of fuel at a time. His hair was cropped short, dark with some grey in it. Every movement he made was held down to the smallest size it could be. No wasted gesture. No slammed door. Rei did not go in. He was not even sure this was the right man, because Gin's file said the second holder was hard to find on purpose. But he watched him work for a long minute. The man looked like a whole life built into one careful shape, a shape that gave his weather somewhere to go where it would hurt no one. The heat poured off the firebox and never seemed to reach his hands. He looked like a holder who had made his peace. Even so, while Rei watched, he glanced north twice, the way a man checks a sky he does not trust."}, {"img": "thirteen_VII_hoshino_haru.jpg", "cap": "Hoshino Haru, holder of Joy, at his stockpot"}, {"p": "On Sakae Street, at two in the morning, the light was still on in a small noodle shop. Through the steamed-up glass Rei could see a stocky man in an apron skimming the top of a big stockpot. The broth took eight hours, Gin had written. This was Hoshino Haru, who held Joy, the seventh seat. His teacher had passed it to him so smoothly that it was the cleanest handover on record. Rei stood across the street for a while and simply felt it. It was the warmest thing he had felt all month, the plain happiness of something good done well. And yet, even here, the steam from the pot drifted north, though there was no wind to carry it."}, {"p": "Outside a pale danchi he looked up. A danchi is a big public housing block, built by the thousand after the war, rows and rows of the same small flats with the same steel doors. On the half-landing of an outside stairwell sat a woman at three in the morning. Her knees were pulled up to her chest. She was not climbing. Fear, the fourth seat. She had not climbed her own stairs in days."}, {"p": "After that Rei walked south beside the Keihin-Tōhoku tracks, toward the centre of the city. The last trains had gone. The rails were silent and the signals glowed red over empty platforms. Vending machines hummed on every corner, lighting the pavement white. A taxi went by slowly with its little red light on, looking for anyone still out. By Ueno station the late counters were still open. A lean man paid for a plate he had not finished, and he was gone before the cook had counted his change. Hunger, the ninth seat. He never stayed anywhere long enough to finish a plate."}, {"p": "Each of them was ordinary in the same way. Each of them was a person who had built a whole life around a weight that could never be put down."}, {"p": "And over that long night, Rei came to understand that each of them was leaning the same way."}, {"p": "Not toward each other. Toward the gap."}, {"p": "Higuchi's note had explained how it was meant to work. Twelve holders, spread around the table, should balance each other. That way no single part of the veil had to carry more than it could bear. For a century, the note said, it had balanced. But a table with one chair pulled out begins to tilt. Every plate on it slides, slowly, toward the empty place."}, {"p": "The man at the boiler checked the northern sky because Rage felt the empty chair as a heat that would not quite die down. The woman on the stairs felt it as a fear with nothing to be afraid of. Watanabe felt it most, because his seat was the nearest and the weakest. But all twelve of them felt it. It was a low, constant pull toward Kita, where a chair had stood empty since the year the city went grey. None of them had the power to fill it. None of them was free to leave their own seat. And none of them, Rei realised, had ever been told why the weather they had carried all their lives had lately been getting worse."}, {"break": "Dawn at the Akebono"}, {"img": "place_akebono.jpg", "cap": "The Akebono Bakery at dawn"}, {"p": "When the first trains began to run, Rei took one north from Ueno. The car was nearly empty. A young man in a crumpled host's suit slept with his mouth open. An old woman held a cloth bundle of flowers and fruit on her knees, an offering for a family grave. Rei rode north with his forehead against the cool glass and watched the sky go from black to grey."}, {"p": "He had not eaten. Near the station a bakery opened at five. Rei stood outside the Akebono Bakery while a woman in her late fifties raised the metal shutter and set out the first trays of bread. The window glowed yellow, and the smell of warm butter came out onto the empty street."}, {"img": "thirteen_III_sakuma_kei.jpg", "cap": "Sakuma Kei, holder of Love"}, {"p": "A man was already waiting. He was neat and ordinary, with gold wire glasses and his collar one button open. The baker handed him a paper bag without being asked. He thanked her, and for the few seconds she spoke to him, she looked like the only person in the world. Then Rei saw that it went the other way too. The man was looking at her as if she were the only person in the world. He was not putting it on. It was simply what his attention did."}, {"p": "Sakuma Kei, Rei thought. Love. The third seat. The most stable of all the thirteen domains. The dawn regular whom Mrs. Kawai fed."}, {"p": "Kei walked past Rei on the pavement. For a moment he glanced at him through the gold frames with a strange, kind attention, as if he had just recognised some bond Rei carried. Then he nodded politely and went on toward the station. He did not look north at all. Rei would remember that. The steadiest of them did not look north. He just walked a little faster."}, {"break": "The last bridge before dawn"}, {"p": "Rei stopped on the small bridge over the river near home as the light came up. Gin's book was in his bag, with the list of holders copied into the back. Twelve names. Twelve kinds of weather. And one wound."}, {"p": "What frightened him was not how strong the far shore was. It was how reasonable the Healer's plan looked from here."}, {"p": "Twelve people were being slowly crushed by an empty chair. As far as Rei could understand it that night, one demon, patient and kind, was offering to end the leaning. To fill the gap. To let Watanabe put down the weight that was killing him. To let the man at the boiler finally let his fire die down."}, {"p": "And the price? The thing in that chair would not be a person carrying a feeling inside for the city's sake. It would be a feeling wearing a person on the outside, for its own sake. The body it meant to do this in was Gin's. And the hand it had spent a month shaping to fit the seat was Rei's."}, {"p": "The trap had never been that the plan was evil. The trap was that, seen from a bridge at dawn with a dying librarian behind him, the plan looked like mercy. Rei was the only one in a position to refuse it. And if he refused, twelve people would go on leaning into a gap he had just told one of them was real."}], "n": 48}, {"title": "The Weakening Holder", "kind": "side", "src": "s_weakening", "blocks": [{"img": "off_library.jpg", "cap": "The library in Itabashi"}, {"p": "Rei went back to the library in Itabashi, because Higuchi had starred the name twice. By now a starred name in that margin was the only kind of warning Rei had learned to obey."}, {"p": "It was the same trip as before, and it felt different the second time. The Saikyō line train was crowded at the end of the afternoon. Everyone was holding a phone or a fan, and the air conditioning lost the fight with the heat every time the doors opened. At Ikebukuro a wave of people poured off, and many of them carried bags of fruit and boxes of sweets wrapped in paper. They were travelling home for Obon, back to the towns where their families kept their graves. On the slow Tōbu Tōjō train out to Tokiwadai, an old man across the aisle held a bunch of white chrysanthemums upright on his knee the whole way, as carefully as if it were a candle."}, {"p": "Watanabe Jun was at the returns cart in the same cardigan two sizes too big. From the periodicals corner Rei watched him work for most of an hour. The wrongness around him was worse than the first time. It was never dramatic. It was just a deepening of that half-step of dimness. The air near him now had the faded colour of an old photograph left too long in a drawer."}, {"p": "He shelved a stack of children's picture books. His hand shook on the last one. He stood very still until the shaking stopped, the way a man stands still when standing is the only thing he has left the strength to do. Rei understood that he was not watching a holder hold. He was watching a holder lose his grip, one finger at a time, and decide each time not to tell anyone."}, {"p": "As the library emptied toward closing, Rei did what he had come to do. He let Watanabe see that he was watching."}, {"p": "Watanabe did not jump. He set the last book down and looked at Rei with the tired recognition of a man who has waited years for someone to notice. The relief on his face was so open it was hard to look at. On a face that gentle, relief was a confession. He had been alone with this since before Rei could read."}, {"p": "\"You can tell,\" he said. It was not a question. His voice was dimmed too, soft at the edges. It was the voice of someone who had spent himself on something invisible for so long that he had forgotten how to be loud. \"Most people just feel cold near me and move their chair. You're the first one in a long time who looked at where the cold was coming from.\""}, {"p": "He made tea in the staff room with a kettle that took too long. In a flat, unhurried voice, the way people talk about the weather, he told Rei what he was. The sixth of the Thirteen. He carried Despair the way a stone wall holds back a hillside. And lately the hillside had been getting heavier."}, {"p": "\"I've tried to retire three times,\" he said. \"There's no way to do it. Nobody built one. The seat just stays where you are.\" He almost smiled. \"I find that very accurate, as a description of despair.\""}, {"p": "\"Gin wrote that you say despair sees more clearly than anything else.\""}, {"p": "\"Despair handled properly is the emotion that sees reality most accurately. That's what I say.\" He shrugged. \"It's either wisdom or a symptom. It's both.\""}, {"p": "He wrapped both hands round his cup to take the warmth he could no longer quite make for himself."}, {"p": "\"It used to be that I held it and it held still. Now I hold it and it leans. North. Always north now. Toward the empty place.\" He was quiet for a moment. \"And there's something else. Every seat has a shadow on the other side. A thing made of the same feeling, at its worst. Most of them we've never seen. Mine has a name now. Kishin. It's in Takinogawa, wearing a woman like a coat, and it is eating my chair from under me. The empty seat pulls. Kishin bites.\" He looked down at the tea. \"We can kill things like that, you know. Holders. It's the one thing they're afraid of. And they can ride us. That's the one thing we're afraid of.\""}, {"p": "Then he asked the question that turned a starred name into a person Rei could ruin or save."}, {"p": "\"I don't suppose you came here to help. People don't, usually. But I find I'd very much like it if you had.\""}, {"break": "The settling"}, {"p": "He let Rei follow him for a day. The day was the horror, because nothing happened in it that anyone else could have seen."}, {"p": "Watanabe woke at five in a one-room flat a few streets from the library. The first thing he did, before the light and before tea, was something he called the settling. He sat on the edge of his futon and reached out, inside himself and across his whole district, looking for its despair. All of it. The man on the night shift who had stopped opening his post. The widow three floors down who had started giving her things away. The boy who studied the train timetables, and not because he wanted to ride the trains. Watanabe gathered it the way a person gathers spilled rice off a floor, grain by grain, into himself, so that none of them had to carry the full killing weight of it alone."}, {"p": "\"That's the seat,\" he said, watching Rei's face. \"Not a throne. A drain. The feeling runs through the district, and a holder is the place it runs to. Keep it moving, keep it passing through you, and it never sits still long enough to pool. When it pools, that's where suffering beings come from. Grief or despair left lying in one place until it thickens into something with a want of its own. My job is to make sure there's never enough lying still for that.\""}, {"p": "Rei spent the morning learning that a holder's mercy is invisible on purpose. Nobody in this ward walked into the river in August, and the reason was a slight man in a cardigan who got up before dawn. He took the weight of their worst hour into a body that was not built to store it. He had done it every single morning for longer than he would say. No one knew. That was the point."}, {"p": "\"If they knew, they'd thank me,\" he said. \"Then they'd feel they owed me. And the owing is its own despair. So I make sure they never find out. A seat held right is a seat no one can see.\""}, {"p": "He said it without bitterness. That was the part that got under Rei's skin. A man with no bitterness about giving up his whole life for no thanks is a man who has used up his bitterness too, along with everything else."}, {"p": "Then, in the middle of a sentence, the leaning took him."}, {"p": "Rei saw it happen. The dimness around Watanabe deepened, fast, toward the north. Rei felt the pull in his own teeth, like a held musical note sliding a half-step lower. Watanabe grabbed the edge of the table and held on until his knuckles went white. He breathed in a slow, careful, counted way, like a man holding up a wall with nothing but his attention."}, {"p": "It passed. He let go of the table."}, {"p": "\"That's new,\" he said, and tried to smile. \"This year. The empty chair pulling on the full ones. Some mornings the settling takes everything I've got and there's nothing left for... you know. Lunch. Standing up. Wanting to.\""}, {"p": "He looked at the window that faced north, toward Kita, toward the wound. For one second the gentleness slipped, and Rei saw what was under it. A man being slowly poured out through a hole he had not made and could not plug."}, {"break": "The fourth seat"}, {"p": "It was not Watanabe who let go first."}, {"p": "The news reached him the way news travels among the Thirteen. Gin's notes said their network had no name and no schedule. It worked by recognition, the way two people carrying the same heavy load can pick each other out across a crowded platform. Watanabe was a telepath. That evening, as the library was closing, he went still over the returns cart and said, very quietly, \"Oh.\" It was the way people say it when the phone rings at the wrong hour."}, {"p": "\"The fourth,\" he said. \"Nakashima Yuna. She carries Fear. She's gone to the place a holder goes when the weight gets heavier than the will.\" He was already reaching for his coat. \"Mori-san will have felt it too. He'll be awake at his boiler, holding on to the rest of us the way he always does. He's the eldest. When something like this happens we all look to him first.\" He stopped in the doorway. \"I'm going to her. You can come if you can keep your feet.\""}, {"p": "They went north together, into the part of the city where the veil was thin. Watanabe said almost nothing in the taxi. He sat with his eyes shut and his hands flat on his knees, listening to something Rei could not hear, and now and then he told the driver to turn. The driver had a little paper lantern hanging from his mirror for Obon, and it swung at every corner. By the time they found the right building, it was the hour before dawn. It was a danchi in Kita. In a small room with a single bed lay a woman who was not dying. Dying would have been ordinary. She was letting go. Her seat was coming open while they watched, and years of held Fear were coming loose all at once."}, {"p": "Rei had stood in thin places before. He had walked lanes that folded back on themselves. He had read his brother's traces in the membrane, the skin between this world and the other side. He had felt the far shore breathing through a torn seam. None of it had prepared him for the sound a register makes when it lets go."}, {"p": "It was not a sound. It was a lurch. The whole membrane over Kita gave one sick shudder, like a ferry hitting the wake of a ship no one can see. For three full seconds, every frightened thing Yuna had been holding back from the northern wards arrived in the people living there, all at once."}, {"p": "Rei felt it as a wave of pure terror with no reason behind it. It had nothing to do with him, and it went through him anyway. He watched Watanabe brace himself against the doorframe and take what he could of it. Not all of it. He did not have the strength for all of it. But he took a share, by instinct, the way a driver throws an arm across the passenger in a crash."}, {"p": "And Rei understood what the Thirteen were. Not rulers. Catchers. Twelve pairs of arms thrown across a city at the moment it brakes too hard."}, {"p": "When it settled, Yuna was asleep. Simply, ordinarily asleep, a tired woman who had held something terrible for years and had finally set it down. Her seat stood empty for now. The fourth chair had been pulled out from the table, and Rei could feel the table tilt further toward the gap because of it."}, {"p": "Watanabe slid down the doorframe and sat on the floor, grey and empty."}, {"p": "\"Two empty chairs now,\" he said. \"Hers, until she can climb back into it, and the thirteenth. The leaning will get worse for the rest of us. For me. Every chair that opens pulls harder on the ones still full.\""}, {"p": "\"What happens if she can't climb back?\""}, {"p": "\"Then the succession reaches for someone else. The CVD keeps a list it doesn't show anyone. People they rank Singularity. Secret candidates for the Thirteen, in case one of us falls. Most of them have no idea that's what they're for.\" He looked up at Rei from the floor. \"I'm next, you understand. Nearest the wound, weakest of the lot. I've about a season left in me. Less, if another one goes. That's not me being sad. I stopped being sad years ago. That's just the arithmetic, and I thought you'd want it straight.\""}], "n": 49}, {"title": "The Memorial by the Water", "kind": "side", "src": "s_weakening#2", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei walked Watanabe home from the danchi as the sky turned the colour of dishwater. Neither of them wanted a train. They followed the Shakujii River upstream toward Itabashi. It is a narrow river that runs through a deep concrete channel, with cherry trees leaning over its walls. At that hour the water was black and slow. A man in a vest was already out walking a small white dog. Delivery bikes clattered over the little bridges. On the way they passed a small memorial on the bank, where someone had set out fresh flowers, a cup of sake and a single paper lantern that had not yet been lit."}, {"img": "ext_griefbeing.jpg", "cap": "Okuma Sumi at the riverside memorial, and the grief that gathers there"}, {"img": "char_okuma-sumi.jpg", "cap": "Okuma Sumi, who tends the riverside memorial"}, {"p": "An old woman knelt there, changing the water in a jar. She was well into her seventies, in a faded summer kimono with the sleeves tied back. She moved slowly and with great care, the way people move in a place they have visited every day for years."}, {"p": "She looked up as they passed. Her eyes went straight past Rei and settled on Watanabe."}, {"p": "\"It's you,\" she said. \"I thought so. Some evenings the water goes very quiet, and very heavy, and then it lifts again. As if somebody upstream had taken a sack off its back. I always wondered who.\""}, {"p": "Watanabe stopped. For a moment Rei thought he would deny it. Instead he bowed, quite deeply."}, {"p": "\"Okuma-san,\" he said. \"I didn't know you'd noticed.\""}, {"p": "\"Grief gathers at the water like silt,\" the old woman said. \"I tend it. Some nights, something answers back.\" She set the jar down. \"This summer it answers more than it should. You look very tired, young man.\""}, {"p": "\"I am,\" Watanabe said simply."}, {"p": "She nodded as if that settled something, and turned back to her flowers. As they walked on, Rei looked back once. She was speaking softly to the river, the way people speak to someone who has just gone into the next room."}, {"p": "\"She lost someone to that river a long time ago,\" Watanabe said quietly. \"She's been coming every evening since. It isn't healthy, what she's built there. But it isn't mine to take from her either.\" He was silent for a few steps. \"That's most of the job. Knowing what isn't yours to take.\""}, {"break": "One morning"}, {"p": "In the grey week that followed, Rei made a decision. He would give Watanabe Jun one morning off."}, {"p": "He walked to Itabashi along the river in the dark, before five, while the streetlights were still on and the first crows were starting up. He sat on the edge of Watanabe's futon and told him to stay lying down. When the hour came, Rei did the settling himself. Rei had two registers, two kinds of feeling the veil could move through him, and he had spent his whole life pressing them down. Now he opened them on purpose. He reached out into the district and found the despair of strangers, grain by grain. The man who had stopped opening his post. The widow giving her things away. The boy with the timetables. He gathered it."}, {"p": "It was the worst thing he had ever done on purpose. He did not stop. A few steps away Watanabe was breathing the slow, even breath of a man resting for the first time in a year. The resting was the whole point. Rei had come to be the place the weight could run to, so that just this once, Watanabe would not have to be."}, {"p": "It came into him, and it did not feel like sadness. That was the lie the word despair told. It felt like gravity. A steady, true, downward pull. The simple physical fact that down exists and everything falls toward it. Holding it meant standing inside that fact and choosing, with his whole body, to stay upright, for people who would never know there had been a choice."}, {"p": "For one morning, Rei felt what Watanabe felt every morning. What Yuna had felt for years before her arms gave out. What the seat really cost the person in it. It was not a wound that someone does to a holder. It was an agreement, made again at every dawn, to be broken a little, so that a whole ward could wake up and think it had simply found the strength to face the day."}, {"p": "By seven he was out in the streets of the district, on his knees on a stranger's stairwell. He understood the registry from the inside now. He understood why the Healer's offer looked like mercy. And he understood exactly how something that is mercy can also be the trap."}, {"p": "When he came back, Watanabe was up and had made the slow tea. He took one look at Rei's face and knew what Rei had done, and what it had taught him. For the first time since they met, his eyes were wet, and not for himself."}, {"p": "\"Now you know,\" he said. \"Now you know it isn't done to us. We say yes. Every morning, we say yes, and the saying is the seat. That's why a thing that wants a chair can't just take it. It has to get a living register to say yes in a body that fits. That's what it's doing to your brother, isn't it. Teaching a hand to say yes.\""}, {"p": "He set the tea in front of Rei and folded Rei's hands round the cup for the warmth, the way he warmed his own. Then he looked at Rei for a long time, the way the telepath in him looked at everything, and his face changed."}, {"p": "\"Oh,\" he said softly. \"You're the shape of the thirteenth.\""}, {"p": "Rei did not answer. He did not know how."}, {"p": "\"Your brother copied the ledger, didn't he. Did he ever write down what the thirteenth actually is?\""}, {"p": "\"He wrote that the CVD files it as null-emotive. That he thought that was wrong, and couldn't say what was right.\""}, {"p": "Watanabe nodded slowly. \"Their instruments measure feeling by how much it moves. Duty doesn't move. It holds. So the needle sits still and they write down nothing there. But it isn't empty. It's full. So full there's no room in it for anything else. It's what duty becomes when it's been the only thing for so long that there's no self left that remembers what came before it.\" He turned his cup. \"That's why it's so hard to fill. The seat needs someone with many doors open at once. Someone deep enough to hold that one feeling that never changes, without letting it turn into grief or rage or any of the rest of ours. Most people would be swallowed. The ones it wants don't get swallowed. The old ones say the next holder of Duty doesn't become less himself. He just becomes the person who carries it, and his ordinary life goes on with that inside it.\""}, {"p": "\"And the ones working against you?\""}, {"p": "\"They don't want to take the thirteenth. They want it never filled. A vacant thirteenth means the Axis is holding it alone, stretched too thin. And a thing stretched that thin makes mistakes. They've been living off its mistakes for three years.\" He pushed the tea an inch closer to Rei. \"You shouldn't have learned any of this. But God help me, I'm glad someone finally did. I've been the only one who knew what it felt like for a very long time.\""}, {"break": "The back half of August"}, {"p": "For the rest of August, Watanabe Jun stayed at the returns cart in his cardigan, holding. That was what the seat was. Nobody had given him a way out that did not cost a brother or a city."}, {"p": "Rei visited when he could. Each time, Watanabe was dimmer, the half-step of darkness deepening toward a whole one. The pull to the north got a little worse every time another chair so much as creaked. Each time he greeted Rei with the same open relief, the same slow tea and the same refusal to complain. And each time Rei carried away the lesson of the morning of the settling. The wall is held up by consent. A register says yes. The whole patient effort on the far side was a plan to win one yes, in one borrowed hand."}, {"p": "That knowledge followed him out of the library and into every room for the rest of the month. On the good nights it felt like a weapon. The chair needs a yes, he would tell himself on the train home, his face reflected in the dark window. So a yes held back is a chair left empty. And a brother who learns not to agree starves the whole plot at the root."}, {"p": "On the bad nights it felt like something else. He had been shaped to fit now. He had done the settling, and the seat remembered him. Lying awake, he caught himself thinking that the cleanest way to save the twelve and spare Gin might be to say yes himself, in a body that fit. Watanabe had shown him exactly how. He did not like how calm the thought was."}, {"p": "And under all of it was grief. A gentle, dimming man counting down his last season. It was the truest thing anyone had told him about what the wall costs the people who are the wall."}, {"p": "On the last good visit, Watanabe asked him not to be there when it happened."}, {"p": "\"You've felt one go,\" he said. \"You don't need to feel mine. Go and do the thing only you can do. You're the one with two of yourself and a hand the chair wants. You're the only piece on this board that isn't already spent.\""}, {"p": "He shelved the last picture book of the day. His hand did not shake this time. Rei could see what that steadiness cost him. He was holding his hand still for Rei's sake, so that the last picture Rei kept of him would be a steady one."}, {"p": "\"Close the chair if you can do it without becoming me,\" he said. \"And if you can't...\" He stopped, and started again, dimmer. \"If you can't, then become me knowing what it is. With your eyes open. The way I never got to choose it. That's the only mercy I've got to give you, and it's a poor one, and I'm sorry it's the truth.\""}, {"break": "Where it started"}, {"p": "On the train home that night, Rei thought back to where all of this had started. It was the night on the floor of the Crooked Shelf with the ledger open in front of him, when he had looked at the starred line with Watanabe's name on it and told Higuchi that he would go looking too. Now he had found the man, and he was losing him."}], "n": 50}, {"title": "The Rage That Won't Cool", "kind": "side", "src": "s_rage", "blocks": [{"p": "Higuchi would not write this name down. He said it aloud only once, in the back room of the Crooked Shelf, with the door shut and the radio turned up. Then he made Rei repeat it back to him, with a hand laid over the margin of the ledger, as if the ink might overhear."}, {"p": "Mori Takao. The second of the Thirteen. He carried Rage. He was forty-eight, the eldest of them, and the one the others looked to. He had held the seat for twenty years. And for most of those years he had made himself impossible to find, on purpose."}, {"p": "\"He's in the CVD system as a retired field operative,\" Higuchi said. \"He is not retired. The others hide because hiding is restful. He hides because a Rage holder you can find is a Rage holder somebody can stand in front of and insult until the wall cracks. Despair you have to coax out. Rage you only have to provoke. So the second seat learned to be nobody, and he is very, very good at it.\""}, {"p": "\"Where do I look?\""}, {"p": "\"Where people go when they have nowhere left to go.\" Higuchi turned the radio up another notch. \"And ask the old man in the freight-yard blocks, if he'll talk to you. Tomi. He counts what crosses that fence at night. He's seen Mori cross.\""}, {"break": "Nine days and three wrong men"}, {"p": "Rei had already seen a man at a boiler on the long night, and he had not trusted what he saw. It took him nine days and three wrong men to be sure."}, {"p": "He searched the places Higuchi meant, the places people end up when they have run out of other places. He rode the trains in the heat of the afternoon and walked the streets at night. The first wrong man was a night porter at a capsule hotel, the kind of cheap hotel where guests sleep in plastic pods stacked two high along a corridor, like drawers. The porter was big and quiet, and he was only a porter. The second was a retired fireman who fed the pigeons in a small park every evening at six. The third was a cook at a soup kitchen in Sanya. Sanya is an old neighbourhood in the east of the city where day labourers once came looking for work, and where many of them stayed on, old and alone, in tiny rented rooms. The cook looked exactly right. He was only a cook."}, {"p": "The fourth was not a man Rei was looking for. He was a witness. Old Tomi, at his window in the condemned blocks, told Rei only that the big man with the short, greying hair came through the yard some nights, walked toward the worst corner, and always walked back out again. He said the man walked like someone carrying a full pan of oil."}, {"p": "Tomi was the only one of them who was worth the walk."}, {"img": "place_freightyard.jpg", "cap": "The condemned blocks beside the freight yard"}, {"p": "The condemned blocks stood beside the freight yard like the last teeth left in a jaw. Long lines of goods wagons sat on the tracks behind a wire fence, and somewhere in the dark a diesel engine idled with a low, steady rumble. Most of the windows in the blocks were boarded up. The city had given up on these buildings years ago. The dead had not. Rei felt them from the street. Forty years of bad deaths had built up there, layer on layer like old paint. In places it was so thick that the air seemed to bend. Gin's book said that one hour inside was a risk of possession for anyone whose aperture was open. Rei's had been opening all month. He did not go inside."}, {"img": "char_old-tomi.jpg", "cap": "Old Tomi, the last man living in the blocks"}, {"p": "He did not need to. A single window on the third floor had a light in it, and an old man sat in it, watching the yard."}, {"p": "\"I live where the city gave up,\" the old man called down, before Rei had said a word. \"Things come through here. I watch which ones.\" He pointed a finger at Rei. \"You're not one of the things. You're one of the ones that comes looking. Like the big fellow.\""}, {"p": "\"A big man. Short dark hair, some grey in it. Walks like he's carrying something.\""}, {"p": "\"Carrying a full pan of oil and terrified to spill it.\" Tomi spat out of the window, neatly, away from Rei. \"Some nights when it's bad over the fence, he comes through the yard. Goes right up to the worst corner, where I won't go. Stands there. Then something that was coming through stops coming through, and he walks back out. I've seen him cross a dozen times.\" He squinted. \"Bathhouse man. Smells of coke smoke. You'll want the bath by the old chimney, if you want him at all. Most people wouldn't.\""}, {"break": "The bath by the old chimney"}, {"img": "scene_rei_mori_boiler.jpg", "cap": "Mori Takao at the bath-house furnace"}, {"p": "So in the end Rei came back to where he had started. It was the boiler room of a public bath in Kita. The wrong ward. The thin ward. A man tending a furnace forty metres from the wound in the world. Rei did not think any of that was an accident. And a public bath at night was exactly where people went when they had nowhere left to go."}, {"p": "He walked there after dark, from the station through the narrow back streets. The heat had not broken. Families sat out on their doorsteps on folding stools, fanning themselves. Every few houses, a small lantern hung by the door for Obon, lit to welcome the family dead. A boy on a bicycle went past with a plastic bucket of fireworks hanging from the handlebars. Rei could see the chimney long before he reached the bath. It was a tall brick stack, rising over the low rooftops like an old finger pointing at the sky."}, {"img": "place_sento.jpg", "cap": "The old bath, its Mount Fuji mural pale with steam"}, {"p": "It was an old neighbourhood sentō, from the years when most small flats in Tokyo had no bath of their own. Men bathed on one side of a wall and women on the other. The painting of Mount Fuji on the tiled wall above the bath had gone pale with years of steam. Somewhere below, out of sight, a boiler kept all that water hot, and somebody had to feed it."}, {"p": "At the front desk sat an old woman in a cardigan, counting coins into a tray. She looked at Rei over her glasses. Then she looked at the empty air just behind his left shoulder, and nodded to it politely, as if to an acquaintance."}, {"p": "\"He's downstairs,\" she said, before Rei could ask. \"The iron stairs past the men's side. Don't go banging down them. He doesn't like banging.\" She went back to her coins. \"And tell your friend there to wait up here with me. He's no trouble.\""}, {"p": "Rei did not turn to look at whoever she meant. He went through the cloth curtain and found the iron stairs."}, {"p": "He went down them slowly, one foot at a time, and the heat came up to meet him."}, {"p": "Dark hair cropped short, greying at the sides. Plain dark clothes, always a second layer, nothing that would stop him moving. People who saw him across a room took him for a retired man. Then they looked at his hands. The first thing Rei noticed was how still the man held them. It was the stillness of a man who has learned that his hands are the part of him most likely to do something he can never take back."}, {"p": "He was shovelling coke into the boiler. Coke is a hard, dark fuel made from coal, and it burns very hot with little smoke. He worked with an economy of movement that was almost beautiful. No wasted gesture. No slammed door. Every movement was kept down to the smallest it could be. The heat off the firebox was enormous, and he stood in it without flinching. He looked completely at home inside something that would burn anyone else."}, {"p": "He did not turn round when Rei reached the bottom of the stairs."}, {"p": "\"You walked careful,\" he said, to the fire. \"Most people come down those stairs like the building owes them money. You came down like you didn't want to wake anything. So either you're afraid of me, which is sensible, or somebody taught you what I am. Which is dangerous. For you. Sit where I can see your hands.\""}, {"p": "Rei sat on the bench by the wall and let him look at his hands. Mori read them the way the others read the air around Rei. Rei watched him take in the two registers Rei had pressed down all his life, the searching, and the shaped thing in him that the empty chair wanted. Mori's jaw tightened. It was not quite anger, but it was the muscle anger lives in. Then, slowly, and in a way Rei could see, he made it let go."}, {"p": "\"Two of you,\" Mori said. \"And neither one's lit. You've spent your whole life sitting on your own boiler, same as me. I can always tell.\""}, {"p": "He threw in another shovelful. The firebox door swung shut on its own before his hand reached it. It closed so carefully that the sound it made was smaller than it should have been. Mori looked at the door with something like irritation, as if it had embarrassed him."}, {"p": "\"Telekinesis,\" he said, though Rei had not asked. \"Rage makes it stronger. It also makes it cost more. I use it anyway. Somebody has to.\" He set the shovel down. \"So I'll ask you the only question that matters down here. Did you come to provoke me? Or did you come because you finally understood that somebody's been trying to, and you wanted to see the man they're aiming at?\""}, {"p": "\"The second,\" Rei said."}, {"p": "Mori looked at him for a long moment. Then he nodded once at the bench by the wall."}, {"break": "The furnace"}, {"p": "He let Rei stay for a shift. That one shift taught Rei that Rage was the opposite of Despair in every way that mattered."}, {"p": "Watanabe's seat was a drain. It was a low place where the district's despair could run down, so that nobody drowned in their own. Mori's seat was a furnace. The difference was simple. Despair wants to pool. Rage wants to spread. It wants to jump, to find the next dry thing and set it alight. So the second holder's skill was not gathering. It was containing. He stood in the middle of the ward's anger the way this boiler stood in the middle of the bathhouse. He took all of it in. He burned it down to a heat he could bear to stand inside. And he let out only enough to keep the water warm, and never one degree more."}, {"p": "\"Rage is the register for one thing,\" Mori said, feeding the fire. \"The feeling that something was taken that shouldn't have been. That kind of feeling pushes harder against the wall than any other. The things on the other side that feed on it come at the wall harder than any other kind, too. More of them try to cross through my seat than through anyone's. There's one over there that circles it. Has done for years. Waiting for me to slip.\" He threw another shovelful. \"You can't put rage out. People who think the job is putting it out burn their own house down inside a year. You can't even let it go cold. A cold furnace with this much fuel in it doesn't stay cold. It goes off. The job is banked. Hot enough that it never builds a head of pressure. Low enough that it never throws a spark. Twenty years I've kept this ward's temper at a simmer, and not one of them knows there's a reason the fights downstairs never quite start.\""}, {"p": "To bank a fire is to cover it and turn it low, so that it burns slowly all night without dying and without blazing up. Rei spent the night watching Mori bank a whole ward. It was the most exhausting thing he had ever seen a man do while standing almost still."}, {"p": "Every time something in the ward flared, Rei felt it arrive in Mori. A drunk three streets away working himself up. A married couple two floors up finding an old wound again. A boy somewhere deciding that tonight was the night he finally hit back. Each one landed in Mori as a small, hard surge. A flush went up the back of his neck. His hands closed. His breath went slow and counted. Then Rei felt him take it down into the furnace inside himself and burn it into something the ward could stand. What came out, through the boiler, was only warmth."}, {"p": "\"That one was a knife,\" Mori said quietly, after a surge worse than the others. \"Kid two wards over had his hand on a knife and his sister's boyfriend in front of him. He doesn't know why he put it down. He'll tell himself he chickened out and hate himself a bit for it. That's the job. They never get to know it was held for them. They just get to keep their hands clean and think less of themselves for it. You learn to be all right with that or you don't last.\""}, {"p": "Near three in the morning, the pull came."}, {"p": "It was the same northward lean that Watanabe had described. But in a furnace it landed differently. It landed as a draught. Rei felt the empty seat pull, and he felt Mori's banked fire want to rise toward it, the way a fire leans toward an open door. In a single breath, Mori's control went from hard to brutal. He gripped the shovel in both hands and planted his feet. Rei watched a forty-eight-year-old man hold a furnace down by plain strength while something to the north tried to open the flue, the chimney pipe that lets a fire breathe."}, {"p": "It passed. Mori leaned the shovel against the wall so carefully that even that was a kind of holding."}, {"p": "\"That's new this year,\" he said. For the first time, his flat voice had a hairline crack in it. \"The empty chair's started pulling on mine. And a furnace that gets pulled on wants to flare. You understand what I'm telling you. Despair leaning north just leans. Rage leaning north wants to go off. If the second seat ever flares, really flares, twenty years all at once, it doesn't drain anywhere. It throws a spark into the thinnest part of the membrane in the whole city. Which happens to be right over our heads.\""}, {"p": "He nodded north, through the wall."}, {"p": "\"Watanabe's going. You've seen him. Nakashima's let go. They'll all have been looking to me this week, the way they do, and I've had nothing to tell them except hold on.\" He wiped his face with the back of his wrist. \"Eldest. It mostly means you're the one who's been tired longest.\""}], "n": 51}, {"title": "The Soft Voice", "kind": "side", "src": "s_rage#2", "blocks": [{"p": "Once Mori let himself look for it, he found the provocation. A Rage holder spends his whole life feeling for the things that try to set him alight. Now that someone had said the pattern out loud, he could point straight at it."}, {"p": "Three times in the last month, he said, it had been the same kind of night. A man came into the bathhouse who did not want a bath. He sat in the changing room and said small, ugly things, just loud enough to start something. He left the moment a fight nearly began. It was as if the fight was the real errand and the bath was only the excuse."}, {"p": "\"Testing the boiler,\" Mori said. \"Walking in here and tapping the gauge to see how far the needle jumps. I thought he was just some sad bastard with a grudge. But he comes on the nights the pull's worst. He leaves the second the room tips. And he's never the one who throws the first punch. He's the one who makes sure somebody else nearly does.\""}, {"p": "\"Who sent him?\""}, {"p": "Mori shook his head. Then his face did the thing Rei had learned to dread. It went gentle, the way a holder's face goes gentle when he has just understood his own ruin."}, {"p": "\"He talks soft,\" Mori said. \"Soothing, almost. Calls it a shame, all this anger. Says wouldn't it be a mercy if a man like me could finally set the weight down. He talks like a doctor.\""}, {"p": "Rei knew the voice before Mori had finished. He had been chasing that bedside manner all summer. The reasonable favour. The kindness that is really a hook. It was the same borrowed warmth he had heard in everything the Healer touched, whatever face it wore for the job. Whoever had sent this man, the campaign against the Thirteen spoke in that voice."}, {"p": "They were not trying to break the Rage seat by attacking it. They were trying to break it by being kind to it. They were walking a tired furnace keeper toward the idea that flaring would be a relief. That letting go would be a mercy. That twenty years was enough and nobody would blame him. And every near-fight in the changing room was a tap on the gauge, to measure how close that idea had brought the needle."}, {"p": "\"They want me to want it,\" Mori said, very quietly, into the firebox. \"Same as the others, I'd bet. They can't make a holder flare. They can only make a holder tired enough to choose it, and then tell him choosing it is kindness. And the hell of it is, some nights, listening to that soft voice, they're not entirely wrong. That's the part that frightens me. A furnace that half agrees to go off is already leaking.\""}, {"p": "It was the same machine Rei had watched working on his brother. The same machine Watanabe had described from inside Despair. One fact lay under all of it. A seat could not be taken by force. It could only be agreed to. And the whole patient work of the far shore was to wear holders down until they were thin enough to agree. Mori Takao was being led gently toward a yes, the way Gin had been led toward a yes, by a voice that called the worst possible choice a mercy. And he had just admitted that on the bad nights, the voice was starting to make sense."}, {"p": "He banged the firebox shut."}, {"p": "\"So now you know why I made myself nobody for twenty years,\" he said. \"It was never the strangers I was hiding from. It was the one who'd come talking soft. I always knew he'd come eventually. I just didn't think I'd be this tired when he did.\""}, {"break": "One night off the shovel"}, {"p": "In the end Rei gave him the night, because Mori asked for it. He asked plainly, the way a man asks when he has not asked for anything in twenty years and has used up all his pride getting the words out. One night off the shovel. One night when the furnace was not his alone. One night of sleep without one ear always listening for the gauge."}, {"p": "So Rei did what he had done for Watanabe. But Rage would not let him do it gently."}, {"p": "He stood in the boiler room in the small hours and took the banking onto his own two unlit registers. The ward's anger came into him, and it did not come as gravity. It came as fire. Every flaring temper in Kita arrived at once, and every one of them wanted to spread. In about ninety seconds, Rei learned why Mori held his hands so still."}, {"p": "The rage did not make him sad, and it did not make him tired. It made him feel right. It handed him a hundred perfectly good reasons to do something unforgivable, and it dressed every one of them up as justice. Holding it was not about strength. It was about refusal. Moment after moment, he had to refuse a hundred real grievances. All of them deserved an answer. None of them could be answered without burning the ward down."}, {"p": "He banked it. He stood in the middle of the city's temper, took it in, burned it down to a heat he could bear, and let out only warmth. Across the room, Mori Takao lay on the changing-room bench with his arm over his eyes, and he slept. He really slept, for the first time in longer than he would say. The sleeping was the whole point. Rei kept the furnace banked over a sleeping man for the worst four hours of his life."}, {"p": "In the middle of it, the soft voice nearly got him."}, {"p": "It was not a man this time. It was only the idea, arriving the way it must arrive in Mori on every bad night. A perfectly reasonable thought. He was holding a hundred real grievances back. The people upstairs, the people three wards over, had done real harm to real people. They deserved the fire he was keeping from them. Letting it out would not be rage at all. It would be justice. Mercy. A putting right."}, {"p": "Rei felt the needle jump in his own chest. He understood, all the way down, how a tired holder is walked toward a yes. It is not weakness that opens the flue. It is the furnace speaking in the holder's own voice, saying that going off would be the right thing to do."}, {"p": "He held."}, {"p": "By dawn he was emptied out and shaking, and he had not flared. Mori woke, sat up and took one look at Rei's face. He knew everything. He knew Rei had stood in his fire. He knew the soft voice had come for Rei too. And he knew Rei had held."}, {"p": "\"Now you know,\" he said. They were the same words Watanabe had used. Rei began to think they were the words the Thirteen said to the rare outsider who finally felt it from the inside. \"It doesn't come at you like a monster. It comes at you like you're finally being honest. That's why they can't just take the seat. They have to get you to agree the fire's only fair. And a register that agrees the fire's fair has already opened the flue a crack. You held it.\""}, {"p": "He said it like a man handing over something he had earned the right to give."}, {"p": "An old black telephone hung on the boiler-room wall, the kind with a curly cord. At ten past six it rang. Mori answered it without surprise and listened for a long time."}, {"p": "\"No,\" he said at last. \"No, it held. Quiet night, for once. You?\" He listened again. \"Then do the settling and eat something after, Jun, I mean it. Eat. And stop counting seasons, it doesn't help.\" A pause. \"Yes. He's here.\" Mori glanced at Rei. \"He held it. All night. I slept.\" Another pause, longer. \"I know. I know what that means. We'll talk.\""}, {"p": "He hung up. For a moment he stood with his hand flat on the receiver."}, {"p": "\"Watanabe,\" he said. \"He rings when it's bad. They all do, sooner or later. The young ones think I've got answers.\" He shook his head. \"I've just got twenty years of not having any, and still being here. Apparently that counts for something.\""}, {"p": "He turned back to Rei."}, {"p": "\"Twenty years I've wondered if anyone else could. You did it one night and it nearly had you. Now you understand what you'd be saying yes to, if it's ever your chair. Most people learn that the easy way. By being told. You'll never be able to unfeel it.\""}, {"break": "Down the careful stairs"}, {"p": "The next night Mori Takao was back on the shovel. Nobody had given him a way out of the boiler room that would not crack the membrane over his head. The second seat could not be left untended any more than the sixth."}, {"p": "Rei visited when he could through the back half of August, down the iron stairs, slowly and carefully. The trip became familiar. The hot walk from the station. The lanterns by the doors. The old woman at the desk with her coins, who always nodded to the air behind his shoulder. Each time, the northward draught was a little worse, and the flue a little harder to hold shut. The soft voice had come once or twice more while Rei was away. Each time it had found Mori just tired enough to listen, and just warned enough not to agree."}, {"p": "\"You bought me time,\" Mori told him on one visit, feeding the fire. \"Not a cure. There isn't one that doesn't end with somebody in a chair. But a man who knows the kindness is the hook can hold a furnace a good while longer than a man who thinks the kindness is real. You gave me that. I'll burn it slow.\""}, {"p": "What Rei carried out of the boiler room was Watanabe's lesson, learned again in a different register. Together the two lessons became the backbone of the rest of his month. The seat is held by consent. The wall is a yes, given again every day. And the whole patient effort on the far side is the making of one yes, in one worn-out body shaped to fit."}, {"p": "But Rage taught him the part Despair could not. It taught him how the yes feels from the inside when it comes. Not as giving up. As being in the right. The furnace hands over a hundred good reasons and calls the worst of them mercy. He would hear that voice again. He would hear it at the river. He would hear it in his brother's diary entries after the fourteenth. And because of one terrible night spent holding the fire for a sleeping man, he would know that voice for what it was. It was the flue opening. It was not the truth arriving."}, {"p": "On the last good visit, Mori asked him the thing the second seat would ask."}, {"p": "\"If it comes to a chair,\" he said, not looking up from the fire, \"don't sit in mine. Despair you can carry tired. Rage you carry angry, and a holder who sits down angry stays angry, and the ward gets forty years of a furnace that resents them. Whatever you do with that keyed thing in you, and you'll do something, I can see it on you, you're the only piece on this board that isn't spent, don't let it be the second chair. Close the empty one if you can. Bank your own boiler if you can't. But the day you feel the fire start to make sense, the day going off feels like the honest thing...\""}, {"p": "He banged the firebox shut, gently, all that strength spent on making the sound small."}, {"p": "\"...that's the day you walk careful down some other man's stairs and ask him to take the shovel. The way you walked careful down mine. Nobody banks a furnace alone forever. That's the whole secret. That's the only one I've got.\""}], "n": 52}, {"title": "The Fear on the Stairwell", "kind": "side", "src": "s_fear", "blocks": [{"p": "Before he went to the address Higuchi had given him, Rei went to see the two people Gin's file said could feel Nakashima Yuna."}, {"img": "place_nightcircuit.jpg", "cap": "The underpass where Kubo plays, his guitar case open"}, {"img": "char_kubo.jpg", "cap": "Kubo, the blind busker in the underpass"}, {"p": "He went to the first one in the middle of a hot afternoon. Up at street level the sun hit the pavement so hard it seemed to bounce. Office workers walked with handkerchiefs pressed to their necks, and a woman at the crossing fanned herself with a folded train timetable. Down in the underpass beneath the station it was cooler, and it smelled of wet concrete and of frying oil from a food stand at the top of the steps. Water dripped somewhere in the dark between the lamps. Footsteps echoed. Kubo, the blind busker, played here by day, with his long grey ponytail tied back and his guitar case open at his feet. He could not see the underpass and did not need to. He could hear when the feeling of it changed. When Rei asked about the woman who carried Fear, Kubo stopped playing."}, {"p": "\"I always hear her pass,\" he said. \"Like a draught going by with heels on. She hasn't passed in eleven days. The underpass doesn't know what to do with itself.\""}, {"img": "place_suzuran.jpg", "cap": "The Suzuran at two in the morning"}, {"p": "The second one he went to see at two in the morning, because that was the only hour she could be found. The Suzuran was a tiny bar on a Kita corner, pressed up against the worst of the thin spots. It had one fridge that hummed too loudly and fluorescent lights that made everyone look a day dead. People said the corner never came out in photographs. Rei believed it. The Woman at the Back sat where she always sat, in the back seat at the Suzuran at two in the morning. She wore the same coat she wore in every season, with a lacquer pin in her long ash-grey hair. She did not look up when Rei stood by her table."}, {"p": "\"I'm not telling fortunes,\" she said. \"I'm telling you what's already on its way. You should listen.\" She turned her cup a quarter turn. \"The one who carries fear. I've felt her coming every night for years, like weather. This week I feel her going. Go up her stairs before she runs out of them.\""}, {"break": "The north stairwell"}, {"p": "The address was a danchi in Kita, one of the long pale housing blocks the city had built by the thousand after the war and then forgotten. It was four storeys high, a rectangle of identical steel doors, going grey at the seams. It stood forty metres on the wrong side of the line where the membrane ran thin."}, {"p": "Rei walked there from the Suzuran in the dead hours, through streets where nothing moved except the moths around the streetlights. A konbini on the corner glowed white and empty, and the clerk inside was asleep on his elbows behind the till. The heat had not gone with the sun. The air was wet and heavy, and the cicadas had fallen silent at last, which somehow made the streets feel emptier."}, {"p": "It was the second week of Obon. Lanterns were hanging out on the ground-floor balconies, paper boxes of orange light, so that the dead could find their way home. Every single one of them hung on the south side of the building. Not one hung by the north stairwell. Rei noticed it the way he would notice someone holding their breath. It was not a fact so much as a pressure. A whole block of living people had quietly agreed, without ever saying it out loud, that there was one part of their own home they did not go into after dark."}, {"p": "He went into it."}, {"img": "ch21_entitysight.jpg", "cap": "The north stairwell"}, {"p": "The north stairwell was open to the air. Concrete steps zigzagged up the outside of the block behind a waist-high wall. The moment his foot left the ground for the first step, the temperature changed. Not the temperature of the air. That was the same wet August heat as everywhere else in the city. It was the temperature of Rei. Something inside him dropped all at once, as if he had stepped into cold water he could not see."}, {"p": "It was not a thought. There was no picture, no figure, no sound he could point at and call a ghost. There was only the body's oldest answer, arriving before any reason for it. <em>Do not go up these stairs.</em>"}, {"p": "His heart was pounding before he had decided anything. His knees did not want to bend. And the worst part, the part that told him what he was really standing in, was that the fear was not about anything. It had nothing to be afraid of. It was simply there in the concrete, the way damp is there, held in the building itself. It was reaching into him and using his own nerves to make itself felt."}, {"p": "Halfway up the first flight, at the half-landing where the stairs turned back on themselves, a woman sat on the bottom step of the next flight. Her back was against the wall and her knees were pulled up. She was shaking."}, {"img": "thirteen_IV_nakashima_yuna.jpg", "cap": "Nakashima Yuna, holder of Fear"}, {"p": "She was about forty. Her black hair had early grey at the hairline and was coming loose from a knot that had once been neat. A good dark blazer lay folded on the step beside her, as if she had taken it off days ago meaning to put it back on. She wore a cardigan buttoned wrong and canvas shoes with no laces. She had the stillness of someone who had made herself small for a long time and had become frighteningly good at it."}, {"p": "She did not look at Rei. She looked at the step above her as if it were the edge of a cliff."}, {"p": "\"You can feel it,\" she said. It was not a question. Her voice was thin and even, and keeping it even was costing her. \"Most people just feel sick and leave. You came up anyway, and you can feel it, which means somebody sent you, which means it's worse than I thought.\" Her hands were pressed flat to the concrete on either side of her. \"Please don't make me explain who I am while I'm sitting in it. Just sit down where you are. Don't come higher. And tell me whether the two of you in there are lit, because I can't hold and read you at the same time any more.\""}, {"p": "Her name was Nakashima Yuna. She was the fourth of the Thirteen, and she held Fear."}, {"p": "In ordinary life she ran a security consultancy. She was a director, the kind of woman who had planned every decision in a building before she walked into it. Her calendar was public. Her assistant knew more than most practitioners. For twelve years she had been building a map of everything the fear domain produced. Not all of it was hostile, she said. Some of it was defensive. Some of it was warning systems older than the Accord. The CVD used her map in its training without ever putting her name on it. She had noticed. She filed nothing and did everything."}, {"p": "Since the night her seat came open, she had been trying to climb back into it. In eleven days she had not got past the half-landing of her own domain."}, {"p": "She told him this in pieces, in that flat, careful voice, never raising it. She had learned that her own feelings were the one thing in this stairwell she could not afford."}, {"p": "\"A holder doesn't fight the register,\" she said. \"You carry it. It runs through you like current through a wire, and the wire doesn't get to have feelings about the current. The wire just has to be a wire. The day I started being afraid of my own seat is the day the seat started leaking.\""}, {"p": "She tipped her head a little toward the flights above. In the dim light the upper landings were a stack of grey shapes."}, {"p": "\"It used to live at the top. In the plant room. I'd go up once a week and sit with it, and it stayed put. Now it comes down to meet me. It's on the third landing tonight. Yesterday it was on the fourth. It's learning that I'm scared. Every time it learns, it comes a step closer. And a fear holder who's running out of stairs is a domain about to come off its hinges.\""}, {"p": "Rei sat with his back against the cold wall, his pulse hammering at a danger that did not exist. He understood that this was what the inside of a seat felt like. It was nothing like the grand picture in Higuchi's ledger, twelve proud pillars holding up the world. It was a woman who could not go upstairs, quietly counting how many landings she had left."}, {"p": "The fear was not hers. She kept saying so, gently, the way a mother corrects a child who has got something backwards. The fear in the concrete was the register, the domain, the thing she had been given to carry. It was as impersonal as the weather. But the fear of the fear was hers. Eleven days of dread that she would not be able to climb. That she would fail. That she would be the seat that fell. That part was hers. It was the crack the register had got its fingers into. And she could not stop feeling it, because she was human, and what was at stake was the wall of the world."}, {"p": "\"I can steady anyone,\" she said. \"That's the strange part. Put a practitioner in front of me in a full crisis and I can bring them level in under thirty seconds. I've done it in car parks. I've done it in a hospital lift. I can't do it for me.\""}, {"p": "\"Is there no one who could climb with you?\""}, {"p": "\"Natsumi would. Okabe Natsumi. She holds Courage, the tenth. Our seats are tied together. Fear is seeing the harm coming. Courage is going forward anyway. We've worked in step for twelve years. The first five were difficult.\" The ghost of a smile. \"I haven't called her. I'm afraid that if I lean my whole weight on her I'll pull her down onto the landing with me. Then there would be two of us sitting here.\""}, {"p": "\"I keep thinking if I were braver I could just walk up,\" she said, and for the first time the flat voice shook. One hairline crack, and then she pressed it smooth again. \"But that's the trap, isn't it. The braver I try to be, the more I'm thinking about being brave. The more I think about it, the more I'm feeling it. And feeling it is the leak. You can't white-knuckle your way back into a fear seat. The only people who can hold Fear are people who genuinely aren't afraid in the moment. I haven't had a moment like that in eleven days.\""}, {"p": "At last she looked at him. Her eyes did what the others' eyes had done. They read the two pressed-down registers, the searching, the shaped thing the empty chair wanted. But the others had looked at him with caution. She looked at him the way a drowning person looks at something that floats."}, {"p": "\"You came up the stairs feeling it,\" she said. \"And your hands were steady. I watched them. How are your hands steady?\""}], "n": 53}, {"title": "The Kind Visitor", "kind": "side", "src": "s_fear#2", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei asked her the question he had learned to ask every weakening holder. He had seen the pattern twice now, and he no longer believed in coincidence."}, {"p": "\"Has anyone been kind to you lately? Anyone soft-voiced. Anyone who turned up around the time it started getting worse, and helped, and made you feel a little better, and left you a little more afraid than before?\""}, {"p": "Yuna went very still, in a new way. It was the stillness of someone fitting together two things she had been keeping carefully apart."}, {"p": "\"The man from the residents' association,\" she said slowly. \"He's not... I've never seen him at a meeting. He started coming by about two weeks ago. He brings me tea on the landing. He sits a few steps down, like you. And he's so calm. He's the calmest person I've ever met. He asks me how the seat is doing, and he listens, really listens. And when he leaves I always feel...\""}, {"p": "She stopped."}, {"p": "\"I always feel worse. I thought it was just the contrast. You feel safe with him, then he goes and you're alone with it again. But that's not it, is it. That's not it at all.\""}, {"p": "It was not. Rei had read the ledger. By now he knew the handwriting of this kind of kindness."}, {"p": "Most housing blocks in Japan have a residents' association, a group of neighbours who organise the rubbish rota, the summer festival and the notices in the lobby. Nobody questions a man with its lanyard round his neck. And this man had been coming to the fourth holder with tea, a soft voice and an open ear. On every visit he drew her fear up to the surface. He let her sit in it. He named it gently back to her. Then he took his calm away and left the fear standing there. It was not to torment her. Nothing so crude. It was to train it. To teach the register in the concrete that its holder was afraid. To walk it down, one landing at a time, toward the moment the seat came loose and Fear stood empty, right beside a membrane that was already thin."}, {"p": "It was the same design Rei had found at the Rage seat in the bathhouse. The same patient cruelty, wearing the face of help. It was not an attack the holders could fight. It was a kindness they could not refuse. Its aim was to thin the wall of the world from the inside by emptying the chairs that held it up. The thirteenth seat was empty. Someone wanted to give it company on the wall that leaned north."}, {"p": "\"He's coming tonight,\" Yuna said. Her flat voice had dropped to a whisper, because understanding it had sent a spike of the very thing that was leaking out of her. \"He said he'd come tonight to see how I was doing. He's always so worried about how I'm doing.\""}, {"p": "She looked up the stairs, to the third landing, where the grey was thickest. Then she looked down past Rei to the lower flight, where the orange Obon light from the south side barely reached."}, {"p": "\"If he comes and sits and does it again, I won't have a fourth landing left. I'll have the plant room, and then the roof, and then nothing. The seat will come off. You felt what one stairwell's worth of it does to a person walking up. Imagine it with no holder. Imagine it loose over Kita during Obon, with the doors already open for the dead.\""}, {"p": "She took a breath that shook all the way down."}, {"p": "\"So I need to know what you are before he gets here. Because I think you might be the only thing in this building that can stand on the landing between us and not break.\""}, {"break": "Half a step ahead"}, {"p": "What Rei did was not what the stories would have chosen."}, {"p": "He did not climb to the third landing and fight the fear like a monster with a throat to cut. There was nothing up there to fight. What Yuna needed was smaller and much harder. She needed to climb back into her own seat. She could not do it while she was afraid. And she would be afraid the whole way up."}, {"p": "So Rei went up the stairs ahead of her. One step at a time. On each step, before she reached it, he took the fear that lived there into his own walled-off chest, where it could not get all the way in. He held it. For the length of one breath, the step she was about to climb onto was the one place in the building where the dread was being carried by somebody else."}, {"p": "He was not removing it. He could not remove it. He was being afraid in the right place, on purpose, half a second ahead of her, so that she stepped onto ground that had just been held."}, {"p": "It was the worst climb of his life. The fear on the second landing was bad. The third was worse. The dread coming off the plant-room door at the top was a physical weight that bent his neck. Every old, deep part of his body was screaming about a danger that had no shape and no end."}, {"p": "The only thing that kept him on his feet was the very thing he had been ashamed of all his life. The suppression. The wall inside him. The two registers held down for so long they had gone half numb. The fear reached into him for the connection it needed, and found it already cut. He was useful here because he was broken in exactly this way."}, {"p": "Somewhere under the dread, a quiet voice he did not like was saying, <em>see, the wall is good, the wall is a gift, why would you ever take it down.</em> He climbed anyway. He left that voice on the landing below, with everything else he was carrying."}, {"p": "Behind him, one step down, Yuna climbed into the air he had just made bearable. She was shaking, and she did not stop. She did not need to be unafraid. For the first time in eleven days, she was not afraid alone."}, {"p": "At the top, at the plant-room door, she laid her hand flat against the steel where the register was thickest. Rei felt it happen. The current found its wire again. The impersonal weather of Fear flowed through its holder once more, instead of pooling loose in the concrete. The whole stairwell let out a cold breath it had been holding."}, {"p": "The dread did not disappear from the building. It was a domain. It was supposed to be there. Fear was one of the true things the world was made of, and somebody had to carry it. But it went back up into the plant room where it lived. It stopped coming down to meet her, because she was in her chair again, and the chair did not flinch."}, {"p": "Yuna stood with her palm on the door, in her wrongly buttoned cardigan and her ruined shoes, and breathed. All the way to the bottom of one breath. The first breath in eleven days that did not feed the leak."}, {"p": "\"Oh,\" she said, very softly, to the door. \"Oh. There you are. I'm sorry. I'm here. I'm not going anywhere.\""}, {"p": "Then Rei's knees gave. He had not known they were going to. He sat down hard on the top step, the dread still ringing in him like a struck bell, his hands shaking now that they were finally allowed to."}, {"p": "Yuna crouched beside him. She did not ask if he was all right. She laid two fingers lightly on the inside of his wrist, the way a nurse takes a pulse, and looked him in the eye."}, {"p": "\"Breathe out longer than you breathe in,\" she said. Her voice had changed. It was the voice she must use in car parks and hospital lifts. Calm, exact, carrying. \"Again. Feel the step under you. Name three things you can see.\""}, {"p": "\"The door. Your hand. The lanterns.\""}, {"p": "Something moved under her fingers and up his arm. It was warm and very precise, like a hand turning a dial he had not known he had. His heart slowed. His breathing came back under his own control. The ringing faded to a hum."}, {"p": "She took her fingers away. \"Twenty seconds,\" she said, with a ghost of professional pride. \"I'm out of practice.\""}, {"p": "\"You could have done that for yourself,\" Rei said."}, {"p": "\"No.\" She stood and looked at the plant-room door. \"That's the thing nobody tells you about the steadiest people. We can't steady ourselves. We were never built to. We were built to need someone on the stairs.\" She looked down at him. \"Twelve years I've been mapping what fear really is. It isn't one thing. Some of it attacks. Some of it defends. Some of it is just a very old alarm, ringing because it's been ringing since before there was a city here. The CVD took my list and put it in their manuals. They never took that part.\" She almost smiled. \"This one's an alarm. It was always an alarm. I forgot.\""}, {"break": "The man with the flask"}, {"p": "He arrived while they were still at the top."}, {"p": "Rei heard him before he saw him. The unhurried scuff of careful shoes on concrete. The small, homely clink of a flask against a cup. A man was climbing into a stairwell that should have been terrifying, as calmly as if he were coming up to water the plants."}, {"p": "He was ordinary in the way the dangerous ones are ordinary. Middle-aged. Soft-faced. A cardigan of his own, kind eyes, and a residents' association lanyard he had no right to wear. He got two landings up before he noticed that the stairwell was wrong for him tonight. The fear had gone home. The holder was in her chair. And somebody was standing at the top who was not afraid, watching him climb."}, {"p": "He stopped. He looked up at the two of them with an expression of such gentle, real-seeming concern that for one full second Rei understood exactly how he had got into so many rooms."}, {"p": "\"Yuna-san,\" he said warmly. \"You're up so high tonight. I was worried. You shouldn't push yourself.\""}, {"p": "Rei watched her hear it. The worry. The <em>push yourself</em>. The soft little weight laid on the fear to draw it back up. And he watched it fail. She was in the seat now, and the seat did not flinch. A flicker of disappointment crossed the man's kind face before he smoothed it away. It told Rei everything about what he had really come for."}, {"p": "He did not fight. His kind never did. Fighting was for registers, and he was not a register. He was a method. He simply took in that tonight's session was not going to happen. He changed his plans with the smooth speed of a man who has many other appointments, and he began, unhurried, to go back down."}, {"p": "\"I'm glad you have a friend,\" he said over his shoulder, mild as milk. The worst part was that he sounded as if he meant it. Somewhere inside him, something really did feel this as kindness, even while it emptied the chairs that held the sky up. \"Everyone should have someone to be brave for them. It's so much harder alone.\""}, {"p": "He was telling Rei that he knew what Rei had done, and how. And he was making a note of it, the way the soft-voiced ones made a note of everything. The fourth seat could be filled again with the help of someone who had a wall where a heart should be. The shaped thing that had walked up these stairs tonight was exactly the kind of tool their work could use. Rei had come to save a holder. Without meaning to, he had also shown the other side a new tool. They had been taking notes the whole time he climbed."}, {"p": "Rei made sure the man was really gone. He stood at the rail and watched the soft shoulders cross the lit courtyard and pass out under the Obon lanterns, until the dark took him. Then Yuna came and stood beside Rei at the top of the stairwell she could not have climbed an hour before."}, {"p": "The orange light from the south side threw long warm rectangles across the courtyard. The paper lanterns burned low for the dead finding their way home. And on the north side, for the first time in eleven days, the fear was only weather in a plant room, and a woman was holding it."}, {"p": "\"He'll go to the next one,\" she said quietly. \"Whoever's weakest. There's always a weakest.\""}, {"p": "She was not wrong. Somewhere across the thin ward there was a fifth seat, a ninth, an eleventh. Each one was being visited by someone with a flask and a kind face and endless patience, and Rei could not be on every stairwell. But he had been on this one."}, {"p": "\"Thank you for being afraid up here,\" Yuna said."}, {"p": "It was the strangest thanks he had ever been given, and the truest. What she meant was <em>thank you for not making me do it alone</em>."}, {"p": "On the way down, Rei stopped on the half-landing where she had sat for eleven days. On the step, tucked against the wall where Rei had sat, there was a paper cup with a little tea still in it. It was cold. It had been there before Rei arrived. The man had already come up these stairs once tonight, early. He had left his cup where Rei would sit, and gone down again to wait. He had not been surprised to find somebody at the top. He had come back to see who it was. Rei put the cup in his pocket and walked out under the lanterns with the back of his neck prickling, and he did not look round."}], "n": 54}, {"title": "The Hunger With No Address", "kind": "side", "src": "s_hunger", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei found the ninth seat the way sailors find weather. He looked for the place where the city bent around it."}, {"p": "Higuchi's note gave no address, because there was no address to give. Gin's file said the only safe way to reach the Hunger holder was through the Grid's emergency network, and that night Rei had nobody in the Grid to ask. So he used what Higuchi had given him instead. Not an address but a habit. A route that was never the same route twice. And one line in Higuchi's cramped handwriting."}, {"p": "<em>He eats so he does not have to stop, and he moves so the appetite cannot find the door.</em>"}, {"img": "place_keihin_train_night.jpg", "cap": "On the train south to Ueno after dark"}, {"p": "So Rei took the Keihin-Tōhoku line south to Ueno after dark. The sky-blue train was packed with people heading into the city for the evening. A group of university boys passed round a bag of crisps. A woman in a summer kimono stood by the door, holding a paper fan, her hair pinned up with a comb shaped like a goldfish. At Ueno the doors opened onto heat, noise and the smell of food."}, {"p": "It was the second week of Obon. The air around the station was thick with grilling fat, incense and the sweet burnt smell of taiyaki, little fish-shaped cakes filled with bean paste, cooked in iron moulds at street stalls. Lanterns swung over the food stalls. Under the railway the crowds moved in slow, warm currents, and the sound of trains passing overhead came down through the steel like thunder. Rei passed ramen counters that stayed open all night, and conveyor-belt sushi places still lit up at one in the morning. He was not looking for a face. He was looking for a kind of movement. Somewhere between the station and the park he began to feel it, the way he had felt the cold on the stairwell. An inward pull. A low, steady note under the noise of the festival."}, {"p": "<em>More.</em>"}, {"p": "It was not Rei's own wanting. It was a wanting that belonged to nothing in particular. It never became a wish for food or drink or company. It was simply appetite with the object rubbed off, reaching into Rei and feeling around in his own emptiness for something to hold."}, {"p": "The man was at a standing counter eating gyoza, little fried dumplings, and he had already half turned to leave before the plate was finished."}, {"img": "thirteen_IX_inoue_satoru.jpg", "cap": "Inoue Satoru, holder of Hunger"}, {"p": "He was in his late thirties and looked older. Tall and thin, all long arms and legs, his hair grown out and not recently cut. He was neat, but it was a neatness that had gone threadbare at the cuffs. He wore a jacket with too many pockets and carried a bag he could pick up in one movement. He moved like a man who owned only what he could carry, and who had learned to eat fast and walk faster."}, {"p": "He did not sit. Rei would come to understand that he had not properly sat down in three years. The stools and benches and chairs of the city were all, in a small way, his enemies. The one piece of furniture he truly feared was a bed."}, {"p": "He noticed Rei the moment the appetite in the air touched Rei and Rei touched it back. He felt his own register find a foothold in a stranger. His eyes went to Rei with the tired, practised caution of a man who has been hunted by things far quieter than this boy."}, {"p": "\"You feel it,\" he said around the last dumpling, already paying, already moving. \"Then walk while you talk. If I stand here much longer it learns this counter. I like this counter. I'd rather not have to give it up.\""}, {"p": "So they walked."}, {"p": "They passed a conveyor-belt sushi bar, where little plates went round and round on a belt under a lit counter. A young salaryman sat at the end of it, his tie loosened, a tower of empty plates by his elbow. He was reaching for another plate before he had finished chewing. His face had the slack, faraway look of a man who is not eating because he is hungry. He was eating because he could not think what else to do with his hands."}, {"p": "As Satoru walked past the window, the man's hand stopped halfway to the belt. He blinked. He looked at the plates beside him as if he were seeing them for the first time, and set his chopsticks down."}, {"p": "\"That's it,\" Satoru said, without looking back. \"That's the whole job. The appetite gets into a man and he doesn't know why he's eating. I walk past and take the empty off him, and he goes home and thinks he's had enough. Fifty of those a night. A hundred, in a bad week.\" He did not slow down. \"If I'd stopped at the counter beside him, I'd have given it to him instead. The empty goes where the holder is. That's why the holder can't be anywhere for long.\""}, {"p": "He set a pace that was not quite a hurry and never once slowed. He wove through the festival crowds, past families coming back from the temple with their lanterns, past the long warm tables of the night markets. As he moved, the wanting moved with him, half a step behind. It was like a thing on a leash, and he stayed just ahead of it by never, ever stopping."}, {"p": "\"Inoue Satoru,\" he said, once they were clear of the counter and the appetite had let go of it. \"Ninth of the Thirteen. I carry Hunger.\""}, {"p": "He said it the way another man might say he had a bad heart. A fact about the body. Heavy, permanent, not up for discussion."}, {"p": "\"And before you ask the thing everyone asks. No. I cannot just put it down for one night and sleep. That is exactly the thing I cannot do. That is the whole job.\""}, {"break": "Under the arches"}, {"p": "He explained it as they walked from Ueno toward Okachimachi, the next station down the line. On this stretch the railway runs high on brick arches, and the spaces under the arches are packed with tiny shops and bars. The late izakaya were still going, the small cheap pubs where people sit close together over beer and grilled skewers. Smoke from the grills drifted out under the red lanterns, and men in shirtsleeves laughed too loudly at plastic tables on the pavement. Satoru explained it walking, always walking, because explaining it standing still would have shown the problem."}, {"p": "\"A holder doesn't satisfy the register,\" he said. \"You can't. Hunger isn't a wanting that gets a thing and stops. It's the wanting underneath all the wantings. The empty that's there before there's anything to fill it. My job isn't to feed it. My job is to keep it from finding out where it lives.\""}, {"p": "His jacket pockets bulged. Rei saw what was in them when Satoru reached into one. Wrapped sweets, rice crackers, a paper twist of roasted chestnuts. He ate the way other men smoke, without noticing, to keep his hands and mouth busy so the rest of him would not slow down."}, {"p": "\"Don't look at me like that,\" Satoru said. \"I'm not hungry. I'm never hungry. It isn't my hunger.\" He offered Rei a chestnut. Rei took it. It was still warm, and it tasted of nothing at all, as if the empty walking beside them had eaten the taste out of it first."}, {"p": "He bought a skewer of grilled chicken from a stall without breaking stride, ate it in four bites and dropped the stick into a bin he had clearly used before."}, {"p": "\"Every time I stop somewhere long enough, it learns the place. It writes the address down. And a Hunger seat with a fixed address starts pulling the empty toward it. Out of the people nearby. Out of the food, which stops being enough. Out of the holder, until the holder is the hungriest thing in the room and the room is starting to come apart. So I move. Three years now. The appetite doesn't get a door because I don't have one.\""}, {"p": "At the next crossing the lights turned red against them. Rei stopped at the kerb. Satoru did not. One moment he was beside Rei. The next he was on the far pavement, thirty metres away, waiting, with no gap in between where he had walked. Rei crossed on the green, and Satoru fell in beside him as if nothing had happened."}, {"p": "\"Teleportation,\" Satoru said. \"Short hops. The seat needs fast hands. Hunger doesn't flare in one place, it flares in fifty little places at once all over the city, and somebody has to be at each of them before it settles.\" He rubbed his eyes. \"I try not to do it in front of people. It feels like cheating at walking.\""}, {"p": "\"Gin's file said you were an emergency succession.\""}, {"p": "\"The man before me died. Three years ago. The seat went empty, and they found me and put me in it in months. It's meant to take years. Years of preparation, years of being taught by the one before you.\" He shrugged. \"I know where the gaps are. I'm managing them. The Axis knows too.\""}, {"p": "Walking that warm midnight beside a man who could not stop, Rei understood the plain, unglamorous truth of a seat. It was not a throne. It was a sentence. It was not power. It was a load a man built his whole life around, so that he would never drop it."}, {"p": "Satoru's tiredness had become part of his body. He slept in ninety-minute pieces on moving trains. He had not tasted a meal in years, because eating was upkeep and never pleasure. He had given up every fixed thing a life is made from. A home. A chair he liked. A person who knew which station to find him at. All of it, so that the empty he carried would never learn an address it could pull the world into."}, {"p": "\"People think the hard part is the hunger,\" he said. \"It isn't. The hard part is that I'd give anything to stop. Anything. And the one thing the register knows about me, the only handle it's got, is how badly I want to sit down.\""}, {"p": "And there it was. The crack. The same shape Rei had seen in two other failing seats. The human want that the impersonal register got its fingers into. The Fear holder had been afraid of failing. This one was starving for rest."}, {"p": "The domain was weather. It was nobody's fault. It was one of the true empties the world was built on, and somebody had to carry it. But the longing to set it down was his own. It was exactly the gap the appetite reached for. And he could not stop feeling it, because he was a tired man not yet forty who looked fifty, and he had not slept in a bed for longer than he could easily remember."}, {"p": "He glanced sideways at Rei as they walked, reading the pressed-down registers, the searching, the shaped thing. The others had looked at Rei with caution or with hope. Satoru looked at him with something worse. He recognised him."}, {"p": "\"You've got an empty too,\" he said quietly. \"Walled up. I can feel the door from here. How do you stand it not eating you?\""}], "n": 55}, {"title": "The Room Near Nippori", "kind": "side", "src": "s_hunger#2", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei asked him the question he asked all of them now. He had stopped believing in coincidence and started believing in a method."}, {"p": "\"Has anyone been kind to you lately? Soft-voiced. Turned up around the time it got harder. Offered you something that felt like exactly what you needed, and left you a little worse than before.\""}, {"p": "Satoru stopped."}, {"p": "He really stopped, for the first time. One full second of stillness, and Rei could see what it cost him. The appetite lurched forward against its leash, and he made himself move again."}, {"p": "\"There's a woman,\" he said. \"Kitagawa-san. Runs a little guesthouse near Nippori. The old wooden kind. She found me. I don't know how. I don't have an address for anyone to find. She said she had a room with my name on it. No charge. A real bed. She said a man my age shouldn't have to sleep standing up. She was so kind about it I nearly cried, and I haven't done that in years.\""}, {"p": "He swallowed."}, {"p": "\"I've been three times. I never stay. But each time it's harder to leave. And each time, for about a week after, the appetite is worse. I thought that was just wanting the bed I couldn't have. The contrast.\""}, {"p": "It was not the contrast. Rei knew this handwriting now. It was the patient kindness that hollowed out the chairs holding up the sky."}, {"p": "Someone had found the one thing a Hunger holder could not afford to want. A place to stop. They had wrapped it in a soft voice, a real bed and a room with his name on it. Every time he came, they let him taste the rest he had refused himself for three years. And every time, they let the appetite taste the address he had spent three years refusing to give it."}, {"p": "They were not trying to feed him. They were teaching the empty where its holder would live if he ever allowed himself to. They were drawing it toward one fixed door, one kind night at a time. In the end the seat would find an address, and the ninth domain would settle and start to pull. It would happen right beside a membrane that was already thin, in the one week of the year when the doors between here and the other side were propped open for the dead."}, {"p": "\"I know what they're after,\" Satoru said, when Rei told him. He did not sound surprised. He sounded like a man hearing a diagnosis he had been avoiding. \"We all feel it in the network. Somebody organised has been leaning on the seats for years. Not loose things. A campaign. It's coordinated from the very top of the scale, the Class S things that have their own institutions on the far side. And mine is the seat they want most.\" He walked faster. \"They've been testing. Twice at the Shibuya crossing, two test events the CVD wrote up as crowd incidents. They keep two contact points in the underground mall at the Ikebukuro west exit. They don't want to take the thirteenth. They want it never to be filled, ever. A vacant thirteenth means the Axis is stretched across two jobs, and a stretched Axis makes mistakes. They've been living off its mistakes for three years. If they get mine as well...\""}, {"p": "\"Two empty seats,\" Rei said."}, {"p": "\"Two empty seats, and the Axis tries to hold mine too and can't, because it's already holding Duty. Then it all starts sliding.\" He nodded north, toward Kita. \"When Hunger goes, it won't go quietly. It'll go to the old works at Kita-Ōji first. The pre-war factories. There are suffering beings in those buildings nobody ever cleared. Hunger is what wakes them. That's where the first crossings would be.\""}, {"p": "\"She asked me to come tonight,\" he said, and the appetite spiked as he said it, the way the fear had spiked when the stairwell holder named her visitor. \"She said the room's made up. She said I looked so tired last time. She's not... I don't think she's pretending. That's the thing. I think she really does want to give a tired man a place to sleep. That's what makes it impossible to say no to.\""}, {"p": "He was walking faster now, as if he could outrun the offer itself."}, {"p": "\"And I want to go. God help me, I want to go and lie down on a real bed and sleep eight hours and let it learn the address and let the whole thing settle and be someone else's problem. Because I am so tired, and three years is so long, and a bed is such a small thing to want.\""}, {"p": "He looked at Rei."}, {"p": "\"So before I walk myself to Nippori like I do every time, tell me what you are. Because I think you might be the only person who could make it so I don't have to.\""}, {"break": "Carried"}, {"p": "What Rei did was not the bed, and it was not the road."}, {"p": "He had walked enough of the night beside Satoru to see the whole trap. Satoru could not rest, because resting meant stopping. Stopping meant an address. And an address meant the seat would settle and pull. The guesthouse offered him stillness and poisoned it. The street offered him safety and never let him stop. They were the same sentence wearing two faces."}, {"p": "So Rei gave him the third thing, the one nobody had offered him in three years. He carried the movement for him."}, {"img": "ch2_class.jpg", "cap": "The whole city moving through the Obon night"}, {"p": "It was Obon, and the whole city was on the move. Families were coming home from visiting the graves. Processions with lanterns wound through the streets. A slow river of people was heading down to the water for the tōrō nagashi, with paper lanterns to set on the current. Each one had a name written on its side."}, {"p": "Rei took Satoru down to the river, into the moving crowd. He walked on the outside, between Satoru and every place he might stop. He set the pace, so that Satoru did not have to. And for the length of the procession, inside the movement of ten thousand people walking the dead home, a man who had not sat down in three years closed his eyes and let himself be carried."}, {"p": "He did not sleep, exactly. But he rested, in a way he had forgotten was possible. He was moving without having to drive the movement himself, held inside a current bigger than his own exhaustion. The appetite found no door, because the whole street was moving, and there was no still place for it to write down."}, {"p": "Rei walked and kept walking. He took the empty into his own walled chest, where it could not reach all the way in. He stayed half a step ahead of Satoru, the way he had climbed half a step ahead of the fear. He was being hungry in the right place, on purpose, so that for one hour the man could run clean."}, {"p": "It was the worst kind of usefulness. Rei felt the old quiet voice start up under the noise of the festival. <em>See, the wall is good, the empty can't eat you, why would you ever take it down.</em> He kept that voice at the edge of the crowd with the rest of what he carried. He walked Satoru down to the water with his eyes closed and his face, for the first time, not braced against anything."}, {"p": "At the river's edge, lanterns were going out onto the black water by the hundred. Small boxes of orange light, released downstream toward the dead."}, {"p": "Satoru opened his eyes and watched them go."}, {"p": "\"They don't stop either,\" he said softly. \"That's the trick of it. They're not at rest. They're at peace because they're still moving.\""}, {"p": "Something in him had eased that no bed could have eased. Not the hunger. That was weather and would always be weather. It was the three-year cramp of a man who had believed his only choices were a chair that would kill him or a road that never ended."}, {"p": "Rei had not cured the ninth seat. He had shown its holder a way to be still inside movement. That was the one thing the soft-voiced woman's bed could never give him. Her stillness came with an address. Rei's came with a current."}, {"p": "\"I didn't know it could be like this,\" Satoru said, watching the lights float away. \"I thought stopping was the only way to rest. Nobody ever told me I could rest by being carried.\""}, {"img": "place_kitagawa_guesthouse.jpg", "cap": "The old wooden guesthouse near Nippori, its screens lit gold"}, {"p": "They walked back up toward Nippori through the old streets, where the houses were low and wooden and the temples stood close together behind their walls. The crowds thinned. A cat watched them from the top of a wall. Kitagawa-san was waiting near the bottom of the guesthouse lane when they arrived."}, {"img": "char_kitagawa.jpg", "cap": "Kitagawa-san at the bottom of the guesthouse lane"}, {"p": "She was exactly as Satoru had described her. A small, kind woman in late middle age, wearing an apron over a summer yukata. Behind her the guesthouse glowed warm, its paper screens lit gold. Rei did not doubt there was a real bed made up inside, with Satoru's name beside it."}, {"p": "She saw that he was not alone. And she saw, the way the soft-voiced ones always saw, that something tonight had not gone the way the method needed."}, {"p": "\"Inoue-san,\" she said. Her voice was truly glad and truly worried, and the worry did its soft work of reaching for the want. \"You look so tired. The room's ready. It's always ready. You don't have to keep doing this to yourself.\""}, {"p": "Rei watched him hear it. The rest. The room. The small mercy that was also a door the appetite would love to learn. And he watched it fail to land the way it had landed three times before. For one hour tonight, Satoru had learned that resting and stopping were not the same thing. A man who has felt that difference cannot quite unfeel it."}, {"p": "She did not push. Her kind never pushed. Pushing would show the method, and the method only worked while it looked like nothing but kindness. She simply took in that tonight's bed would go unslept in. She adjusted with that same smooth ease, and folded her hands."}, {"p": "\"The room will keep,\" she said, mild and warm, to him and past him to Rei. \"It's no trouble. Everyone needs somewhere to stop, in the end. Even him.\""}, {"p": "The <em>even him</em> was aimed at Rei. It was small and precise. A note being filed. The ninth seat could be carried through movement by someone with a wall instead of an address. The shaped thing that had walked the river tonight was exactly the kind of tool their work could use. Without meaning to, Rei had shown the other side a tool while he thought he was only saving a man. He had come to keep a holder away from her door. He had also taught the people who were thinning the wall a new way to think about doors. And about him."}, {"p": "When she had gone back inside and the gold light of the screens had closed behind her, Satoru stood in the lane. The festival murmured its way down toward the river. Beyond the rooftops, lanterns were still floating out on the black water."}, {"p": "He was not fixed. He would be moving again before dawn, because the seat was a sentence and Rei had not shortened it. But he stood in the lane like a man who had remembered that there is a difference between a road and a current."}, {"p": "\"She'll find another one,\" he said. \"Somebody who hasn't learned the river yet. There's always somebody more tired than they can stand to be.\""}, {"p": "He was not wrong. Somewhere across the thin ward there was a seat for every want, and a soft voice with a bed or a meal or a kindness shaped exactly to that want. Rei could not walk every river. But he had walked this one."}, {"p": "\"Thank you for carrying it,\" Satoru said."}, {"p": "It was the oddest thanks and the truest. What he meant was <em>thank you for showing me I don't only get to choose between starving and stopping</em>."}, {"p": "Then he was gone, between one step and the next, the way he had crossed the road."}, {"p": "Rei walked home alone along the river. Despair, Rage, Fear, and now Hunger. Four seats, he thought, and every one of them visited by someone soft, and all of it in the same two weeks. A campaign that had been patient for years had suddenly begun to hurry. He wondered what it knew about the calendar that he did not."}], "n": 56}, {"title": "The Longing at Shin-Ōkubo", "kind": "side", "src": "s_longing", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei found the eleventh seat the way the bookseller's note told him to. Not by an address. By a hunger that was not his own, in a street that had spent thirty years learning to smell like home for people who had left theirs behind."}, {"p": "He went there late. He took the Saikyō line from Jūjō to Ikebukuro and changed to the Yamanote line. The train was full of people going out for the night. Two girls in matching sandals shared one pair of earphones, a wire running from one ear to the other, and nodded to the same song. A man in a crumpled shirt slept standing up, holding the strap, and woke at every station just long enough to read the sign. Three stops later the doors opened at Shin-Ōkubo. It is a small station with a narrow platform, and the crowd squeezed down the stairs and out through the ticket gates in a long beeping line of Suica cards."}, {"img": "place_toyoko.jpg", "cap": "Shin-Ōkubo at midnight"}, {"p": "Shin-Ōkubo is Tokyo's Koreatown. At midnight, in the second week of Obon, its main street was a long bright corridor of Korean signs and Korean cooking. Shops sold face masks and pop-star posters. The air was thick with grilling pork, fermented chilli and the sugary smoke of street toast. Under all of it, threading through the festival crowd the way the cold had threaded the stairwell, ran a low, steady pull. Rei recognised it at once now. It was not the pull of <em>more</em>, like the Hunger seat. It was the pull of <em>back</em>. A leaning toward something behind him. A reaching for a place that was always one country and several years away."}, {"p": "If he let it, the pull turned into a picture. A particular kitchen. A particular voice calling him in to eat. A particular evening light. He was fairly sure he had never lived that evening. He was homesick for it anyway, with his whole chest."}, {"p": "The note had named the person and the room. Park Junho, eleventh of the Thirteen. A shrine room behind a restaurant called Eomma's. Rei read the sign from across the street and understood that the name was not an accident. <em>Eomma</em> is the Korean word for mother."}, {"p": "The restaurant itself was full and loud and ordinary. Families. Drunk students. A grandmother working the grill with both hands, her sleeves pinned back. At a corner table a young man in a good suit sat alone over a bowl of instant ramyeon, the Korean instant noodles that were the cheapest thing on the menu. He ate it slowly, the way people eat when they need to feel real. Between mouthfuls he glanced at his reflection in the black window. Once he moved his lips at it, as if the glass had spoken first."}, {"p": "Rei felt it as he passed. Something thin and fond in the glass, very faint, fading the way a voice fades down a corridor. A dead man giving advice to a living one. He noted it, the way he noted everything that month, and kept walking."}, {"p": "There was a second door at the back, past the toilets. It was an old door of paper and wood that did not match the bright tiles of the rest of the place. The <em>back</em> was loudest there. It gathered and waited, the way water gathers at the lowest point of a floor. Through the gap Rei could see candlelight and the dull shine of a brass bowl. A man knelt in front of a small altar with his back to the door. He was holding very still, and the stillness cost him. It was the stillness of someone bracing a great weight so that it would not slide."}, {"p": "He spoke without turning round. His Japanese was over-careful. It was the Japanese of a man who had learned it as an adult and had never stopped feeling like a guest in it."}, {"p": "\"You feel it from the street,\" he said. \"Then you're not here to eat. Come in. But understand the room first.\""}, {"p": "Rei slid the door open and stepped through. The pull thickened around him like warm water. He felt it test his own walled-off places, the way every register had tested them. It found the homesickness he kept packed away with everything else. The want for a kitchen and a voice and an evening, all of it sealed off by the suppression along with the rest."}, {"p": "\"This room makes people miss what they can't have,\" Park Junho said. He was still facing the altar, his shoulders set against the load. \"That's all it does. It doesn't lie. It turns the longing all the way up and won't let it down again. And during Obon, with the dead so close, that is a very dangerous thing to do to people who came here because they were already missing someone.\""}, {"p": "He told Rei the rest while kneeling. To tell it standing, he would have had to leave the altar. The altar was where he kept the room from spilling out through the second door into a street full of the homesick."}, {"p": "\"A holder doesn't end the longing,\" Park Junho said. \"You can't. Longing isn't a want that gets the thing and stops. It's the gap itself. The distance between a person and the place they came from. That distance is real and it is permanent, and somebody has to carry it so it doesn't pull the whole street toward a door that opens the wrong way.\""}, {"p": "He looked younger than the registry made him. Rei knew from the bookseller's ledger that he was forty-one, born in Seoul, seventeen years in Tokyo. On Tuesday and Thursday evenings he taught Korean to children at the community centre two streets away. The face in the candlelight was gentle and exhausted. It was the face of a man who had left his country at twenty-four and had quietly grieved for it ever since. When the seat found him, he had turned that grief into a job."}, {"p": "\"This room is the lowest point in the neighbourhood,\" he said. \"Every homesick person who walks this street leaks a little. It all runs downhill. It pools here. I kneel in the pool so it doesn't rise high enough to drown anyone. That's the whole work. I am a drain with a face.\""}, {"break": "The mudang"}, {"p": "The paper door slid open behind them. A woman in her fifties came in without knocking, down the narrow stair from the rooms above the restaurant. A drumstick was still tucked through her belt. She set a cup of barley tea by Park Junho's knee and another by Rei's, and looked at Rei for a long moment with small black eyes that missed nothing."}, {"img": "place_mudang.jpg", "cap": "Pak Sun-ja, the mudang, at work in her shrine room"}, {"p": "\"Pak Sun-ja,\" Park Junho said, by way of introduction, without lifting his eyes from the altar. \"She lives upstairs. Her shrine is behind the curtain up there. She keeps my secret, and she let me have this room when I needed a low place.\""}, {"p": "Rei had seen the name in Gin's book, on the page about the places people go when the CVD would only make things worse. <em>The mudang in Shin-Ōkubo. She negotiates rather than banishes. Best for grief-anchored spirits.</em> Gin's note meant that she talked to the dead instead of throwing them out."}, {"p": "\"The dead are not gone, only unquiet,\" Pak Sun-ja said. Her Japanese was rough and fast. \"I can ask them. Sometimes they answer. Grandmothers who followed their grandsons here. Husbands who don't know they're finished. I ask them what they want and I tell them where to go. Most of them listen.\" She jerked her chin at the kneeling man. \"This one doesn't need asking. This one needs someone to sit in his place for an hour. I can't do it. The room knows my drum. You, it doesn't know yet.\""}, {"p": "She went out again. The door slid shut on the noise of the front room."}, {"p": "Rei knelt in the candlelight with the dull brass bowl between them, and he understood the plain, unglamorous truth of the eleventh seat. It was not a power. It was a posture. A man held the lowest place so that the flood had somewhere to go that was not a person. He did not go home. He could not. It was not a matter of money or papers. The seat was tied to the gap. If he closed the gap, if he really went back to the kitchen and the voice and the evening light, the lowest point would fill up, and the pool would rise up the street."}, {"p": "\"People think the hard part is being far from home,\" Park Junho said. \"It isn't. The hard part is that I could go. There's a flight. There's a city. There's a house with my mother still in it, getting older while I kneel here being the reason a hundred strangers don't drown in missing their own. The room knows that. The only handle the register has on me is exactly how much I want to go home.\""}, {"p": "And there it was. The crack. Rei had seen the same shape in the fourth seat and the ninth. It was the human want that the impersonal register gets its fingers into. The Fear holder had been afraid of failing. The Hunger holder had been starving for rest. This one was homesick to the bone, and he held the very seat that punished him for it. The domain was distance. It was nobody's fault. It was one of the true gaps the world is built from. But the longing to go home was his own, and it was new again every single day. It was exactly the want the room reached for. He could not stop feeling it, because he was a son whose mother was growing old in a house he still kept the lights on in, inside his head."}, {"p": "Park Junho turned his head and looked at Rei across the bowl. He read the pressed-down registers, the searching, the sealed homesickness. The others had looked at Rei with hunger or with hope. This one looked at him with the flat recognition of a man meeting his own condition in a stranger."}, {"p": "\"You've got a home you can't get to either,\" he said quietly. \"Walled off. I can feel it leaking from here. How do you kneel in this room and not go under?\""}], "n": 57}, {"title": "Aga", "kind": "side", "src": "s_longing#2", "blocks": [{"p": "Rei asked him the question he asked all of them now. He had stopped believing in coincidence and had learned to read the handwriting."}, {"p": "\"Has anyone been kind to you lately? Soft-voiced. Turned up around the time the kneeling got harder. Offered you the one thing you'd given up letting yourself want?\""}, {"p": "Park Junho went still in a new way. It was not the braced stillness of the seat. It was the stillness of a man touching a bruise."}, {"p": "\"There's a woman,\" he said. \"She comes to the restaurant. Sits in the front. Always orders the things my mother used to make. Han-ssi, she says her name is. Three weeks ago she found her way back to this room. Nobody finds their way back to this room. Even Sun-ja has to knock. She knelt beside me and she didn't leak homesickness at all, which should have told me something. She put her hand on my shoulder and she called me <em>aga</em>.\""}, {"p": "His voice did something complicated on the word."}, {"p": "\"It means baby. It's what a mother calls a small child. My mother hasn't called me that since I was six, and I have not heard it in this country in fifteen years, not since the last time I went home. She said it like she had every right to. And I knelt here and I cried, which I have not done since the seat found me.\""}, {"p": "It was not kindness. Rei had read the ledger. He knew this handwriting now. It was the patient mercy that hollowed out the seats holding up the sky. Someone had found the one thing a Longing holder could not afford to be given. The feeling of having arrived. Of being home. A mother's word in a mother's language. They had wrapped it in a soft voice, a familiar dish and a pet name no stranger should know to use."}, {"p": "Every time she came, she gave him a few minutes of the homecoming he had refused himself for fifteen years. And every time, the room tasted what it would be like for the gap to close, the gap it existed to keep open. She was not trying to comfort him. She was teaching the longing what arriving would feel like. She was raising the pool, one kind homecoming at a time. In the end the gap would close, the lowest point would fill, and the eleventh domain would spill up a street packed with the homesick. It would happen right beside a membrane that was already thin, during the festival, when the doors between here and the far shore are propped open for the dead to come home."}, {"p": "The thirteenth seat was empty. Their work needed the wall to lean. And this cruelty wore the face of a woman who called a lonely man <em>baby</em>."}, {"p": "\"She's coming tonight,\" Park Junho said. The pull in the room surged as he said it. The candle flames leaned. \"She said she'd bring me <em>tteokguk</em>. The soup you eat at New Year. The one that means you've grown a year older, that you've come through. She said I'd done so well. I'd been so far from home for so long. Didn't I think it was time I let myself come back.\""}, {"p": "He was gripping the edge of the altar now. His knuckles were pale."}, {"p": "\"And I want to. God help me. I want to eat my mother's soup from a stranger's hands and let the gap close and let the pool rise and let it be someone else's turn to kneel in the cold. Fifteen years is so long. A bowl of soup is such a small thing to want. And she says it like coming home is allowed.\""}, {"p": "He looked at Rei."}, {"p": "\"So before I let her in, tell me what you are. Because I think you might be the only one who could kneel here in my place long enough for me to want this and not do it.\""}, {"break": "The lowest place"}, {"p": "The answer was not the soup. It was not the flight home. Rei had knelt in the pool long enough to see the whole trap. Park Junho could not go home, because going home would close the gap and raise the flood. He could not stay, because staying meant kneeling forever in a cold that was draining him faster than he could drain it. The woman offered him homecoming and poisoned it. The room offered him purpose and never let him arrive. It was the same sentence wearing two faces."}, {"p": "So Rei gave him the third thing, the one nobody had offered him in fifteen years. He took the lowest place for one hour, so that Park Junho could stand up."}, {"p": "\"Move over,\" Rei said. \"Show me where the floor is.\""}, {"p": "It was Obon, and all over the city the dead were coming home. Past the second door the street was a river of the living, missing the living and the gone. Rei knelt where Park Junho had knelt, in the pool at the bottom of all that leaking. He let the homesickness of a hundred strangers run downhill into his walled chest, where it could not reach all the way to the kitchen and the voice he kept sealed away. He was homesick in the right place, on purpose, so that the drain ran clean while its keeper stood."}, {"p": "He did not arrive anywhere. But he held the lowest point, the way he had climbed half a step ahead of the fear and walked half a step ahead of the appetite. He took the gap into the place in him that the suppression had sealed. Park Junho stood up from the altar for the first time in longer than he could bear to count. Rei did not go under. The pool did not rise. The lowest place was taken."}, {"p": "The old quiet voice started up under the noise of the festival. <em>See. The wall is good. The home can't reach you. Why would you ever take it down.</em> Rei left it at the second door with the rest of what he carried. He knelt in the cold of every stranger's missing while a man who had been a drain with a face remembered that he had legs."}, {"p": "Park Junho stood in the candlelight, rubbing his knees. He looked at Rei with an expression Rei had seen twice before that month and had come to dread. Recognition. Gratitude. And the terrible beginning of an idea about what Rei was for."}, {"p": "\"I forgot you could be in the room without being the floor of it,\" he said, very quietly, watching Rei kneel where he had knelt."}, {"p": "Out past the door someone in the restaurant had started singing an old song in Korean, badly and lovingly. Pak Sun-ja's rough voice joined in from the stair. Half the front room took it up. A hundred homesick people were turning the gap into music instead of a flood."}, {"p": "\"That's the trick, isn't it,\" Park Junho said, listening. \"They're all missing home and none of them are drowning, because they're missing it together, out loud, instead of alone in the lowest room.\""}, {"p": "Something in him had eased that the soup could never have eased. Not the longing. The longing was distance and would always be distance. What eased was the fifteen-year cramp of a man who thought there were only two roads open to him. A flight home that would flood the street, or a floor he would kneel on until he died. Rei had not freed the eleventh seat. He had shown its holder that the lowest place could be shared, that a gap could be carried with someone else beside him. That was the one thing the soft-voiced woman's homecoming could never give him. Hers closed the gap. Rei only knelt beside it."}, {"break": "Han-ssi"}, {"p": "Han-ssi came while Park Junho was still standing. In Korean, <em>ssi</em> is a polite word added after a name, a little like Mr. or Ms. She was exactly as he had described her. A warm, ordinary woman in late middle age, holding a covered bowl in a cloth against its heat. The steam of the <em>tteokguk</em> reached the room before she did. It carried the smell of New Year, of growing older and of coming through."}, {"p": "She saw that he was on his feet and not on his knees. She saw, the way the soft-voiced ones always saw, that someone was in the lowest place who should not have been able to be there."}, {"p": "\"Junho-ya,\" she said. The little ending on his name landed soft and exact, a mother's name for a grown son, and the worry in it did its gentle work of reaching for the want. \"You're standing. You must be so tired of kneeling, aga. I brought you the soup. Sit down and eat it while it's hot, and let yourself be home. You've done enough. You've been far enough.\""}, {"p": "Rei watched him hear it. The soup. The name. The small mercy that was also a door the flood would love to learn. And Rei watched it fail to land the way it had landed before. For one hour that night, Park Junho had stood in a room he thought he could only kneel in. A man who has felt that difference cannot quite unfeel it."}, {"p": "She did not push. Her kind never pushed. A push would show the method, and the method only worked as long as it looked like nothing more than a mother's love for a son who had come a long way. She simply took in that tonight's homecoming would go uneaten. She adjusted with that same smooth ease. She set the bowl down on the edge of the altar, where its steam thinned and faded."}, {"p": "\"The soup keeps,\" she said, mild and warm, to him and past him to Rei kneeling in the pool. \"It always keeps. Everyone comes home in the end. Even him.\""}, {"p": "The <em>even him</em> was aimed at Rei. It was small and precise, a note being filed. The eleventh seat could be drained by someone with a wall instead of a country. The shaped thing kneeling in the lowest place was exactly the tool their work could use. Rei had come to keep a holder away from her door. He had also shown the people thinning the wall a new idea about who could hold a gap. He had shown them a stranger who could kneel in any lowest place, because the lowest place inside himself was already sealed."}, {"p": "On her way out she passed Pak Sun-ja at the foot of the stair. The mudang did not look up. She threw a pinch of coarse salt onto the grill coals behind Han-ssi's back. Salt is an old protection against spirits and bad luck. The coals spat, and the soft-voiced woman's step faltered for half a pace before she went out into the bright street."}, {"p": "When she had gone, the covered bowl cooling on the altar between them, Park Junho stood in the candlelight of the room he had knelt in for fifteen years. He listened to the singing die down in the front. He was not free. The gap was still the gap. He would kneel again before the night was out, because the seat was a sentence and Rei had not lifted it. But he was standing like a man who remembered the difference between a drain and a person."}, {"p": "\"She'll find another,\" he said. \"Someone newer to the gap. There's always somebody further from home than they can stand to be.\""}, {"p": "He was not wrong. Somewhere across the thin ward there was a seat for every want, and a soft voice with a soup or a bed or a name shaped to exactly that want. Rei could not kneel in every lowest place. But he had knelt in this one."}, {"p": "\"Thank you for taking the floor,\" Park Junho said. It was the strangest gratitude Rei had ever been given, and the truest. What he meant was that a gap is something a person can carry standing up, beside someone else, instead of drowning in it alone."}, {"p": "On the way out Rei passed the corner table. The young man in the good suit had finished his ramyeon and was looking at the window again, at his own face in the black glass. He looked tired in a way that had nothing to do with the hour. For a moment the reflection seemed older than he was, and kinder, and sad. Then a group of students pushed past, and the glass was only glass. Rei did not stop. He had no hour left to give that night. But he knew the face, the way a person knows a door he will have to come back to."}, {"p": "Outside, the street was still bright. Rei walked back to the station through the smell of grilling meat and sweet toast. He caught one of the last Yamanote trains, its carriage half empty, a couple asleep on each other's shoulders at the far end. All the way home he could still feel the pull of <em>back</em>, faint now, like a hand letting go of his sleeve."}], "n": 58}, {"title": "Why the Wall Leans North", "kind": "main", "src": "m_leans", "blocks": [{"p": "It was past one in the morning when Rei came out of Jūjō station. The shopping street by the station had pulled down its shutters, and only the konbini on the corner was still lit, its doors sighing open for a man buying cigarettes. Rei walked the short way home past the shrine. Its wooden gate was dark, and a single Obon lantern hung by the offering box, burning low. Behind the shrine, the windows of the flat were black. On the floor of the genkan lay a sheet of folded paper that someone had pushed under the door. Rei picked it up, put it on the table without opening it, and went to fetch the rest."}, {"p": "He took everything back to the kitchen. The kitchen was where all this had begun. That month he had learned that he understood a thing best in the room where it had cost him the most."}, {"p": "He laid the bookseller's ledger, Gin's book and his own rough copies across the table where the kettle had gone cold on the first morning. Then he made himself write the whole machine out as one sentence. That was how his father had taught him to find the lie in a set of accounts. \"If you cannot say it in a sentence,\" his father used to say, \"you do not yet know what you are looking at.\" Rei had been looking at this for a month without saying it."}, {"p": "So he wrote it."}, {"p": "<em>A chair emptied in 2009 has leaned on Kita-ku for six years, and a patient demon wearing my brother is helping the wall finish a fall the empty chair began, so that on the last night of Obon something can step into the open thirteenth office and call the wall's collapse a mending.</em>"}, {"p": "One sentence. He read it three times. It did not get any less true."}, {"p": "He turned to the back of Gin's book, to the map Gin had drawn of the ward. The sentence had a direction in it, and he wanted to see that direction on paper. The map was there, in Gin's small, exact printing. The flat behind Jūjō Shrine. The station four hundred metres to the southwest. And a pencilled circle about three hundred metres to the northeast of the flat, with no label at all, only a note in the margin. <em>Warmer here. I chose the flat by it without knowing what I was choosing.</em> The wall did not lean north by accident. It leaned toward whatever Gin had felt in that circle, and toward the seat that had stood empty in Kita since the year the lights went grey."}, {"p": "Under the sentence he wrote three words, one under the other. Then he sat looking at them while the kettle ticked as it cooled. <em>Fall. Gin. Me.</em>"}, {"p": "He had spent four weeks hoping the fog was hiding a fourth word. It was not. He made himself look at each word long enough to see where it led."}, {"p": "Behind the first word, the seat stayed empty and the seam finished tearing. He could see it with horrible ease, because he had walked the ward all month. The lanterns would never stop coming down the river. Watanabe would go grey at the edges and then go out, at the returns cart in Itabashi, between one book and the next. After him the others would go, one seat at a time. Obon would simply never end. The dead would keep coming home to a district that could no longer tell them apart from the living."}, {"p": "Behind the second word was Gin. Gin's worn body, already shaped to fit, already most of the way into the chair the Healer had walked it toward. The wall would mend. The twelve would stop being crushed. And Rei would carry home a grey cardigan two sizes too big and hang it on the hook beside the empty one for keys. He would visit something in the north of the city that had his brother's face and no brother in it."}, {"p": "Behind the third word was himself. Two pressed-down registers. A hand already printed with the grain of the wound, the one living person the far shore had made fit by accident when it sent him in to touch the tear. He could only picture it one way, and that way was Watanabe. A weather with a person left over. Holding up the north of the city every morning by agreeing to be crushed, until he weakened, and some stranger with a borrowed newspaper sat in a library to watch him put books back on the shelves."}, {"p": "He put the pen down. His hand was shaking, and that annoyed him, as if the hand belonged to someone else."}, {"break": "A note under the door"}, {"p": "There was a fourth word after all, and it was not his. It was in the sheet of paper from the genkan floor. It had been pushed under the door some time in the evening, while he was in Shin-Ōkubo. It was a single sheet folded twice, in handwriting he knew from the small white room at Ōji."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Folded note, unsigned, in Okada Shizuka's hand", "lines": ["Rei. I am not supposed to know this and you are not supposed to have it.", "The Office of Public Confidence has a draft on the Minister's desk. Hagiwara Mizuki calls it the Closing. Catalogue every medium in the city. Contain them. Then force the veil shut by sealing the axis at the centre of it. Permanently.", "It would trap the good, the evil and everyone in between on the wrong side, forever. The dead who want to come home. The ones who should never come back. Whatever is left of anyone who is in between. She knows that. She thinks it is the kind price.", "Kita is her proof that the veil cannot be managed. She will use whatever happens on the last night as the reason.", "Adachi cannot stand her and cannot move against her. Several of the Tower houses back her. He is the only door to the President, if anyone can move him.", "Burn this."]}}, {"p": "Rei did not burn it. He laid it flat beside Gin's book. Then he opened the book to the chapter Gin had put off writing the longest, the one about who controlled what."}, {"img": "char_director-adachi-kenshin.jpg", "cap": "Director Adachi Kenshin of the CVD"}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's book, Chapter Twenty-Three. The Political World", "lines": ["A Tuesday. Rain again. I put this one off the longest, because politics keeps better in the dark, and so, lately, do I.", "The CVD operates under the Ministry of Internal Affairs as a registered public agency. The Ministry provides budget, legal authority and political cover. The CVD provides expertise the Ministry cannot replicate and results the Ministry can present publicly. Neither discusses this explicitly. It is the kind of relationship that functions until it doesn't.", "Three positions exist on veil policy.", "Full Integration. Veil-aware governance. Practitioners get legal personhood protections. The CVD expands its public mandate and cuts classified operations.", "Current Management. Keep the 1962 Accord. The present balance between public acknowledgment and classified management is the correct one. Registered practitioners stay in the asset development pipeline. The Grid stays in a legal grey area.", "Suppression. The veil should be denied, contained and weaponised quietly. 'Practitioners are assets of the state, not citizens with rights.' It would absorb the CVD into intelligence and end the Grid. It has been losing ground since 1985 but keeps influence in specific ministry offices.", "Director Adachi built the Division from an intelligence apparatus into something closer to its stated mandate. This is real. The asset development pipeline still exists. Both things are true and the tension does not resolve.", "The political map the Division circulates puts the families in one colour, the Grid in another, and the CVD in the centre, larger than both. Every map is drawn by someone, and the someone is always in the middle of it. I keep my own version. The middle of mine is left empty on purpose.", "G.U. Keys on the hook."]}}, {"p": "Rei had to read the page twice before it came clear, so he put it into plain words for himself. The CVD was not free. It answered to a government ministry, which gave it money and legal cover in return for results. And in the offices above it, people argued about what to do with the veil. Some wanted to bring it into the open and give practitioners rights. Some wanted to keep things as they were under the old Accord of 1962. And some wanted the opposite, to deny the veil, lock it down and treat every practitioner as a tool owned by the state."}, {"p": "He read the line about the map three times, the way he had read his own sentence. <em>The middle of mine is left empty on purpose.</em> Gin had drawn the city with a hole where the power should be. Hagiwara Mizuki wanted to fill that hole with a lid."}, {"img": "ch27_politics.jpg", "cap": "The political world above the CVD"}, {"p": "He understood her better than he wanted to. She sat in a glass office in Marunouchi, above the CVD's public face, and she was the third position on Gin's page, given a budget and a kind voice. It was not cruelty. It was tidiness. The public slept soundly because someone kept the lie tidy, and she was that someone. Her lid did not save Gin. It did not save the twelve. It only made sure that whatever happened to them happened where nobody would have to look at it. Rei crossed it out before he had finished reading it. Crossing it out felt like the only easy decision of the month."}, {"p": "That left the three words, the kettle and the dark outside."}, {"break": "The design of the trap"}, {"p": "Rei sat in the cold kitchen with three words written under one sentence, and at last he saw the whole design of the trap. The trap was not any one of the words. The trap was that all three were true."}, {"p": "The Healer had not built a lie. It had built a true situation, where every honest way out cost Rei the very thing he had come to save. Then it had stood his loving brother in the middle of it, so that Rei could not walk away. Then it had waited. It had forty years behind it, and Rei had a month. An empty chair was always going to win a waiting game against a man who has to sleep."}, {"p": "He understood the registry now, completely, the way a man understands a sentence he would give his hands to unwrite. Twelve people held up the city by agreeing every morning to be crushed. One chair had stood open since the year the lights went grey. And the only people in Tokyo in a position to close it were two brothers the empty chair had spent six years quietly reaching for. One of them was already inside its reach. The other was sitting at a kitchen table at dawn, copying the wound into a notebook in his own rough handwriting."}, {"p": "Against every instinct he had left, he began to wonder whether love was the thing that would make him refuse the chair, or the thing that would put him in it."}, {"p": "Somewhere beyond the rooftops the first trains began to rumble, and a crow started up on the roof. The sky over the shrine roof turned from black to the colour of weak tea. There was one night left before the wall went double over the river, and one night after that before the festival ended. Rei pulled Gin's book back across the table and turned to the chapter he had skimmed and never read properly, the one with the drawings in it. Whatever he chose, he was going to need his brother's hands. The only place left to find them was on paper."}], "n": 59}, {"title": "From Gin's Book. The Glyphs", "kind": "gin", "src": "gin_42_glyphs", "blocks": [{"p": "Before the sky was fully light, Rei read the chapter Gin had headed <em>Symbols, Seals and Tattoos</em>. Then he read the dictionary of glyphs that came after it. He read all of it, in order, the way Gin had asked to be read. He had watched his brother draw these shapes at the desk a hundred times and had never once asked what they were. Gin had noticed that too. He had written it down."}, {"img": "scene_tattoo_stool.jpg", "cap": "Gin under the needle, Rei on the plastic stool"}, {"p": "Halfway through the preface, Rei stopped and sat very still. Gin wrote that one late night, going down his left arm, he had twice come to a mark he did not remember having tattooed. Rei remembered both. He had been there both times, sitting on a plastic stool in a back room that smelled of antiseptic and green tea, watching the needle and pretending to read. Gin had blamed the late hour and the number of marks. Reading it now, in the month when Gin's handwriting leaned, Rei did not blame anything."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's book, Chapter Twenty-Two. Preface", "lines": ["Written late, sleeves down. Rei made tea and I let it go cold, which he will mention in the morning, and be right to.", "There is an obvious joke about the man covered shoulder to fingertip in configurations sitting down to write the chapter on why the disciplined ones stay clean. I am the joke. I may as well tell it first.", "Cataloguing the body-seals for this chapter, I went down the left arm in order, the way I always have, and twice I reached a mark I did not remember sitting for. Both times Rei could tell me when and where, because he was there, because he is reliably the one who remembers the parts of my own life I misplace. I am putting it down to the number of them and the hour.", "G.U. Keys on the hook. I counted them, which is new, and did not care for the fact that counting had started to feel necessary."]}}, {"p": "The next pages explained how the shapes worked. Gin wrote them like a teacher, plainly, as if he expected someone to learn from them one day. Rei understood now that the someone was him."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "How symbols work", "lines": ["Symbols work because the knowledge behind them is real. Not faith, not belief. Specific geometric configurations interact with the membrane in documented and repeatable ways. A correctly drawn binding constrains entity movement whether or not the one who drew it believes it will work. The tool functions on precision, not intention. The veil is not mystical. It is real. We have incomplete models for it. Those are different problems.", "Strength scales with the practitioner's Symbol Work and rank. The configuration is the same. The practitioner's veil depth decides how much of it actually executes.", "Substrate. Standard ink on paper lasts hours. Specific inks on the right substrates last days. Carved configurations in the right materials last until damaged. Tattooed configurations are permanent and uncontrollable. Always active. No off switch.", "Preparation. Ten minutes for a standard configuration, with materials. Anything rushed under three minutes is unreliable. I have never drawn one in a hurry that held the way it should.", "Resistance. A standard binding holds a Class E demon indefinitely. Against Class B, contested every scene. Against Class A, contested every round of active pressure. No standard configuration permanently holds Class S. It buys time.", "Noise. Quiet preparation makes none. Activation on entity contact makes a little. A rushed configuration pushed with active ability makes the most. This is why experienced people prepare before the situation asks."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the page slowly. It was simpler than it looked. A symbol was not a prayer. It was a shape, and the shape did the work, the way a key opens a lock whether or not the person turning it believes in keys. A practitioner's skill at drawing these shapes was called Symbol Work, and it was measured in levels. Ink on paper faded in hours. A tattoo never faded and never switched off. And the stronger the thing on the other side, the less time any shape could hold it."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Tattoos. The Grid standard, and why the CVD stays clean", "lines": ["You are not covered in a weekend. You are covered over years, one configuration at a time, each tested before the next goes under. I drew every line first in ink that washes off and wore it for a week. The hands come last. Done right, you read clean and you are anything but.", "The CVD says it keeps practitioners unmarked because tattoos make assessment noisy. The true reason is institutional. A practitioner whose rank is partly masked by permanent ink is less controllable by the asset development pipeline. The CVD prefers readable people. The Grid inks because the one reaching for paper mid-encounter has already lost time.", "Anti-possession. Reinforces membrane integrity at the skin. Raises the class needed for opportunistic possession by one. Does not stop a high-class demon prepared for that specific practitioner. Buys time. My first tattoo.", "Rank mask. Reads one rank lower. Less effective than natural suppression, the Uedera technique. Side effect, you feel slightly more stable than you are.", "Stability anchor. Slows depletion from one source chosen at tattooing. Only that one.", "Binding, preloaded. On the forearm, always active at a low threshold. Fire it by pushing ability through the existing lines. Cheaper and with no preparation, against what it was designed for.", "The ones on my hands, between the knuckles, are not spells. They are keys. Each is tied to a working seal inked on the trunk or arms, and a touch in the right place, in the right order, fires that seal without paper. I developed the keying myself. It is in no CVD training material. From across a room it looks like a man rubbing his hands, pressing his sternum, steepling his fingers. If you ever have to read me and cannot find the configurations, that is the point. They are on me, always, and the hands are how I reach them."]}}, {"p": "So that was what the marks on his brother's skin were. Rei had always thought of them as Gin's, the way a face is someone's. Now he saw that each one was a tool, chosen and tested and added over years. The small marks between Gin's knuckles were not decoration either. They were switches. When Gin rubbed his hands together or pressed a palm to his chest, he was firing a seal somewhere else on his body. Rei had seen him do it a thousand times and had thought it was a habit."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Skin glyphs. The control problem", "lines": ["Paper is off until you turn it on. An untrained skin glyph is always on. In a high-entity place it fires by itself. In a strong emotion it surges. A practitioner with an untrained Restraining Circle tattoo can trap himself inside his own field in a moment of distress. A Threshold Ward that fires in a panic blocks the entities it was made for and also cooperative spirits, communication glyphs and any practitioner with a strong signature. You are warded against the world.", "Cascades. Two skin glyphs that work alone can wreck each other together. Anti-possession and Banishment on the same person fire at once in a possession event and produce neither, with full backlash from both. The Grid has documented eleven serious cascade incidents in the past eight years. Three needed Psychometabolic intervention.", "A heavily tattooed practitioner walking a thin spot makes noise as if using abilities while doing nothing. A tattoo running passively for six hours in a contaminated area may fire at forty percent when you finally reach for it. Skin does not reset the way paper does. The slow version has a name in the Grid's medical notes. Configuration bleed. You feel slightly worse every morning and never find the cause.", "Training. Symbol Work 3 to begin. A mentor who has trained his own. In the northern wards Tanizaki is one of four who can teach it. At least three months of practice per glyph, specific to that glyph. Exposure exercises, stress exercises, precision exercises at half strength. After the first, each one takes half the time. This is why sensible people get one, train it fully, then think about the next.", "I left eighteen months between my first, anti-possession, and my second, the forearm binding. The exercises are in the year-three pages.", "This happened to someone I know. The cascade took three months to stabilise. She is fine now. She would prefer I not say who she is."]}}, {"p": "Rei found the year-three pages. The exercises ran down the left margin in Gin's small print. Down the right margin, in the same pen, ran notes about Rei. <em>R. maths test, did badly, did not care. R. did not eat lunch again. R. laughed at something on the train today and I did not ask what, and should have.</em> The method was sound. The margin was the part Rei had to put the book down for."}, {"p": "He made more tea and let it go cold, the way Gin used to. Then he read on, to the page about what his brother carried every day."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "What I carry", "lines": ["Six prepared configurations at any time. Three containment variants, chosen by the expected entity. One banishment. One communication, for the encounters where talking is right. One I developed in year four, a slow-acting disruption, which Grid contacts keep finding on me and asking about.", "A standard set of threshold wards, folded in the left front pocket of every bag I own. I replace them weekly whether I used them or not.", "The most powerful thing I carry is the exorcism circle, in five parts, sealed separately. I have used it once in six years. I prepared for three months before that operation. The preparation made the difference.", "Prepare in advance. That is the only advice on symbol work that matters before you learn the rest. And learn from someone who knows them. Documentation makes configurations learnable. It does not make you able. Find a teacher first."]}}, {"p": "After that came the dictionary itself. A glyph, Gin explained, is a shape drawn on paper ahead of time, folded and carried until it is needed. All the real work goes into drawing it. Using it is only a touch."}, {"img": "cvdroom_sigil_room.jpg", "cap": "Glyphs drawn in advance, with time and a flat surface"}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "How glyphs work", "lines": ["A glyph is ink on paper, drawn in advance with time, a flat surface and enough light. You fold it and carry it. Activation is immediate. Touch it, direct your intent, done. The cost is paid in the preparation, when the configuration forms in the veil layer. There is no way to tell a bad glyph from a good one by looking at it. You find out when you activate it.", "The CVD registry lists three hundred and twelve standardised configurations, and that is the conservative count. The eighteen below are the ones I trust with my own hands.", "A warning, because someone always tries. A configuration is a geometry and can be adapted. People who understand why a glyph works do this carefully. People who copy a shape and change it to suit themselves draw something that no longer does what they think. A glyph does not fail politely. It fires wrong, and it fires on the person who drew it.", "Paper. Any paper works, a receipt, washi, a torn envelope. Size sets the scale. A business card gives the base effect. A5 gives base plus one. A4 gives base plus two, or doubles the duration, and doubles the preparation time. Folding does not reduce it. Every glyph is single use.", "Floor. A circle drawn on a floor has no size limit. Each extra metre of radius adds one percent to the stability cost. A ten-metre floor exorcism circle costs base plus nine. A floor glyph cannot be reclaimed. Smudge it and it breaks without firing and the cost is paid for nothing. Tanizaki's floor still shows the chalk ghosts of the same circles drawn and redrawn for years.", "Carrying. More than ten prepared glyphs on one person can be felt by a Clairsentient at Level 5. More than fifteen and anyone in range feels them. Carry what the operation needs."]}}, {"p": "Each entry in the lists that followed gave the glyph's name in English and in Japanese characters, then how long it took to draw, then what it did, then the level of Symbol Work needed to draw it. Gin had split them into groups. The first twelve protected and contained. The next six did harm. And the last few, at the very bottom of the chapter, he did not recommend at all."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "The twelve configurations", "lines": ["1. Restraining Circle 拘束陣 · 15 min · A 3 m field. Entities inside when it fires cannot leave under their own will for two hours. Class B contests it every round. Practitioners walk through freely. Symbol Work 3.", "2. Threshold Ward 閾守印 · 10 min · Pressed across a doorframe or sill. Spirits, suffering beings and Class E demons cannot cross. Class B crosses at a cost and arrives shaken. 24 hours. One per entry point. The most portable. Symbol Work 2.", "3. Silence Mark 沈黙符 · 5 min · Pressed to the forearm or collar. Hides your passive signature for 2 hours. Using an ability under it stands out more, not less. Symbol Work 2.", "4. Anchor Seal 錨封 · 20 min · Holds an anchored spirit within 10 m in place for an hour and makes it more visible. Meant to keep a conversation partner from leaving. The fold is part of it. Symbol Work 3.", "5. Banishment Glyph 退魔文 · 30 min · Sealed in an envelope, fired by tearing. A 5 m pulse. Spirits sent veil-side for 1 to 6 hours. Suffering beings weakened for a day. Carry no more than two. Symbol Work 3.", "6. Stability Mark 安定符 · 5 min · Pressed to the inside of the wrist. Your stability cannot fall below a quarter for one scene. Can be made for someone else, on their skin, at your cost. Symbol Work 2.", "7. Alert Seal 感知文 · 5 min · Any surface. When an entity crosses within 5 m you feel it up to 500 m away, most say as pressure behind the eyes. 8 hours. The most cost-efficient of the twelve. Symbol Work 1.", "8. Purification Seal 浄化印 · 45 min for a set of three · Placed at three corners within thirty seconds. Clears register contamination and blocks new contamination for 72 hours. All three from one session. Symbol Work 3.", "9. Communication Glyph 通言文 · 10 min · Unfolded on a flat surface. Lets one entity within 20 m speak in human-accessible form without Telepathy. Spirits use it fluently. Class B demons will use it if they want to, so be careful what you say. Symbol Work 2.", "10. Iron Wall 鉄壁 · 10 min, on iron-powder paper · Slapped against a hard surface. A barrier for one scene or three strikes. Stops the first telekinetic blow outright. Symbol Work 2.", "11. Suppression Mark 隠符 · 5 min · Pressed to skin. Your readable rank drops two levels for four hours. Vanguard reads as Channel. Channel reads as Sensitive. One hour if you fight. Does not stack with suppression training. Symbol Work 2.", "12. Exorcism Circle 除霊陣 · 1 to 2 hours, never under 60 minutes · Four outer glyphs and a centre mark, made as one set in one session, the centre drawn last. Placed at the compass points around the host. It fires when all five touch the floor. The entity cannot jump to a new host. It must go deeper or leave through the open top of the circle. The host surfaces longer and more often. The definitive possession recovery configuration. Symbol Work 4. I prepared three complete sets. Two are in the archive's bottom drawer, sealed in washi."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "The six that do harm. 'The geometry is the instruction.'", "lines": ["13. The Severance 断絶 · 20 min · Pressed on the anchor, never the entity. A vertical line cut by a horizontal, two arcs that do not meet. Cuts a spirit from what holds it. For a demon in a host, severs its main grip for a few moments, when exorcism is easiest. You feel the cut too. Have your grounding object ready. Symbol Work 3.", "14. The Weight 重圧 · 15 min · Dropped on the floor. Five circles sharing one bottom point, the innermost filled. The entity is that centre. Its presence drops by two for the scene. Symbol Work 2.", "15. The Mirror 鏡反 · 25 min · Held facing the entity, then released. Two triangles, one inverted, six lines to six points. What the entity broadcasts comes back to it. A demon managing its face is readable as it really is for a moment, and a Class B demon's social engineering fails. Anyone in range sees through the presentation. You also feel your own broadcast reflected. Symbol Work 3.", "16. The Threshold Burn 閾焔 · 20 min · Six flames under a line, not quite touching. Does not block. Lets the entity cross and then burns it. Once per entity. You sleep a little worse while you carry it. Symbol Work 2.", "17. The Arrest 制止 · 30 min · Four capped lines meeting at a filled centre inside a circle. Pressed against the entity or its surface. Freezes it, Class B for less time. Does not harm. Completely stops. You freeze too, for half a second. Be ready to take a hit. Symbol Work 4.", "18. The Corrosion 腐食 · 40 min, cedar ash in the ink · A worn circle around a spiral that never reaches the centre. Placed in the anchor space. Wears the entity down a little at the end of each scene for three scenes. Preparing it feels like melancholy. Symbol Work 3."]}}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Two I will not recommend", "lines": ["The eighteen above I have tested and I stand by. These last two I document because the archive should be honest, not because you should ever draw them. One ends you. The other decides for you where you go. I have drawn the first twice, for others who asked, and never the second.", "If you are reading this far down the chapter you are either very thorough or in real trouble. If it is the second, think once more before the ink is dry. G.U.", "19. The Final Refusal 自決 · 90 min, in your own blood, by a master of the form · A self closed and struck through, a mouth sealed and the thing inside it burned, a door drawn shut over the body, ringed in locks. Pressed over the heart and finished by your own hand. The seal is not finished until you finish it. It ends you instantly and burns what an enemy wanted. No Telepath can read a secret from the body. A possessing entity is refused the host. It cannot be used on anyone but yourself. A wrong stroke makes it inert, which is its own danger if you were counting on it. There is no backlash. There is only the cost, which is everything. I have drawn it correctly twice, both times for others who asked, both times burned unused. I do not write about those nights.", "20. The Scatter 散転 · 20 min · Crushed in the fist. A single point throwing lines out in every direction, none chosen. The veil throws you somewhere within roughly fifty to three hundred metres, direction and distance not yours. You will not be where the threat is. You may be somewhere worse. It costs ten stability, and if you land below fifteen percent, whatever is waiting where you land may try for you. Use it to not die here. Accept that you have chosen nothing else.", "21. Custom configuration · Not fixed. The practitioner's own geometry, adapted from one of the above or built new by someone who understands why a glyph works. Improvise only if you can predict the backlash. A glyph drawn wrong fires on the one who drew it."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the entry for the Final Refusal twice. He did not want to know which two people had asked his brother to draw it, or why. He turned the page quickly. Stuck inside the back cover of the chapter was a yellow post-it note."}, {"doc": {"kind": "postit", "title": "Post-it, inside the back cover of the chapter", "lines": ["Studying this gives understanding. It does not give the hand. R. will need someone to show him. Tanizaki for the standard set, Banishment, the Exorcism Circle, the Weight and the Arrest. Shimizu for the Burn. Kurosawa knows the offensive ones and will not volunteer it."]}}, {"p": "Under the post-it there was one more page. It was dated only by the day of the week, like most of Gin's personal notes, and it was about paper."}, {"img": "res_washi.jpg", "cap": "A washi shop in Kanda, where Gin bought his paper"}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "End of Chapter Twenty-Two. Personal note", "lines": ["A Monday. The paper from Kanda is on the desk. Forty sheets of washi, the kind the Grid contact recommended. I prepared six configurations this morning before I wrote anything. Threshold ward, binding, banishment, the custom disruption, two communication variants. The hand moves. The configuration emerges.", "Rei walked past while I was working and looked at the paper without asking what it was. He already knows. He has started to know what the preparations mean without knowing that he knows.", "G.U. Keys on the hook. The washi will outlast the need for it. I prepared more than I will use. I do not know why I did that today specifically."]}}, {"p": "Rei closed the book. He went to the desk and opened the bottom drawer. Two bundles of washi lay there, each tied with plain cord, each marked in Gin's hand with a small compass rose. Rei thought of the Exorcism Circle, its glyphs laid out at the compass points around the host. Rei did not untie them. He laid his palm flat on the top one and felt, under the paper, the faint, patient hum of something drawn correctly a long time ago by someone who had expected to need it. Then he shut the drawer and went to find his shoes. He could not draw a single one of these shapes. But Gin had already drawn them, and a prepared glyph does not care whose hand releases it."}], "n": 59}, {"title": "The Two-Demon Trap Closes", "kind": "main", "src": "m_trapcloses", "blocks": [{"img": "ch7_preconflict.jpg", "cap": "Rei on the long way down to the river"}, {"p": "Rei walked to the river the long way, because Gin's map said the long way was the honest one."}, {"p": "It was the evening before the last night of Obon. The heat of the day had not gone anywhere. It sat in the streets like water in a bath that nobody had let out. The cicadas were still going in the shrine trees behind the flat, a hard dry noise that rose and fell, and Rei could feel his shirt stick to his back before he reached the end of the lane. On almost every doorstep there was a paper lantern, white or pale blue, hung for the dead who had come home for the week. Some families had already set out little trays for the fires of the next night. Nobody looked at him. Everyone was busy with their own dead."}, {"p": "He went down past Jūjō Station first. The green-striped Saikyō line trains came and went. On the southbound platform there was a patch of floor, about the size of a bath mat, that was always a little cold. It was a weak thin spot, and Gin's notes said it had been there since at least 1998. It was too small to do harm. Commuters stepped around it every day without knowing why. Rei watched a man in a white shirt do it now, a small sideways step with his eyes on his phone, and then the man stood on the warm side of the line and waited for his train as if nothing had happened."}, {"p": "Rei touched his Suica card to the gate. It beeped. He took the Saikyō line one stop north to Akabane. The carriage was half full. A woman fanned herself with a folded train timetable. Two schoolgirls in summer uniforms shared one pair of earphones, a wire each, and laughed at the same moment at something only they could hear. An old man near the door held a bundle wrapped in a purple furoshiki, and from the shape of it Rei guessed it held offerings for a family grave. Fruit, maybe, or a box of sweets. The man held it on his knees with both hands the whole way, very carefully, as if it might spill."}, {"img": "place_akabane_ichibangai.jpg", "cap": "The drinking lanes of Ichibangai in Akabane"}, {"p": "At Akabane he came out of the station and turned into Ichibangai. Ichibangai is a tangle of narrow streets beside the station, packed with tiny bars and standing counters and yakitori grills, some of them open since the morning. Gin had written that these drinking streets were so loud with warm, ordinary feeling that two people could talk there and no Clairsentient in the ward could pick their words out. Rei did not need to talk to anyone. He needed one last stretch of noise to stand in."}, {"p": "So he stood in it. Smoke from the grills hung under the paper lanterns. Salarymen with their ties pulled loose sat elbow to elbow on beer crates. Someone was singing badly behind a curtain, and a whole room was clapping to keep him going. Rei let it wash over him for ten minutes. It felt like standing under a warm shower. Then he turned south and began to walk toward Ōji."}, {"p": "He walked along the tracks of the Keihin-Tōhoku line. The sky-blue trains passed him every few minutes, bright and full and humming. He went past Higashi-Jūjō, past shut shops and lit vending machines, past a konbini where a tired student was stacking rice balls into the cold shelf. The sky over the rooftops went from orange to purple to a dirty dark blue. The air did not cool at all."}, {"p": "Then the ground rose beside the tracks, and he was at Asukayama."}, {"p": "Gin had warned about Asukayama. The park held the old city in it the way cloth holds a smell. Three hundred years of picnics, blossom, executions and lovers, all pressed into one hill. To someone who could read the dead, it was so dense that a quiet lawn sounded like a crowd. <em>Not hostile,</em> Gin had written. <em>Just loud.</em> A Toden tram ground its way up the slope beside the park, lit inside like a small kitchen, with three passengers looking at nothing. Rei kept his registers closed and went past the hill with his head down."}, {"p": "Below Ōji the streets flattened out into the Kita-Ōji yards. These were low pre-war sheds, rail sidings and loading bays, all shuttered for the festival. Long ago many people had suffered here, and the Grid had never cleared it. Here the old misery lay in the corrugated walls and the oil-black ground in long, untreated bands. Gin's map marked the whole district in pencil with two words. <em>Likeliest crossing.</em>"}, {"p": "Tonight Rei could feel why. All those bands of old misery were leaning the same way, toward the water. They leaned like iron filings lean toward a magnet."}, {"p": "He came up the embankment toward the wound. The air over the river was doing what Higuchi had warned it would do. The bookseller had written about this night in his smallest, most frightened letters."}, {"p": "The air was thinning. It was thin the way the skin over a knuckle goes thin and tight when a person makes a fist. Rei could see the far bank. He could also see a second far bank through it, one that was not there. It was as if two cities had been laid on top of each other, and one of them had slipped a little out of place."}, {"p": "Below him on the near bank stood the riverside memorial, with flowers all around it. People called this the March ground. Every year the grief of the eleventh of March came here and settled, like mud settling at the bottom of a pond. An old woman in a flowered apron was changing the water in the vases by the light of a torch. She did not look up. She was speaking quietly to the river."}, {"p": "An hour earlier the neighbourhood had floated paper lanterns on the water. They were still going downstream, little orange lights on the black river. Rei let his suppressed registers come halfway up, just enough to look. Behind every lantern there was a second light, and the second light did not move with the current. It was something on the other side, leaning close to watch the small fires the living send to guide their dead home. Leaning close and holding its breath."}, {"p": "That was the obscene part. It was beautiful. The wall was failing, and the failure looked like a festival. Rei understood at last why the Healer had chosen Obon. It was not for the cover of the crowds. It was because during these few nights the whole city agrees to call the thinning of the wall a kindness."}, {"p": "Gin was already there."}, {"p": "He stood at the rail where the embankment path turned. He wore the grey cardigan he had worn all month, the one Rei could no longer look at without his throat closing. His long black hair hung loose past his jaw, and the river wind moved it a little. He was looking at the doubled river."}, {"p": "Rei had learned to read his brother's face in two layers, like everything else about him now."}, {"p": "Underneath was Gin. Tired, frightened and trying. That layer came up when the thing wearing him loosened its grip to let the body rest. Over it, calm and attentive and almost tender, was the Healer. Yushiro, the demon of Duty. If anyone had ever caught a demon between two skins, the registry would have given it a number. It turned his brother's face toward the wound the way a surgeon turns a calm face toward an open chest."}, {"p": "Every instrument in the CVD had read that register as stillness. Its machines had found no feeling in the Healer at all. They called it null-emotive and filed it under the thirteenth register, XIII, as an empty space. Only one archivist, in an entry twenty-two years old and buried in Yamamoto's stacks, had ever written down the right word, and nobody upstairs had believed it. Rei had felt the Healer in his kitchen, on his phone, and across a table at the Far-Shore house. He knew what it was now. It was not empty. It was a feeling so complete that there was no room in it for anything else."}, {"p": "Both of them turned when they heard Rei on the gravel. Both of them, in the same body, were glad he had come. The two kinds of gladness were the worst thing he had ever had to look at."}, {"p": "\"You found the registry,\" Gin said. It was the Healer's even voice coming out of his brother's mouth, and it was not a question. \"I hoped you would. I left the shelf unlocked for you. The one door this whole month that did not lock itself. You noticed that. You notice everything. It is the best of you, and it is why I could never have done this with anyone else holding the other end.\""}, {"p": "He waved a hand at the doubled river, the lanterns, the thin bright seam where two cities almost touched. It was a gentle gesture, like a teacher showing a pupil the one diagram that explains the whole lesson."}, {"p": "\"So you know what this is. You know there is a chair. You know it has stood open since the year the lights went grey. And you know, because you are the only person left alive who can feel both sides of a thing at once, that an open chair does not stay open. It only waits for the two who can close it. We are both here. The waiting is the part that is over.\""}, {"p": "Rei never had to answer. The cult answered for him."}, {"p": "Someone came up the far embankment, on the other side of the wound. It was a young man in a courier's jacket, as thin as a coat hanger. He walked with a loose, obedient step, like a body that had been emptied and filled again with something else. Kaito. The boy who preached in the drained pool. Kishin's mouth. Rei had hunted him through the city for days and never cornered him. He did not come now as an ambush. He came like the second guest at an appointment."}, {"p": "Behind Kaito's eyes something else looked out across the water. Rei knew its weight from the Far-Shore house in Takinogawa, from the tea and the white rooms and the wall of photographs of the missing. It was Kishin. The demon of Despair that wore the body of a dead woman, Fukai Rinka, and called itself Kanna-sensei. It was the thing eating Watanabe Jun's seat out from under him, and growing toward becoming the Inverted God, the dark mirror of the Axis. It did not need to come itself tonight. It had sent its mouth to watch."}, {"p": "Seeing it there, across the thin bright seam from the Healer, rearranged the whole month in Rei's chest at once."}, {"p": "The two demons were not on the same side. They did not even want the same thing. But with the river doubled between them and the lanterns burning down, Rei saw that they had been working on the same wound from two ends. The Healer had thinned the wall along its grain. Kishin's Circle had cleared away every medium in the ward who might have noticed. And both of them had come out on the thin night. Whatever the chair was for, the trap had always been built for two brothers."}, {"p": "One brother to fill the seat. One brother to be the cost of filling it. The Healer had never needed to defeat Rei. It only needed them both here, alive, on the thin night, loving each other."}, {"p": "\"Now you see the second jaw,\" the Healer said. There was no triumph in it. That was the unbearable part. His brother's voice, used by the thing, sounded sorry."}, {"p": "\"I told you the truth at every step, because the truth was the trap. I did not have to lie to a man who notices everything. I only had to let him notice in order. The wall thins these nights no matter what either of us does. That work is six years done. It was done before you could read a ward. What is still open is the chair, and a chair this old closes for one of two living things keyed to it. You.\""}, {"p": "He did not point at himself. He let the silence say the second name."}, {"p": "\"Or the body I am standing in. One register goes into the seat, and the office is filled, and the wall is mended, and the twelve stop being crushed, and Watanabe sleeps for the first time in a decade. And one register is left over, on the embankment, alive, to spend the rest of a life knowing it could have been the other way. That is the whole of it. There is no third thing across the river except the one that has come to make sure neither of us walks away from the chair. That is what the cult's friend is for. It is the lock on the door I have already opened.\""}, {"p": "Across the water Kaito smiled with a mouth that did not belong to him. It was the smile of something that wanted the chair to stay empty a little longer. It was happy to watch two brothers argue over it while the wall wore thin."}, {"p": "Then, under the Healer's calm, for one breath, Rei's real brother came up."}, {"p": "Rei felt it happen. It was like a swimmer breaking the surface of the water, sudden and desperate, without asking permission. Gin's hands moved before his mouth did. His right thumb pressed the knuckles of his left hand in a quick order, one, three, two. Then the heel of his hand went flat against his breastbone. To anyone watching, it was a tired man rubbing his hands against the chill off the river. But Rei had read the chapter on glyphs that morning. He knew a key when he saw one turned. Gin had marks inked into his skin, and that small movement woke one of them. Something on his brother's forearm, under the cardigan, grew warm. For the length of one sentence, the Healer lost its grip on his tongue."}, {"p": "Gin looked at Rei across the lanterns with his own eyes. He spoke fast and low, in the voice that had read stories to Rei when he was small."}, {"p": "\"Rei. Don't let it be you. Whatever it says, however right it makes the arithmetic sound, the chair is the thing it wants, and it does not care which of us sits in it, and that means the one it is steering you toward is you. Let it be me. I am already most of the way gone. Let it...\""}, {"p": "Then the Healer took the tongue back. Smoothly. Without hurry. The way a man takes a pen out of a child's hand. Gin's hands went still at his sides. They went still on purpose, the way they had always gone still when he was thinking. And the calm, sorry teacher was looking at Rei again."}, {"p": "The river ran doubled and bright between everything Rei still had to decide."}], "n": 60}, {"title": "The Axis Overstretched", "kind": "main", "src": "m_overstretched", "blocks": [{"p": "The Axis came up the way weather comes. Rei did not call it. It simply arrived."}, {"p": "It came up through the one register the two brothers shared. Under both their suppressions there was the same family grain, the Uedera grain. The far shore had found that shape in Rei's hand when it sent him into the tear, and it had keyed the two brothers to a single office. When Rei first felt the Axis, he had felt it as a balance point. A fulcrum is the small fixed point a seesaw rests on, the one point that lets both ends move. That was how it had felt to him, one living point with two suppressed registers leaning on it from either side. On the thin night he understood it differently. It was the only thing in the city that did not want either of them in the chair."}, {"p": "It rose up between the brothers and the wound, and it reached out in two directions at once. It reached for Rei's register. It reached for what was left of Gin's. Against every line of the Healer's arithmetic, it tried to keep both of them off the seat by holding both of them up."}, {"p": "For one impossible second it worked. The chair asked, and neither register answered, because the Axis was holding them both, balanced and hanging in the air. Two brothers were held an inch above the chair by the thing in them that would not let the other one fall."}, {"p": "In that second Rei saw it."}, {"img": "char_the-axis.jpg", "cap": "The face at the centre of the light, there and gone"}, {"p": "It was not a force. It was a face. It was there and gone at the centre of the light, the way a face appears in a window at night when the room behind the glass is lit. A young man, perhaps thirty, in a plain dark coat of a cut nobody had worn for a hundred and fifty years. His hair was tied back. His expression was calm and attentive and very tired. He looked at Rei the way a man looks at a word he once knew and loved and has lost the meaning of. His back was turned to everything else. His whole attention was on the wall."}, {"p": "Gin's book had described the Axis in its careful, clinical way, in the pages he could not prove. <em>It answers one question per approach. It does not show its face. It returns the practitioner.</em> In plain words, a person who reaches the Axis may ask it one thing, and then it pushes them back out. But now it was showing its face. It was not pushing anyone back. It was too busy holding two people up."}, {"p": "Rei had one question. He knew the rule. He did not ask how to save Gin, or which of them the chair would take, or how long the wall would hold. He asked the thing he had wanted to ask ever since the Axis had shoved him back into the world."}, {"p": "\"What are you holding?\""}, {"p": "The answer did not come in words. It came as the shape of an old word settling down inside him. <em>The seat I became.</em>"}, {"p": "Rei kept it next to something else. In the kitchen, with the last of his clear strength, Gin had managed half a sentence. <em>The chair doesn't need a new occupant. It needs its old one.</em> Rei did not understand either of them yet. He knew he would."}, {"p": "Then the Axis began to stretch."}, {"p": "Rei felt something that Higuchi's smallest letters had never had room to say. The Axis was built to carry one. It was a balance point, not a bridge. It held the city level the way the thin edge under a pair of scales holds the scales level. Ask that edge to carry both pans at once and it does not balance. It bends. Rei felt it bend inside his chest. The register the brothers shared went tight and thin and bright, like the seam over the river. The two of them were held. But the thing holding them was paying for it in the only money it had, which was itself."}, {"p": "It had been paying already, Rei understood. Since 2009 it had carried the thirteenth seat directly from the centre, with no living holder to take any of the weight. An Axis that is stretched too far makes mistakes. The thinning Kita had suffered for six years was partly the sound of a man at the centre holding too much for too long."}, {"p": "The Healer watched this with an attention that was, for the first time all month, truly surprised. It had not planned for the Axis to refuse the chair. It had planned for the shared register to be the very thing that made one of the brothers fit the chair. Now that register was straining itself to pieces to keep both of them out of the seat the Healer had spent so long preparing. For the first time, Rei had made the trap do something it was not built to do."}, {"p": "The cost was that he could feel exactly how long the Axis could keep it up. Not long."}, {"p": "\"Ah,\" the Healer said softly, in his brother's tired voice. The surprise in it slowly turned into something like respect. \"You would do that. Of course you would. You would rather burn the fulcrum than let it choose. You would rather both of you fall than either of you sit, because to you the unbearable thing was never the open chair. It was being made to pick. I did account for your love, Rei. I built the whole engine on it. I did not account for your love taking this shape. The shape that would rather end the thing that connects you than let it hand down a verdict.\""}, {"p": "Across the water Kaito tilted his borrowed head. He looked interested, the way a cat is interested in a bird that has flown into a window."}, {"p": "The seam over the river answered the strain. It thinned faster. The two cities slid another degree apart, because the Axis was spending itself, and the wall felt that spending the way the holders feel everything, as weight pressing on a seam."}, {"p": "\"But understand what you are doing,\" the Healer said. \"The Axis is what has kept the wound honest. Stretch it to breaking and the chair does not close. It tears. There is no seat left to fill, only a hole, and a city does not survive an unmade office any better than a vacant one. You have found the one move I did not see. It is also the one move that ends with no door at all.\""}], "n": 61}, {"title": "Late", "kind": "main", "src": "m_lastminute", "blocks": [{"img": "place_riverside.jpg", "cap": "The riverside memorial as the last lantern goes out"}, {"p": "The last lantern on the river went out a few metres from the pier. It did not float away. It simply stopped being lit. Behind it, where the second light had leaned close to watch, something on the far side let out a breath. Rei felt it on his face."}, {"p": "His father used to say that the truth of any account comes down to a single line. Rei found that line now, standing on the gravel with his chest bent around the strain of the Axis. The light at the centre could not hold both brothers much longer. The seam could not stay this open until morning without tearing. And the chair was speaking."}, {"p": "It did not speak in the Healer's voice, or in Gin's. It spoke in Rei's own voice. That was the thing he had not understood from his notes, and understood now in his body. It used the voice he thought in. <em>You could. You fit. Sit down and it stops hurting everyone.</em> An empty office cannot be argued into being filled. A living register has to agree to carry it, and the only voice that can agree is the voice of the one who will sit. The Healer could not put him in the chair. The thing watching through Kaito's eyes could not. And Gin, with every sentence he stole back, could not keep him out."}, {"p": "So the whole month had come down to this. Every knot in the ledger. Every soft-voiced woman with a bowl of soup. The six-year lean of an empty office on a sleeping ward. All of it had brought him to this rail on this night, then stepped back and left him alone with his own voice."}, {"p": "He felt the pulls one after another, the way he had felt the registers test his walled-off places in Shin-Ōkubo."}, {"p": "First the warm downward pull of the chair. It felt exactly like being tired enough to lie down in snow."}, {"p": "Then the sick lurch of looking at his brother's hands and thinking, for half a second, <em>he said let it be him</em>. He hated how much lighter that half second felt."}, {"p": "Then the black temptation to walk away up the embankment and let the wall do what walls do when no one holds them."}, {"p": "And under all of it, something worse and quieter. The Axis was straining in his chest like a rope. He could pull. He could pull until it snapped and the chair came apart with it. Then there would be no chair for anyone, only a hole, and no one would ever be made to sit."}, {"p": "\"You are counting,\" the Healer said gently. It was watching his face. \"Good. Count. A seat taken is every other seat untaken. A city saved this way is a city not saved that way. A brother kept is a chair left empty. A chair filled is a brother spent. There is no reading of the ledger, however long you stare, in which the columns come out even.\""}, {"p": "Rei understood the trap completely. Understanding it did not get him out of it."}, {"p": "He thought about the face at the centre of the light. The tired young man in the old coat, who had answered one question and was now spending himself to keep two strangers off a chair. <em>The seat I became.</em> He thought about the ledger the Healer kept describing, with its neat columns. There was no column in it for that man. Every sum the Healer had shown him ended with the man at the centre burning a little more, and the Healer had never once counted him."}, {"p": "And he thought about something the Healer had said a few minutes before, almost proudly. <em>I only had to let him notice in order.</em>"}, {"p": "A thing that needs a man to notice things in order needs him to be on time."}, {"p": "Rei decided to be late."}, {"break": "Not tonight"}, {"p": "\"Not tonight,\" he said."}, {"p": "He said it to the chair first, out loud, in the only voice it would listen to. He felt the question in the seam go unanswered and stay unanswered, like a phone left ringing in an empty flat. Then he said it again, more quietly, to the light. He said it the way someone would tell a man holding a heavy door that he could rest his arms for a moment. Rei stopped leaning on the balance point. He stopped asking it for anything."}, {"p": "The Axis felt the demand go out of the night. The bending in Rei's chest eased by a degree, then another. The light at the centre did not go out. It simply stopped breaking. For a heartbeat, before it sank back toward the wall, Rei thought the face looked at him with something like surprise."}, {"p": "He crossed the gravel to the rail and took his brother's hand."}, {"p": "It was Gin's hand. Cold, too still, and his. Under Rei's thumb the knuckles were faintly raised where small keys had been inked between them years ago. The Healer let him take it. Rei felt it decide to let him, the way a doctor lets a relative hold a patient's hand on the ward round."}, {"p": "\"We're going home,\" Rei said. \"Both of us. You can come too, since you're wearing him. But we're going.\""}, {"p": "For a long moment the Healer looked at him through Gin's eyes. Then it smiled. It was the dry half smile Gin always made and never followed with the joke."}, {"p": "\"The ice is only soft tonight,\" it said. \"Tomorrow is the last night. The sending fires. The dead go home and the wall goes thinner than this, and for longer. You have bought one day, Rei. I do not mind. A day is nothing to me.\" It glanced at the far bank. \"It is nothing to that one either. Read your clocks.\""}, {"p": "Across the river Kaito turned and walked back down the far embankment into the dark, without hurry. Whatever looked out of him had seen enough. The chair would still be open tomorrow, and Kishin could wait one more night as easily as the Healer could."}, {"break": "The walk home"}, {"p": "Rei walked his brother off the embankment as the sky went grey over the yards."}, {"p": "They went back through the Kita-Ōji sidings. The old misery in the sheds had stopped leaning toward the river. It only lay there now, heavy and tired, like dust that had settled. A freight wagon stood on a siding with its doors open and nothing inside. Somewhere a dog barked twice and gave up."}, {"p": "They climbed toward Ōji. The dark hill of Asukayama rose beside them, and the crows in its trees had started their morning shouting. At the foot of the hill the tram tracks of the Toden line ran empty and shining. The first tram had not come yet. Under the elevated tracks at Ōji station the shutters were still down, and the only light came from a konbini, white and bright and humming. Through its window Rei could see a clerk mopping the floor in slow lines. A delivery van had stopped outside, and a man was unloading crates of morning newspapers in their plastic straps. He glanced up at the two brothers walking hand in hand, and then went back to his crates. Tokyo had seen stranger things at five in the morning."}, {"p": "They walked on through the shut streets toward home. The paper lanterns on the doorsteps were dark now, waiting for the last night. A grandmother in a cotton yukata was already sweeping the step in front of her house with a short broom, and she nodded to them as if she knew them. Maybe she did. They had lived in the ward all their lives. A newspaper boy on a bicycle passed them, standing on the pedals, his basket full."}, {"p": "Hand in hand, like boys. Neither of them spoke. Twice Gin's fingers pressed Rei's. Once, then once, then once. It was the knock from the inside. Their old signal. It was true, and not the Healer wearing truth like a coat."}, {"p": "At the foot of the stairs to the flat, in the first real light, the hand let go."}, {"p": "\"I'll be at the rail tonight,\" the Healer said kindly, in Gin's voice. \"So will you.\""}, {"p": "It turned and walked back the way they had come, in his brother's body and his brother's cardigan. Rei stood at the foot of the stairs and watched until the street bent and hid it. Somewhere behind the houses the first cicada of the day started up, then another."}, {"p": "Only when it was gone did he look down and see what it had left in his palm when it let go. It was a square of washi folded small and still warm from his brother's hand. On the outside, in Gin's upright pencil, there was a single letter. <em>R.</em>"}], "n": 62}]}, {"title": "Book Five", "sub": "The Last Night", "chapters": [{"title": "The Three Clocks", "kind": "main", "src": "m_clocks", "blocks": [{"p": "The square of washi lay on the tatami where Rei had put it down at dawn. The old mat was soft and smelled faintly of grass in the heat. For three hours of bad sleep Rei had not let himself touch the paper."}, {"p": "When he woke, the light had gone the flat grey of a typhoon sky that never quite arrives. The cicadas had stopped. The air was heavy and still, and the electric fan on the floor only pushed the heat from one side of the room to the other. Somewhere down the street someone was testing a drum for the night. Two beats and a pause. Two beats and a pause. A neighbour's radio was reading the weather. The highs would be thirty-four degrees again, it said, with humidity at eighty percent, and people should drink water and stay out of the sun."}, {"p": "Rei sat up on the futon and looked at the paper. He thought, very clearly, <em>if this is from the Healer I will know the moment I open it.</em> Then he opened it."}, {"p": "It was not from the Healer. He knew that before the last fold came undone, because the paper did not feel kind. It felt careful. Inside, in fine brush strokes the colour of weak tea, was a small dense figure he recognised from the glyph dictionary he had read the morning before. It was number six in Gin's list. The Stability Mark. Pressed to the inside of the wrist, it would hold a person's floor for one scene. In plain words, it would stop them sliding under for a while, long enough to get through one hard thing. It could be made for someone else, on their skin, and the maker paid the cost."}, {"p": "In the corner, small, was the letter again. <em>R.</em> Under it was a date from June, from before the handwriting began to lean."}, {"p": "Gin had drawn it for him weeks before he went quiet. He had carried it in the cardigan pocket all month, right under the Healer's nose. And at the foot of the stairs at dawn he had come up just far enough to put it in Rei's hand. Rei sat with it on his knee for a long time. Then he folded it again along its own creases and put it in his shirt pocket, over his heart. He got up to do the thing he had been putting off since the river."}, {"break": "Three pages"}, {"img": "ch10_develop.jpg", "cap": "Three pages laid out side by side"}, {"p": "He cleared the low table, and then the floor around it, because the table was not big enough. He laid the three pages down on the tatami, corner to corner, the way someone lays out the clothes of a person who is not coming back. Carefully. So the creases fell right, as though neatness could still do them any good."}, {"p": "The registry page went down first. The bookseller's locked shelf had finally given it up. It had the Thirteen ruled into it, and the thirteenth line was empty except for a date. Next came the smallest of Higuchi's own notes. It was written in the cramped hand a man uses only when he is afraid of his own paper, and it said how the wall over Kita would thin on these nights and what it would look like when it did. Last, because he could not make his hand go any faster, came a page in Gin's handwriting from after the fourteenth. It was the entry where the loops of the letters first leaned a degree the wrong way. Rei had read it so often that the fold had gone soft as cloth."}, {"p": "For a month he had carried all of it as one thing. A countdown. One single dread ticking toward one last night. That was how the Healer wanted it carried. Light enough to run with. Simple enough to panic over. <em>Read your clocks,</em> it had said. Clocks, more than one. It had not been careless. It had been telling him."}, {"p": "On the floor, laid flat in the grey light, the dread came apart. It came apart the way a chord of music comes apart when the ear finally hears the separate notes inside it. There was not one clock. There were three. And the quiet, mathematical horror of the three was that they did not run at the same speed. They would not all reach the bottom of the hour together. One of them would get there first."}, {"p": "He let his registers come halfway up. Only halfway, the way Kurosawa had taught him in the cold room. Enough to read, not enough to be read. The flat took on the second grain it always had when he did this. The paper remembered being a tree. The dead leaned closer to anything that was paying attention. Under that grain the three pages stopped being documents and became what they had been all along. Timetables."}, {"break": "The wall"}, {"p": "He started with the wall, because it was the honest clock, and honesty was the only mercy in the room."}, {"p": "Higuchi had written it in the hand of a man who had measured the thing he was describing and wished he had not. The veil over Kita did not fail all at once, and it did not fail forever. At Obon it thinned, the way ice thins over a warm spring. Through the festival's last nights it opened along the river. Then, at the first true light after the sending fires, one of two things happened. Either it closed back to its old hard quiet. Or, if something had been pushed through it while it was soft, it tore along the line of the wound and did not close at all."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Higuchi's note, in letters barely a millimetre tall", "lines": ["Last night of the festival. Okuribi. The ice is softest from the lighting of the fires to first light.", "If nothing is pushed through, it heals by itself by morning. It always has. It does not need us.", "If something is pushed through while it is soft, it will not heal.", "Whoever reads this should be someone who will leave it alone. I do not know who that is. I am locking it up."]}}, {"p": "There was a clean edge to this clock that the other two did not have. It opened tonight. It shut at dawn. Everything that would be decided about the wall would be decided in the dark between."}, {"p": "Rei read the small letters twice. He felt the special cold of a deadline that cannot be argued with. The empty chair could wait for years. It had. Gin could be pleaded with. Rei had tried. But the wall did not bargain. It was like weather, or the tide. It was the one thing on the embankment that would happen exactly the same way if Rei and Gin and the Healer had never been born. That indifference was almost a comfort. In this whole disaster, there was at least one clock that was not aimed at him on purpose."}, {"p": "Under his half-raised registers, the page showed him something Higuchi had been too disciplined to write down. The bookseller had measured the thinning, and then he had locked the shelf. A man does not lock a true measurement away from a careful reader unless he has guessed what that reader would do with it. Higuchi had guessed that someone would come who could feel both sides of the wall at once. And he had guessed that such a person, handed the exact hour the ice went soft, would be the perfect tool for saving the wall, or for finishing it."}, {"p": "The measurement was honest. The hand that wrote it had been afraid of Rei, of Rei himself, reading it. Both things were true at the same time. That was the shape of the whole month."}, {"break": "The chair"}, {"p": "The chair was the patient clock, and it frightened him in a different way."}, {"p": "The registry ruled the Thirteen into their columns. Twelve of the lines held names Rei had come to know that month as people, tired and weakening and holding on. Watanabe going grey at the edges. Mori Takao white-knuckled at his boiler. Nakashima Yuna in her stairwell. Inoue Satoru, who never stopped moving. Park Junho on his knees in a room behind a restaurant. The whole exhausted choir, keeping the wall up by being something."}, {"p": "The thirteenth line was ruled like the others, and it held only a year. 2009. The year the lights went grey. Under it, in a second ink, Higuchi had added lines of his own. The ledger had been started by an exorcist whose name appeared nowhere in it. Higuchi only kept it up to date."}, {"doc": {"kind": "table", "title": "The registry, line XIII, with Higuchi's annotations", "lines": ["XIII · Duty · vacant since 2009 · Kita.", "The holder died. Nobody with the right aperture was present at the crossing, so the succession did not take.", "The centre has carried it directly since. It cannot do so forever.", "Kita has thinned since that year. After the eleventh of March 2011 it thinned faster.", "The Division's instruments read this register as stillness and file it as null-emotive. It is not empty. It is full. It does not vary because it does not need to.", "Who can sit it. A practitioner who carries more than one path and can hold one unchanging register without letting it turn into any of the other twelve."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the last line and did not move for a while. More than one path. One unchanging register. He thought of the kitchen table, and a younger Gin teaching him how to be quiet. Gin had called it a family habit, a trick for sleeping, passed down to him the same way. Rei thought of the grain in his own palms that the far shore had left there."}, {"p": "Years of nothing ran down that column. The line stayed open for the plain, boring reason that nobody had ever crossed it out. Rei had read enough of the bookseller's shelf to know what that meant. An empty seat is not a hole in the wall. It is a chair standing at the wall, and an empty chair is a question the wall keeps asking."}, {"p": "This clock had been running the longest and moving the slowest, and that was exactly why the Healer had been patient with it. A seat does not stay open. It waits, and waiting is not the same as forgetting. It waits for the ones who can close it. Tonight, for the first time since the lights went grey, the two people who could close it would both be standing in the city while the ice was soft."}, {"p": "The chair had no dawn line. It was older and quieter than any dawn. But it had a condition. The condition was met when a person of the right register reached the right place at the moment the wall could be written on. So the patient clock had no hour of its own. It borrowed the wall's hour. That was the trap folded inside the registry, and the reason the page had been locked away. The patient clock and the honest clock would arrive together, tonight, at the seam, by design."}, {"p": "He held the page up to the lamp and let his registers come the rest of the way to halfway. He could almost feel the chair from the flat. This was not a figure of speech. There was a real cold spot over the river in the shape of an absence. It was a pressure like a breath that had been held for six years and was, very slightly, beginning to let go."}, {"p": "The Healer wanted the seat. Rei had known that for days. What the registry told him, and the Healer's reasonable voice never had, was what the seat itself wanted. It wanted to be filled by whoever was standing in front of it when the wall went soft. It was not loyal to the Healer. It was not loyal to Rei. It was a chair, and a chair takes whoever sits."}, {"p": "And then something caught, the way a thread catches on a nail. Rei frowned at the page. <em>The centre has carried it directly since.</em> So the chair was not empty. It only read as empty, because the one holding it was not alive to be counted. Somebody had been sitting in it for six years, on the far side of everything, with his back to the world."}, {"p": "<em>The seat I became.</em>"}, {"p": "Rei set the registry down very carefully, as if it might go off. Then he reached for the third page, the one in his brother's hand, and turned it over."}], "n": 63}, {"title": "Which Clock Runs Fastest", "kind": "main", "src": "m_fastest", "blocks": [{"p": "The ink had leaned before the man did. That was what Rei saw first, with the page turned to the lamp. It was not a shake. It was a lean."}, {"p": "He had saved his brother's clock for last. He knew that once he looked at it, he would not be able to look at anything else. Here was Gin's handwriting across the whole month. The early entries stood upright, tired and his. The loops were closed the way Gin had closed them since he was a boy learning to write his name small enough to fit in the boxes on school forms. Then, after the fourteenth, came the drift. Every downstroke was one degree off true. It was steady and deliberate and almost graceful, as though a second person with very good handwriting had begun, gently, to correct him."}, {"p": "A shaking hand Rei could have grieved over cleanly. This was worse. He did not need Kurosawa to read the body for him any more. He had read it himself, in the cardigan, in the way the thing said <em>we</em>, in the two kinds of gladness in one face at the rail. The handwriting only turned it into a measurement."}, {"p": "And a measurement over time is a speed. That was the sum he had refused to do all month."}, {"break": "Eight weeks"}, {"p": "Gin had done that sum for other people. Rei found it in the back third of the book, in a section with the dry title Gin gave to everything that frightened him."}, {"doc": {"kind": "case", "title": "Gin's book. Partial possession. What the case files show", "lines": ["Compiled from the Satō file and four others. Times vary. The shape does not.", "Before. A patient entity can watch a practitioner for a year and a half and do nothing visible. It waits for one night when his real stability drops below the floor his suppression is pretending to hold. The suppression smooths over the very signal he would have used to notice. It may wait a week. It may wait two years.", "The night. It takes approximately the time between two thoughts. In the files, the host often does one small ordinary thing at the last moment, as if part of him knew.", "After. The entity runs the surface well. Messages in his voice. Work attended. Assessments read clean. The tells are tiny. One missed appointment he would never miss. One file pulled without reason. One joke that does not land.", "Two weeks. It notices questions. People who ask begin to find his contacts harder to reach.", "Four weeks. The critical point. Without a recovery team being built, consolidation speeds up. His surfaces get shorter.", "Six weeks. Ejection risk. His own consciousness begins to be pushed out. Reaching him now needs work at the Threshold.", "Eight weeks. The window closes. Full possession. Without Threshold transit, ejection cannot be undone. What remains of him is what he wrote down.", "It speeds up when someone makes loud direct contact, when a practitioner near it breaks his own suppression where it can see, when watched contacts are burned. Each costs days.", "It slows when someone reaches him in a surface, even briefly. When someone learns the entity's history. When someone builds a team able to do the exorcism. Each buys days.", "The clock is never announced. It is felt."]}}, {"p": "Put simply, a partial possession has a timetable. For the first weeks the person is still inside, coming up now and then. Around six weeks the demon starts to push him out. At eight weeks he is gone, unless someone crosses the Threshold to reach him. Every loud mistake by the people around him makes it faster. Every quiet, true contact makes it slower."}, {"p": "<em>One small ordinary thing at the last moment.</em> Rei thought of the walkway outside the flat on the first night. Gin's keys had been lying on the concrete by the door, set down neatly, as if put there on purpose. The desk lamp had still been on. For a month Rei had thought it meant Gin had been taken in a hurry. It meant the opposite. Gin had had just enough time to leave his brother a sign."}, {"p": "He counted the weeks from the Wednesday Gin went quiet. He counted them twice on his fingers, like a child. More than four. Not yet six."}, {"p": "Then he made himself do the second sum. It was the kind of sum people do about a parent in a hospital ward, where the number is small and the units are unbearable. He went back through the month and found the days that had moved the clock."}, {"p": "The night he went through at the Senju node and came back with a stranger's tools in his hands, his suppression broken wide open in a place the Healer was watching. Days lost."}, {"p": "The kitchen at two in the morning, when Gin came up long enough to say <em>don't take it</em>. Days bought."}, {"p": "The phone call. The knock on the receiver. Days bought."}, {"p": "The embankment, and a cold hand held all the way home through Ōji while the knuckles pressed back, once, then once, then once. Days bought."}, {"p": "He let his registers up and read the page the way only he could, both sides at once, and the lean told him its speed. It was gaining. It was not finished. There was still a margin. It was as thin as the wall and as soft as the ice. A hand's width of his brother not yet corrected."}, {"p": "The Healer knew this too. It had always known. It had been patient because it was letting this clock run slow on purpose, keeping a little of Gin in reserve. A living brother was the bait that kept Rei coming back to the river. An empty body would not have been. The cruellest clock was the one the Healer had its thumb on. It was holding the clock back so that the bait stayed fresh until the wall and the chair arrived."}, {"break": "A second hand"}, {"p": "He might have stopped there. He nearly did. But the lean was not even. On one page near the end, an entry about the weather and a broken heater, the corrections stopped looking like corrections. For six lines the second hand wrote by itself."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's book, an entry after the 14th. The second hand", "lines": ["Subject's rank is presented as C and has been for six years. Real rank Vanguard, suppressed, held under repeated assessment, unnoticed.", "Aperture strong. Multi-path. Works alone. No institutional welfare check would flag his stability falling.", "Lives three hundred metres from the warm place and inside the range of the vacant thirteenth.", "Line of Uedera. The development points toward the seat. Not his body. His access.", "He does not know he is tired."]}}, {"p": "It was a clinical note. A doctor's note, about a patient. The patient was Gin. Under it, at the foot of the page, the pen had begun a signature in old-fashioned characters, firm and practised, as though from a habit of forty years. 橘 癒士郎. Then a single line had been drawn through it, almost gently."}, {"p": "Gin had once written, on the page about the thing inside him, that it wrote truer than it meant to. Rei sat looking at the crossed-out name until the characters stopped being shapes and became sounds. <em>Tachibana. Yushiro.</em> Tachibana was a family name, a common one. The given name was written with the character for healing in it, so that it meant something like <em>healing man</em>."}, {"p": "He rang Tanizaki. The old man answered on the fourth ring, which meant he had been sitting by the phone."}, {"p": "\"I've got a name,\" Rei said. He read it out. He heard the silence on the other end change shape."}, {"p": "\"The Grid's old books remember a doctor,\" Tanizaki said at last. His voice was thin on the line and very steady. \"In this ward. Forty years ago or near it. There were people dying who would have been left, and he would not leave them. He stayed at it past the point a living man can. When he crossed, he crossed through Duty. The charge he would not put down. The books say afterwards he wanted no seat and no sensation. Only to finish.\""}, {"p": "\"The CVD calls that register nothing,\" Rei said."}, {"p": "\"The CVD calls a great many things nothing.\" Somewhere a cup was set down. \"Listen to me, boy. I have known your brother since he was smaller than you were when I met you. Most of them, you say the name and it's a blade. Not this kind. Said by someone the vessel loves, the name doesn't cut it. It holds it. Anchors it in place. Like a hand on a shoulder.\" A pause. \"You understand what I'm telling you. It isn't a weapon. It's a leash, and only one person in this city can hold the end of it.\""}, {"p": "\"And if I say it and it doesn't want to be held?\""}, {"p": "\"Then you'll have told a doctor his own name,\" Tanizaki said. \"In my experience they don't hear it often.\""}, {"break": "The family note"}, {"p": "After the call, Rei sat for a while with the phone in his lap. Through the window he could hear the street getting ready for the last night. A man was setting up folding tables outside the shrine. Children were running between the houses with sparklers they had been told not to light until dark. Someone's grandmother was calling a name, over and over, in a voice that was not worried yet."}, {"p": "Rei went back to the section of the book that Gin had marked only with a small drawing of a hook. He had read it once, weeks ago, and not understood it. Now he did."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's book. Family note", "lines": ["Two Uedera ancestors were field operatives in the early years of veil work. Their case files show outcomes no single-path practitioner of their recorded rank could have managed. They never opened their paths on purpose. They held them shut and used them, now and then, under extreme pressure, without understanding what they were doing.", "I recognised the pattern after my own exposure. The multi-path aperture seems to run in the family. So does the habit of hiding it. I taught Rei the suppression at the kitchen table as if it were a trick for sleeping. It is the only configuration I have ever drawn that was entirely an act of love.", "If it is ever me inside something, there are two ways in. Clairsentience at the tenth level. Frequency Isolation. Finding one consciousness inside a possessed body, as one voice is found in a crowd. And if I am thrown out and not yet gone, Teleportation at the tenth level. Threshold Crossing. Reaching a soul that has been ejected but has not dissolved.", "Rei will never have the second. He may have the first without knowing.", "Some of the old ones have known about our line for longer than I have. I do not know which. I have felt one of them look at me as if remembering something."]}}, {"p": "<em>As if remembering something.</em> Rei thought of the face at the centre of the light, looking at him like a word it had lost. He turned the registry page over. On its blank back he began to write, slowly, everything he knew about the man who held the empty chair."}, {"img": "thirteen_XII_kasumi_shin.jpg", "cap": "Kasumi Shin, holder of Awe, who keeps the Account Book"}, {"p": "Gin's page about the Account Book gave him most of it. Kasumi Shin held Awe, and of all the Thirteen he stood nearest the centre. He had once shown Gin a book older than the Accord of 1962. Gin had copied out one entry from its first pages, because it had disturbed him."}, {"img": "scene_gin_shin_account_book.jpg", "cap": "Gin copying from Kasumi Shin's Account Book"}, {"p": "The entry was about an investigator of the Edo period who served the Twilight Office. That was the old office of Ōmagatoki, the twilight hour whose name Rei had seen brushed on a paper lantern when the Axis shoved him back. Its officers looked into whatever came through at dusk. This investigator would not take either side of any line. He walked the middle way between them. He walked it until he had opened every path and every register at once, and there was nothing left of him that was only one thing."}, {"p": "Gin had copied the family name, Kuze. Then he had copied one character for the given name, 要, with a question beside it. <em>Kaname? The rivet of a fan. The pin everything turns on.</em> A folding fan is held together by one small pin at its base. Every rib turns around it. Take the pin away and the fan falls apart in the hand."}, {"p": "Rei wrote it all out in six lines and looked at them."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "The back of the registry page, in Rei's hand", "lines": ["His name is Kuze Kaname.", "He comes from the Edo period.", "He served the Twilight Office.", "He walked the middle way.", "He was an investigator.", "He became the Duty seat. He is still sitting in it."]}}, {"p": "He had been wrong about the chair all month. Everyone had. It was not empty. It had a man in it who had forgotten he was a man."}, {"break": "Three needles"}, {"p": "Now he laid the three speeds side by side and let them be what they were. Three clocks that could not all reach the bottom of the hour together, because the bottom of the hour was one single hour. The soft middle of the last night."}, {"p": "The wall would open tonight and shut at dawn. Fixed, honest, indifferent."}, {"p": "The chair would borrow that hour and take whoever was closest when the ice went soft. Patient, conditional, hungry."}, {"p": "And Gin was moving toward the end of his eight weeks. Not yet there, held open on purpose. Cruel, slowed, baited."}, {"p": "Three needles were falling toward the same number at three speeds."}, {"p": "Under the whole month's dread, a clear thought had been waiting for him all along. Whichever clock finished first would not only finish itself. It would close the other two."}, {"p": "If dawn came and nothing had been pushed through, the wall would heal in its own time. The chair would go on asking. And Gin would be left at whatever degree he had reached. If the chair was filled while the ice was soft, the wall would be written on before dawn could heal it, and everything else would bend to whoever had sat. And if the Healer's thumb slipped, or it decided the bait had done its job, and the last degree of Gin went over first, then there would be no brother left to weigh against anything. The night would only be about cost."}, {"p": "The drums started up across the ward. It was the slow Obon rhythm that the whole city agrees means <em>welcome home</em> to the dead. Under Rei's half-raised registers, the second light came up behind every sound, the way it came up behind every lantern. He gathered the three pages and squared their edges. He did not pretend any more that he was reading them. He knew which clocks were gaining."}, {"p": "He had a name for the thing in his brother. He had a name for the man in the chair. He had a square of washi over his heart. It was not a plan. It was what Gin would have called a kit."}, {"p": "He went to the desk and opened the bottom drawer."}], "n": 64}, {"title": "The Foreclosure", "kind": "main", "src": "m_foreclosure", "blocks": [{"p": "In the bottom drawer there were two bundles of washi, and between them lay a third sheet. It was the colour of rust."}, {"p": "Rei lifted it out by the edges. He knew what it was before he had it in the light. He had read about it in the glyph chapter the morning before, and he had hoped he would never see one, the way a person hopes about a lump. A self, closed and struck through. A mouth sealed, and the thing inside it burned. A door drawn shut over the body, ringed with locks. It was the most complex figure in Gin's book. It was drawn here in a brown that had once been red, by a hand that had not started to lean."}, {"p": "This was the Final Refusal. Gin's book explained it in a few hard lines. It is drawn in the maker's own blood and pressed over his own heart. When he finishes the last stroke, it ends him at once. A demon is refused the body, and whatever an enemy wanted from him burns with him. It can only ever be used on oneself."}, {"p": "It was finished except for one stroke. The last one. The one a person makes himself, over his own heart."}, {"p": "<em>I have drawn it correctly twice, both times for others who asked, both times burned unused.</em> There was no name on this one. There did not need to be. Gin had drawn it for himself, sometime in the last good weeks. Something had started thinning the wall toward him then. He had felt watched, and he had blamed the wrong thing. He had drawn the Refusal the way he drew everything, early and carefully, so it would be there if he needed it. Then he had put it in the drawer beside the circle that could save him. He had gone to work, and come home one Wednesday too tired to know he was tired."}, {"p": "<em>Let it be me,</em> he had said at the rail. <em>I am already most of the way gone.</em>"}, {"p": "Rei stood at the kitchen sink with the sheet in one hand and the lighter in the other for a long time. The lighter was the cheap one he had carried for years. He turned it over in his fingers whenever he was waiting for something, and he only noticed now that he had been turning it all afternoon. Then he burned the Final Refusal over the drain and watched the brown figure curl up and go. Gin had drawn it twice for others, and both times it had burned unused. Rei made it three."}, {"break": "The kit"}, {"p": "He packed Gin's field kit on the kitchen table, the way Gin had packed it in front of him a hundred times while Rei pretended to watch television. It looked like the kit of a man going camping. That was the point."}, {"p": "Threshold wards, folded into strips, went into the left front pocket, where Gin had kept them in every bag he owned. Three sealed vials of sulphur, small glass, unbroken, went on the left side. Then six prepared configurations from the desk, each in its own sleeve, each labelled in Gin's small print. He counted them twice and stopped at nine prepared glyphs in total. Gin had written that more than ten could be felt by anyone who was reading for them. Into the inside pocket of his jacket went the two washi bundles with the compass roses on them. They held the exorcism circle in five parts, each part sealed separately, two complete sets. In six years Gin had used one set once, after three months of preparation. Rei had had an afternoon."}, {"p": "He could not draw a single one of them. He had understood that while reading the chapter. Gin's post-it said it plainly. <em>Studying this gives understanding. It does not give the hand.</em> But the hand had already been given. Gin had given it, on a Monday morning with forty sheets of Kanda washi on the desk. <em>I prepared more than I will use. I do not know why I did that today specifically.</em> Rei thought he knew now. Part of Gin had been packing for this night before either of them knew it would come."}, {"p": "Last, because his hands would not do it any sooner, he took the folded Stability Mark out of his shirt pocket. He looked at the <em>R.</em> in the corner. Then he put it back over his heart."}, {"break": "Entry Four"}, {"p": "He was sitting in the genkan with his shoes on when he remembered the page he had always skipped. It was near the end of the book, with a heading in Gin's tidy print. He had skipped it the first time because it looked like a list of types, the kind of thing a recruitment office would print."}, {"doc": {"kind": "note", "title": "Gin's book. The Eight Patterns. How people come to this work", "lines": ["A Saturday. Rei is playing video games. I am writing about the eight ways a person can begin to understand what they are, because no two practitioners find the veil through the same door.", "Entry point is not class. It is starting position.", "One. The Registered Practitioner. Official, inside the system. Access and surveillance in equal measure. Kato is Entry One. Adachi is Entry One.", "Two. The Grid Operative. Unregistered. Network trust. Tanizaki is Entry Two. He has been Grid since before I was born.", "Three. The Civilian Medium. Sensitive, unframed. The world just became very large.", "Four. The Suppressed. Hiding what they are. Real rank at least two levels above what they show. The performance is the protection. This is the one I know best. I am aware that I am describing myself.", "Five. The Institutional Enforcer. The system's instrument. Interesting when the system asks for the wrong thing.", "Six. The Possessed. A good spirit sharing your body with your full awareness and consent.", "Seven. The Witness. Zero rank or near it. Survives by being the person no one sees coming.", "Eight. The Demon. A demon in a Vessel Program body, under formal contract with the CVD. You remember being human.", "Rei is Entry Four. He does not know this yet. He will. Documentation is not exposure. It is care. G.U. A Sunday. Rei is in the next room."]}}, {"p": "Rei read the last line and laughed once, out loud, in the empty entrance, because it was so exactly like Gin to write a thing like that and then go and make dinner. Rei had been in the next room. He could almost remember the Sunday. He had been annoyed about something. He had had no idea, then, that he was being written down with love."}, {"p": "Entry Four. Hiding what they are. The performance is the protection. For six years Rei had performed being nothing much. A crime-scene temp with bad sleep and a headache habit. His brother had taught him to do it, at a kitchen table, as a trick for sleeping. Tonight, at the river, the performance would end. Whatever he was, he would be it in front of everything."}, {"p": "He thought about what the Healer had shown him all month without ever saying it. There were two ways to grow into a seat."}, {"p": "The first way was hunger. The grain the far shore had left in his palms was hungry. He could feel it. It wanted other people's registers the way a mouth wants bread. If he fed it, if he took in other people's feelings to grow fast, he would become strong very quickly. That was what a Qliphoth did. It ate a holder out from under a seat and wore the register like an empty shell."}, {"p": "The second way was the way the twelve did it. Hold one register open and steady with one's own self, sane and willing, every morning, for as long as it took. It was slower. It was the only clean way."}, {"p": "He closed his fists over the grain and did not feed it."}], "n": 65}, {"title": "The Click", "kind": "main", "src": "m_foreclosure#2", "blocks": [{"p": "In the genkan, with his shoes on and three folded pages over his heart, the whole night stopped being an idea. It became a fact in his body, which is the only place ideas ever really land."}, {"p": "He could not be at the seam and at the chair and at his brother's side all at the same hour. By the cruelty of how this had been built, they were the same hour. And a body is one body in one place, even a body that can feel both sides of a wall. To put his hand on the wall was to take it off the chair. To reach for his brother was to leave the wall unread at the moment it could be written on. There was no way to arrange the night so that all his hands were where he wanted them. That was all the month-long dread had ever been. A simple animal fact, dressed up as something complicated, because complicated is bearable and a single forced step is not."}, {"p": "He thought about the twelve. Each of them must have stood once in a doorway like this, with their own pages folded over their own hearts, and gone out, and become what going out made them. Watanabe weakening. Mori Takao at his boiler for twenty years. Park Junho on his knees. None of them had got to hold every clock. That was what a seat cost, and what a wall cost, and, Rei understood now, what a brother cost. Not the price paid at the river, which would be loud and quick. This. The doors that close softly behind a person as he walks out of a flat, the ones he never gets to find out were the right ones."}, {"p": "The drums were louder now. The grey outside had turned to the deep blue that meant the festival's last night had begun. Up and down the ward people were carrying the small fires out to their doorsteps to send their dead home. Somewhere along the embankment his brother was already standing at the rail in his grey cardigan, looking at a doubled river, glad in two ways at once that Rei was coming."}, {"p": "Rei put his hand on the door. He did not feel ready. He had stopped believing that being ready had ever been possible. Instead he felt the thing Higuchi's smallest letters had really been about. It was not fear of the wall. It was the terrible privilege of being the one person who can feel both sides of a closing door, and who has to decide anyway which side to be on when it shuts."}, {"p": "He heard his own door click shut behind him. The sound was smaller than the thing it was, the way important sounds always are. That was the real foreclosure. Not the river. This small click. The flat with its three pages, and the version of the night where he stayed inside in the grey hour reading and never had to go. All of it sealed away behind the sound a key makes in a lock."}, {"break": "The way down"}, {"p": "He went down through the streets toward the water, and the ward came out to meet him."}, {"p": "It was the night of okuribi, and the whole of Kita was outside. On every second doorstep a little fire of hemp stalks burned in a clay dish or a tin tray. Old men crouched over them in their undershirts, feeding the flames one stalk at a time. Families stood around in summer yukata and talked quietly to the smoke as if someone were standing in it. A girl of about six held her grandmother's hand and asked where the smoke was going. \"Home,\" the grandmother said. Rei walked between the little fires. The heat of the day had not broken. Sweat ran down his back under the kit bag. Overhead a Keihin-Tōhoku train went by on its embankment, lit and full, its sky-blue stripe flashing between the roofs, carrying people home from work to their own small fires."}, {"img": "place_arcade.jpg", "cap": "The dead market arcade, shutters down"}, {"p": "He passed the dead market arcade, two streets north of the market. Its shutters were down. Its storm drains breathed cold air into the August night. Gin's book called it a crossing point and said <em>never stand over one you have not warded</em>. Rei walked in the middle of the road."}, {"p": "Above the arcade, in a cheap flat, one window was lit. A man in a vest sat at it with his head in his hands. He had the look of someone who has been tired for a year and thinks it is only age. On the windowsill beside him stood a row of potted plants. All of them had died, but only on the side of the room nearest the arcade. The nameplate by the street door said Tachibana. Rei stopped dead on the tarmac. Then he made himself breathe. It was a common name. It was not the same man. It was only a tired stranger being slowly drained by the arcade beneath him, and Rei had no hour to give him tonight. He put the window away in his memory with all the others he meant to come back for."}, {"p": "He passed under the railway arches. Inside the third arch was Kemuri, a tiny bar with six stools and one red bulb, behind a door with no handle set into the brick. Its bartender, Goro, leaned beside the door with a cigarette, watching the festival go by. Goro looked at Rei's face, and then at the kit bag. He lifted two fingers from his cigarette in a kind of salute. He did not say anything. At Kemuri, nobody ever did."}, {"img": "char_nagai-haruto.jpg", "cap": "Nagai Haruto at the north bridge"}, {"p": "At the north bridge a group of kids sat on the concrete steps above the water with cans and phones. They were loud and sitting too close to the edge. A boy of seventeen stood a little apart from them with his hands in his pockets. His black hair had a faded brown inch at the ends, and he was turning something over in one pocket. A river stone. This was Nagai Haruto. Rei knew the name from the ward's talk about the riverside kids. He was the one the others listened to."}, {"p": "As Rei watched, the boy glanced at a patch of water just downstream, as if someone there had spoken. Then he stepped between two of the others, a second before one of them would have shoved the other in. The water was quieter where he stood. Rei, with his registers half up, saw who stood in it with him. A small boy, wet to the shoulders, keeping watch on the same stretch of river, so that no other child would drown there and join him."}, {"img": "ext_riverboy.jpg", "cap": "The drowned boy who keeps watch beside him"}, {"p": "Rei kept walking. Silently, he promised the two of them that he would come back."}, {"p": "Below the embankment, at the riverside memorial, the old woman in the flowered apron was setting out her lanterns. Her name was Okuma Sumi. Rei had heard it from Choi Hana, who read grief for a living and said she worried about the memorial keeper and did not know what to do. Mrs. Okuma looked frailer than she had the night before. She was setting out an extra lantern, the one she kept for the bad anniversaries. The memorial felt warmer with her there. Rei could feel the grief of the March ground leaning toward her the way the lanterns leaned toward the far bank. It was drinking from her, gently."}, {"p": "\"You'll want to hurry,\" she said, without looking up. \"The river's going a long way tonight. I've a mind to go with it one of these years. I gave my good umbrella to the girl next door this afternoon. She'll get more use of it.\""}, {"p": "Rei stopped. He did not have time. He stopped anyway."}, {"p": "\"Will you be here tomorrow night?\" he asked. \"And the night after? My brother's going to need someone to light one for him. I don't know how. I've never done it right.\""}, {"p": "She looked up at him then, properly, with wet, kind eyes. For a moment the warmth around her seemed to hesitate."}, {"p": "\"Somebody has to show you, then,\" she said slowly. \"Don't they.\""}, {"p": "\"Somebody has to,\" Rei said."}, {"p": "Ahead of him the river was doing what Higuchi's most frightened letters had promised. There were two banks where there should be one. Two cities a degree out of line. The seam between them was bright and soft, and it was thinning toward the hour all three of his clocks had been falling toward since the year the lights went grey."}, {"p": "He stopped once, at the turn of the path where he could see his brother. The grey cardigan. The rail. The two kinds of gladness already turning toward the sound of his shoes on the gravel."}, {"p": "And across the river, on the far embankment, it was not Kaito this time. It was a woman in her late thirties in a pale cardigan, with a few grey hairs left in on purpose. She stood very straight with her hands folded. Behind her, coming up the far steps in a slow, silent line, were thirty or more people in mourning clothes. Kanna-sensei had come herself."}, {"p": "Rei let his two suppressed registers come all the way up for the first time in a month. Both sides at once. No half measure. Half measures are for reading, and he was done reading."}, {"p": "The city went double and bright and unbearable and beautiful around him. The lanterns, and their held-breath shadows. The wall, and its soft thin wound. The brother and the Healer in one body. The thing in Fukai Rinka's body, smiling across the water with a dead woman's gentle face. And at the centre of it all, very far down, a tired man in an old coat with his back to the world, holding a door."}, {"p": "The three clocks touched the same hour. Rei stepped onto the embankment."}], "n": 66}, {"title": "The Anchor", "kind": "ending", "src": "end", "blocks": [{"img": "place_sumida_lanterns.jpg", "cap": "The embankment on the last night of Obon, lanterns going out on the river"}, {"p": "All along the river the sending fires were being lit. There were thousands of them. From the embankment the whole of Kita looked like a city of candles laid out on black cloth. The air smelled of smoke and river water and the sweet burnt sugar of a festival stall somewhere behind the houses. On the near bank the smoke went straight up. On the far bank, the one that was not there, the same smoke went up a degree crooked, and something tall and patient leaned over every fire to warm its hands."}, {"p": "Gin turned from the rail. The Healer smiled at Rei with his brother's mouth."}, {"p": "\"You came on time,\" it said. \"I knew you would.\""}, {"p": "Across the water Kanna-sensei had stopped at the top of the far steps. Behind her the mourners of the Far-Shore Circle stood in a line in their dark clothes. There were thirty-five of them, and each one held a framed photograph against their chest. A husband. A daughter. A boy in a school uniform. Kaito stood at her elbow with his eyes rolled up white."}, {"p": "Rei could feel what the line was. All month the Circle had worked like a battery. Every Saturday, new grief had been gathered and stored in those white rooms in Takinogawa. Tonight it was being spent all at once."}, {"p": "\"Uedera-kun,\" Kanna-sensei called across the river. Her voice was soft, and it carried perfectly. It was the voice of a teacher who had never once had to raise it. \"You look so tired. Come and sit with us. There is tea.\""}, {"p": "The wall shivered. The mourners' grief went into it in one long breath. The seam over the river opened another degree. And the chair at its centre, the cold absence Rei had felt from his own flat, leaned toward the far bank like a flame toward an open window."}, {"p": "Rei understood then what Kishin had come for. It had not come to watch, this time. A Qliphoth does not make a feeling of its own. It takes a seat out from under the one holding it and wears the register like a shell. Kishin had been eating Watanabe's seat for months. Tonight, with the ice at its softest, it meant to take the one seat in the city that had no living holder at all. Then it would stand the Tree of Death up on this side of the wall, with itself at the top."}, {"p": "\"There,\" the Healer said quietly, beside him. \"Do you see it now, Rei? That is what I have been waiting for.\""}, {"break": "Frequency"}, {"p": "Rei did not answer it. He did not look at Kishin either. He turned to his brother and did the one thing in Gin's book that nobody had ever taught him."}, {"p": "He listened."}, {"p": "He did not listen to the voice. He listened under it. Gin's body was loud with two things at once, the way a room is loud when two radios are tuned to stations side by side. One of them was calm and even and very old. It was a long clean note of duty that never changed. The other was ragged and frightened and tired and very nearly drowned. And it was humming, under its breath, a song from an old film about a cat bus. They had watched that film together on a borrowed television when Rei was seven."}, {"p": "<em>Finding one consciousness inside a possessed body, as one voice is found in a crowd.</em> That was what Gin's book called Frequency Isolation. Rei had never done it. But he had done the thing underneath it every day of his life without a name for it, sitting in rooms where people were lying to him and hearing the lie. He set everything else aside. The fires. The mourners. The chair. He followed the humming down through the old even note, the way he would have followed his brother's voice through the rush-hour crowd at Ōji station. When he was close enough, he spoke to it directly."}, {"p": "\"Gin,\" he said. \"I'm here.\""}, {"p": "The humming stopped. Under the calm face, something turned its head. Rei felt his brother see him from inside, very small and very far away and completely awake."}, {"p": "Then Rei looked up into the Healer's eyes, and said its name."}, {"p": "He did not say it like an accusation. On the walk down he had thought he would. Now he found that he could not. This thing had worn his brother for a month, and it had kept him alive while it did. It had mended a cut on Gin's hand in the second week that should have needed stitches. It had made the tea. So Rei said the name the way a family thanks a doctor at the end of a long night on a hospital ward, when he is still in his coat and they are still in their chairs and nobody has slept."}, {"p": "\"Tachibana Yushiro.\""}, {"p": "The name did not break it. Tanizaki had been right. The name held it. Rei watched it land. He watched the old even note in his brother's body go still in a new way, the way a hand goes still when someone else's hand is laid over it. The Healer did not leave. It could not leave. It was anchored, held in place in the light by the one person its vessel loved. And for the first time in forty years, someone was calling it by its own name and meant it kindly."}, {"p": "Something in Gin's face changed. It was not the Healer going. It was the Healer staying, and his brother coming up beside it. For a moment both were there at once, and neither had to hide. Gin's eyes. The Healer's stillness. One face."}, {"p": "\"Ah,\" it said, very softly. It sounded almost relieved. \"There it is. Nobody has said that in a long time.\""}, {"p": "\"Tell me the truth,\" Rei said. \"All of it. You can't do anything else now.\""}, {"break": "What the Healer wanted"}, {"img": "ext_sojo.jpg", "cap": "Tachibana Yushiro, the Healer, speaking with Gin's face"}, {"p": "\"I never wanted your chair,\" Tachibana Yushiro said."}, {"p": "It said it simply, in Gin's voice, the way a doctor gives a diagnosis."}, {"p": "\"I died with my hands in a ward full of people who would otherwise have been left. I would not leave them. That is all I am. On the far side there is a seat for what I am. It has stood empty as long as yours has. I mean to take it, and sit in it, and keep it beside the few of my kind I would trust to sit near me. I have no wish to rule the living. I have had my life.\" It looked across the river. \"That one has a different wish. That one means to rule here. To stand the dead tree up on your side of the wall and sit at its crown and keep the living as its harvest. It cannot do that while your chair is filled. And it cannot be killed on this side except by one of us from inside a body. I needed a body. I needed the thin night, so that it would come to the chair in person. And I needed one of you in the chair when it was over.\""}, {"p": "\"Why?\" Rei asked. \"If you didn't want it.\""}, {"p": "\"Because I am a doctor, Rei.\" The Healer smiled with Gin's mouth, and it was a tired, real smile. \"A doctor does not walk off a ward with no one on duty. I could not go to my seat and leave this district with an empty chair and a thinning wall and the dead coming home every summer to a place that could not tell them apart from the living. Someone had to be on duty here. One of you was always going to fit. I did not much mind which. That is the cruelty you saw in me. It is true. I am sorry for it.\""}, {"p": "Inside, Gin was listening. Rei could feel him there, awake, watching the thing in him explain itself, the way he had been watching it work for a month."}, {"p": "\"Let me do it now,\" the Healer said. \"It is coming. Your brother's body is the only knife on this side of the water. I will be quick with it. I will try to give him back.\""}, {"p": "Across the river Kanna-sensei began to walk down the far steps toward the water. The mourners came after her. The seam bent toward them like a bow."}, {"p": "\"No,\" Rei said. \"Nobody gets spent tonight. Not him. Not anyone.\""}, {"break": "Kuze Kaname"}, {"p": "He went down to the edge of the gravel, where the near bank met the doubled water. He did not look at Kishin. He looked down. Through the river. Through the wall. All the way to the centre, where a tired young man in an old coat stood with his back to the world and his arms around a door."}, {"p": "Rei had six lines written on the back of a registry page. He did not need to read them."}, {"p": "\"Your name is Kuze Kaname,\" he said."}, {"p": "Nothing happened. Then something at the very bottom of the light went still. It went still the way a man stops in the street when he hears someone call a name he has not answered to since he was a child."}, {"p": "\"You were born in Edo. You worked for the Twilight Office. Ōmagatoki. You were an investigator. You went out at dusk and looked into what came through.\" Rei's voice shook, and he let it. \"You wouldn't stand on either side of any line. You walked the middle way. You walked it so far you opened every path and every register at once, and there was nothing left of you that was only one thing. And then you sat down in the Duty seat. You became it. You've been holding it for six years on top of everything else, because nobody living came to take it off you.\""}, {"p": "He took a breath that hurt."}, {"p": "\"You answered me once. I asked you what you were holding. You said, the seat I became. My brother said the chair didn't need a new occupant. It needed its old one. He meant you. You're the old one. You never left. You just forgot you were sitting in it.\""}, {"img": "scene_kuze_named.jpg", "cap": "The man in the light turns around"}, {"p": "Down at the centre of the light, the man turned around."}], "n": 67}, {"title": "The Interval", "kind": "main", "src": "end#2", "blocks": [{"p": "For one terrible interval, nobody held the wall."}, {"p": "Rei felt it go. It was not a tearing. It was a letting go, the way a held breath goes out of a body all at once. The veil fell. The two cities slid together and were one city. Every fire on both banks became a single fire. And every dead person in Kita who had come home for the festival was suddenly standing on the streets among the living, where anyone could see them, bewildered and lit from below."}, {"p": "And behind each of the twelve holders, all across Tokyo, a shell rose up."}, {"p": "Rei saw them all at once, because for that interval there was no distance in the city at all. There were thirteen hollow things, each one in the shape of a single feeling at its worst. Each one turned toward a living person who held the same feeling at its best. Two trees with the same fruit. And the living ones, the light ones, felt them come."}, {"p": "In a public bath in Kita, a man of forty-eight set down his shovel at the boiler door. Mori Takao had held Rage for twenty years, and the others deferred to him. He straightened his back. His voice did not travel through the air. It travelled through the seats. <em>Hold,</em> he said, the eldest, and every one of them heard him."}, {"p": "They held."}, {"p": "In a tea house in Kyoto, Asakura Mio put down a cup and kept Grief open and steady with both hands. In a quiet office Sakuma Kei held Love. Nakashima Yuna held Fear at the top of her stairwell, with Okabe Natsumi beside her holding Courage, the two of them linked as they had been for twelve years. Chen Lian held Wonder among the Grid's oldest books. On Sakae Street, Hoshino Haru held Joy over a pot of broth that had been on the stove for eight hours. Kobayashi Emi held Shame. Inoue Satoru stopped walking for the first time in three years and held Hunger standing still. In a room behind a restaurant in Shin-Ōkubo, Park Junho held Longing on his feet, and through the paper door the whole front room was singing. Somewhere in the city Kasumi Shin opened an old book to its first page and held Awe. He was the closest of them all to the man at the centre, who had just turned around."}, {"p": "In Itabashi, in a cardigan two sizes too big, Watanabe Jun held Despair. The shell that rose behind him was not a stranger. It had a teacher's soft face. It had been eating his seat for months. Now it reached for him with both hands."}, {"p": "Thirteen light anchors against thirteen shells. But only twelve of the anchors were lit. The thirteenth seat stood open in the middle of the city like a door, and Kishin's whole weight was leaning toward it."}, {"p": "Rei took the chair."}, {"p": "He did not take it as a throne. He took it as a duty. It was his own register, the one his family had buried in him before he could read. The one Gin had taught him to hide at a kitchen table as a trick for sleeping. The one that did not change because it did not need to. He did not feed the hungry grain in his palms. He did not take anyone's feeling to make himself strong. He opened the single unchanging thing he had always been, and he held it, sane and willing, with himself."}, {"p": "It was heavier than anything he had ever carried. It was also, to his astonishment, familiar. It was the weight of getting up every morning for a brother who did not come home."}, {"p": "Thirteen."}, {"break": "Light anchors"}, {"p": "The moment the last seat lit, the shells had nothing to wear."}, {"p": "A Qliphoth lives by taking an anchor out from under a holder. Thirteen holders, every one of them alive and willing, left no empty seat to climb into. Light anchors can kill demons. Demons can only possess anchors. With every anchor lit, the shells stood in the falling veil like coats with no one inside them."}, {"p": "Kishin did not stand still. It came across the water."}, {"p": "It came in Fukai Rinka's body, walking on the river as if the river were a floor, and despair came with it like weather. Rei felt it hit the bank. Every person on the embankment sat down where they stood. On the far side the mourners dropped their photographs. The despair was aimed at the chair, and at Rei sitting in it. It was the strongest thing he had ever felt. It was a soft voice saying <em>none of it matters, none of it ever did, sit, rest, drink the tea.</em>"}, {"p": "Gin's hands moved."}, {"p": "Right thumb on the left knuckles, one, three, two. The heel of the hand flat on the breastbone. Fingers steepled. Rei saw it and knew it and could have wept. The keys. Under the grey cardigan, the binding on his brother's forearm had been loaded and waiting for six years at a low hum. Now it fired at full strength. The Healer did not stop him. The Healer added its own weight to it. Two of them in one body, the man and the doctor, threw one binding across the gravel together."}, {"p": "It caught Kishin at the water's edge and held it for a breath."}, {"p": "Rei had his hand in the kit bag. Restraining Circle, the second sleeve, drawn on a Monday morning with forty sheets of Kanda washi on the desk. He slapped it down on the wet stones at the edge of the river and felt it fire, with Gin's precision and his own rank behind it. It would not hold a thing like Kishin for long. Gin's book had said so. Against the strongest, every configuration only bought time."}, {"p": "Time was all they needed."}, {"p": "In Itabashi, Watanabe Jun felt the hands on his seat loosen. He was thirty-five and looked fifty and had tried to retire three times. Never in his life had he been less alone in Despair than he was in that interval, with eleven others holding beside him and a new one in the thirteenth seat. He took hold of his own register with both hands and pulled it back. The shell tore off his seat like wet paper."}, {"p": "Kishin screamed."}, {"p": "With the veil fallen, everyone saw it. Rei had not understood that until it happened. There was no wall to hide behind. Thirty-five mourners on the far bank looked at their gentle teacher and saw what was wearing her. The despair register with nothing left to hold. A shape like a coat over a coat, the dead woman's soft face slipping on something that had never had a face. On the bridge above the north stretch of the river, a young man in a paint-stained shirt stood with three phones held up in a fan in front of him. He was filming, and crying, and not stopping. Itsuki. Rei had told him where to stand."}, {"p": "Kishin fled. It went back into Fukai Rinka's body the way a hand goes back into a pocket. It turned, and it ran, and the despair went out of the air behind it like a tide going out. On the far steps Kaito sat down hard, blinking. He started to cry like a boy who has woken from a fever and does not know where he is. He was alive. All of them were alive. The Circle's photographs lay on the steps, and the mourners were picking them up, one by one, and looking at them as if for the first time."}, {"break": "The man at the centre"}, {"img": "finale_two_thirteens.jpg", "cap": "Rei and Kuze Kaname face to face at the centre"}, {"p": "When Rei looked down again, the man at the centre was looking back at him."}, {"p": "Kuze Kaname had his name back. It sat on him oddly, like a coat taken out of the cupboard after a long winter. He was very young. He looked at Rei in the thirteenth seat, and Rei understood, without any words passing between them, that he was being thanked. He was being thanked by a man who had not been allowed to put anything down since the Edo period."}, {"p": "<em>You took the weight,</em> he said. It was the first sentence he had spoken in his own voice in a very long time. He said it carefully, as if checking that his voice still worked."}, {"p": "\"Some of it,\" Rei said. \"You're still holding the rest. Can you hold it as you? Not as the chair?\""}, {"p": "Kaname thought about this with the serious attention of an investigator turning over a piece of evidence. Then he turned back to the wall. But he did not stand as he had stood before, with his back to the world and his whole self gone into the holding. He turned like a man going back to work after a night's sleep. With his name on."}, {"p": "The veil came back."}, {"p": "It came back held by living hands, thirteen of them, and by one man at the centre who was a person again. The two cities slid apart into their proper places. The dead stood a moment longer among the fires. Then they turned and went down to the river, following the smoke home, the way they were meant to. The seam over the Arakawa closed along its whole length like a healed cut. It left only a faint bright line on the water. Rei somehow knew that line would still be there every August."}, {"p": "Rei sat in the chair, and the chair was a weight, and the weight was his. He did not become a weather with a person left over. He was a person carrying a weather. It was not the same thing. He could feel the difference the way Park Junho had felt it, standing."}, {"p": "The Axis, mostly light now, stood with him."}, {"break": "The doctor"}, {"p": "Gin was sitting on the gravel against the rail with his knees drawn up. The Healer was still in him, still anchored, still looking out of his eyes with that serious, tired patience."}, {"p": "\"Well,\" it said. \"There is someone on duty.\""}, {"p": "\"There's someone on duty,\" Rei agreed."}, {"p": "\"And the other one is seen. Running. It will not stand the dead tree up on this side now, with every seat lit and a living tree in the way.\" The Healer was quiet for a moment. \"I wanted it dead. I thought that was the only way to be sure. You have shown me another. I do not have to like it.\""}, {"p": "\"No,\" Rei said. \"Your seat's still there. On the other side. Go and take it. Sit with the ones you trust. Keep that one from climbing over there, if you can. That's a duty too. You don't need him for it.\" He crouched down in front of his brother. \"Please. Give him back.\""}, {"p": "He took the two washi bundles out of his jacket and untied the first. Inside were four outer glyphs and a centre mark. Each had its compass position pencilled on the back in Gin's hand. Rei laid them out on the wet gravel around his brother. North, east, south, west. Gin had prepared three sets of this circle. He had used one, once, in six years, after three months of preparation, and it had worked. The circle fired when all five pieces touched the ground, the centre last. Rei felt it close around his brother like cupped hands. It was not made to banish. Gin's book said the entity inside could not jump to a new host. It could only go deeper, or leave through the open top of the circle."}, {"p": "It was a door. Not a tear. A door, with someone holding it open politely."}, {"p": "Tachibana Yushiro looked at the circle, and at Rei, and something like amusement crossed his brother's face."}, {"p": "\"You are offering me the way out instead of throwing me through it,\" it said. \"That is a physician's manners. Where did you learn them?\""}, {"p": "\"From you,\" Rei said. \"You made the tea.\""}, {"p": "The Healer laughed. It was Gin's laugh, quiet and surprised. Then it laid Gin's hand flat on Gin's own chest for a moment, over the heart, the way a doctor checks a patient before he signs him out."}, {"p": "\"He is very tired,\" it said. \"Feed him. Let him sleep longer than he wants to. He will not be the same as the man who went in. Time inside does things. But he is there, and he is watching you, and he has been proud of you the whole time. I have heard him.\""}, {"p": "It went up and out through the open top of the circle, toward a seat on the far side that had waited for it as long as Rei's had. Rei felt it go. It did not feel like an exorcism. It felt like a man in a white coat walking out of a ward at dawn, after handing over his notes."}, {"p": "Gin fell forward into Rei's arms."}, {"p": "He weighed nothing. He weighed everything. He was cold and shaking, and he smelled of the cardigan and the river and himself. After a long time he said, into Rei's shoulder, in a voice that had not been used properly for a month, \"You burned it. The one in the drawer.\""}, {"p": "\"Yes.\""}, {"p": "\"Good,\" Gin said. \"I didn't want it.\""}, {"p": "Up on the far embankment, the first light came."}], "n": 68}, {"title": "After", "kind": "main", "src": "end#3", "blocks": [{"p": "Kishin was exposed by morning. Itsuki's footage could not show what a camera cannot see. But it showed thirty-five people dropping their photographs at the same moment and backing away from a woman in a pale cardigan, and that was enough to start a file. Higuchi's ledger and Gin's book did the rest. The Far-Shore Circle's house in Takinogawa was found empty. The white rooms were bare. The wall of photographs of the missing was still up. Very quietly, the CVD began to hunt a woman who had been marked alive in an old file. Watanabe Jun slept for eleven hours."}, {"p": "A week later Rei went to meet Okada Shizuka. Obon was over. The lanterns had come down from the doorsteps, and the trains were full again of people coming back from their family graves in the country, with paper bags of local sweets for the office and sunburn on the backs of their necks. Rei walked to Higashi-Jūjō and rode the sky-blue Keihin-Tōhoku line one stop to Ōji. The cicadas in Asukayama were as loud as ever. It was still thirty-three degrees."}, {"img": "scene_okada_mikazuki.jpg", "cap": "Okada and Rei in the back booth of Café Mikazuki"}, {"p": "Okada was waiting in the back booth of Café Mikazuki. It was two floors above the street near the ward office, and the booth sat in a small dead spot where nobody's register could be read. The owner, Maruyama, brought two coffees and went away without being asked. Okada had a second file with her. It was the draft of the Closing, the Office of Public Confidence's plan to catalogue every medium in the city and seal the axis shut. Rei had seen what the axis was now. He had talked to him."}, {"p": "\"Adachi read both files last night,\" Okada said. \"He sat with them for an hour. Then he told me something he has never told anyone. He has known about the Thirteen for twelve years. He met one of them, once, and it retired him from the field. And two years ago he assessed your brother's real rank, in a corridor, in the time it takes to say good morning. He knew exactly what Gin was. He let the suppression run because he thought Gin was safer hidden.\" She turned her cup on its saucer. \"He said he should have gone to him eighteen months ago. He said that twice.\""}, {"p": "\"Where are the files now?\""}, {"p": "\"On the President's desk,\" Okada said. \"He took them up himself this morning.\""}, {"p": "The Director of the Office of Public Confidence was not dismissed. That was not how things were done. She was buried instead. She was buried under the proof, under an inquiry, under a quiet line in the budget that was moved somewhere else. For a while her glass office in Marunouchi was still lit at night, with the radio on. Then one week it was not."}, {"break": "An ordinary life"}, {"p": "Rei lived an ordinary life in Tokyo after that. He also channelled something vast. Both things were true, and the tension between them did not resolve. After a while he stopped needing it to."}, {"p": "Most mornings he was up at five, because Duty got up early. At that hour the ward was blue and quiet. The first trains were starting to run, and their station jingles carried a long way in the empty streets. Crows argued on the rubbish nets. A man in white gloves was hosing down the pavement in front of a pachinko parlour that would not open for hours. Rei walked to the Akebono Bakery by the station, on the night-bus route, just as it opened. Mrs. Kawai sold him an an-pan, a soft bun filled with sweet red bean paste, without asking what he wanted. He sat by the window until it stopped, whatever it was that morning. The weight settling. The city's long, unchanging hum of things people kept doing because stopping would cost too much. Sometimes it took ten minutes. Sometimes a stranger sat down at the next table who carried Love for a living, or Joy, and nodded at him, and said nothing, and they ate their bread together."}, {"p": "When Gin was strong enough to walk that far, the two of them warded the dead market arcade. Gin talked him through every fold. Rei drew the Purification set himself, badly the first time and well the second, under Tanizaki's eye, on a floor with faint chalk ghosts on it. They placed the set on a Sunday when the shutters were down. Over the next weeks the man in the flat above the arcade began to sleep through the night. He was Mr. Tachibana, a salaryman nobody would ever look at twice. His face lost its grey. He bought new plants for the windowsill. He never learned why he had nearly not recovered. Rei did not tell him that his name was the name of a doctor. It was a common name. It had only been a coincidence. Rei had started to like coincidences again."}, {"p": "Mrs. Okuma taught him to light a lantern properly, both the bad-anniversary lantern and the ordinary kind. She came down to the memorial every evening to see whether he was doing it right. He never quite was. So she had to keep coming. By the next March she had stopped talking about the river as a place she was going."}, {"img": "ext_kishi.jpg", "cap": "Mr. Kishi, who never left Tsuru-no-yu"}, {"p": "He went to Tsuru-no-yu at last, the way Gin had been telling him to for years. It was the old public bath behind the market, under a tall brick chimney. In the changing room the floor was made of polished wood boards, and a fan turned slowly under the ceiling. Mrs. Hara looked up from the high desk where she took the money and said, \"Mind the step.\" Then she said to the empty bench by the far tub, \"That's the younger one, Kishi-san. Finally.\""}, {"p": "The far tub was warm with no fire under it. Mr. Kishi had soaked there for forty years. He died in 2009, the same year the thirteenth seat went empty, and he simply stayed. Mrs. Hara was seventy-one. She kept a list of every dead person in the ward and the corner each one had died on. She meant to teach it to one person before she went, so that whoever kept Mr. Kishi company after her would know who he was talking to. She had chosen Rei. He came on Tuesdays."}, {"img": "ext_child.jpg", "cap": "The child in the empty seat at the Suzuran"}, {"p": "One night, unable to sleep with the weight, he went out at two in the morning. The streets were empty. The vending machines on the corners glowed and hummed to nobody. A taxi went by slowly with its red light on, looking for a fare. He walked into the Suzuran, the little bar pressed up against the worst of the ward's thin spots. A cat slept on the till there, and the rule was that nobody pointed at it."}, {"p": "A woman with long ash-grey hair sat at the back in the same coat she wore in every season. She was murmuring to the empty seat beside her. A child sat in it. Only she could see him. Only she, and now Rei. Beside her, nursing a whisky somebody had bought him, sat Sugimoto Hiroshi, the homeless professor, the one person she would talk to."}, {"p": "Rei did not try to clear anything. He sat with the two of them until four, and listened. The second time he came, Sugimoto had got her to say it out loud. There had been a fire, years ago. She had been the neighbour who got out. The thing that needed releasing had never been the child. It was her guilt. It took a month. Then one night the seat beside her was empty, and she went home. The next evening the barman said she had not come in. Rei hoped with his whole heart that it was because she was asleep."}, {"img": "ext_mentor.jpg", "cap": "Leo's teacher, the voice in the club mirror"}, {"p": "He went back to Shin-Ōkubo on a Thursday. He took the green Saikyō line from Jūjō down to Shinjuku and changed to the Yamanote line for one stop."}, {"img": "place_shinokubo_night.jpg", "cap": "Shin-Ōkubo after dark"}, {"p": "The narrow main street of Shin-Ōkubo was packed, as it always was, with teenage girls hunting for posters of their favourite singers, with the smell of grilled meat and spicy rice cakes, and with music pouring out of every shop door. He went to Park Junho's class at the community centre first. Afterwards he went to Eomma's, and he found the young man in the good suit eating ramyeon alone at the corner table again."}, {"p": "His working name was Leo. His real name was Sato. Rei sat down across from him and told him what he was. A medium, untrained and unregistered, who read the room at the club with a gift he had always called intuition. And Rei told him that the voice he sometimes heard in the mirror before a hard table was real. It was a man who had loved him and taught him the trade and wanted him out of it before it ate him too. Leo cried into his noodles, very elegantly. The next time Rei passed the black window at the corner table, there was only one face in it."}, {"p": "And he went back to the north bridge, as he had promised. Nagai Haruto was there with his river stone, keeping the others back from the edge. Rei sat on the steps beside him and asked him, quietly, who he talked to downstream. The boy went very still. Then he told him. The small drowned boy in the shallows had been keeping the same watch for years. Rei told Haruto there was a Grid that would teach him what he was before the CVD found him. He told him that the boy downstream had been doing, for free, all this time, exactly the work the institution said only it could do. Haruto turned the stone over twice in his pocket. Then he said he would think about it. That was how Rei knew he would."}, {"break": "Three"}, {"img": "Rei_Gin_Together.jpg", "cap": "Rei and Gin on a night out in the city"}, {"p": "Gin cooked for three again."}, {"p": "It took him a long time to come back. He was not the man who went in. He was slower. He forgot things, and the forgetting frightened him. Some mornings he stood at the cooker with the pan in his hand and could not remember what he had meant to make. Rei would say <em>curry</em> or <em>the egg thing</em>, and Gin would say <em>yes</em>, as if Rei had handed him back a word. He did not wear the grey cardigan again. He threw it out himself. But he was there. He wrote again, in a hand that stood upright. Sometimes in the evenings he read aloud from the archive in a voice that was entirely his own. If anything in him leaned, Rei never felt it."}, {"img": "char_sasaki-daiki.jpg", "cap": "Sasaki Daiki, Rei's oldest friend"}, {"p": "Daiki came round on the first Friday Gin was well enough to have people. Sasaki Daiki lived two stops away on the line. He was Rei's oldest friend. He had kept texting all month without ever getting a straight answer, and he had never once stopped. He sat down at the kitchen table where the kettle had gone cold on the first morning. Gin put a plate in front of him, and Daiki ate like he had been starving. He had been, a little. He had been worried. He did not ask what had happened. He asked for seconds."}, {"img": "scene_sunday_three_cups.jpg", "cap": "Tanizaki pours three cups"}, {"p": "Tanizaki came on the Sunday. He brought his own tea, because he did not trust theirs. He made it the old way at their table and poured three cups. He pushed one across to each brother and kept the third for himself. Nobody had gone. There was no cup for the absent. He drank, and watched Gin over the rim, and watched Rei, and he did not say anything about it."}, {"p": "That night, after Gin had gone to bed, Rei sat at the desk under the lamp with the archive open in front of him. There was one chapter Gin had never finished. Chapter Fourteen. It was a guide to finding the frequency of a domain anchor, written for someone who would need it. Gin had written it for Rei. Rei picked up the pen and began to finish it, in Gin's format, in his own bad hand."}, {"p": "When he had done the first page he signed it the way Gin signed everything. Then he got up and went to the genkan, and hung both sets of keys on the hook by the door. His and his brother's. Side by side."}, {"p": "<em>R.U. Keys on the hook.</em>"}], "n": 69}]}], "words": 158234, "pages": 575};
